var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Reading-Results-and-Comparing-Models","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nSee the Visualization Gallery for Wright Maps, Pathway Maps and MGMFRM response surfaces with worked reading guidance.\n\nA completed fit produces numbers, but an analysis needs an answer supported by those numbers. Read the output in this order: computational diagnostics, uncertainty for the quantity of interest, predictive adequacy, and sensitivity to defensible assumptions. Model comparison then addresses a specified question.","title":"Reading Results and Comparing Models"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Separate-three-kinds-of-uncertainty","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Quantity What it describes What it does not establish\nPosterior interval Uncertainty about a parameter or contrast conditional on the model, prior and observed data. Repeated-study coverage at every possible fixed truth.\nMonte Carlo standard error, or MCSE Numerical uncertainty in an estimated posterior summary due to a finite MCMC run. Small measurement uncertainty or a correct model.\nSimulation replication standard error Uncertainty in an average performance measure across independently generated datasets. Precision obtained by treating many parameters or correlated draws as independent datasets.\n\nFor example, a wide ability interval with a small MCSE means that its posterior summary is computed precisely but the ability remains uncertain. More adequate MCMC draws can reduce numerical error; they do not replace more informative ratings. A narrow interval also needs diagnostic review and checks of the model and prior before substantive use.\n\nR-hat compares behavior across chains, effective sample size (ESS) describes information in dependent draws, and divergences flag problematic HMC behavior. Inspect bulk and tail ESS, because interval endpoints can be much less precise than a posterior mean. Passing diagnostic thresholds is useful computation evidence, not a proof of convergence, identification, recovery or substantive validity. The fitting guide explains the available diagnostic output.","title":"Separate three kinds of uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#credible-interval-levels","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"A credible interval (CrI) needs both a probability mass and an interval rule. The current summaries and plots use central, equal-tailed intervals (ETIs): a 90% ETI runs from the 5th to the 95th posterior percentile, while a 99% ETI runs from the 0.5th to the 99.5th percentile. Neither 90% nor 95% is a package-wide requirement; defaults differ across functions. Choose a level appropriate to the reporting question and state it explicitly.\n\nFor an existing scalar MFRM fit, set both the main columns and interval list:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\n\nrows99 = posterior_summary(fit;\n    lower = 0.005, upper = 0.995, intervals = (0.99,))\n\n# The top-level columns and this nested entry now both describe 99% ETIs.\nfirst(rows99).lower\nfirst(rows99).upper\nonly(first(rows99).intervals)\n\n# Several levels can be retained together; main columns still use lower/upper.\nrows_all = posterior_summary(fit;\n    lower = 0.005, upper = 0.995, intervals = (0.90, 0.95, 0.99))\n\nlower and upper are quantile probabilities; intervals contains interval masses. Supplying only intervals = (0.99,) adds the requested nested interval but leaves the main lower/upper columns at their default 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. There is no interval = 0.99 keyword for this summary function.\n\nFor experimental GMFRM and MGMFRM, posterior_summary describes raw computational coordinates. Use the qualified model-scale summary for positive loadings and rater consistencies:\n\nmodel_rows99 = BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(fit;\n    lower = 0.005, upper = 0.995, intervals = (0.99,))\n\nFixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM also supports these two summary calls; its direct summary reconstructs model coordinates. Always report the coordinate and units of the quantity being summarized.\n\nThe plotting and response-surface interfaces instead take a single mass:\n\nOutput Explicit 99% request Scope\nParameter interval plot BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit; interval = 0.99) Supported saved-fit types; model coordinates by default.\nWright Map BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit; interval = 0.99) Stable scalar MFRM.\nNumerical response surface BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface(fit; item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", interval = 0.99) Independent fixed-Q MGMFRM; substitute actual labels.\nRendered response surface BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(fit; item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", interval = 0.99) Same surface scope; includes interval-width panels.\n\nLoad CairoMakie for the plotting calls; the numerical surface needs no renderer. Titles and figure notes remain outside images by default. In a Wright histogram, person bars count posterior medians; they are not individual credible intervals. The interval argument applies to the interval summaries, not to histogram bins. Surface intervals concern each conditional probability or expected rating at one ability grid point. They are not simultaneous bands or predictive intervals for individual future scores.\n\nChanging the interval level reuses the existing draws and does not require refitting. Means and medians remain unchanged; 99% ETIs are at least as wide as 90% ETIs computed from the same draws. However, farther-tail endpoints need adequate numerical precision. Request MCSE at the endpoints actually reported:\n\nprecision99 = posterior_mcse(fit; probabilities = (0.005, 0.5, 0.995))\n\nThe default coordinate for this MCSE call matches the identified scalar MFRM or the direct generalized/multidimensional model coordinates. To match a raw GMFRM/MGMFRM summary instead, add parameter_space = :raw_unconstrained. Inspect the returned status and endpoint MCSE together with chain diagnostics; a returned number alone does not establish tail precision. The Stan posterior documentation explains quantile MCSE. Existing 90% simulation coverage results do not become 99% coverage evidence simply by changing an output setting.","title":"Choose 90%, 95% or 99% credible intervals"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#highest-density-intervals","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"BayesianMGMFRM currently has no integrated HDI/HPD summary or plot option. An ETI is already a Bayesian credible interval; it is not an approximation to an HDI. For a continuous unimodal posterior, an HDI selects the shortest interval containing the requested mass. Unlike an ETI, its excluded tail probabilities need not be equal. The two can differ substantially for skewed posteriors.\n\nFor example, a unit-rate exponential posterior has a 90% ETI approximately 0051329957 and a 90% HDI 023026. Both contain 90% probability; they answer different interval-selection questions. The density is highest at zero, explaining why the HDI starts there. With separated modes, a highest-density region can have disconnected pieces; forcing one interval can span low-density values. HDIs also depend on parameterization: computing an HDI for a log loading and exponentiating its endpoints need not give the HDI of the positive loading. Choose the scientific reporting scale first.\n\nThe separate Julia package PosteriorStats.jl provides hdi(values; prob = 0.99) and explicitly documented multimodal methods. Here values must contain draws of one chosen scalar parameter or draw-wise contrast on the intended scale, not a matrix pooling different parameters. This is an optional external calculation, not a BayesianMGMFRM API or dependency; it is not installed or exercised by this guide. Retain the original chain ordering for diagnostics, label the resulting interval as an HDI, and assess its numerical stability. The ETI endpoint MCSE call above does not by itself supply MCSE for estimated HDI boundaries. Changing interval rules cannot repair a poorly explored posterior.","title":"Is a highest-density interval available?"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Interpret-a-contrast,-not-overlapping-marginal-intervals","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Suppose the question is whether P1 has higher communication ability than P2. For each joint posterior draw s, calculate\n\nDelta^(s)=theta_mathrmP1communication^(s)\n             -theta_mathrmP2communication^(s)\n\nSummarize these differences directly. Do not subtract the endpoints of two marginal intervals: the two abilities can be dependent in the posterior. A hypothetical 90% posterior interval 010070 supports a positive difference under the fitted assumptions, but its practical importance requires a justified ability unit and a substantive benchmark. An interval spanning zero does not by itself establish equivalence.\n\nFor raters, a positive severity contrast means the first rater is more severe under the model's sign convention. A consistency ratio describes relative probability concentration; it does not label one rater more reliable or fair. The saved-fit contrast example keeps joint draws, diagnostics and MCSE together without fitting again.","title":"Interpret a contrast, not overlapping marginal intervals"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Ask-what-a-predictive-check-actually-checks","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"A posterior predictive check generates replicated ratings from the fitted model and compares relevant features with the observations. Examine category use and patterns by rater, item and person, not only an overall average. An aggregate match can hide a systematic mismatch in a subgroup. Prior predictive checks perform the corresponding simulation before conditioning on the observed scores, to inspect the implications of the chosen priors. These operations and their different roles are explained in the Stan predictive-check guide.\n\nReproducing existing rating rows assesses conditional fit. It does not establish performance on withheld ratings, new people, new items or new raters. Good score predictions also do not demonstrate that separate loadings and abilities are recovered correctly: different parameter combinations may predict similarly.","title":"Ask what a predictive check actually checks"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Compare-models-against-the-same-prediction-task","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"First state the prediction target. Predicting an additional rating of a known person is different from predicting a new person's work. Then use the same observations, category meanings, split and scoring weights for competing models. If all ratings of a response should be withheld together, keep that response in one fold and check that the remaining training design is still connected and contains the required facet levels.\n\nIn K-fold cross-validation, fit each model to the training portion of each fold and evaluate its held-out ratings. A common score is the sum of log predictive probabilities assigned to the observed held-out categories:\n\nwidehatmathrmelpd\n=sum_ninmathrmheldout\nlogleftfrac1Ssum_s=1^S\nP(Y_n=y_nmidphi^(s)_mathrmtraining)right\n\nHigher scores mean better predictions of those outcomes under that scoring rule. Average probabilities before taking their logarithm; averaging log probabilities is a different quantity. This expression scores ratings individually. Grouping ratings into the same fold does not turn the score into the joint predictive density of a whole response. Equal weight per rating also differs from equal weight per person when rating counts differ.\n\nAs a deliberately invented example, totals -120 and -116 give a difference of 4 in favor of the second model. The difference alone does not show whether the advantage is reproducible or useful. Examine uncertainty appropriate to the sampling/design structure, failures, diagnostic warnings and the magnitude relevant to your decision. Do not treat overlapping training folds as independent study replications. Predictive superiority also does not prove that the larger model's dimensions represent the intended constructs.\n\nWAIC and importance-sampling LOO estimate predictive quantities from fitted draws under their own assumptions. Importance-sampling instability matters; see the official loo example for reference PSIS-LOO and its diagnostics. The package candidate's raw IS-LOO and experimental Hill-smoothed option must not be presented as independently validated equivalents of that reference implementation.\n\nThe current common comparison guide describes saved-fit scoring, explicit refit requests and complete-fold comparison across supported types. This unreleased candidate retains missing folds and warnings. Opt-in delta MCSE measures numerical error, not data-sampling SE. Automatic multi-model refitting, winners and new-level prediction remain unavailable. Family, dimension and Q comparisons have distinct compatibility checks; a returned table does not validate model selection.","title":"Compare models against the same prediction task"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Use-simulations-to-plan-informative-studies","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Simulation studies answer different questions depending on how parameters and data are generated:\n\nQuestion Suitable simulation Evidence to inspect\nDoes the inference procedure reproduce the intended posterior under its own joint prior model? Simulation-based calibration (SBC). Rank behavior across independent prior-generated datasets, accounting for ties and MCMC dependence.\nCan particular abilities, loadings or severity contrasts be estimated under a proposed design? Parameter recovery at specified truths and design conditions. Bias, RMSE, interval width/coverage, diagnostics and failure rates, with replication uncertainty.\nHow often would the proposed design meet a precision or decision requirement? Repeated studies under explicitly justified generating scenarios. The fraction meeting a prespecified criterion, its uncertainty and sensitivity to the scenarios.\n\nIn SBC, draw parameters from the actual prior, generate data conditional on them, fit the same model, and locate each generating value among posterior draws. Across independent datasets, ranks should follow the appropriate discrete uniform distribution when computation is calibrated. MCMC dependence and ties need explicit treatment. A flat-looking histogram is a diagnostic, not proof that all errors are absent or the model describes real ratings. See the Stan SBC guide.\n\nFor design planning, vary features that can change the intended answer: for example, the number of people, items per dimension, raters per response, linking coverage, or missingness. Specify plausible generating values separately from fitting priors, and evaluate the actual target quantity. Assessing one truth cannot establish performance throughout a population of truths. Prior-averaged SBC calibration does not guarantee nominal coverage at every fixed truth.\n\nKeep failed and incomplete planned datasets in the accounting. If difficult datasets disappear, reported precision and coverage can look better than the procedure actually delivers. The current common recovery functions evaluate saved fits against named truths and summarize fixed-truth replications. They are components for this workflow, not a complete design-planning simulator or completed SBC validation.","title":"Use simulations to plan informative studies"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Write-an-answer-that-matches-the-evidence","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"A useful report identifies the question, model family and dimensions, data and rating design, priors and scale constraints, backend and computational controls, diagnostic concerns, and estimates or contrasts with relevant uncertainty. Explain what predictive checks or comparisons tested and what assumptions remain. Retain the saved fit and environment information so that results can be revisited.\n\nFor example: “Under the specified two-dimensional model and priors, the communication contrast was positive, with the reported posterior interval. The interval's numerical precision was assessed separately. Prediction was evaluated for withheld ratings of existing persons, items and raters; this does not establish prediction for new persons.” Add the actual values and warnings from the analysis; this is a reporting template, not an empirical finding.\n\nReturn to Getting Started, follow the runnable examples, or consult the Bayesian Workflow for the detailed sequence of calls.\n\nFor saved alternative loading priors in the normalized MGMFRM candidate, see Would a different prior change my answer? for named contrasts, MCSE, failed variants, interval/ridge views and verified exports.","title":"Write an answer that matches the evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Bias,-reliability-and-a-complete-MFRM-report","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"The reporting question should determine the statistic. “Do two raters give the same scores?”, “Can we distinguish persons?”, and “Does a rater treat one group differently?” are different questions. A single coefficient cannot answer all three. The following assessment covers the current development candidate; an available function is not evidence that every model family supports it.","title":"Bias, reliability and a complete MFRM report"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#What-kind-of-bias-is-being-investigated?","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Separate three meanings:\n\nOverall severity/leniency: a rater tends to score everyone lower/higher. This is a modeled facet main effect, not by itself differential bias.\nDifferential facet functioning (DFF): severity depends on an item, group, response type or occasion, after accounting for the intended model. Examples include rater-by-item interactions and rater-by-group contrasts. An observed group mean difference alone is not evidence of bias.\nEstimator bias: repeated estimates differ systematically from known truth in a recovery study. This is what simulation bias summaries address; it is not evidence that a rater is unfair to an observed group.\n\ndff_report already screens ordinary MFRMFit results. It returns cell counts, expected-score residual contrasts, a local logit approximation, intervals and optional practical-magnitude probabilities. Its scope explicitly excludes fitted DFF effects. The approximation divides score residuals by predictive variance; it is not a new jointly estimated interaction parameter. The existing FACETS procedure also needs to be distinguished: it estimates additional bias terms conditionally after fixing the main estimates. Reproducing that conditional procedure and fitting a fully joint Bayesian interaction model are separate validation tasks.\n\nThe default adjustment=:assignment removes a count-weighted additive surface from the observed cell residuals separately in every joint draw. The reference is the observed allocation: each rating has equal weight, so cell weights equal observed counts, including sparse cells. These are not model likelihood weights, inverse-variance weights, or weights for a balanced target population. Missing cells are reported but never imputed. The score-residual surface and the local cell-logit surface are projected separately.\n\nFor cell counts [9 1; 1 9], the purely additive cell residual surface [0 0.2; 0 0.2] now yields zero interaction residuals. The historical observed- margin arithmetic gives +0.08 for the first row and -0.08 for the second. This is a deterministic arithmetic check, not an empirical finding or a fitted bias estimate. adjustment=:observed_margins reproduces that historical screen with a warning. Default outputs can change even on a balanced table on the local logit scale, because averaging and division by predictive variance do not commute.\n\n# Existing ordinary MFRM fit; this call does not refit the model.\nrows = dff_report(fit_result;\n    terms = (:rater, :item), adjustment = :assignment,\n    interval = 0.99, expected_score_practical_threshold = 0.2)\n# The integrated report forwards the same choice:\nreport = fit_report(fit_result;\n    include_dff = true, dff_terms = (:rater, :item),\n    dff_adjustment = :assignment)\n\nRead contrast_status alongside n_observations, assignment_n_components, assignment_additive_rank, assignment_interaction_df and cell_residual_fraction. A tree-shaped observed-cell graph has no independent interaction contrast. A bridge cell attached to a cycle also has no support for its own interaction, even when the whole table has positive interaction degrees of freedom. Such cells retain their raw residuals but have NaN interaction summaries and :no_independent_cell_contrast; a missing cell is :empty_cell. Disconnected components can support contrasts within themselves, not contrasts between components. min_n flags sparse cells rather than silently changing the reference allocation. Rank/support tolerances and numerical zero cleanup concern floating-point arithmetic, not statistical significance or a practical margin.\n\nIntervals propagate the fitted base model's parameter uncertainty conditional on the existing scores and assignment. They are not predictive intervals for another rating study, sampling confidence intervals, or fitted DFF-parameter intervals. Use n_expected_score_dff_draws and n_logit_dff_draws to check availability. An undefined local logit shift in any observed cell makes that entire term's logit projection unavailable for that draw. Partial availability remains flagged; its finite-draw summaries must not conceal missing numerical support. Additive projection does not remove every possible composition effect within cells or identify a causal mechanism, and it supplies no calibrated significance test.\n\nA defensible bias report should specify the contrast and direction, practical margin, logit and/or score-scale effect, interval, cell support and linking, assignment adjustment, prior/shrinkage assumptions, and the complete set of comparisons. If many interactions are screened, marginal posterior probabilities do not automatically provide familywise or false-discovery error control. Unexpected residuals may reflect multidimensionality, dependence or assignment, not a uniquely identified bias mechanism. Neither a flag nor an interval excluding zero by itself establishes unfairness.\n\nFor a future fitted interaction API, begin with an identified MFRM interaction model, explicit row/column constraints and partial pooling, then validate sparse and unbalanced designs, reference contrasts, and practical decision accuracy. Group DIF also requires separating group ability distributions from item effects; adding every possible interaction can destroy that identification. Extend to GMFRM/MGMFRM only with a declared response equation, dimension and reference population. Multipliers change the relation between latent contrasts and score changes, so a common raw-logit interpretation cannot be assumed.","title":"What kind of bias is being investigated?"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Reliability,-separation-and-Strata","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"The conventional separation summary uses a declared variance of estimated facet measures V and mean squared measurement SE E. Its positive-part convention is A = max(V - E, 0), giving\n\nG=sqrtAEqquad R=fracAA+Eqquad H=frac4G+13\n\nHere G is separation, R separation reliability, and H Strata. For example, G=2 gives R=0.80 and H=3. Specify sample/population variance, error definition, weighting and extreme-score treatment. The FACETS summary definitions make these distinctions. Strata is an approximate resolution index; it does not identify actual latent classes, assign persons to three groups, or establish classification accuracy at a substantive cutoff. Its derivation uses distribution and precision assumptions; see Wright and Masters. A value below one should not be silently relabeled as an established class count.\n\nRater separation reliability is not inter-rater agreement. High rater separation means severity differences are distinguishable relative to their estimated error. Low separation can reflect similar severities or insufficient information. It is not proof of equivalence. Person separation can be useful for distinguishing people but depends on population spread, design and measurement precision. See the FACETS reliability explanation. Do not equate a GMFRM/MGMFRM consistency multiplier with any of these coefficients.\n\nThe current separation_reliability_summary exists for ordinary MFRM, but its historical algorithm is a screen requiring methodological revision, not a validated FACETS or EAP reliability estimator. It computes cross-person variance within each joint posterior draw, subtracts average marginal posterior variance, truncates at zero, and summarizes the ratios. Finite counterexamples expose why that matters:\n\nFour artificial draws [-1 -1; -1 1; 1 -1; 1 1] give identical posterior means for both persons, yet reported mean reliability is 1/6, about 0.167.\nWith a person contrast fixed at 2 in every draw, changing only a common random location shift changes reported reliability from about 0.993 to 0.\n\nThese are tests of the formula, not fitted posterior samples or a universal replacement definition. They prevent treating the result as an established measure of rank resolution. No reporting Strata API was found, and applying (4G+1)/3 to this historical screen would inherit its unresolved meaning.\n\nThe candidate now provides BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability for an explicit finite-cohort target, described below. This does not make it a general population, test–retest or generalizability coefficient. Population reliability requires the corresponding population and response distribution. Nuisance-parameter uncertainty must also be handled; Yang et al. demonstrate why ignoring item-calibration uncertainty can overstate score precision.\n\nFor multiple dimensions, report precision/reliability separately by declared dimension. A composite w'θ requires justified weights and full covariance: Var(w'θ | y) = w' Cov(θ | y) w. An average of marginal reliabilities is not a composite reliability. Strata should be offered only for a justified scalar measure with a validated separation definition, not as a single number for an arbitrary multidimensional model.","title":"Reliability, separation and Strata"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#An-explicit-posterior-reliability-target","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Choose the people, items or raters whose locations you want to distinguish. For one selected dimension and normalized level weights w_i, let X_i be either the fitted location (target=:absolute) or its difference from the selected cohort's weighted mean in the same joint draw (target=:centered). With m_i = E(X_i | y) and v_i = Var(X_i | y), define\n\nbar m=sum_i w_i m_iqquad\nB=sum_i w_i(m_i-bar m)^2qquad\nW=sum_i w_i v_iqquad R_mathrmcohort=fracBB+W\n\nThis has a direct interpretation by the law of total variance. Independently select a level I with probability w_i and a joint posterior draw. The variance of X_I is exactly B + W: variation in posterior means plus average posterior uncertainty. R_cohort is the fraction due to the first component. The EAP formula in TAM's official documentation has the same ratio structure; this implementation explicitly defines its cohort, centering and joint uncertainty rather than claiming estimator identity.\n\nFor target=:centered, let C be the posterior covariance matrix of the selected locations and A = I - 1w'. The transformed covariance is A*C*A'. Centering joint draws computes this transformation implicitly, including all off-diagonal terms. Adding any draw-specific shift to every selected location leaves the centered result unchanged. The absolute result deliberately retains that uncertainty. Neither target is chosen automatically.\n\nTwo equal posterior means give B=0 and R_cohort=0 when W>0. If all selected contrasts are exactly fixed in the supplied draws, centering can give W=0 and R_cohort=1; the result flags empirical zero variance and withholds delta MCSE. A constant or stuck sampler is not proof of perfect measurement. If both components are zero, reliability is undefined and returned as missing.\n\nThe eight common saved-fit types share one entry. Use the inventory to select estimated locations in a single facet/dimension:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\nfit_result = load_fit_cache(\"my-fit.jls\") # trusted local cache\ncatalog = BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit_result)\npeople = filter(r -> r.block == :person && !r.fixed, catalog.rows)\nchosen_dimension = first(people).dimension # inspect its dimension_label first\nparameters = [r.parameter for r in people if r.dimension == chosen_dimension]\n\nabsolute = BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability(\n    fit_result; parameters, target=:absolute,\n)\nrelative = BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability(\n    fit_result; parameters, target=:centered,\n)\nrelative.reliability\nrelative.between_variance\nrelative.within_variance\nrelative.mcse\nrelative.sampling_warning\n\nBy default every selected level receives equal weight, regardless of rating count. Optional positive weights follow the order of parameters; changing the selection or weights changes the target. Fixed anchors are rejected rather than treated as perfectly measured members. Derived estimated constraints remain eligible. Loadings, category steps, mixed facets and mixed dimensions are rejected. See examples/saved_posterior_reliability.jl in the candidate source for the cache-only wrapper; this file is not yet released.\n\nThe calculation uses normalized probability moments across levels and retained draws, with no sample-variance correction. The returned ratio is one functional of the posterior, not one value per draw from a posterior distribution of reliability. Consequently it has no reported CrI. Its optional numerical MCSE uses a first-order expansion, retaining the original complete chains. If d_i(s)=X_i(s)-m_i, the influence is\n\npsi(s)=fracW2sum_i w_i(m_i-bar m)d_i(s)\n-Bsum_i w_i(d_i(s)^2-v_i)(B+W)^2\n\nThe existing autocorrelation-aware estimator applied to psi supplies MCSE. It is unavailable at B=0 or W=0, with insufficient chains/draws or degenerate influence. It does not remove finite-Monte-Carlo bias, establish convergence, or measure repeated-study uncertainty; no automatic precision cutoff is applied. The saved source/model/prior identities and sampling warnings remain in the output.\n\nA large ratio can reflect a wide selected cohort or informative priors, not just better data. Do not use it alone to rank models, certify validity, plan a new rating design or claim rater agreement. Calibration uncertainty is retained when it is present in the saved joint draws; uncertainty omitted by fixed calibration cannot be recovered here. Strata is intentionally not derived from this ratio. The historical separation_reliability_summary now emits a migration warning; its numerical outputs remain unchanged for reproducibility.","title":"An explicit posterior reliability target"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Saved-simulation-example","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"The first planned R1 block of the existing fixed-facet assessment, at loading-prior SD 0.50, supplies 4,000 retained joint draws. Reusing that one saved fit gives:\n\nSelected cohort Target Posterior reliability Numerical MCSE\n50 persons, dimension 1 Absolute location 0.777329 0.001184\n50 persons, dimension 1 Centered location 0.794889 0.001053\n50 persons, dimension 2 Absolute location 0.901848 0.000723\n50 persons, dimension 2 Centered location 0.920046 0.000510\n5 raters Absolute severity 0.989180 0.000180\n5 raters Centered severity 0.989180 0.000180\n\nRemoving the selected persons' shared location uncertainty reduces W, so the centered ratio increases. The rater results agree because these equally weighted severities already have a sum-to-zero constraint. Their high ratio indicates distinguishable severity differences, not high score agreement.\n\nThis is a descriptive saved-simulation example, not an empirical application, a prior recommendation or validation of population reliability. The source study retains all 192 planned/completed fits, including eight unqualified fits; this single example adds zero fits and zero independent replications. The saved fit's sampling checks passed, but numerical MCSE is not interval calibration or a scientific acceptance decision. Download the numerical summary and provenance.","title":"Saved simulation example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Agreement-and-practical-decisions","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"rater_homogeneity_summary already provides paired severity contrasts, optional ROPE probabilities and support information for ordinary MFRM. It explicitly states that severity homogeneity is not observed score agreement. It also separates fixed anchors from estimated contrasts. rater_overlap counts shared units; it does not calculate an agreement percentage.\n\nThe candidate now provides BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement across the eight common saved-fit types. It answers: How often did these raters give the same score to the same response and criterion, and how much agreement does the fitted model expect? It does not ask whether rater severity parameters differ.\n\nagreement = BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement(fit_result;\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\",\n    observation_ids=rating_ids,      # unique rating IDs, in fitted-row order\n    response_ids=performance_ids,   # same performance can have several ratings\n    adjacent=1, interval=0.99,\n    se_unit=:person)\nagreement.overall.exact\nagreement.rater_pairs\nagreement.unmatched_observation_ids\n\nSupply globally unique performance identities from the data collection records; they may repeat across raters and criteria. If the fitted data already carry response_id metadata, omit response_ids. Supplying conflicting identities is an error. The API also checks person and every recorded optional context field, including occasion/task/form, and rejects duplicate response–item–rater rows. Matching by person and item alone can join different performances, so it is never inferred. Unrecorded context cannot be checked; callers must verify that the IDs represent the same performance and scoring occasion. Repeated independent scoring rounds require distinct response/occasion identities, not silent averaging.\n\nEach shared response–item and unordered rater pair contributes one opportunity. Three raters on one criterion create three opportunities. Overall results weight these opportunities equally; they do not average pair percentages equally. Each rater's denominator counts its incident opportunities, so per-rater denominators sum to twice the overall denominator. All fitted rater pairs appear, including zero-overlap pairs with missing rates. The opportunity ledger preserves both rating IDs, scores and response/item/person identity. Thus a selective assignment cannot be mistaken for universal agreement: report coverage and denominators too.\n\nExact agreement compares original scores. adjacent=1 includes exact agreement and a gap of at most one ordinal category position. A tolerance covering the entire scale always gives one and receives a visible structural flag; it is uninformative about rater agreement. Categories are not recentered at each rater's lowest observed PCM score. This can differ from the category alignment in the FACETS agreement report.\n\nFor an opportunity involving raters A and B, each joint posterior draw supplies category probabilities. Conditional independence gives expected exact agreement sum_k p_A(k) * p_B(k); adjacent agreement sums products for all permitted category pairs. Average these probabilities across opportunities within each draw, then summarize those aggregate draws. Shared posterior parameter dependence is retained: if both raters have probabilities (0.9, 0.1) in one draw and (0.1, 0.9) in the other, expected exact agreement is 0.82, whereas multiplying their marginal mean probabilities gives 0.50. The latter answers a different question.\n\nThere are three distinct uncertainty statements:\n\nexpected_lower and expected_upper are equal-tailed posterior intervals for the conditional expected agreement rate for these fitted responses and raters. Choose interval=0.99 for a 99% interval. They are not predictive intervals for realized future agreement or frequentist CIs for the observed rate.\nmcse reports chain-aware numerical uncertainty in the expected-rate posterior mean and quantiles. Short or degenerate chains keep unavailable flags, alongside the fit's sampling warnings. MCSE does not establish convergence or calibration.\nOptional se_unit=:person estimates an observed-rate sampling SE by keeping all opportunities from the same person together. With M opportunities, G persons, overall rate p, and person totals z_g = sum_j (I_gj - p), it computes sqrt(G/(G-1) * sum_g z_g^2) / M. This is a ratio-linearization approximation under independent, exchangeable person clusters, conditional on these raters and items. It accommodates unequal numbers of opportunities per person, but does not adjust arbitrary crossed dependence or sampling-design weights. Fewer than two persons is unavailable and zero empirical cluster variance is flagged. The default se_unit=:none makes no observed-rate SE claim.\n\nNo observed-rate CI, realized predictive interval, kappa, automatic cutoff or reliability correction is produced. The observed-minus-expected mean difference is descriptive, not a p-value or a chance-corrected agreement coefficient.","title":"Agreement and practical decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Choosing-an-ICC","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"An ICC answers a different question from the proportion of exactly matching scores. For example, two raters can rank performances identically while one always awards a point more. Consistency can then be perfect although absolute agreement is lower. Choose the definition from the intended use of the scores, not from which coefficient is larger.\n\nUse BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation for one explicit item and complete crossed ratings: every response has one rating from the same raters. The initial candidate computes an observed-score ANOVA point estimate, with no fitting and no confidence/credible interval or SE. These choices are required:\n\nChoice Question answered\ndefinition=:agreement Do scores agree, counting systematic rater offsets?\ndefinition=:consistency Do scores vary together after removing additive rater offsets?\nunit=:single What is the reliability of one rating?\nunit=:average What is the reliability of the mean of all K recorded raters?\nmodel=:two_way_random Are the raters treated as a sample from a larger rater population?\nmodel=:two_way_mixed Is the target this fixed rater panel?\nscore_scale=:interval Can equal numerical score gaps be justified substantively?\n\nThe fixed/random distinction changes the scope of generalization, while the point formulas for a given definition and unit coincide. The design must justify that interpretation; naming a random model does not establish representative rater sampling. See the psych author's ICC documentation for this distinction and Liljequist et al. (2019) for the ANOVA formulation and systematic rater differences.\n\nThe complete no-fit example compares all four definition/unit combinations. The API page shows how to supply response identities and inspect the ANOVA table.\n\nNegative estimates are retained when the denominator is positive. A nonpositive denominator produces an unavailable coefficient with an explicit status; constant scores do not prove reliability. Inspect the returned ANOVA table. The ICC API gives the four equations and the component/denominator interpretation.\n\nThe equal-gap assumption is not automatic for ordinal rubric codes. Use the ordinal alpha operation below when agreement under an ordinal distance is the intended question; it is not an interchangeable estimate of the same ICC target. No severity adjustment, trait-dimensional averaging, item pooling, arbitrary future rater-count projection or population reliability guarantee is supplied. The mean-score target refers to the actual K raters, not a planned replacement panel. Interpreting it for future random panels additionally requires the declared sampling model.\n\nIncomplete or uneven rosters and duplicate response-item-rater cells are rejected, without imputation or listwise deletion. Responses shared by raters must refer to the same performance and context, as for score agreement. Multiple performances from one person remain separate response units; n_people and repeated_person_units disclose that dependence. A point estimate is not evidence for independent sampling. Entirely unobserved responses cannot be recovered from these data. One-way ICC, sparse-design mixed models and uncertainty estimation remain outside this candidate's scope.\n\nFor a saved fit, put the same choices under reporting.icc with shared dataset and rating IDs. The intraclass_correlation report section includes summary, ANOVA and response-ledger tables in the public JSON/Markdown bundle. The statistic uses ratings rather than posterior draws; computing it does not remove or repair sampler warnings in other sections. Report the design, item, score-scale rationale, all three ICC choices, N/K and absence of an interval alongside the value.","title":"Choosing an ICC"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Observed-score-alpha","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"To describe chance-corrected agreement on one criterion, use BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha. This reads FacetData directly; it needs no MCMC fit. Select one item label and a measurement level explicitly:\n\nalpha = BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha(data;\n    item=\"fluency\", level=:ordinal, dataset_id=\"speaking-study\",\n    observation_ids=rating_ids, response_ids=response_ids)\nalpha.alpha\nalpha.status\nalpha.marginal_counts\nalpha.singleton_observation_ids\n\nUse :nominal when categories distinguish kinds and :ordinal when their declared order is meaningful. Category labels in FacetData are consecutive integers; nominal analysis uses them only as codes. Ordinal analysis does not assume equal numeric gaps. A response ID denotes one performance/occasion and must agree with the recorded person/context. Different items are not pooled; the meaning of the selected criterion still requires substantive justification.\n\nFor pairable units, the implementation evaluates alpha = 1 - observed_disagreement / expected_disagreement. With category counts n_cu in unit u and m_u >= 2 ratings, coincidence entries are O_ck = sum_u n_cu * (n_ku - I(c=k)) / (m_u-1). Their margins n_c count pairable ratings, with N = sum_c n_c. Expected coincidences are E_ck = n_c * (n_k - I(c=k)) / (N-1). Each disagreement is the sum of coincidences times squared distances, divided by N. Nominal squared distance is one for different categories and zero otherwise. Ordinal squared distance is the squared difference between cumsum(n_c) - n_c/2 midpoints. This differs from squared category-code distance. See the author's computational reference, sections C–D.\n\nAbsent ratings are omitted rows; literal missing scores are not accepted by FacetData. Singletons remain in the returned unit ledger but do not affect either disagreement or the ordinal distances. Entirely unobserved responses cannot be counted without an allocation roster. :no_pairable_units and :zero_expected_disagreement both return missing alpha. Thus identical scores everywhere do not yield an informative reliability coefficient. Negative alpha is retained. The result includes both coincidence matrices and distances for inspection; unlike the exact-agreement rate, it does not weight every rating pair equally when units have different numbers of raters.\n\nFor an existing fit, add alpha=(item=\"fluency\", level=:ordinal, response_ids=response_ids) to fit_report's reporting request, with the shared dataset/rating IDs. Its krippendorff_alpha section survives public report save/reload. Sampler warnings remain attached to the fit, while this observed-data coefficient does not depend on its posterior draws. This is not severity-adjusted agreement, an ICC, a posterior reliability ratio or proof of construct validity. No SE, confidence interval, missingness correction or automatic acceptable-value cutoff is provided; population uncertainty needs a separately justified sampling design and resampling method. An observed rate outside the expected-rate posterior interval is not, by itself, a calibrated lack-of-fit test: that interval excludes realized-score variation, and these same ratings were used to fit the model. All original retained draws are used. Explicit prediction-cell and rater-pair budgets reject oversized jobs before extraction; the API never silently samples draws or drops pairs to meet those budgets.\n\nHigh observed agreement beyond a model's expectation warrants investigation; it does not uniquely diagnose copying or halo. Report uncertainty at the response/person level when pairs share responses. ICC or weighted kappa may be useful complementary summaries when their design, scale and agreement target are appropriate, but cannot be substituted for facet separation. For pass/fail or ranking decisions, report the relevant posterior decision probabilities and conditional precision near the cutoff, not Strata alone.","title":"Observed-score alpha"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Saved-simulation-example-2","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"This example reuses the same B01/R1, loading-prior SD 0.50 fit used above: 4,000 retained draws for 50 persons, five items and five raters. The recorded generator draws one score per person–item–rater cell on one simulated occasion. We explicitly declare each simulated person–item cell a common assessment; that declaration would require evidence from data collection in an empirical file. All 1,250 ratings are matched. Ten rater pairs each contribute 250 opportunities, giving 2,500 overall opportunities; each rater participates in 1,000.\n\nAgreement definition Observed rate Model-expected mean 95% posterior interval for expected rate\nExact (911 of 2,500) 0.364400 0.375509 [0.361266, 0.389984]\nWithin one category, including exact (2,003 of 2,500) 0.801200 0.802674 [0.783463, 0.820635]\n\nThe optional observed-rate person-cluster SEs are 0.015176 and 0.012846, respectively. Numerical MCSEs for the two model-expected means are much smaller: 0.000125 and 0.000164. The distinction matters: additional MCMC draws may reduce numerical error but do not create additional independent persons or ratings. Expected-rate intervals represent posterior parameter uncertainty; neither these intervals nor the observed SEs are a realized predictive interval.\n\nThis single saved simulation demonstrates the workflow, not empirical rater quality, predictive calibration or a recommended prior. The original assessment retains its full denominator of 192 planned/completed fits, 184 qualified; this example adds no fit or independent replication. It was selected as the first planned R1 block at the middle prior width, not for favorable agreement. Download the numerical summary, all pair rows and source provenance.","title":"Saved simulation example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Category-functioning-across-model-families","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Use BayesianMGMFRM.category_report to ask whether the declared categories are used, what their transitions mean in this model, and how category probabilities change for a specified item and rater. It reads the eight common saved-fit types without refitting. The ownership of a step is determined by the fitted model; the report cannot change that ownership after estimation.\n\nFitted category model Owner of centered category steps Conditional adjacent-category crossing\nOrdinary MFRM rating scale Shared across items and raters Item position + rater severity + shared step\nOrdinary MFRM PCM Item Item position + rater severity + item step\nScalar GMFRM Rater Item position + rater severity + rater step\nMGMFRM and canonical fixed-Q multidimensional MFRM Item Item position + rater severity + item step, minus contributions of the fixed other abilities, all divided by the plotted dimension's loading\n\nThese are the implemented families, not every possible PCM/GPCM specification. In scalar GMFRM, the item-discrimination and rater-consistency product multiplies the whole adjacent-category contrast. For given position/step values it changes steepness, not the crossing. MGMFRM instead puts item loadings on abilities and uses the literal 1.7 rater scale; fixed-Q MFRM retains its unit scale. These conventions and centering constraints are recorded in model_contract and step_contract. Compare models only after checking their category equations and identification.\n\nusage_rows retain every declared category overall, per item and per rater, including unused categories. Counts use actual rating rows as the denominator; they do not give equal weight to persons or correct an unbalanced allocation. The threshold_levels field lists the step-owner groups that actually contributed to each row. For example, a GMFRM item row can combine several raters' different step systems. Rater differences in raw category use can reflect the abilities and items allocated to them, so they do not alone diagnose centrality or bias.\n\nEach row's expected summary describes the posterior distribution of its conditional expected category proportion. Its SD and interval propagate parameter uncertainty. They omit the additional variation from realizing a new set of ratings. By contrast, the existing ordinary-MFRM category_functioning_summary simulates replicated category proportions and retains that predictive interpretation. The two intervals answer different questions; observed-minus-expected is neither a p-value nor a calibrated lack-of-fit test.\n\nthreshold_rows contain the centered transition parameters, excluding the dummy reference-category zero, with posterior SD, pointwise equal-tailed intervals, joint-draw step gaps and ordering probabilities. Posterior SD is not a repeated-sampling SE. A reversed pair is a review prompt, not an automatic category-collapse rule. Constant sampled ordering indicators do not establish zero Monte Carlo error or certain ordering. Check mcse, mcse_warning and sampling_warning alongside any summary.\n\nFor a two-dimensional fit, a conditional profile can be requested as follows. Replace item/rater labels with their exact stored values. For a unidimensional fit, omit fixed_abilities; for more dimensions, supply every other dimension.\n\ncategories = BayesianMGMFRM.category_report(fit_result;\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    interval=0.99,\n    profiles=[(;id=\"item-1-rater-1\", item=\"I1\", rater=\"R1\",\n        dimension=1, ability_grid=range(-3, 3; length=41),\n        fixed_abilities=Dict(2 => 0.0))])\ncategories.usage_rows\ncategories.threshold_rows\ncategories.curve_rows\ncategories.crossing_rows\n\ncurve_rows are plot-ready pointwise category probabilities. A profile is a slice at the stated other abilities, not an average over them or a population curve. A grid does not establish empirical support. To compare items or raters, add separately named profiles; preserve their conditions in the figure caption, outside the image. An item that does not load on the plotted dimension has a flat curve and no unique crossing along that dimension.\n\ncrossing_rows calculate adjacent equal-probability ability values in each joint posterior draw before summarizing. This preserves associations among positions, steps and loadings; a ratio of posterior means would generally differ. With three or more categories, equality of two adjacent probabilities need not mean that either probability is 0.5. Centered steps, these conditional crossings and expected-score half-points are distinct quantities.\n\ninterval=0.99 applies a pointwise 99% equal-tailed interval to expected use, steps, gaps, probabilities and crossings. This API does not calculate HDIs or simultaneous bands. Results condition on the fitted model and its calibration policy; fixed anchors omit external calibration uncertainty. All original draws, chain layout, observation IDs, source identity and warnings are retained. Resource limits fail explicitly rather than selecting a subset of draws. The optional fit_report(...; reporting=(...)) integration retains these definitions in a separate category_report section. Neither entry point establishes category calibration, a new-rater guarantee or model validity.","title":"Category functioning across model families"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Saved-draw-category-example","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"The same simulated B01–R1 assessment used above has 1,250 ratings, four declared categories and 4,000 retained draws. The existing normalized MGMFRM fit gives:\n\nCategory Observed count Observed proportion Expected proportion, posterior mean Pointwise 99% interval for expected proportion\n1 288 0.230400 0.231021 [0.210600, 0.251277]\n2 327 0.261600 0.261749 [0.234347, 0.289999]\n3 347 0.277600 0.275724 [0.247963, 0.305068]\n4 288 0.230400 0.231506 [0.207982, 0.255021]\n\nThese are in-sample expectations, not an independent category-calibration check. Similar observed and expected proportions do not establish good fit. The next table illustrates why an item-centered step should not be labelled an ability crossing. It selects I1 and R1, fixes the other dimension's ability at zero, then varies the dimension on which I1 loads.\n\nTransition Centered I1 step, posterior mean I1/R1 ability crossing, posterior mean Pointwise 99% crossing interval\n1 → 2 -1.175840 -5.827442 [-9.383504, -3.608826]\n2 → 3 0.166463 -3.273050 [-5.337658, -2.007449]\n3 → 4 1.009378 -1.676133 [-3.141294, -0.709509]\n\nThe downloadable report contains nine profiles: all five items with R1, plus I1 with R2–R5. Each varies the relevant ability at −2, 0 and 2 and fixes the other ability at zero. Crossings outside that display grid remain reported; the grid does not truncate their intervals or imply observed support there.\n\nThe saved fit has no sampling warning in its stored diagnostic summary, and there are no nonfinite numerical summaries. Nevertheless, mcse_warning=true: all 4,000 sampled indicators for each of the ten step-order comparisons equal one, so their mean MCSE is unavailable (degenerate_draws). This is not a claim of zero error or certain ordering. Per-quantity MCSE remains in the download. The original study denominator remains 192 planned/completed fits, 184 qualified. Selection is the first planned R1 block at the middle prior width, not a favorable category result; no fit or independent replication was added. Download the counts, steps, conditional curves, crossings and source provenance.","title":"Saved-draw category example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Reading-one-integrated-report","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Use the Saved-fit Reporting API to include category, reliability and agreement results in one fit_report bundle. The integration retains each statistic's target and uncertainty definition: expected category-use CrIs omit realization noise; a reliability estimate has numerical MCSE but no reliability CrI or Strata; agreement separates observed rates/optional observed-rate SE from conditional expected-rate posterior intervals. A common report format does not make these uncertainty measures interchangeable.\n\nNamed reliability cohorts are evaluated separately. A failed cohort does not erase successful ones; its failure makes report execution incomplete. Inspect the named error rows in a partially failed report before correcting a request. A report can also be execution-complete while containing MCMC warnings, unavailable MCSE, zero-overlap pairs or unrequested analyses. Inspect both section status and quantity-level warnings before interpreting results. JSON, tables and Markdown retain the relevant identities and limitations, and saved-fit replay needs no new estimation.","title":"Reading one integrated report"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Download-an-integrated-example","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"The same B01–R1 saved assessment above now has one public report bundle combining nine category profiles, agreement on its shared simulated person–item responses, and two reliability cohorts. Each cohort deliberately selects all 50 fitted persons in one dimension with equal weights and target=:centered.\n\nSelected cohort Posterior reliability Numerical MCSE\nAll 50 persons, dimension 1 0.794889 0.001053\nAll 50 persons, dimension 2 0.920046 0.000510\n\nThese are finite-cohort functionals, not population/design reliability or Strata; the MCSE measures numerical approximation error, not a posterior interval or repeated-sampling SE. Category intervals in this example are 99% equal-tailed, whereas agreement intervals are 95%, matching the earlier saved standalone examples. Both definitions remain visible in their own rows.\n\nThe report is execution-complete and reuses all 4,000 draws from 1,250 ratings. Its category-order MCSE warning remains visible because all saved order indicators are one; combining sections does not remove that warning. Category and agreement results have exactly the same standalone-result hashes as the earlier examples. No new fitting or independent simulation was performed; the originating study still accounts for 192 completed fits, 184 qualified.\n\nDownload the JSON, Markdown and table bundle and its example summary and source provenance. After extracting the archive, use load_fit_report_bundle(\"assessment-report\"; require_complete=true) to verify and read it. Keep the fit cache separately if you need to recompute or change requests; a report bundle is a result, not a saved sampler state.","title":"Download an integrated example"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#What-should-normally-be-reported?","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"Use the following as a question-based reporting framework. Not every exploratory index is mandatory in every paper, and unavailable results should remain explicit.\n\nReporting component What the reader needs Current candidate status\nData and rating design Persons/items/raters/responses; category counts; ratings per unit; missingness; assignment, linking and rank/identification concerns Design validation, rating_design_audit and rater_overlap provide components. Common-response metadata are essential for repeated performances.\nModel and scale MFRM/GMFRM/MGMFRM, dimensions/Q, category-step ownership, constraints/anchors, priors, fixed versus estimated parameters Specification, model metadata and reports exist; support differs by family.\nMeasures and uncertainty Person ability, item position, rater severity, steps, and estimated loadings/consistency; units, SDs and interval mass/method Posterior summaries and common saved-result adapters exist. Fixed values must not appear as empirical certainty.\nScore interpretation Raw averages versus adjusted expected scores; reference persons/items/raters and their weights fair_average_summary supports ordinary MFRM on a balanced reference grid; this is not automatic population standardization.\nFit and unexpected ratings Infit/outfit, observed–predicted residual patterns and support; rationale for any flag fit_stats provides ordinary MFRM posterior summaries. facets_compatibility_stats supplies a separate approximate plug-in MnSq/ZSTD table, not FACETS JML identity. No universal cutoff or automatic deletion follows.\nCategory functioning Use, steps, ordering uncertainty, probability curves and item/rater differences category_report gives owner-aware steps, expected use and conditional curves across the common saved-fit types. Ordinary-MFRM category_functioning_summary retains replicated-use intervals. These targets differ; a disordered step alone does not justify merging categories.\nRater behavior and DFF Overall severity, pairwise contrasts, substantive margins, cell counts, assignment and multiplicity limitations Severity homogeneity and residual DFF screening exist for ordinary MFRM. Joint fitted DFF remains a gap.\nScore agreement Matched performance/criterion/occasion, exact/adjacent definition, full pair denominators, observed and model-expected rates, appropriate uncertainty rater_agreement supports the common saved-fit types with explicit response IDs, joint-draw expectations and optional person-cluster observed SEs. It does not identify a dependence mechanism or provide a predictive interval.\nReliability and resolution Named facet/dimension, intended population/design, uncertainty definition, individual precision; justified separation/Strata Explicit finite-cohort posterior_reliability supports one dimension at a time across the common fit types. The historical separation screen warns. Population/design reliability and validated Strata remain unavailable.\nDependence and dimensionality Response-level residual association, shared stimuli, repeated ratings, Q restrictions and estimated dimension correlations Local-dependence and prediction tools exist within specific fit/metadata scopes; a residual correlation does not identify a causal mechanism.\nSensitivity and model comparison Substantive prior alternatives, prior/posterior predictive checks, heldout unit and prediction target, unresolved comparisons Saved sensitivity and comparison components exist. Predictive weights are not posterior model probabilities.\nComputational quality and reproducibility Chains/warmup/draws, convergence diagnostics, divergences, ESS and focal MCSE; software, seeds, saved records and exclusions Diagnostics, MCSE, persistence and report components exist; numerical success is not measurement validity.\n\nThe finite-cohort posterior functional now has an explicit implementation and counterexample checks. Population/design reliability and conventional Strata remain separate validation targets. Common-response agreement now has a scoped implementation. Assignment-aware DFF and owner-aware category summaries now have bounded implementations. Optional common report integration preserves explicit cohorts, response identities and section failures; fitted interactions and additional rater mechanisms require separate validation. Bayes factors remain a separate task requiring validated marginal likelihoods. Existing predictive and recovery studies continue to determine scientific support; these reporting improvements do not replace them.","title":"What should normally be reported?"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Posterior-classification-and-conditional-ability-precision","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"A practical question is: Does this person's ability meet the standard, and how uncertain is that judgment given these ratings? This differs from asking whether a method achieves an acceptable false-pass rate across repeated studies. BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification addresses the first question using an existing fit. It requires two distinct choices: the substantive ability cutoff c and the probability rule t used to report a pass.\n\nFor person i, let q_i = Pr(theta_i >= c | ratings, model, prior). The function estimates q_i by the fraction of their saved ability draws at or above c. It reports pass when q_i >= t, otherwise fail. Equality belongs to the qualifying/passing side. This is an explicit reporting rule, not an automatically recommended standard. Both c and t are required; the cutoff uses the exact fitted coordinate system, including its identification constraints. The Stan guide's event-probability example explains estimation by posterior indicator averages.\n\nReported quantity Interpretation\nAbility mean, SD and equal-tailed interval Conditional ability estimate and uncertainty, including calibration uncertainty represented in the saved joint draws. SD is not numerical MCSE or a repeated-sampling standard error.\nQualification probability Posterior probability of ability at or above the specified cutoff.\nError probability for a displayed pass 1 - q_i, the posterior probability that this person is below the cutoff.\nError probability for a displayed fail q_i, the posterior probability that this person is at or above the cutoff.\nExpected false-pass / false-fail counts Sums of the relevant individual error probabilities, each selected person counted once.\nProbability of any error Posterior probability that at least one displayed decision is wrong, retaining dependence among people.\n\nFor example, if q_i=0.92 and the required probability is t=0.90, the decision is pass and its posterior error probability is 0.08. With t=0.95, it is fail and its error probability is 0.92. The latter is possible because a strict pass rule can prioritize avoiding false passes. The API does not infer those costs, select the rule, or certify it as optimal. An indeterminate/review category is not part of this binary interface.\n\nCohort risk must respect shared uncertainty. Consider two people with qualification probabilities of 0.75, both reported as pass. The expected error count is 0.50 regardless of dependence. But if their below-cutoff events always occur together, the probability of any error is 0.25; if the events never occur together, it is 0.50. Multiplying marginal probabilities would produce a misleading independence-based value. The implementation evaluates the decisions against each complete joint draw and only then summarizes the cohort.\n\nInspect probability MCSE as well as the saved convergence diagnostics. A choice near t can change with numerical noise. Constant sampled indicators retain probabilities of zero or one but unavailable MCSE; they do not establish certainty. A false-pass count is structurally zero if nobody was passed, and a false-fail count is structurally zero if everybody was passed. Count/probability MCSE conditions on the displayed decisions; it does not account for changing those decisions in a different finite MCMC run. No automatic precision gate or refit is applied. The Stan reference manual distinguishes posterior uncertainty from simulation error with dependent draws.\n\nSelect exact estimated person coordinates in one dimension. Another dimension needs its own substantive scale and standard; separate marginal calls do not define a combined, compensatory or conjunctive decision. Fixed anchors are not uncertain people and are rejected. The API does not center the selected cohort, link models, rank people, infer an ability-precision curve over unobserved levels, or predict new ratings. Shared calibration uncertainty absent from the fit cannot be restored by this report.\n\nFinally, these posterior risks condition on the fitted model, prior and observed data. They are not observed mistakes against known truth, population sensitivity or specificity, repeated-study coverage, or future-design error rates. Those claims need suitable validation data or a simulation with known truth and an explicit target population. The current unfavorable loading-recovery finding is not repaired by adding this reporting API; the model's scientific acceptance remains separate.","title":"Posterior classification and conditional ability precision"},{"category":"section","location":"interpreting-results/#Posterior-ranks-within-an-explicit-cohort","page":"Reading Results and Comparing Models","text":"A rank answers a relative question: Among these people, who is likely to have higher ability on this dimension? It does not answer whether someone meets an absolute standard or whether two abilities differ enough to matter in practice. Use the classification report for a justified absolute cutoff and inspect ability differences when substantive magnitude matters.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking requires exact person coordinates and an explicit direction. With :higher, the largest ability is rank 1; with :lower, the smallest is rank 1. Only the selected people compete for these positions. Adding or removing a person changes the estimand; the result is not a population percentile. A multidimensional fit can be inspected one dimension at a time, but the API does not supply an overall multidimensional ranking.\n\nThe function ranks each complete posterior draw and summarizes the resulting ranks. This preserves shared calibration uncertainty represented in the fit and posterior dependence between people. Sorting each person's marginal samples separately would destroy that dependence. It also differs from ranking posterior means. For example, across four equally weighted joint draws, let A have abilities [1000, 0, 0, 0], B [1, 1, 1, 1], and C [2, 2, 2, 2]. A has the largest mean ability but is first in only one draw and last in three, giving expected rank 2.5. The expected ranks of B and C are 2.25 and 1.25. This deliberately extreme finite example shows the distinction, not a fitted model recommendation. Posterior functions and event indicators can be averaged over draws as described in the Stan guide.\n\nOutput What it means\nMean rank and rank SD Expected relative position and its conditional posterior spread, using the declared tie policy. An expected rank need not be an integer.\nRank median and pointwise interval Quantiles of that person's sampled midrank distribution. Empirical inverse-CDF endpoints remain on observed rank support; no interpolation invents unsupported fractional positions.\nMean-rank MCSE Numerical uncertainty in the estimated expected rank, accounting for chain autocorrelation. It is not the rank SD.\nTop-k lower / upper probabilities Bounds on inclusion under unspecified tie-breaking at the kth position. These coincide if no sampled tie crosses that boundary.\nTie probability Fraction of draws in which this person shares an ability value with someone in the cohort; the numerical equality test is exact.\n\nFor a tied group occupying positions a through b, every member receives midrank (a+b)/2. Suppose A and B tie for first while C is third, and only one place is available. A and B each have midrank 1.5, but their top-one inclusion probabilities depend on the tie-breaking rule. The lower bound for each is zero and the upper is one. The report does not silently grant each probability 0.5 or choose by input order. If two places are available, both are included. The API counts inclusion as certain in a draw when the tied group's last position is at most k, and possible when its first position is at most k. The difference between the two posterior probabilities is the probability of a boundary-crossing tie. This range is not a credible interval. Near-equal floating-point values are ordered numerically; exact ties do not implement a practical-equivalence margin. No selection action is performed.\n\nMarginal rank intervals need not form one consistent ordering, can overlap, and are not simultaneous uncertainty bounds for the whole cohort. With discrete ranks, their empirical probability mass may exceed the requested level. The mean rank need not lie inside its equal-tailed interval. Rank-interval/quantile MCSE is not computed; its absence is explicitly recorded. Short or constant sampled ranks/events also have unavailable mean MCSE rather than established certainty. Requesting top_k equal to cohort size is instead the structural event that everybody is included. Read diagnostics and event MCSE before interpreting small differences between reported probabilities.\n\nThese results are conditional on the fitted model, prior and data, for the specified observed cohort. They do not establish known-truth ranking accuracy, future-performance rankings or selection performance under a new design. Ranks can be unstable even when ability differences are negligible; conversely, a stable ordering does not establish a practically important gap. Model and prior sensitivity, scientific acceptance, and substantive decision criteria remain separate from successful rank reporting. No new MCMC is required to change the reported cohort, direction, interval level or top-k query.","title":"Posterior ranks within an explicit cohort"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Would-a-different-prior-change-my-answer?","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"This development candidate compares already fitted prior variants for the same data and model. Start with a scientific question: for example, whether one rater is more severe than another, or how uncertain a particular loading is. Declare plausible alternative priors and the quantities of interest before examining their answers. A prior is not preferable simply because it produces a preferred conclusion, a smaller interval, or a better recovery score.\n\nThe initial supported scope is the normalized-prior MGMFRM with the same Q matrix, dimensions, category scale, identification, data and all prior settings except the SD of log item loadings (log_discrimination_sd). These functions require the BayesianMGMFRM. prefix and are not part of a released stable API. They do not launch fits. MFRM/GMFRM, different identifying scales, free latent correlations and the joint-rating prototype are not yet supported by this particular comparison entry point.","title":"Would a different prior change my answer?"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#From-saved-fits-to-a-scientific-contrast","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"Use the ordinary saved-fit loader for fit caches. Earlier sample records have a separate validated constructor; the repository example examples/prior_sensitivity.jl demonstrates that path for trusted records.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\nconst B = BayesianMGMFRM\n\nnarrow = load_fit_cache(\"narrow-fit.jls\")\nwide = load_fit_cache(\"wide-fit.jls\")\nB.recovery_quantities(narrow) # Inspect exact names, dimensions and units.\n\nresult = B.prior_sensitivity(\n    [\"Narrow\" => narrow, \"Wide\" => wide];\n    planned_prior_ids = [\"Narrow\", \"Wide\", \"Third planned prior\"],\n    failures = [\"Third planned prior\" => \"Sampling failed; no usable fit\"],\n    dataset_id = \"ratings-v1\",\n    observation_ids = rating_ids, # Unique IDs in the original fitted row order.\n    estimands = [\n        \"Rater 1 minus rater 2\" => (\"rater[R1]\", \"rater[R2]\"),\n    ],\n    cutoffs = Dict(\"Rater 1 minus rater 2\" => 0.0),\n    interval = 0.99,\n)\n\nReplace the names with those actually returned by your fit's quantity inventory. Positive severity differences mean the first named rater is more severe in this parameterization. The difference is calculated within each joint posterior draw, preserving dependence between raters. Subtracting separately shuffled draws, or subtracting two marginal interval endpoints, would answer a different question. Contrasts across dimensions or coordinate blocks are rejected.\n\nKeep the full planned prior roster. Failed and missing variants remain visible; they are not silently removed from the denominator. Numerical warnings remain attached to completed fits. Changing the number of retained draws between variants is allowed and does not create artificial pairing.","title":"From saved fits to a scientific contrast"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Three-kinds-of-uncertainty","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"Posterior uncertainty: the median and equal-tailed credible interval within each fit. Set interval=0.90 or 0.99 explicitly. These are not HDIs.\nMonte Carlo uncertainty: each mean, SD and reported quantile has its native chain-aware MCSE and availability status. A wider credible interval may still have precisely estimated endpoints; a narrow interval can have imprecise ones.\nSensitivity to prior choice: changes in corresponding summaries across fits. There is no implicit posterior distribution for “the difference between two priors.” Cross-fit covariance is unknown; the report gives the sum of the two MCSEs as a conservative estimated standard-error upper bound for a summary change, not a confidence interval or an independent-fit standard error.\n\nFor a declared cutoff, the report says whether the interval is above, below, or includes it. An endpoint within mcse_multiplier MCSEs of the cutoff is marked unresolved by that numerical screen. The default multiplier is 2; it is not a simultaneous confidence guarantee. This interval rule does not calculate a posterior sign probability, a ROPE probability, or a Bayes factor.\n\nNo numerical precision threshold is silently selected. Supply max_mcse in estimand units to screen the mean and all three displayed quantiles; otherwise precision_qualified is nothing. A single tolerance is appropriate only if its meaning is defensible for every selected estimand. Sampling diagnostics, precision and scientific adequacy remain separate. MCSE uses the existing MCMCDiagnosticTools estimator.","title":"Three kinds of uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Interval-and-ridge-views","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"using CairoMakie\nfig = B.plot_prior_sensitivity(result; kind=:interval)\nridge = B.plot_prior_sensitivity(result; kind=:ridge)\nB.save_prior_sensitivity_bundle(\"prior-comparison\", result)\nrestored = B.load_prior_sensitivity_bundle(\"prior-comparison\")\n\nEach estimand has its own panel; all prior rows in a panel share the same horizontal scale. Missing/failed rows and orange warning rows remain visible. Structural constants are diamonds, not estimated distributions. Titles and complete notes are written outside the image by default. The bundle includes PDF, SVG, PNG, numerical JSON, external notes and a reloadable record, together with file hashes. The reader verifies those files without requiring CairoMakie. Existing destinations are refused. Load only trusted Julia serialization files.\n\nThe ridge view uses a Gaussian kernel with a shared bandwidth within each estimand. For positive loadings, it estimates density in log coordinates and transforms back with the Jacobian, so the plotted distribution has positive support. Default bandwidth is 0.9 * min(SD, IQR/1.34) * n^(-1/5) of pooled transformed display draws. bandwidth allows an explicit alternative in those coordinates; the numerical export records the actual choice and grid. Draw count is used for display smoothing, not as a claim of independent samples or KDE precision. The log-KDE methodology explains why transformation and the Jacobian matter for positive support.\n\nDensity heights share a scale within each panel and are not comparable across panels. Inspect bandwidth sensitivity when interpreting shape. Overlapping marginal ridges are not a paired contrast test, evidence of equivalence, or a picture of the multidimensional joint correlation.","title":"Interval and ridge views"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Saved-simulation-illustration","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"The figures below reuse the first planned block, B01, of panel R1 from the completed fixed-facet assessment. They compare loading-prior SDs of 0.25, 0.50 and 1.00 using 4,000 retained draws per fit. The three focal quantities are the I1 loading, the P1−P2 ability difference on the first dimension, and the R1−R2 severity difference. Zero cutoffs are illustrative interval decision rules, not retrospectively registered scientific thresholds.\n\n(Image: Prior sensitivity intervals for one saved simulation block)\n\nThe dots are posterior medians and the bars are 90% equal-tailed credible intervals. The axes compare the same quantities across priors. Full external notes and the underlying values are included below.\n\n(Image: Prior sensitivity ridges for the same saved block)\n\nIn this block, the I1 loading median changes from 0.654 to 0.531 to 0.488 as the prior SD widens. Its 90% intervals are [0.507, 0.840], [0.391, 0.718] and [0.341, 0.667]. Both named contrasts retain intervals entirely below zero under all three priors, and their endpoint MCSE screens remain stable. Thus the loading estimate is sensitive while these particular interval-based directional decisions are unchanged; this is a statement about this saved block, not a general robustness or calibration claim.\n\nThese are actual saved simulation posteriors, not newly estimated results or empirical data. This illustration contributes zero new fits and zero new independent replications. The original assessment completed 192 fits, of which 184 passed its own diagnostic/precision qualification. Displaying this one block neither replaces that assessment nor establishes a default prior. In the full R1 assessment, the narrow prior's 90% interval covered the I1 loading truth in only 1 of 32 blocks despite all 32 fitting qualifications; numerical success alone did not establish calibration.\n\nDownload the interval PDF, interval SVG, interval note, ridge PDF, ridge SVG, ridge note, numerical summaries and selected draws, and density coordinates and bandwidths.","title":"Saved simulation illustration"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#What-this-comparison-cannot-decide","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"Prior predictive comparison is a complementary step, detailed below. It does not validate substantive plausibility automatically. Inspect the rating design, within-person dependence and patterns beyond the displayed summaries too. Predictive model comparison answers a different question. Bayes factors need separate validated marginal-likelihood computation; these summary changes and kernel densities do not supply it. See Reading Results and Comparing Models for model choice and interval interpretation.","title":"What this comparison cannot decide"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#What-do-these-priors-predict-before-seeing-scores?","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"A prior over parameters also implies a distribution over possible ratings. Draw parameters from the prior, then generate a complete replicated rating dataset from the response model. Repeating this procedure gives p(y_rep) = ∫ p(y_rep | theta) p(theta) dtheta. The recorded persons, items, raters, category scale and assignment rows stay fixed. Observed scores provide reference marks; they do not update the generated parameter distribution. This is the prior predictive check described in the Stan User's Guide.\n\nUse B.prior_predictive_sensitivity before fitting to examine scientifically plausible alternatives. Pass explicitly constructed B.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior objects as id => prior pairs. Its first supported scope matches the saved posterior comparison: only the log-loading SD varies. The existing normalized-prior generator supplies the draws, including the covariance induced by zero-sum blocks. A centered block's supplied scale is a kernel SD, not each constrained coordinate's marginal SD.\n\nFor a saved comparison, the following connects the exact priors to the original specification without fitting again. The widths in this example are illustration settings, not recommended defaults.\n\nposterior = B.load_prior_sensitivity(\"posterior-comparison.jls\")\npriors = [\n    e.prior_id => B.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(;\n        prior_model=e.evaluation.prior.prior_model,\n        source_rater=e.evaluation.prior.source_rater,\n        e.evaluation.prior.scales...,\n    ) for e in posterior.ledger if e.evaluation !== nothing\n]\n\nchecks = B.prior_predictive_sensitivity(spec, priors;\n    planned_prior_ids=posterior.planned_prior_ids,\n    dataset_id=posterior.dataset_id,\n    observation_ids=posterior.observation_ids,\n    seeds=Dict(id => 41250+i for (i, (id, _)) in enumerate(priors)),\n    ndraws=500,\n    interval=0.90,\n    posterior=posterior,\n)\nB.save_prior_predictive_sensitivity(\"prior-predictions.jls\", checks)\nrestored = B.load_prior_predictive_sensitivity(\"prior-predictions.jls\")\n\nusing CairoMakie\nfig = B.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity(restored)\nitems = B.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity(restored; statistic=:item_mean)\nraters = B.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity(restored; statistic=:rater_mean)\n# Select exact level IDs when a design has many items or raters:\nselected = B.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity(restored;\n    statistic=:item_mean, levels=[\"I1\", \"I2\"])\n\nHere spec must be the same original specification and data. The comparison checks data, row IDs and order, dimension labels, prior records and target identities before generating anything. Merely naming two variants “Narrow” does not make their priors identical. posterior is optional; omitting it lets you check priors before fitting. Missing/failed prior checks stay in their own ledger, and a linked posterior's missing/failed/warning status stays separate. A successful prior simulation does not repair a failed posterior fit.\n\nexamples/prior_predictive_sensitivity.jl saves this workflow's numerical table and complete record. The complete record retains generated statistics for every rating summary, including person and observed assignment-cell summaries, seeds, actual priors, generator/RNG names, Julia version and the optional posterior hash. The generator can be replayed in the recorded software environment. It is not a promise of identical random streams across Julia versions. Loading the saved statistics reproduces the comparison without regenerating draws.","title":"What do these priors predict before seeing scores?"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Predictive-spread-and-numerical-error-answer-different-questions","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"The displayed 90% intervals describe variation in replicated score statistics under the prior and response model. They are neither posterior parameter CrIs nor uncertainty intervals around the Monte Carlo mean. Set interval=0.99 for 99% equal-tailed predictive intervals. Extreme endpoints require more draws for stable estimation; this version does not estimate their MCSE or automatically qualify them.\n\nEach replication is a fresh independent parameter draw followed by conditional responses. Mean MCSE is the sample SD of a statistic across replications divided by sqrt(ndraws). The same formula applied to the inclusive indicators statistic <= observed and statistic >= observed gives tail-proportion MCSE. These calculations do not use MCMC ESS. A zero empirical MCSE may mean that a rare event was never sampled, so degenerate estimates are flagged explicitly. Inclusive tails overlap at ties and need not sum to one. Tail probabilities, interval-outside flags and existing implication diagnostics are descriptive, not calibrated hypothesis tests or a method for ranking priors. Statistics from one replicated dataset remain dependent; there is no simultaneous acceptance rule across categories, persons, items and raters.","title":"Predictive spread and numerical error answer different questions"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Illustration:-a-reasonable-average-can-conceal-a-category-mismatch","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"The next figures use the same preselected B01/R1 design as the posterior example: 50 persons, five items, five raters and 1,250 rating rows. Each prior receives exactly 500 newly generated IID prior/response draws, with seeds 41251–41253. There are zero new MCMC fits and zero new independent recovery replications. All three actual prior/target identities agree with the saved posterior comparison. The observed data are simulated, not empirical ratings.\n\nLoading-prior SD Replicated mean score (MCSE) Category 2 expected proportion (MCSE) Category 3 expected proportion (MCSE)\n0.25 2.499 (0.012) 0.128 (0.003) 0.136 (0.003)\n0.50 2.501 (0.011) 0.130 (0.003) 0.129 (0.003)\n1.00 2.481 (0.011) 0.123 (0.003) 0.121 (0.003)\nObserved 2.508 0.262 0.278\n\nThe overall expected score is close to the observed mean, while all three priors expect markedly fewer middle-category ratings and more extreme ratings. This is a concrete reason to inspect category use as well as averages. The pattern is shared across these alternatives; it does not isolate the loading prior as its cause. It calls for examining the joint prior and response model against substantive expectations, not automatically choosing the closest prior. The 500-draw illustration does not establish endpoint precision, a formal prior–data conflict conclusion or recovery/calibration performance.\n\nPrior predictive category use\n\n(Image: Prior predictive category proportions across loading-prior widths)\n\nNote. Dots are predictive medians; bars are 90% equal-tailed predictive intervals. Dashed lines mark observed proportions. Every panel uses the same 0–1 proportion scale. The distribution is over replicated datasets on the fixed rating design, not over posterior parameter estimates. Full external note, PDF, SVG.\n\nPrior predictive item means\n\n(Image: Prior predictive means for all five items)\n\nNote. All five items are displayed in their recorded order. Dots, intervals and dashed lines have the same meaning as above, now on the 1–4 mean-score scale. Each item averages its actual assigned ratings; uneven assignments in other designs may mix person and rater composition with item effects. Full external note, PDF, SVG.\n\nPrior predictive rater means\n\n(Image: Prior predictive means for all five raters)\n\nNote. All five raters are displayed. These are mean-score predictions, not rater-severity posteriors; each rater's observed assignment rows are retained. Good agreement of rater means alone would not validate response dependence, connectivity, dimensionality or item–rater interactions. Full external note, PDF, SVG.\n\nDownload the numerical summaries, priors, seeds and linked-result hash.","title":"Illustration: a reasonable average can conceal a category mismatch"},{"category":"section","location":"prior-sensitivity/#Candidate-API-reference","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"","title":"Candidate API reference"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity(fits; planned_prior_ids, estimands,\n    dataset_id, observation_ids, baseline=first(planned_prior_ids),\n    interval=0.90, failures=Pair[], cutoffs=Dict(), max_mcse=nothing,\n    mcse_multiplier=2.0)\n\nCompare saved prior variants without fitting. fits contains id => fit pairs; declare every planned ID, including failures and missing fits. The first supported scope is Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit with identical data/specification and prior, except log_discrimination_sd. Different dimensions, identifying priors, models and unsupported fit types are rejected. Use exact coordinate names from recovery_quantities(fit). An estimand maps a label to a name, or to (left,right) for a within-fit draw-paired difference in the same block/dimension/scale.\n\nIntervals are equal-tailed (e.g. interval=0.99), never HDI. All retained chains and draws, native mean/SD/quantile MCSE, sampling warnings, actual prior records, and sample/target identities are preserved. max_mcse optionally screens the mean and all three reported quantiles in estimand units; no default precision acceptance is inferred. cutoffs maps labels to declared scientific thresholds. Interval-relative decisions carry an endpoint MCSE screen, not a simultaneous error bound or proof of practical equivalence. This does not compute P(X>cutoff).\n\nAcross-prior differences are differences of summaries, not paired draws or credible intervals for an across-model random variable. Their MCSE upper bounds sum the two marginal MCSEs, allowing unknown covariance; these are estimated standard-error bounds, not confidence bounds. Scientific acceptance, calibration, prior predictive adequacy and recommendations are not established by this report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity_plot_data","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity_plot_data(result; bandwidth=nothing, points=256)\n\nCompute reproducible Gaussian kernel densities from all selected draws. Each estimand shares a grid and bandwidth across priors. The default bandwidth is 0.9 * min(SD, IQR/1.34) * n^(-1/5) of pooled display draws; bandwidth overrides it in real or log units. Positive coordinates use log-KDE with the inverse Jacobian, avoiding negative density support. Fixed or accidentally constant draws receive markers, not fabricated densities. Density height is descriptive; no KDE precision or posterior calibration is inferred from draw count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_sensitivity_plot_data"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_sensitivity(result; kind=:interval, annotation_io=stdout,\n    size=nothing, bandwidth=nothing, points=256)\n\nDraw same-scale prior rows in separate estimand panels; kind=:ridge adds KDEs. Requires CairoMakie. Warning/missing/failed rows remain visible. Figure titles and notes are external by default. Ridge heights share one density scale within each panel; density overlap is neither a contrast test nor evidence of equivalence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity(path, result)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity(path)\n\nSave/reload the complete sealed result, including selected draws and chain metadata. Existing paths are refused. Load trusted Julia serialization files only; integrity hashes detect accidental changes and do not authenticate an untrusted file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity(path, result)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity(path)\n\nSave/reload the complete sealed result, including selected draws and chain metadata. Existing paths are refused. Load trusted Julia serialization files only; integrity hashes detect accidental changes and do not authenticate an untrusted file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity_bundle","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity_bundle(directory, result; bandwidth=nothing, points=256)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity_bundle(directory)\n\nExport PDF/SVG/PNG interval and ridge figures, external notes, numerical JSON, and the reloadable result. CairoMakie is required only for writing. The directory must not exist; all figures are staged before publication. The reader checks every file hash, then reloads the sealed result. Only load trusted bundles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity_bundle","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_sensitivity_bundle(directory, result; bandwidth=nothing, points=256)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity_bundle(directory)\n\nExport PDF/SVG/PNG interval and ridge figures, external notes, numerical JSON, and the reloadable result. CairoMakie is required only for writing. The directory must not exist; all figures are staged before publication. The reader checks every file hash, then reloads the sealed result. Only load trusted bundles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_sensitivity_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predictive_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predictive_sensitivity(spec, priors;\n    planned_prior_ids, dataset_id, observation_ids, seeds, ndraws,\n    interval=0.90, failures=Pair[], posterior=nothing)\n\nCompare explicitly supplied normalized MGMFRM priors on the same observed rating design. priors contains id => Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior pairs; only log_discrimination_sd may differ. seeds maps each available ID to an explicit nonnegative integer seed. ndraws >= 2 is required, with no automatic extra draws. Each variant uses the existing prior-predictive generator with its own seeded MersenneTwister. Observed scores are reference values, not conditioning data. This generates IID prior parameter/response draws, never posterior fits or SBC.\n\nThe sealed result retains all planned IDs, missing/failed variants, full actual priors, target/data identity, seeds and per-draw statistics for overall/category, person/item/rater, optional-facet and grouped checks. Replicated intervals are equal-tailed predictive intervals, not parameter CrIs or HDIs. MCSE of means and inclusive tail proportions uses sample SD / sqrt(ndraws), not MCMC ESS. Zero empirical MCSE can reflect an unobserved event; endpoint MCSE is not computed. Tail probabilities and flag are descriptive, not calibrated tests, automatic prior rankings, or proof of adequacy. They are dependent across statistics.\n\nOptionally bind a saved prior_sensitivity result through posterior. Its seal, planned IDs, row IDs/order, canonical design/data, dataset ID and every available variant's actual prior/target must agree before any simulation. Posterior failures remain distinct from prior-generation failures. The linked result hash and source sample hashes are retained; existing posterior draws are neither updated nor fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predictive_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity(result;\n    statistic=:category_proportion, levels=nothing, annotation_io=stdout, size=nothing)\n\nCompare a declared statistic across priors. levels=nothing displays all levels; an explicit collection selects exact level IDs. Dots/bars are prior predictive medians/intervals; dashed lines mark observed statistics. Requires CairoMakie. Every level has a panel on a common scale; failed/missing rows remain visible. Figure notes are external by default. This does not visualize parameter CrIs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior_predictive_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_predictive_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_predictive_sensitivity(path, result)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_predictive_sensitivity(path)\n\nSave/reload the sealed comparison, including generated statistic draws, seeds, actual priors and optional posterior link. Existing paths are refused. Load only trusted Julia serialization files; integrity hashes do not authenticate a source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_predictive_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"prior-sensitivity/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_predictive_sensitivity","page":"Prior Sensitivity Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_prior_predictive_sensitivity(path, result)\nBayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_predictive_sensitivity(path)\n\nSave/reload the sealed comparison, including generated statistic draws, seeds, actual priors and optional posterior link. Existing paths are refused. Load only trusted Julia serialization files; integrity hashes do not authenticate a source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_prior_predictive_sensitivity"},{"category":"section","location":"api-validation-evidence/#Validation-and-Evidence-API","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.","title":"Validation and Evidence API"},{"category":"section","location":"api-validation-evidence/#User-facing-runtime-and-provenance-utilities","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"These bindings support ordinary package workflows. CmdStan remains an optional runtime detected when requested; it is not required to install or load the package.","title":"User-facing runtime and provenance utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api-validation-evidence/#Research-only-planning-and-evidence-helpers","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"The bindings below remain package-root exports for 0.1.x source compatibility. They plan, score, or record package research and release evidence; they are not part of the stable MFRM user workflow, and their presence is not validation of GMFRM, MGMFRM, or local-dependence claims.\n\ncomparison_evidence_summary, benchmark_summary, simulation_grid, simulation_grid_summary, and falsification_rule_summary accept view = :public for compact reader-facing records. Their default full payloads remain available for research reproduction.\n\nevidence_metadata() returns portable environment, package, project, and git metadata without machine-local paths by default. Set include_paths = true only for a private reproduction record whose access controls and retention policy permit complete local paths and free-form execution notes. Environment discovery is best-effort: an unavailable optional command or file does not stop report creation, and collection.issues records its stage and short reason.\n\ncmdstan_backend_check() validates local runtime/toolchain availability without compiling a model or running MCMC, and omits local paths by default. Stable MFRM/RSM/PCM and both guarded generalized configurations now have executable backend = :cmdstan routes. cmdstan_backend_contract() keeps this implementation status separate from the remaining recovery, sparse-design, cache, parallel-chain, independent-review, and analysis-scale evidence.\n\nlocal_dependence_simulation_grid and simulate_local_dependence form the known-truth generator and design-validation surface. The 22 declared scenarios use a standalone adjacent-category ordinal kernel rather than the fitted likelihood implementation and record the complete generating truth. Their magnitude labels are study-local simulation settings. These generators do not provide repeated-calibration evidence or diagnostic cutoffs, enable a decision in local_dependence_summary, or identify an observed-data dependence mechanism.\n\nThe calibration-protocol functions validate one-result-per-planning-row provenance and summarize candidate pair, family, and global reference behavior across repeated known-truth simulations. They keep failed, rejected, missing, and unsupported replications visible in the denominator. Pair declarations within a replication are dependent, so pooled pair fractions are descriptive; Monte Carlo intervals are attached only to replication-level binary rates. This protocol-validation surface does not run MCMC, does not yet provide pairwise power under alternative mechanisms, and does not change the report-only status of local_dependence_summary. local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract and local_dependence_calibration_pilot_check validate a pilot plan. They declare 30 replications for each of 22 scenarios (30 × 22 = 660 planned jobs) and validate the study-specific sampler and diagnostic requirements. The supported route is AdvancedHMC/NUTS with ForwardDiff; unsupported backend, algorithm, or gradient choices are rejected. These functions validate the plan but do not execute it.\n\nThe related diagnostic functions expose rank-normalized split R-hat, bulk ESS, and tail ESS as the primary diagnostic fields. Classical rhat and ess remain compatibility fields. For odd split chains, bulk ranks exclude the center draw, folding uses the untrimmed pooled median before that exclusion, and tail ESS uses untrimmed pooled quantiles before splitting. ESS uses all valid split-chain lags, matching Stan/posterior semantics. Guarded GMFRM/MGMFRM checks require both raw unconstrained and direct constrained rows to pass when the rows are quality-gate applicable. A direct coordinate fixed by a zero-raw-dimension transform remains visible as :structurally_fixed with quality_gate_applicable = false and does not enter extrema or failure counts. A reconstructed coordinate that varies with free raw coordinates remains gated. Cache identity records the versioned diagnostic contract.\n\nNo official pilot result is included in this release. These APIs therefore provide no repeated-calibration, power, diagnostic-decision, or mechanism-identification evidence and do not make clustered effects available for fitting.","title":"Research-only planning and evidence helpers"},{"category":"type","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.CmdStanError","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"CmdStanError\n\nTyped failure raised by the CmdStan runtime check, model compilation, command execution, or output parser. stage identifies the failed operation, reason is a stable symbolic category, and detail contains a concise diagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.CmdStanError"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.cmdstan_backend_contract","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"cmdstan_backend_contract([family = :all])\n\nReturn the implementation and release contract for CmdStan fitting backend. family may be :mfrm, :gmfrm, :mgmfrm, or :all.\n\nThe contract deliberately distinguishes a working CmdStan installation from a working package backend. CmdStan fitting is a required gate before stable promotion, but CmdStan remains an optional external runtime so loading and using Julia-only package features does not depend on a machine-local install. Stable MFRM/RSM/PCM designs and both guarded generalized configurations can be sampled through CmdStan. Broader same-target recovery, sparse-design, cache, parallel-chain, independent-review, and analysis-scale evidence is still required; release_gate_satisfied is therefore false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.cmdstan_backend_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.cmdstan_backend_check","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"cmdstan_backend_check(; cmdstan_path = nothing,\n    include_paths = false, require_ready = false)\n\nCheck whether a local CmdStan toolchain is ready for the package's backend = :cmdstan adapter. Discovery uses an explicit cmdstan_path first, then CMDSTAN, CMDSTAN_HOME, and finally the newest versioned directory under ~/.cmdstan. An explicitly supplied or configured invalid path is reported; it is not silently replaced by another installation.\n\nThe check validates the CmdStan root, makefile, stanc --version, make, and a C++ compiler. It does not compile a Stan model or run MCMC. Machine-local paths are omitted by default. Set require_ready = true to throw a CmdStanError when a check fails.\n\nMAKE, when set, selects one executable name or path, not a shell command; additional arguments are not parsed or silently dropped. A literal executable path may contain spaces. CXX discovery still checks only its first program; Make resolves the effective compiler command and options during a build.\n\nReadiness does not check the compile-time environment policy: compilation requires MAKEFILES, MAKEFLAGS, and GNUMAKEFLAGS each to be unset or exactly empty, including when inherited flags only control logging or parallelism. Nonempty values are rejected without clearing them. This check can report ready while compilation rejects those settings; it still runs stanc --version.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.cmdstan_backend_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.evidence_metadata","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"evidence_metadata(; include_packages = true, include_paths = false)\n\nReturn reproducibility metadata for the active Julia session, including Julia, OS, BLAS, optional R/CmdStan discovery, git/project hashes, and direct package status. Machine-local paths are omitted by default while safe basenames and content hashes are retained. Set include_paths = true only when a private reproduction record explicitly requires complete local paths and free-form execution notes. Optional probe failures do not stop metadata creation; collection.issues records their stage and short reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.evidence_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_responses","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulate_responses(spec_or_design, params; rng = Random.default_rng(),\n    output = :data, parameter_space = :direct, preview = false)\n\nSimulate one response dataset from the current fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM likelihood, or from a specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM preview design for simulation and recovery evaluation. MFRM/RSM/PCM designs use direct identified parameters. GMFRM/MGMFRM previews accept constrained direct parameters by default, or raw fit-ready candidate parameters with parameter_space = :raw. output = :data returns a FacetData object with the same person/rater/item/optional facet structure and category levels as the original design. Use output = :table for a column-oriented named tuple, or output = :scores for just the simulated score vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_responses"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.benchmark_result_row","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"benchmark_result_row(; benchmark, engine, model, elapsed_seconds,\n    effective_sample_sizes = nothing, time_to_quality_seconds = elapsed_seconds,\n    time_to_quality_threshold_seconds = nothing, idle_machine = true,\n    target_quality = :predeclared_quality_gate, hardware = nothing,\n    software = nothing, artifact = nothing)\n\nSummarize one repeated benchmark result row. elapsed_seconds must contain one or more repeated timings and is summarized with median and IQR. When effective_sample_sizes is supplied, the row also reports median/IQR ESS/sec. time_to_quality_seconds records repeated time-to-quality measurements, checked against time_to_quality_threshold_seconds when provided.\n\nThis helper records benchmark evidence from an idle-machine run; it does not run the benchmark itself or enforce hardware isolation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.benchmark_result_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.benchmark_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"benchmark_summary(rows; required_engines = (:julia, :stan),\n    min_repetitions = 3, view = :full)\nbenchmark_summary(row, rows...; required_engines = ..., min_repetitions = 3)\n\nSummarize repeated idle-machine benchmark rows. The summary checks required engine coverage, minimum repetitions, idle-machine flags, time-to-quality threshold failures, and per-benchmark Stan/Julia elapsed-time and ESS/sec ratios. It aggregates recorded benchmark rows; it does not run benchmarks. view = :public produces a compact reader-facing projection; view = :full preserves the complete existing payload.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.benchmark_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.comparison_evidence_row","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"comparison_evidence_row(; comparison_class, target_model, comparator,\n    metric, estimate, reference, tolerance = 0.0,\n    pass_if = :within_tolerance, evidence = :declared_comparison,\n    label = nothing, artifact = nothing, source = nothing)\n\nReturn one machine-readable comparison-evidence row for a precomputed result against a faithful Stan/BridgeStan model, an overlapping R/frequentist tool, or a simpler nested model. estimate is the focal Julia/Bayesian value and reference is the comparator value. pass_if = :within_tolerance checks absolute agreement, while :greater_equal and :less_equal check directional comparisons such as heldout ELPD gains.\n\nThis helper records already computed evidence; it does not run Stan, call R, fit nested models, or refit heldout folds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.comparison_evidence_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.comparison_evidence_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"comparison_evidence_summary(rows; required_classes =\n    (:stan_faithful, :r_frequentist, :nested_model), view = :full)\ncomparison_evidence_summary(row, rows...; required_classes = ...)\n\nSummarize declared comparison-evidence rows and check whether the default critical-review comparison classes are present and passing: faithful Stan/BridgeStan models, overlapping R/frequentist tools, and simpler nested models. The summary is a coverage check over recorded comparison rows; it does not execute external tools or refit models. Use view = :public for a compact reader-facing summary of the recorded comparison results. The default :full contract is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.comparison_evidence_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.falsification_rule_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"falsification_rule_summary(rows; required_domains =\n    (:simulation_grid, :design_validation, :computation, :recovery,\n     :calibration, :predictive_check, :decision_stability, :sensitivity,\n     :baseline_comparison, :reproducibility), view = :full)\n\nSummarize predeclared falsification-rule rows and check whether every required claim domain is represented. This is a rule-coverage summary; it does not evaluate study results or decide whether a claim has passed. view = :public returns a compact reader-facing summary; view = :full preserves the complete rule-coverage record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.falsification_rule_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.falsification_rules","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"falsification_rules(; claim = :sparse_hierarchical_priors_stabilize_mgmfrm,\n    max_rhat = 1.01, min_bulk_ess = 400, max_divergences = 0,\n    max_max_treedepth_hits = 0, min_interval_coverage = 0.90,\n    max_block_mean_absolute_error = 0.35,\n    max_parameter_absolute_error = 0.75,\n    max_expected_score_calibration_error = 0.25,\n    max_category_probability_error = 0.10,\n    min_predictive_tail_probability = 0.05,\n    max_decision_flip_rate = 0.10,\n    max_prior_sensitivity_shift = 0.25,\n    min_heldout_elpd_gain = 0.0,\n    required_grid_axes = ...)\n\nReturn predeclared falsification-rule rows for the claim that hierarchical priors stabilize sparse MGMFRM designs. Rules cover simulation-grid coverage, design validation, computation, recovery, calibration, predictive checks, decision stability, sensitivity, baseline comparison, and reproducibility.\n\nThe rows are a claim contract, not run evidence. A triggered rule should narrow or block the corresponding sparse-stability claim until the study is revised or new evidence is generated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.falsification_rules"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_contract","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_contract(;\n    profile = :ld1b0_protocol_v1,\n    diagnostic_contract = local_dependence_contract(),\n    candidate_pair_raw_alpha = 0.05,\n    candidate_pair_bh_alpha = 0.05,\n    candidate_family_alpha = 0.05,\n    candidate_global_alpha = 0.05)\n\nReturn the LD1b0 protocol-validation contract for repeated calibration of the existing report-only local-dependence diagnostic. The four alphas define candidate decisions computed from finite-sample-corrected unadjusted pair tails, within-family BH-adjusted tails, family maximum-statistic tails, and the all-family maximum-statistic tail. They do not enable decisions in local_dependence_summary or authorize a mechanism interpretation.\n\nReplication-level binary rates use a fixed 95% Wilson interval. Pairwise power is unavailable because the current known-truth generator has no pair-specific null/non-null oracle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_check","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_pilot_check(plan_rows;\n    contract = local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract())\n\nCheck the complete 660-job LD1b1 pilot plan without generating scores or fitting models. The result records workload counts, seed separation, resource limits, precision references, and sampler-diagnostic capability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract(;\n    calibration_contract = local_dependence_calibration_contract())\n\nReturn the fixed LD1b1 planning contract for the 30-replication diagnostic pilot. The contract fixes the complete LD1a scenario set, computation and quality requirements, operational completion bounds, Wilson-interval precision references, and bounded generator workload. Evaluation uses either 50 or 100 replications, selected after the pilot and before any evaluation result is observed; a mid-evaluation extension is not part of this protocol.\n\nThe MFRM sampling route is fixed to AdvancedHMC/NUTS with ForwardDiff. MFRMLogDensity is an order-0 target and does not expose the analytic-gradient method required by ad_backend = :analytic.\n\nThis object is planning evidence only. It does not execute fitting, complete the pilot, freeze an evaluation profile, enable diagnostic decisions, or support a dependence-mechanism interpretation. Pairwise power remains outside this protocol because the current generator has no pair-specific truth oracle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_preflight","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_pilot_preflight(plan_rows;\n    contract = local_dependence_calibration_pilot_contract())\n\nValidate the complete 660-job LD1b1 pilot plan without generating scores or fitting models. The plan must be the unmodified 22-scenario by 30-replication cross returned by local_dependence_simulation_grid with phase = :pilot and the fixed base dimensions. The result records compact job rows, deterministic workload counts, Wilson precision references, pilot/evaluation seed separation, and the currently available sampler-diagnostic capability.\n\nExecution-protocol authorization requires an executable MFRM backend, algorithm, and gradient route as well as rank-normalized R-hat and bulk/tail ESS capabilities matching the frozen quality contract. Authorization does not execute the pilot: a successful planning preflight alone is not calibration evidence and does not enable decision or mechanism labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_pilot_preflight"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_row","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_row(plan_row;\n    contract = local_dependence_calibration_contract(),\n    status = :completed, simulation = nothing, diagnostic = nothing,\n    failure_code = missing, max_pair_rows = 200_000)\n\nCreate one LD1b0 result row for a canonical LD1 planning row. Completed rows require matching known-truth simulation and report-only diagnostic bundles. Pre-fit rejection and failure statuses retain their planned denominator rather than being silently dropped. Candidate decisions are derived without modifying the supplied diagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_calibration_summary(plan_rows, result_rows;\n    contract = local_dependence_calibration_contract(),\n    max_plan_rows = 1_000_000, max_result_rows = 1_000_000,\n    max_pair_rows = 5_000_000, max_group_rows = 100_000)\n\nSummarize LD1b0 result rows against the complete set of planned rows. Missing and failed replications remain explicit. Wilson intervals are used only for replication-level binary candidate rates; pooled pair rates are descriptive. The result remains protocol-validation evidence and does not enable public diagnostic decisions or mechanism labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_calibration_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_simulation_grid","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"local_dependence_simulation_grid(;\n    profile = :ld1_preflight_v1, repetitions = 1,\n    base_seed = 20260720, phase = :smoke,\n    grid_id = \"ld1_preflight\", n_persons = 40,\n    n_testlets = 4, items_per_testlet = 3,\n    n_raters = 4, n_categories = 4)\n\nReturn the matched LD1a known-truth planning rows for local-dependence simulation. The rows cover exact null and zero-boundary controls, near-zero through large person-by-testlet variation, connected sparsity, pair-support boundaries, pre-fit rejection controls, rater-response halo, rater-by-task severity, omitted multidimensionality, randomized severity drift, and ability-confounded order and ability-informed rater assignment. Scenarios within a replication share component seed streams so comparisons use common random numbers.\n\nThese rows are a generator and design-validation plan. They are not repeated calibration evidence, do not supply diagnostic cutoffs or decision labels, and do not identify an observed-data mechanism. At most 10,000,000 replications are accepted per phase so the reserved :smoke, :pilot, and :evaluation seed namespaces do not overlap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_simulation_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_decision_stability_score","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_decision_stability_score(\n    reference,\n    candidates;\n    cutpoints = (),\n    condition_labels = nothing,\n    unit_labels = nothing,\n    reference_role = :known_truth,\n)\n\nCompare rows of candidate point estimates with a reference vector. Each row reports absolute shift, pairwise ordering disagreement, and—when the caller supplies substantively meaningful ordered cutpoints—classification flip rates. Reference ties are excluded from pairwise ordering denominators; candidate ties against a non-tied reference pair count as disagreements.\n\nNo default practical cutpoint or pass/fail threshold is assumed. The function is intended to score prior, backend, Q-specification, or replication conditions after those conditions and cutpoints have been frozen externally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_decision_stability_score"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_predictive_recovery_score","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_predictive_recovery_score(\n    predicted_probabilities,\n    truth_probabilities;\n    category_levels = nothing,\n    probability_tolerance = 1e-8,\n    log_probabilities = false,\n)\n\nScore known-truth predictive recovery without applying a scientific pass/fail threshold. truth_probabilities is an observations-by-categories matrix. predicted_probabilities may have the same shape or may add a leading posterior draw dimension; posterior draws are averaged before scoring.\n\nWith log_probabilities = true, both arrays contain normalized natural-log probabilities, not unnormalized logits. -Inf denotes exact zero support; NaN, positive entries, and rows with no mass are rejected. Draws are averaged in probability space using log-sum-exp, and KL is evaluated without first exponentiating its inputs. The tolerance still measures deviation of row mass from one. Category-probability and expected-score errors use Float64 probabilities and may round vanishing contributions to zero.\n\nThe result reports category-probability MAE/RMSE, expected-score MAE/RMSE, and mean proper log-score regret (the mean KL divergence from truth to prediction), plus observation-level rows. Exact predicted zeros where truth is positive are retained as infinite log-score regret rather than silently clamped. This is a descriptive Stage-A scorer, not validation evidence by itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_predictive_recovery_score"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_fit_attempts","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_response_stress_fit_attempts(plan;\n    profile = :wiring_smoke, backends = (:advancedhmc,),\n    prior_regimes = (:implementation_reference,), maximum_attempts = 1,\n    truth_scale = 0.15, cmdstan_path = nothing,\n    cmdstan_cache_dir = nothing)\n\nRun a resource-bounded fit-wiring smoke over an explicit response-stress plan. The plan is expanded across the selected backends and prior regimes, and maximum_attempts is checked before generation or fitting. Every admitted attempt terminates as :generation_failed, :pre_fit_rejected, :fit_failed, :diagnostic_failed, or :completed; caught errors retain their exception object, concrete type, message, and phase.\n\nOnly profile = :wiring_smoke is currently executable. It uses four warmup and four retained draws in one chain, does not assess convergence, and cannot be used for recovery, backend, prior, or scientific decisions. profile = :analysis fails before execution until the Stage-A protocol and independent threshold review are frozen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_fit_attempts"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_plan","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_response_stress_plan(; design_strata = (...),\n    response_patterns = (...), replications = 1,\n    base_seed = 20260814, n_persons = 12, n_items = 4,\n    n_raters = 3)\n\nReturn a small predeclared plan for five-category fixed-Q MGMFRM response- pattern stress cases. The default covers dense and connected systematic-link designs with ordinary category use, an unused interior category, an all- maximum person, and an all-minimum rater. Their exact combination is included only in the sparse design, where the target person and rater have no common observation; exact all-maximum and all-minimum constraints cannot coexist in a fully crossed person-rater design.\n\nRows are planning records, not fitted attempts or scientific evidence. Seeds are assigned by row order without repository paths or source hashes. Odd item counts are supported: pure items are divided between the two fixed dimensions with a count difference of one, rather than imposing equal counts as an identification condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_preflight","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_response_stress_preflight(\n    plan = mgmfrm_response_stress_plan(); truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nGenerate and preflight every response-stress attempt while retaining the full attempt denominator. Each row terminates as :preflight_passed, :pre_fit_rejected, or :generation_failed. Caught generation errors are not converted to nothing: the in-memory exception, concrete type, message, phase, and elapsed time are retained in the result row. Interrupt, out-of-memory, and stack-overflow exceptions are rethrown.\n\nThis function does not run MCMC, apply computational/scientific thresholds, or resolve the Stage-A attempt-complete evaluation-runner blocker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_response_stress_preflight"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_analysis_contract","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_analysis_contract()\n\nReturn the non-executing analysis-profile contract for the narrow fixed-Q MGMFRM validation program. The result separates Stage-A components that are already specified from decisions that still block fresh-seed evaluation.\n\nThe contract fixes the four-chain sampler controls, computational diagnostics, estimands, interval and scale policies, prior regimes, held-out design, retry/remediation rules, stratified sensitivity cells, backend-conformance cells, terminal statuses, seed rules, and all-attempt denominator. It does not choose unresolved sample-size cells, replication counts, resource caps, or scientific thresholds.\n\nThis function generates no data, runs no sampler, and cannot authorize an analysis. Runtime pilots may inform resource choices only; they cannot set scientific thresholds. The provisional staged primary-grid review narrows the next decisions but does not freeze cells or require automatic short-NUTS progression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_analysis_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_execution_design_contract","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_execution_design_contract()\n\nReturn the frozen, non-executing design choices for held-out prediction, retry/remediation, and stratified MGMFRM sensitivity cells. The contract uses the documented package API surface and explicit semantic options.\n\nThe primary held-out target is five-fold observation prediction conditional on person, item, and rater levels represented in every training fold. New-level person, item, or rater prediction remains unsupported. Remediation never overwrites a primary attempt, and the listed sensitivity cells are role cells, not a workload count. The 16 primary-grid rows are non-executing candidates, not frozen cells; they do have a sampler-free four-category generator and structural preflight. The resource surface includes an inert-by-default, single-cell Julia worker for process-level peak-RSS attribution; it does not authorize execution. No data are generated and no model is fitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_execution_design_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_probe","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_probe(\n    cell_id = :default_sparse_short_nuts;\n    execute_measurement = false, timeout_seconds = 300,\n    minimum_free_memory_bytes = 2 * 1024^3,\n    maximum_observations_per_cell = 1_000, truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nPlan or explicitly launch one stress/scaling or primary-grid short-NUTS resource cell in a dedicated Julia process. Parent and child memory preflights run before MCMC, and the parent terminates a worker that exceeds the wall-time bound. The child returns one small JSON receipt through stdout; stderr is retained separately.\n\nPrimary rows are selected by the cell identifiers returned from mgmfrm_validation_primary_resource_plan. Only rows inside the current short-NUTS workload bound can execute; larger gradient-only rows fail before a worker is started.\n\nThe recorded peak RSS belongs to the dedicated worker process as a whole, including startup, package loading, compilation, generation, sampling, and diagnostics. It is not sampler-only peak memory, and the result cannot establish convergence, recovery, performance superiority, or scientific thresholds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_probe"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_review","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_review(results)\n\nSummarize a tuple or vector of results returned by mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_probe. The review preserves each parent/child memory preflight, worker outcome, elapsed time, worker-process peak RSS, and the retained short-NUTS geometry fields. It checks submitted cell order within either the stress/scaling sequence or the primary short-NUTS subset. Duplicate cell IDs are rejected; mixed collections are retained as requiring attention.\n\nThis function runs no process or MCMC, applies no resource or scientific threshold, and never advances automatically to another cell. Peak RSS remains whole-worker memory, including startup and compilation, rather than sampler- only memory. stage_1_operability checks whether both provisional Stage 1 primary cells have complete submitted receipts. Even a complete pair remains operability metadata pending manual scope review; it cannot establish convergence, recovery, portable performance, or scientific validity. Missing receipts request recovery or submission of existing records before any rerun.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_isolated_resource_review"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_candidates","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_candidates()\n\nEnumerate the 16 fixed-Q MGMFRM primary-grid candidates implied by the two dense/sparse designs and the source-anchored person, item, and rater sample sizes. Each row can be passed to simulate_mgmfrm_validation_primary_candidate.\n\nThese rows are candidates, not a frozen analysis grid. staged_review provisionally separates a minimum operability pair, a larger-support design pair, and a sparse rater-coverage extension. Its five-cell union is minimal only for those three declared contrasts; it is not a globally minimal or factorial validation design. Candidate seeds are only for structural generation preflight. The function runs no sampler, applies no scientific thresholds, and cannot authorize evaluation. Final cell selection, evaluation replications, and a resource envelope covering the selected cells remain unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_candidates"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_preflight","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_preflight(\n    contract = mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_candidates();\n    truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nGenerate and structurally inspect every candidate in the primary grid without running MCMC. Generation errors remain typed exceptions; this planning preflight does not convert failures into missing values or analysis outcomes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_preflight"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_resource_plan","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_primary_resource_plan()\n\nReturn four ordered, non-executing four-category primary-grid cells for MCMC-free gradient resource profiling. The cells cover the minimum sparse shape, the only dense shape inside the current short-NUTS observation bound, an intermediate dense shape, and one of the largest shapes inside the current gradient bound.\n\nPass one row at a time to mgmfrm_validation_resource_probe. The plan does not authorize automatic progression, short-NUTS execution, final grid selection, or scientific interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_primary_resource_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_protocol","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_protocol()\n\nReturn the machine-readable Stage-A draft for scientific validation of the narrow fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM branch.\n\nThe protocol separates decisions already fixed by the implemented likelihood from execution and decision components that remain blocked. It does not run a simulation, fit a model, authorize a stable claim, or treat earlier pilots as evaluation evidence. status == :stage_a_draft_execution_blocked remains in force until every item in readiness.blockers has an executable contract and independent review.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_protocol"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_replication_precision","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_replication_precision(replications;\n    nominal_coverage = 0.95,\n    coverage_mcse_target = nothing,\n    binary_rate_mcse_target = nothing,\n    bias_error_sd_reference = nothing,\n    bias_mcse_target = nothing)\n\nReturn an MCMC-free Monte Carlo precision reference for proposed independent simulation replication counts. Coverage-rate MCSE is evaluated at nominal_coverage; decision, calibration, and failure rates use the conservative Bernoulli variance 0.25. Bias MCSE is available only when an external or predeclared replication-error standard deviation is supplied.\n\nOptional targets report the algebraic minimum replication count and whether each proposed count reaches it. The function does not select a count, freeze a study, authorize execution, or define scientific thresholds. Bias precision is conditional on replications that produce numeric errors; an attempt-complete study must still report failures on the full planned denominator and specify a failure-sensitivity analysis rather than dropping failed fits silently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_replication_precision"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_resource_probe","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_resource_probe(\n    plan = nothing; execute_measurement = false, repetitions = 3,\n    maximum_cells = 2, maximum_observations_per_cell = 10_000,\n    minimum_free_memory_bytes = 2 * 1024^3, truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nPlan or execute a portable, MCMC-free resource probe for fixed-Q MGMFRM. The default plan contains one dense and one connected-sparse regular-response cell. Execution measures generation and repeated ForwardDiff log-density/ gradient evaluations after one untimed warmup. Hard cell and observation bounds and an available-memory preflight are checked before generation. The threshold cannot be lowered below 1 GiB. This preflight is a screening check, not a memory reservation or a guarantee that measurement will complete.\n\nThe result records local runtime and allocation metadata without repository paths, commit IDs, source hashes, or fit objects. It cannot assess convergence, recovery, coverage, priors, Q, backend superiority, or scientific thresholds. Gradient timings are not extrapolated to full NUTS runtime. A separate bounded short-NUTS probe is available when a cell-specific resource decision requires one, but no result authorizes automatic progression to it. Rows returned by mgmfrm_validation_primary_resource_plan are accepted one at a time for four-category primary-grid measurement. The first two rows also fit inside the current primary short-NUTS workload bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_resource_probe"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_scaled_resource_plan","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_scaled_resource_plan()\n\nReturn four ordered, non-executing resource cells for the fixed-Q MGMFRM short-NUTS probe. The sequence adds a small dense cell, matched-observation sparse and dense cells, then a larger sparse cell. Every row remains compatible with mgmfrm_validation_short_nuts_resource_probe, but must be passed and executed one at a time.\n\nThe plan is operational rather than scientific. It does not select the final sample-size grid, run MCMC, use evaluation seeds, or authorize automatic progression after a failure or memory rejection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_scaled_resource_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_short_nuts_resource_probe","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"mgmfrm_validation_short_nuts_resource_probe(\n    plan = nothing; execute_measurement = false,\n    minimum_free_memory_bytes = 2 * 1024^3,\n    maximum_observations_per_cell = 1_000, truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nPlan or explicitly execute one memory-guarded, short AdvancedHMC/NUTS resource probe for fixed-Q MGMFRM. The default cell is connected-sparse with one chain, 25 warmup transitions, and 25 retained draws. The memory and workload gates run before data generation or MCMC; the minimum memory requirement cannot be lowered below 1 GiB.\n\nThe probe accepts one five-category response-stress row or one four-category primary-grid row. This probe assesses only local short-chain operability. It does not assess convergence, recovery, coverage, backend performance, prior choice, Q choice, or scientific thresholds. Returned fit-attempt rows preserve typed failures, but fit objects are discarded. Cumulative Julia allocations and endpoint free memory are recorded; peak memory is not claimed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mgmfrm_validation_short_nuts_resource_probe"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"parameter_recovery(fit::MFRMFit, truth; interval = 0.95)\nparameter_recovery(fit::GMFRMFit, truth; interval = 0.95,\n    parameter_space = :direct)\nparameter_recovery(design::FacetDesign, draws, truth; interval = 0.95,\n    parameter_space = :direct)\n\nCompare posterior draws with known simulation truth. Rows include the true value, posterior mean/median/interval, bias, absolute bias, squared error, relative bias, interval width, and whether the posterior interval covers the true value. MFRM/RSM/PCM rows use the fit-supported direct parameterization. GMFRM/MGMFRM fit objects and preview designs use direct constrained parameters by default; pass parameter_space = :raw to evaluate raw fit-ready candidate coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery_plot_data","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"parameter_recovery_plot_data(recovery_rows)\nparameter_recovery_plot_data(fit::MFRMFit, truth; interval = 0.95)\n\nReturn plotting-ready rows for a parameter-recovery scatter/interval plot without depending on a plotting package. Suggested mappings are x = true_value, y = estimate, and ymin/ymax = interval_lower/interval_upper; the diagonal reference line is y = x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery_plot_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"parameter_recovery_summary(recovery_rows; by = :block)\nparameter_recovery_summary(fit::MFRMFit, truth; interval = 0.95,\n    by = :block)\n\nAggregate parameter-recovery rows by block, or use by = :all for a single overall row. Summaries include mean bias, mean absolute error, RMSE, median absolute error, max absolute error, interval coverage, and mean interval width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_recovery_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_local_dependence","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulate_local_dependence(row;\n    max_ratings = 1_000_000,\n    max_probability_cells = 5_000_000,\n    max_truth_cells = 5_000_000)\nsimulate_local_dependence(scenario_id::Symbol;\n    seed = 20260720, n_persons = 40, n_testlets = 4,\n    items_per_testlet = 3, n_raters = 4, n_categories = 4,\n    max_ratings = 1_000_000,\n    max_probability_cells = 5_000_000,\n    max_truth_cells = 5_000_000)\n\nGenerate one LD1a ordinal known-truth bundle from a row returned by local_dependence_simulation_grid, or from its scenario_id. Response probabilities use a separately coded adjacent-category partial-credit kernel; sampling does not depend on a fitted model's probability or likelihood implementation. The bundle keeps the intended category scale even when a random realization omits an extreme category, and records exact sequence positions, event-keyed uniforms, every additive truth component, requested design checks, and resource counts.\n\nmax_ratings, max_probability_cells, and max_truth_cells bound the number of generated ratings, category-probability cells, and dense truth-surface cells before their corresponding allocations. Response and missingness uniforms use named component seeds and the semantic key (person, testlet, rater, within_testlet_item) to initialize a fresh Julia MersenneTwister draw for each event. Thus common events are invariant to row enumeration and to adding later within-testlet items. The seed derivation is stable in source and avoids Julia's session-dependent hash, but bitwise identity across Julia versions or alternative RNG implementations is not claimed.\n\nThe result is generator and pre-fit evidence only. It does not run posterior sampling or local_dependence_summary, calibrate Q3/FDR/FWER references, provide a diagnostic decision, or identify the generating mechanism in observed data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_local_dependence"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_mgmfrm_response_stress","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulate_mgmfrm_response_stress(plan_row; truth_scale = 0.15)\n\nGenerate one five-category, two-dimensional fixed-Q MGMFRM stress case from a row returned by mgmfrm_response_stress_plan. Responses are first drawn from the package MGMFRM kernel. Non-baseline scenarios then apply a declared deterministic intervention to create the requested category gap or boundary pattern while preserving the intended 1:5 scale.\n\nThe returned object retains raw/direct generating parameters, pre-intervention probabilities and scores, changed-row indices, design validation, Q validation, and the sampler-free ordinal-pattern audit. No model is fitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_mgmfrm_response_stress"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_mgmfrm_validation_primary_candidate","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulate_mgmfrm_validation_primary_candidate(candidate;\n    truth_scale = 0.15, seed = nothing)\n\nGenerate one regular four-category, two-dimensional fixed-Q MGMFRM dataset from a row returned by mgmfrm_validation_primary_grid_candidates. The default seed belongs only to structural preflight; an explicit seed may be supplied for later evaluation protocols.\n\nThe returned case preserves the generating parameters and probabilities and checks data validity, fixed-Q identification, category support, and expected observation count. It does not fit a model or produce validation evidence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulate_mgmfrm_validation_primary_candidate"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulation_grid","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulation_grid(; densities = (:sparse, :moderate, :near_complete),\n    anchor_sizes = (0, 2, 5), ratings_per_target = (1, 2, 4),\n    category_pathologies = (:none, :skipped_middle, :top_set),\n    rater_noise = (:low, :moderate, :high), dff = (:none, :rater_by_group),\n    dimensionalities = (1, 2),\n    misspecifications = (:none, :wrong_thresholds, :omitted_dff),\n    repetitions = 1, base_seed = 20260620, grid_id = \"default\",\n    n_persons = 48, n_items = 12, n_raters = 6, n_categories = 4,\n    view = :full)\n\nReturn predeclared simulation-study rows that cross sparse-to-near-complete design density, anchor size, ratings per target, category pathologies, rater noise, DFF patterns, dimensionality, and misspecification. Rows are planning metadata for reproducible simulation/recovery studies; this helper does not simulate responses, fit models, or evaluate claims. Use the rows with simulate_responses, parameter_recovery, calibration, predictive-check, and model-comparison helpers when executing a study. Use view = :public for compact reader-facing rows; view = :full preserves the complete planning record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulation_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.simulation_grid_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"simulation_grid_summary(rows; required_axes =\n    (:density, :anchor_size, :ratings_per_target, :category_pathology,\n     :rater_noise, :dff, :dimensionality, :misspecification),\n     view = :full)\n\nSummarize whether predeclared simulation-grid rows cover the default critical axes for sparse Bayesian MFRM/GMFRM/MGMFRM validation. The summary reports missing required axes, single-value axes, varied axes, row/scenario counts, and the repetition/seed envelope. It is a coverage check for a planned grid, not evidence that the grid has been run. view = :public returns a compact reader-facing summary; the default :full payload remains unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.simulation_grid_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.stan_validation_row","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"stan_validation_row(data::ScalarValidationData, x, stan_log_density;\n    stan_gradient = nothing, tolerance = 1e-9, label = nothing,\n    size = nothing, known_log_density = nothing, known_gradient = nothing,\n    known_tolerance = tolerance, fixture_path = nothing,\n    known_fixture_path = nothing, stan_model = nothing,\n    fixture_sha256 = nothing, known_fixture_sha256 = nothing,\n    stan_model_sha256 = nothing)\n\nReturn one machine-readable validation row comparing the Julia scalar GMFRM-style validation log density and analytic gradient with a Stan or BridgeStan fixture evaluated at the same raw parameter vector.\n\nThe function deliberately accepts already parsed numeric fixture values instead of reading JSON files, so the package does not depend on a JSON parser at runtime. Use stan_validation_summary to aggregate rows for the predeclared small/medium validation gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.stan_validation_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api-validation-evidence/#BayesianMGMFRM.stan_validation_summary","page":"Validation and Evidence","text":"stan_validation_summary(rows; required_sizes = (:small, :medium))\nstan_validation_summary(row, rows...; required_sizes = (:small, :medium))\n\nAggregate stan_validation_row results into a gate-level summary. By default, the gate requires both :small and :medium scalar Stan/BridgeStan fixtures to be present and passing. The returned summary explicitly records that this evidence is a scalar log-density/gradient fixture check, not a broad Stan sampling or generalized-fit comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.stan_validation_summary"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#Model-Equations","page":"Model Equations","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nFor a first explanation with a worked three-category example, read How Rating Models Work. This page gives the more detailed mathematical and source conventions.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.jl keeps model-equation metadata separate from fitting availability. The model_equation contract records the intended family, category kernel, primary-source references, required parameter blocks, identification restrictions, implementation gaps, and whether a specification is fit-ready. fit_ready describes the stable compiler route; experimental_fit_available separately identifies an executable generalized configuration.\n\nFitting supports one-dimensional MFRM/RSM/PCM, experimental fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM, scalar GMFRM with item discrimination × rater consistency, and fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM with dimensions >= 1. Fixed-coefficient MFRM uses independent abilities by default; Experimental.correlated(spec) explicitly selects population correlation for two between-item dimensions. See Correlated ability dimensions for its priors, coordinate transformation and fitting constraints. For both fixed-coefficient models, prior scales and facet labels explain the reconstructed rater/step distributions and prior-anchored locations. Broader GMFRM/MGMFRM specifications expose source-aligned manifests and preview compiler rows for review. Only the restricted generalized configurations in Scope and Releases can be fitted.","title":"Model Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#Source-Map","page":"Model Equations","text":"Partial-credit lineage: Masters (1982), DOI 10.1007/BF02296272.\nRating-scale lineage: Andrich (1978), DOI 10.1007/BF02293814.\nGeneralized MFRM target: Uto and Ueno (2020), DOI 10.1007/s41237-020-00115-7.\nMultidimensional generalized MFRM target: Uto (2021), DOI 10.1007/s41237-021-00144-w.\n\nThe model_family_contract complements this equation manifest. Calling it without an argument returns the family skeleton; calling it with a specification or design returns the exact category kernel, multidimensional structure, aggregation rule, loading policy, step owner, latent-correlation policy, identification status, and fitting boundary for that object.\n\ncontract = model_family_contract(spec)\ncontract.dimensionality.classification\ncontract.steps.owner\ncontract.support.implementation_status\n\nSee API for the rendered model_equation and model_family_contract docstrings.","title":"Source Map"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#Generalized-Partial-Credit-and-Multidimensional-Structure","page":"Model Equations","text":"The guarded scalar GMFRM kernel follows Uto and Ueno (2020), Eq. 9. Its multiplier is alpha_i * alpha_r; that original equation does not include a fixed 1.7 factor. Uto (2021), Eq. 4 restates the scalar model with 1.7, whereas the multidimensional Eq. 6 below also uses 1.7. The implementation keeps the original 2020 scalar convention and the 2021 multidimensional convention explicit in category.source_scale_contract. Direct numerical comparison of discrimination parameters across those families therefore requires a predeclared scale harmonization rather than an implicit comparison.\n\nThe related constants 1.7 and 1.702 have different contractual roles. 1.702 is the conventional minimax scaling constant used to approximate a normal-ogive response curve with a logistic curve; 1.7 is its commonly rounded form and is the literal value printed in Uto (2021), Eqs. 4 and 6. Consequently, the source-reproduction likelihood uses exactly 1.7, not 1.702. The family contract records 1.702 only as normal_ogive_minimax_reference_constant. Changing the executable constant would define a new likelihood version and require regenerated reference results; it is not a numerical-precision correction to the current source model.\n\nThe guarded MGMFRM kernel follows the conditional response form in Uto (2021):\n\nP(Y_pir=k mid boldsymboltheta_p)\npropto\nexpleft\nsum_m=1^k 17alpha_r\nleft(\nsum_ell=1^Lalpha_ielltheta_pell\n-beta_i-beta_r-d_im\nright)\nright\n\nThis is an adjacent-category cumulative-softmax, or GPCM-form, kernel. There is no integral over ability inside the conditional response probability. The package samples person abilities as explicit posterior parameters. Integrating other parameters out of a marginal posterior conceptually, or approximating a posterior marginal with HMC draws, is a different operation from defining the response function.\n\nUto (2021) describes the multidimensional GPCM basis and proposed MGMFRM as non-compensatory. At the same time, the displayed predictor aggregates active dimensions through the additive weighted sum sum_l alpha[i,l] * theta[p,l]. The package records the source label separately from the algebra. On a cross-loaded item, an increase in one ability can offset a decrease in another while preserving the weighted sum and category probabilities; see the worked conditional comparison. No alternative conjunctive or product-of-dimension response kernel is fitted.\n\nThe present fixed-Q restriction gives these structural cases:\n\nQ structure Meaning Current status\nOne Q column, active for every item Unidimensional restriction of the MGMFRM kernel Executable with an explicit one-column Q; not the scalar rater-step GMFRM.\nTwo or more Q columns, exactly one active dimension in every item row Between-item multidimensionality Executable in the guarded fixed-Q branch when the remaining design checks pass.\nMultiple active dimensions in at least one item row Within-item multidimensionality for those items A fixed confirmatory zero mask with estimated positive active loadings is executable but warning-bearing; it is not exploratory loading selection.\nBoth single-dimension and cross-loaded item rows Mixed between-/within-item structure Representable through fixed Q, subject to Q rank, person support, pure-item, and connectivity review.\nDense or freely estimated item-dimension structure as in the unrestricted source parameterization Every alpha[i,l] is potentially estimated without a fixed zero mask Not implemented by the guarded fit.\nConjunctive, product, minimum, or another non-additive dimension rule A separate operationally non-compensatory response function Not implemented.\n\nUto's Eq. 6 assigns an alpha[i,l] parameter to each item-dimension pair and therefore describes an unrestricted, within-item-capable loading surface rather than a between-item Q design. The guarded package branch deliberately masks some loadings to zero with a fixed Q. An all-active mask is not automatically a valid substitute: the current validator also requires distinguishable Q columns and adequate identification/design support. The guarded branch is therefore a restricted Uto-equation candidate, not the complete source loading model.\n\nThe model_family_contract now exposes this taxonomy directly as :unidimensional, :between_item, :within_item, or :mixed_between_and_within_item, while q_matrix_validation continues to record the lower-level Q rank, person support, pure-item, and connectivity evidence. A missing Q is rejected during specification construction; an empty-row Q is rejected by validation and is never classified as a valid multidimensional structure.\n\nStep sharing is not generated once for every facet:\n\nFamily Category kernel Step ownership and sharing\nStable MFRM/RSM Rating-scale PCM form One shared step vector across items and raters.\nStable MFRM/PCM Partial-credit form Item-specific step vectors shared across raters.\nGuarded scalar GMFRM Generalized partial-credit form with item discrimination and rater consistency Rater-specific step vectors shared across items and persons.\nGuarded fixed-Q MGMFRM One- or multidimensional generalized partial-credit form with item-dimension discrimination and rater consistency Item-specific step vectors shared across raters and dimensions.\n\nConsequently, adding a new facet does not automatically increase the number of step vectors. Arbitrary facet-specific, nested, crossed, or partially pooled step structures require a new likelihood, identification rule, prior, and reporting contract.","title":"Generalized Partial-Credit and Multidimensional Structure"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#One-versus-multiple-dimensions-under-the-same-kernel","page":"Model Equations","text":"A useful comparison asks whether items need distinct ability dimensions while keeping the response equation and prior conventions aligned. Set family=:mgmfrm, dimensions=1, q_matrix=trues(n_items,1) for the explicit one-dimensional restriction. It has adjacent-category log odds\n\nlogfracP(Y_pir=k)P(Y_pir=k-1)\n=17alpha_r(alpha_itheta_p-beta_i-beta_r-d_ik)\n\nThis is not the package's scalar family=:gmfrm equation, whose log odds are alpha_i * alpha_r * (theta_p - beta_i - beta_r - d_rk). Besides the fixed 1.7 multiplier, the models differ in step owner and whether item discrimination multiplies locations and steps. Comparing those two named families is possible, but does not isolate dimensionality.\n\nThe one-dimensional MGMFRM keeps positive item loadings, zero-sum rater severities, product-one rater consistencies, item-owned zero-sum nonbaseline steps and the same prior rules as its multidimensional counterpart. With K categories each item has K-2 free steps. Ability origin and scale remain prior-anchored; admitting one dimension is not a new likelihood-identification constraint. Raw, normalized exchangeable and normalized source priors remain explicit choices. The correlated wrapper still requires exactly two dimensions.\n\nFor a between-item comparison with one active loading per item, the loading count and each parameter block's prior convention can be kept the same; only the ability dimension structure and item assignment change. Cross-loaded models also change the number and roles of loadings, so the example excludes them. Equal numeric prior settings do not imply equal joint prior predictive laws: sharing one ability or using independent dimension-specific abilities changes dependence between item responses. This is not a nested comparison obtained by fixing a supported correlation parameter to one.\n\nSee the matched-dimension workflow for common training/held-out splits. The one-dimensional extension is an implementation restriction of this equation, not a claim that the published scalar GMFRM has been reproduced, or evidence of calibrated intervals or reliable dimensionality selection.","title":"One versus multiple dimensions under the same kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#Estimands,-origins-and-units","page":"Model Equations","text":"For fixed-Q MGMFRM, positive loadings specify orientation but do not by themselves identify ability origins and units in the conditional response model. Write the predictor as a_i' theta_p - b_i, with the prime here denoting transpose. For arbitrary shifts c_d and positive scales t_d, the following simultaneous change preserves every category probability:\n\ntheta^*_pd=t_dtheta_pd+c_dqquad\na^*_id=a_idt_dqquad\nb^*_i=b_i+sum_d a_idc_dt_d\n\nRater severities, consistencies and item steps are unchanged. This preserves the fixed zeros of Q, including mixed Q. The declared priors generally distinguish the transformed parameters; the invariance is a likelihood property, not equality of posterior targets with unchanged prior formulas. The normal population ability prior fixes a distributional origin and unit; it does not force the fitted persons' sample mean to zero or variance to one.\n\nFor pure-Q items i and j in the same dimension, this has concrete consequences:\n\nQuantity Effect of the change Interpretation\nAbility difference theta_p - theta_q Multiplied by t_d Sign and ordering are invariant; magnitude and nonzero practical-equivalence bounds require an ability unit.\nLoading ratio a_i/a_j Unchanged Same-dimension relative sensitivity; cross-dimension ratios have separate units.\nItem-location difference b_i-b_j Adds (a_i-a_j)c_d/t_d Generally origin-dependent, including its sign.\nDifference b_i/a_i-b_j/a_j Multiplied by t_d Difference between centers in ability coordinates; sign is invariant but magnitude needs a unit.\nItem location relative to the panel, b_i-a_i*mean(theta_d) Unchanged Refers to the same fitted-person panel; changing that panel changes the estimand.\nRater severity difference or consistency ratio Unchanged Conditional on the specified zero-sum/product-one constraints; this does not remove prior sensitivity or establish rating reliability.\nMatched category probabilities Unchanged Person coordinates and item parameters must move together.\n\nFor example, loadings (0.8,1.6) and item locations (0.2,0.1) give an item-location difference of 0.1. Shifting that ability dimension by one changes the locations to (1.0,1.7), and the difference to -0.7, with exactly the same probabilities. A difference in b remains a valid estimand under a declared population origin; it is not automatically an origin-free item comparison. The existing location diagnostics already report panel-relative locations, using draw-specific loadings.\n\nFor a complete crossed, pure-Q design, with finite parameters, at least one item per dimension and nonconstant person abilities in every dimension, these location/positive-scale changes exhaust the conditional response model's ambiguity. To see this, take adjacent log odds divided by 1.7. A nonzero person difference is proportional to each rater's consistency; product one fixes all consistencies. Dividing them out, then averaging with the zero-sum rater and item-step constraints recovers severities, steps and the person-item predictors. Within each dimension, person differences in these predictors determine loadings and abilities up to a common positive scale; their levels leave one origin. This argument uses the entire probability table, not estimated cell frequencies from a single rating panel.\n\nIf all persons have the same ability in a dimension, an individual item's loading and location can compensate each other, so even loading ratios need not be identified. The completeness claim does not extend here to sparse or cross-loaded designs, or to a model with abilities integrated out. Structural Q checks and invariance of a selected contrast alone do not establish those broader identifiability results or adequate finite-data precision.\n\nFor the first core candidate's proper normal raw prior and a finite set of valid category observations, posterior existence follows separately: each likelihood is strictly positive and at most one, so its prior integral is in (0,1]. A normalized posterior therefore exists. This does not establish numerical representability, MCMC convergence, recovery, calibration, or the scientific appropriateness of the prior.\n\nThe MGMFRM prior comparison distinguishes the raw prior from explicit normalized priors, explains their rater asymmetry and gives explicit scale-matching conventions.","title":"Estimands, origins and units"},{"category":"section","location":"model-equations/#Independent-Known-Truth-Ordinal-Kernel","page":"Model Equations","text":"The research helper simulate_local_dependence does not call the fitted probability or likelihood implementation. Its optional generating effects below are not additional effects available through fit. For row i, category 0 has log weight zero and the standalone adjacent-category recurrence is\n\nell_i0=0 qquad\nell_ik=ell_ik-1+eta_i-delta_ik qquad\nP(Y_i=k)=fracexp(ell_ik)sum_h exp(ell_ih)\n\nThe generating location records every additive component separately:\n\neta_i = A_piji-beta_ji-rho_ri\n       +u_piti+h_risi-g_riti\n       -d_riz_i\nqquad\nA_pj=sqrt1-(lambda q_j2)^2theta^(1)_p\n       +lambda q_j2theta^(2)_p\n\nHere the optional terms represent person-by-testlet variation, rater-response halo, rater-by-task severity, an omitted second dimension, and sequence drift. The omitted-dimension control crosses active and inactive items within every testlet and uses the square-root weight above so its active-item latent variance does not increase merely because a second independent ability was added. Individual simulation scenarios activate only their declared components. The zero/near-zero/small/moderate/large scales are study-local inputs, not model defaults or universal practical thresholds. This independent data-generating equation describes generated data only; it does not calibrate an observed-data diagnostic or enable clustered effects in the fitted model.\n\nEvery generated row is sampled independently after conditioning on the full recorded truth. This differs from the baseline MFRM assumption: omitting a shared testlet, halo, or second-dimension component leaves shared latent variation, whereas rater-by-task and sequence terms misspecify the baseline mean structure. Generated bundles report these cases separately through baseline_mfrm_assumption_status instead of an unqualified global-local- independence boolean.","title":"Independent Known-Truth Ordinal Kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Fitting-and-Artifact-API","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.","title":"Fitting and Artifact API"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Stable-fitting-and-shared-artifacts","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"","title":"Stable fitting and shared artifacts"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Optional-analysis-figures","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"Load CairoMakie to use these qualified entry points. They return editable Figures and use the numerical summary functions documented in this manual. plot_wright and plot_pathway accept stable unidimensional MFRM fits only.","title":"Optional analysis figures"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Experimental-saved-multidimensional-MFRM-reports","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"For an existing fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM cache, use load_fit_cache followed by fit_report. Reports include the rating-design audit, free and reconstructed parameter summaries, diagnostics, conditional predictive checks and reproducibility information. Fixed coefficients and derived coordinates are labelled separately; unsupported analyses state their reasons. Reporting uses the fit's saved diagnostic settings. Fits made with Experimental.ExchangeablePrior use these same operations; their saved prior supplies the kernel, marginal and contrast severity scales.\n\nSet include_prior_predictive = true to add simulation summaries from the saved model and priors. Control its draw budget with prior_predictive_ndraws, its parameter interval with prior_interval, and reproducibility with seed. Figure bundles additionally accept prior and prior_predictive; their PDF/SVG figures and numerical JSON use the same report summaries.\n\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"multidimensional-fit.jls\")\nreport = fit_report(restored; posterior_lower = 0.05, posterior_upper = 0.95,\n    seed = 42)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"multidimensional-report\", fit_report_public(report))\nartifact = fit_artifact(restored; view = :public)\n\nPosterior bounds must define a central interval strictly inside (0, 1). These experimental models use unit logits and fixed Q coefficients. Abilities are independent by default; Experimental.correlated(spec) estimates population correlation for two between-item dimensions. Correlated reports default to view = :public and include rho intervals and diagnostics. Estimate with BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit; see the multidimensional example. Earlier independent-model caches remain readable. Saving a correlated fit records its model and actual priors separately. These saved-result reports need neither sampling nor CairoMakie. A complete report means that its requested sections ran successfully; inspect the MCMC diagnostics separately.\n\nLoad CairoMakie to plot an ability dimension by its stored name and save a report with figures:\n\nusing CairoMakie\ndimension = fit_metadata(restored).dimension_labels[1]\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; block = :person, dimension)\nsave(\"ability.pdf\", figure)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"multidimensional-figures\", restored; view = :public,\n    figures = (posterior = (; block = :person, dimension),\n        diagnostics = (; block = :person, dimension), predictive = (;)),\n    posterior_lower = 0.05, posterior_upper = 0.95,\n    predictive_interval = 0.9, seed = 42)\nreopened = load_fit_report_bundle(\"multidimensional-figures\")\n\nPosterior and trace/rank plots use model coordinates in unit logits. Fixed coefficients have no credible interval or convergence diagnostic. The predictive figure checks the observed rating design using the report's exact replicated category summaries. Bundles include PDF/SVG figures and their numerical JSON inputs; reopening and verifying a bundle does not require CairoMakie. For large fits, select a smaller set of exact parameter names or explicitly increase max_parameters and the figure size. Whole-fit MCMC warnings remain visible.","title":"Experimental saved multidimensional MFRM reports"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Experimental-model-scale-summaries","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"Use this qualified function for transformed GMFRM/MGMFRM parameters. posterior_summary retains its raw-coordinate meaning for those fits.","title":"Experimental model-scale summaries"},{"category":"section","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#Experimental-compatibility-types","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"Earlier code may use GMFRMFit and MGMFRMFit at the package root. New generalized workflows should access these result types through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental; see Experimental Models.","title":"Experimental compatibility types"},{"category":"type","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMPrior","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"MFRMPrior(; person_sd = 1.5, rater_sd = 1.0, item_sd = 1.0, step_sd = 1.0)\n\nIndependent zero-centered normal priors for the identified minimal MFRM parameter vector returned by getdesign. The scales apply to free person, rater, item, and threshold-step coordinates after reference, exact hard-anchor, and sum-to-zero constraints have been imposed. Fixed anchors receive no prior density contribution. The prior centers are not shifted when a hard-anchor value changes, so likelihood-equivalent anchor reparameterizations are not in general prior- or posterior-invariant. All scales must remain finite and strictly positive after conversion to Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMLogDensity","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"MFRMLogDensity(design::FacetDesign; prior = MFRMPrior())\nMFRMLogDensity(spec::FacetSpec; prior = MFRMPrior())\n\nLogDensityProblems.jl target for the current minimal Bayesian MFRM/RSM/PCM posterior. This object exposes the same log posterior as logposterior through LogDensityProblems.logdensity, so external samplers and automatic differentiation wrappers can use the package's design compiler without relying on the built-in random-walk Metropolis sampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMLogDensity"},{"category":"type","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMFit","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"MFRMFit\n\nPosterior draws and sampler metadata returned by fit for a stable additive MFRM/RSM/PCM model. Warmup draws are excluded. draws is a matrix with one retained posterior draw per row and one identified design parameter per column, in fit.design.parameter_names order. When multiple chains are requested, rows are grouped by chain and recorded in chain_ids and iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.MFRMFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit(spec_or_design; prior = MFRMPrior(), backend = :julia,\n    ndraws = 1000, warmup = 1000, chains = 1, kwargs...)\n\nEstimate a stable one-dimensional MFRM from a specification made by mfrm_spec. Return an MFRMFit containing retained posterior draws, the model/data description and sampling information.\n\nChoose backend = :advancedhmc for Julia NUTS or :cmdstan for CmdStan NUTS. The compatibility default :julia is random-walk Metropolis, not NUTS. ndraws and warmup are counts per chain. The default single chain is insufficient for between-chain convergence checks; request multiple chains and inspect diagnostics and numerical precision before interpreting results.\n\nExamples\n\nAfter constructing spec using the example in FacetData, this code runs a deliberately short sampler demonstration:\n\nfit_result = fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc,\n    chains = 2, warmup = 50, ndraws = 50, seed = 102)\ncheck = diagnostics(fit_result; view = :public)\ncheck.summary.flag\nposterior_summary(fit_result)\nposterior_mcse(fit_result)\n\nThese short settings are not adequate-inference recommendations. A completed fit or a passing diagnostic flag does not establish model validity or interval coverage. Select priors with prior_predictive_check before an analysis; use save_fit_cache and load_fit_cache to reuse a fit.\n\nGMFRM, multidimensional MFRM and MGMFRM require the documented experimental route and its model-specific priors and restrictions; see BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit. Type ??fit for all backend controls.\n\nExtended help\n\nfit(spec_or_design; prior = MFRMPrior(), backend = :julia, ndraws = 1000,\n    warmup = 1000, chains = 1, step_size = 0.05, init = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), seed = nothing, target_accept = 0.8,\n    max_depth = 10, max_energy_error = 1000.0, metric = :diagonal,\n    ad_backend = :ForwardDiff, init_jitter = 0.0, progress = false,\n    cmdstan_path = nothing, cmdstan_cache_dir = nothing)\nBayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc, ...)\nBayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec; backend = :cmdstan, ...)\n\nFit a Bayesian MFRM with rating-scale or partial-credit thresholds using the selected backend. backend = :julia uses a random-walk Metropolis kernel, backend = :advancedhmc uses AdvancedHMC/NUTS directly, and backend = :turing wraps the same MFRMLogDensity target in a Turing/NUTS model. backend = :cmdstan compiles the package-owned stable MFRM Stan model, runs CmdStan's NUTS command-line interface, and converts its retained draws and sampler columns to the same MFRMFit result. CmdStan is discovered through cmdstan_path, CMDSTAN, CMDSTAN_HOME, or a versioned ~/.cmdstan installation. Supplying seed uses a local MersenneTwister(seed) and records the seed in sampler_controls; otherwise the supplied rng is used without a replayable seed record.\n\nstep_size must remain finite and positive after conversion to Float64. For NUTS backends, the converted target_accept must lie strictly between zero and one, max_energy_error must be finite and positive, and init_jitter must be non-negative and remain finite. CmdStan requires max_energy_error = 1000.0. Turing adapts for exactly warmup transitions per chain, independently of ndraws; zero warmup disables adaptation. Its initial state and all adapting transitions are excluded from the retained result.\n\nBoth guarded generalized configurations accept :advancedhmc and :cmdstan. Their CmdStan adapters sample the same raw-coordinate targets, apply the Julia direct-parameter transforms, and check generated pointwise log likelihoods against Julia at every retained draw. MGMFRM remains limited to its fixed-Q, identity-correlation confirmatory contract.\n\nThe AdvancedHMC backend accepts ad_backend = :ForwardDiff by default. ad_backend = :ReverseDiff can be used when the corresponding AD package is available in the active environment, and ad_backend = :analytic uses a target-provided LogDensityProblems.logdensity_and_gradient method when one exists. The Turing backend currently accepts ad_backend = :ForwardDiff; analytic target gradients are not consumed by Turing's model trace, and the ReverseDiff path is left to a future adapter after the Turing AD interface can support this wrapped target reliably.\n\nThe BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental namespace is intentionally narrow. The legacy experimental = true keyword remains source-compatible. Both routes are accepted for the scalar source-aligned experimental GMFRM configuration with family = :gmfrm, dimensions = 1, thresholds = :partial_credit, and discrimination = :rater, returning GMFRMFit, and for the fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM candidate with family = :mgmfrm, dimensions >= 1, thresholds = :partial_credit, discrimination = :none, and a fixed q_matrix, returning MGMFRMFit. Both generalized surfaces require empty anchors and no fitted validation-bias/DFF terms. Multidimensional GMFRM, rating-scale generalized kernels, exploratory MGMFRM loadings, free latent correlations, non-rater GMFRM discrimination, non-default generic MGMFRM discrimination, and MFRMPrior priors for generalized raw-coordinate fits are rejected before compilation or cache lookup. Guarded generalized fits accept BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior; its scales apply only to raw unconstrained coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_metadata","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_metadata(fit::MFRMFit; view = :full)\n\nReturn report-ready metadata for a fitted minimal MFRM object, including data dimensions, model family, threshold structure, posterior draw dimensions, backend, sampler, warmup, step size, sampler controls, and prior scales. This metadata helper does not itself report chain-aware convergence diagnostics; use diagnostics, sampler_diagnostics, and mcmc_diagnostics for sampler and R-hat/ESS summaries.\n\nThe compatibility default view = :full preserves the complete fitting record. Use view = :public for a reader-facing projection with an explicit family and stability label and a compact set of portable reporting fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_artifact","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_artifact(fit; view = :full, include_draws = false,\n             include_log_posterior = include_draws,\n             include_sampler_stats = false,\n             include_environment = true,\n             include_packages = false,\n             include_environment_paths = false,\n             split_chains = true,\n             rhat_threshold = 1.01,\n             ess_threshold = 400)\n\nReturn a reproducibility artifact for a fitted MFRM, experimental scalar GMFRM, or experimental fixed-Q MGMFRM object. The artifact combines the model manifest, selected diagnostic surface, posterior summary, sampler controls, RNG replay metadata, and optional environment metadata. Draws, log-posterior values, and sampler-stat rows are omitted by default to keep the artifact compact; set the corresponding include_* keyword to retain them for a cached-draw report path. Environment metadata omits machine-local paths by default; include_environment_paths = true is an explicit private-record opt-in. The compatibility default view = :full retains the complete archive contract and is intended for private reproduction records. Use view = :public when sharing a reader-facing, path-free projection with its own verifiable content hash. Stored fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM results support both views and use their recorded diagnostic settings. Their artifact retains both free and reconstructed unit-logit summaries and the source sample identity. The public view labels support experimental. Estimate with BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit. New full artifacts use mfrm_fixed_q_fit_artifact.v2; cache readers also validate old v1 artifacts with their original metadata and hashes unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_artifact"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_archive_manifest","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_archive_manifest(artifact; label = nothing, source_path = nothing)\nfit_archive_manifest(fit; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a compact long-term archive manifest for a fit artifact. The manifest records the artifact schema, an embedded SHA-256 content hash, selected fit/diagnostic metadata, and optional archive labels or source paths. Passing a fit object first builds its fit_artifact with the supplied artifact keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_archive_manifest"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.artifact_content_hash","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"artifact_content_hash(artifact)\n\nReturn a stable SHA-256 content hash for an exported fit artifact or report bundle. The hash is computed from the package's cache-stable representation after recursively removing content_hash and archive_manifest metadata fields, so the value can be stored with an export and recomputed later for verification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.artifact_content_hash"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.cached_fit","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"cached_fit(spec_or_design; cache_path, refresh = false, return_record = false,\n           kwargs...)\n\nFit with an RDS-like serialized cache. Automatic cache keys require seed = <integer> so cache reuse is tied to replayable posterior draws. If cache_path exists and its cache key matches the current data/spec/design, prior, sampler controls, seed, Julia version, and initialization hash, the cached fit is returned without recomputation. If the file is absent, or refresh = true, the model is fit and the result is saved. A key mismatch raises an error rather than silently reusing stale draws. Without refresh = true, a cache created while fitting is also preserved and saving raises an error. An exception or interruption during fitting propagates without publishing a partial fit. A failed refresh = true fit leaves the previous cache intact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.cached_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_cache_key","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_cache_key(spec_or_design; kwargs...)\n\nReturn the deterministic cache key used by cached_fit. The key hashes the data/spec/design manifest, prior scales, backend-specific sampler controls, RNG seed metadata, Julia version, initial-parameter hash, and versioned MCMC diagnostic contract. The progress keyword is accepted for API symmetry with fit but is not included in the key because it does not affect posterior draws. Cache keys require seed = <integer> so automatic cache reuse is tied to a replayable fit request. Anchored designs use the canonical semantic design identity, so reordering otherwise identical anchor declarations does not change the key. Numeric sampler controls use the same bounds as fit and are checked before cache lookup. For independent experimental MGMFRM, sampling_coordinates also distinguishes the optional :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset computation. Omitting it or passing :raw retains the existing default key and model/prior identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_cache_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_cache","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_cache(path, fit;\n               cache_key = nothing, overwrite = false,\n               artifact = nothing, ...)\n\nSerialize a fitted object and a reproducibility artifact to path using Julia's standard Serialization format. This is intended as an RDS-like cache for avoiding recomputation in the same Julia analysis environment. The cache contains the full fitted object; artifact draw duplication is omitted by default unless requested with the artifact_include_* keywords. Fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM results use the v2 cache format and require an artifact that agrees with their canonical sample. Existing MFRM, GMFRM and MGMFRM caches retain v1. Explicit correlated MGMFRM results use a separate correlated_mgmfrm_fit_cache.v1 schema with mandatory sample/artifact agreement.\n\nThe record is written to a temporary file in the destination directory before publication. A failed write leaves any existing cache intact. With overwrite = false, a destination created during serialization is also preserved. With overwrite = true, the completed file replaces the destination path (including a symbolic link itself).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_cache","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"load_fit_cache(path; expected_cache_key = nothing, verify_hash = true,\n               return_record = false)\n\nLoad a serialized fit cache. By default the cached fit object is returned. Set return_record = true to inspect the cache metadata and artifact. When expected_cache_key is supplied, loading fails unless the stored key matches. By default loading also verifies the stored artifact content hash and archive manifest hashes, while cache/hash metadata shape is always checked. Set verify_hash = false to inspect an older or exploratory cache record with an artifact payload hash mismatch. For fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM v2 caches, sample integrity, artifact agreement and archive hashes are always verified, including when verify_hash = false is supplied. Loading does not run a sampler or regenerate the saved environment metadata. Explicit correlated MGMFRM caches likewise always verify their sample, artifact and archive integrity, even with verify_hash = false. Recorded-fit readers allow only roundoff-sized differences in recomputed summary means/SDs. Saved payload hashes, other summary fields and metadata remain exact; loading never rewrites the stored summaries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report(fit; view = :full, kwargs...)\n\nBuild a compact, machine-readable report bundle for a fitted MFRM, guarded GMFRM, or guarded MGMFRM object. The report combines fit metadata, provenance, diagnostics, fixed-coordinate identification rows, prior, pooling, and MGMFRM local MCMC-budget guidance rows, posterior summaries, posterior predictive summaries, category-functioning and rater-homogeneity rows, calibration rows, WAIC/LOO summaries and diagnostics, optional DFF rows, and a compact archive manifest. Stable MFRM category/rater practitioner sections are included by default and can be disabled independently. Stable MFRM fixed-coordinate rows state explicitly that reference and hard-anchor values are constants rather than posterior estimates; hard anchors also produce concise fixed-coordinate and coordinate-dependent-prior warnings, plus a within-facet contrast warning when two or more anchors occur in the same facet. Section-level failures are captured by default with status = :error and make the top-level report_status = :incomplete. Use on_section_error = :throw to make the first failing section raise, or require_complete = true to evaluate all captured sections and then reject an incomplete report. InterruptException, OutOfMemoryError and StackOverflowError always propagate, including under the default capture policy.\n\nSet include_prior_predictive = true for MFRM fits to include prior predictive summary rows. Use include_full_artifact = true to embed the full compact fit_artifact; otherwise only the artifact schema, content hash, and archive manifest are included in the report. The default view = :full preserves the complete bayesianmgmfrm.fit_report.v1 compatibility payload. Set view = :public to return the reader-facing structured projection produced by fit_report_public.\n\nThe warmup section includes chain-level adaptation coverage and event counts from sampler_diagnostics(fit; phase = :warmup). It is included in structured, public, Markdown, table and bundle outputs. Unrecorded history has missing counts; known zero-warmup runs are labelled :not_run. Older Turing fits without an explicit adaptation count remain :not_recorded even with zero requested warmup. These events do not change retained-draw warnings, R-hat/ESS or posterior summaries. A computed section means reporting succeeded, not that adaptation passed a quality rule. Older saved reports without this section remain readable without adding rows. DFF sections default to the count-weighted additive projection. Set dff_adjustment=:observed_margins only to reproduce the historical screen; see dff_report for assignment support and uncertainty limits.\n\nreporting is an optional named tuple for the common category, agreement, named reliability-cohort, classification, ranking, observed-score alpha and ICC sections. It preserves explicit identities/targets, all retained draws for posterior quantities, warnings and section failures in public/Markdown/JSON/bundle output. Omitting it keeps previous report behavior. See the Saved-fit Reporting API guide for its request fields and uncertainty definitions. Completeness is execution status, not scientific or numerical acceptance. The alpha and ICC sections use observed ratings, not posterior draws, and supply no SE or interval.\n\n\n\n\n\nfit_report(fit::MultidimensionalMFRMFit; view = :full,\n    posterior_lower = 0.025, posterior_upper = 0.975, seed = 1, ...)\n\nReport a saved fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM result, including its rating-design audit and reproducibility artifact summary. Use include_full_artifact = true to embed the full artifact, or view = :public for reader-facing output. This experimental result uses unit logits, fixed Q coefficients and independent abilities unless Experimental.correlated(spec) was selected. Correlated results default to view = :public and include rho intervals and diagnostics. Estimate it with BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit. Unsupported analyses are labelled in the report.\n\nPosterior bounds must be central and strictly inside (0, 1). Prediction uses the existing rating rows and a local seed; posterior summaries and diagnostics always use all retained draws. Diagnostic settings must match the saved fit. The prior section explains free-coordinate SDs, the last-rater/step induced variances, rater-label dependence and prior-anchored ability/item locations. For between-item Q, diagnostics.location_rows also reports finite-panel ability and item means and their difference, with a separate summary and warnings.\n\nSet include_prior_predictive = true to simulate from the saved model/prior, with prior_predictive_ndraws = 100 and prior_interval = 0.95 by default. This adds parameter and rating summaries, correlation intervals when applicable, and prior-implication diagnostics. Prior and posterior predictions use separate local RNGs initialized from seed; neither updates nor refits the saved result. prior_interval controls parameter prior intervals; predictive_interval controls rating prediction intervals for both prior and posterior checks.\n\nSet include_waic = true or include_loo = true with a nonempty dataset_id and unique persistent observation_ids in the fit's original rating-row order. Both flags default to false; unrequested criteria are :not_requested. Criteria use all original retained draws, independently of prediction draw selection, and preserve sampling warnings and flagged rating IDs in saved reports. LOO uses raw importance sampling, not reference-validated PSIS; its controls are loo_threshold = 0.7, loo_tail_fraction = 0.2, loo_min_tail_draws = 5. The target omits one rating at existing levels with specification/level maps fixed. Native pointwise score SE is not MCMC MCSE or a crossed-cluster correction. Use posterior_mcse(fit) for parameter MCSE. Computed scores and complete reports do not establish scientific acceptance. No refitting or new-level integration is performed. The same options apply to correlated and exchangeable-rater fits. Legacy fixed-Q v1 records remain unsupported by this public report; use the common prediction/criteria API for their saved-row scores.\n\nreporting is an optional named tuple for the common category, agreement and named reliability-cohort sections. It preserves explicit identities/targets, all retained draws, warnings and section failures in public/Markdown/JSON/bundle output. Omitting it keeps previous report behavior. See the Saved-fit Reporting API guide for its request fields and uncertainty definitions. Completeness is execution status, not scientific or numerical acceptance.\n\n\n\n\n\nfit_report(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; view = :public,\n    posterior_lower = 0.025, posterior_upper = 0.975,\n    predictive_interval = 0.9, include_prior_predictive = false,\n    prior_predictive_ndraws = 100, prior_interval = 0.95, seed = 1, ...)\n\nExplain the saved correlated MGMFRM, its prior, MCMC warnings, model-coordinate summaries and MCSE, and conditional existing-row predictions. Optional prior checks reconstruct the saved prior; they never substitute default scales. Posterior bounds must be central. Summaries/diagnostics use all retained draws; only posterior prediction uses ndraws or ordered draw_indices.\n\nReports default to reader-facing output. view = :full retains reproduction details. on_section_error = :capture exposes failed optional sections; require_complete = true rejects them. Report completeness does not certify MCMC quality, identification or scientific acceptance. Save portable Markdown, JSON and tables with save_fit_report_bundle; this report is not a fit cache.\n\nNormalized and correlated MGMFRM reports accept include_waic=false and include_loo=false. To request either, provide dataset_id and unique persistent observation_ids in the fit's original row order. Both use ALL retained draws, independently of any posterior-prediction ndraws or draw_indices selection. LOO is raw importance sampling, with loo_threshold=.7, loo_tail_fraction=.2 and loo_min_tail_draws=5; it is not reference-validated PSIS. Sections retain sampling/criterion warnings and affected IDs in public/Markdown/table/bundle outputs. Native score SE is neither MCMC MCSE nor a crossed-cluster correction. Unrequested sections return :not_requested; other unsupported analyses remain unsupported. No new-level integration or refitting is performed.\n\nreporting is an optional named tuple for the common category, agreement and named reliability-cohort sections. It preserves explicit identities/targets, all retained draws, warnings and section failures in public/Markdown/JSON/bundle output. Omitting it keeps previous report behavior. See the Saved-fit Reporting API guide for its request fields and uncertainty definitions. Completeness is execution status, not scientific or numerical acceptance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_health","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_health(report)\n\nDerive the completeness of a full or public fit report from its section statuses. A report is :incomplete when at least one section has status = :error; :not_requested, :unsupported, and status-bearing diagnostic sections do not by themselves make it incomplete. New reports store the same result in report_status and report_health; recorded values are validated against the sections. Legacy v1 reports without these fields remain readable because their health is derived on demand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_health"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_public","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_public(report)\nfit_report_public(fit; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a reader-facing structured projection of a full fit_report payload. The result uses schema bayesianmgmfrm.fit_report_public.v1, preserves model results and diagnostic rows, normalizes support labels, and recursively omits implementation-only fields and machine-specific paths. The complete bayesianmgmfrm.fit_report.v1 payload remains unchanged for compatibility and reproducibility workflows. User-supplied label values remain unchanged, nonfinite numeric values use portable string representations, and the public content hash is normalized to the JSON representation so it remains stable after save/load.\n\nPassing a fit object first builds fit_report(fit; view = :full, kwargs...). The projection is idempotent and may also be applied to a JSON-loaded public report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_public"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_markdown","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_markdown(report; title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report\",\n    max_rows = 6, include_empty = false)\n\nRender a portable Markdown review draft from a fit_report payload. The output includes report metadata, a prominent warning summary when a section supplies warning rows, section status/row counts, and table previews for each tabular row field. report may be the in-memory NamedTuple returned by fit_report or a JSON-loaded payload from load_fit_report. Table columns use the same display order in both forms: identifiers, sampler coverage, estimates and uncertainty first, followed by other columns by name. Empty row fields remain visible in the section summary but their table previews are omitted by default; set include_empty = true to render an explicit zero-row preview. JSON, table, and bundle exports retain empty row fields regardless of this Markdown presentation choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_markdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_dossier","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_dossier(reports::Pair...; comparison_rows = (),\n    sensitivity_rows = (), evidence_rows = (), include_reports = false,\n    require_complete = false, label = nothing)\nfit_report_dossier(report1, report2, ...; names = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nBuild a reader-facing review dossier from one or more fit_report payloads. Inputs are normalized through fit_report_public, including reports embedded with include_reports = true. Supplied comparison, sensitivity, and evidence rows are projected through the same field-aware language boundary, and the portable dossier retains only reader-facing fields. Each dossier records aggregate report health. Set require_complete = true to reject any input report containing a captured section error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_dossier"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_dossier_markdown","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_dossier_markdown(dossier;\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report dossier\", max_rows = 6,\n    include_empty = false)\n\nRender a portable Markdown review dossier from a fit_report_dossier payload. The output includes dossier metadata, per-report summaries, per-section summaries, and previews of supplied comparison, sensitivity, and evidence rows. Tables use the same column display order as fit_report_markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_dossier_markdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_reproduction_manifest","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_reproduction_manifest(fit; view = :full, cache_record = nothing, cache_path = nothing,\n    report_bundle_manifest = nothing, report_bundle_path = nothing,\n    artifact = nothing, verify_cache_hash = true, kwargs...)\n\nBuild a machine-readable reproduction manifest for a fitted object. The manifest treats full rerun evidence and fast cached-draw evidence as separate required paths: full rerun is ready only when the fit artifact records a replayable RNG seed and sampler controls, while fast cached draws are ready only when a hash-verified fit-cache record is supplied. Optional fit-report bundle metadata can be attached for review exports. The compatibility default view = :full retains complete compatibility metadata. Use view = :public for a reader-facing projection without machine-local paths or maintainer workflow fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_reproduction_manifest"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_section","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_section(report, section)\n\nReturn one section from a fit_report payload. section may be a Symbol or string, and report may be either the in-memory report NamedTuple or a JSON-loaded Dict{String,Any}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_section"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_sections","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_sections(report)\n\nReturn a compact summary of the tabular/status-bearing sections in a fit_report payload. report may be the original NamedTuple returned by fit_report or the Dict{String,Any} payload returned by load_fit_report.\n\nEach returned row includes section, status, row_fields, and n_rows. JSON-loaded status strings are normalized to symbols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_sections"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_rows","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"fit_report_rows(report, section; row_field = :auto)\n\nReturn rows from one fit_report section. By default, :auto selects :rows when present and otherwise selects :diagnostic_rows; pass row_field to request a specific row field such as :parameter_rows from the diagnostics section.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_report_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"load_fit_report(path; verify_hash = true, return_record = false,\n    require_complete = false)\n\nLoad a JSON fit-report export written by save_fit_report. The JSON payload hash is verified by default, and the export/hash metadata shape is always checked. The report payload is returned as Dict{String,Any} / Vector{Any} data; set return_record = true to inspect the export metadata and hash records as well.\n\nSet require_complete = true to verify report completeness after the payload and hashes have been checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_dossier","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"load_fit_report_dossier(path; verify_hash = true, return_record = false,\n    require_complete = false)\n\nLoad a JSON fit-report dossier export written by save_fit_report_dossier. Hash metadata is checked, the JSON payload is verified by default, and the loaded dossier payload is projected to the current reader-facing shape before it is returned as ordinary Dict{String,Any} / Vector{Any} data. This projection also accepts v0.1.0 dossier exports, removes their stored source path and implementation-only fields, and converts embedded full reports to the public report schema. Set return_record = true to return a current export record containing that projected dossier; both content hashes then describe the projected payload.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_dossier"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_bundle","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"load_fit_report_bundle(directory; verify_hash = true,\n    return_manifest = false, require_complete = false)\n\nLoad a fit-report bundle written by save_fit_report_bundle. By default this verifies the bundle manifest hash, JSON report export hash, table manifest and table-file hashes, and Markdown content hash before returning the loaded fit_report payload. Set return_manifest = true to inspect the bundle manifest instead. Set require_complete = true to reject a report containing captured section errors. For v2 figure bundles, verification also checks every declared PDF, SVG and numerical-input JSON file. CairoMakie and a saved fit are not needed to read or verify a bundle. The returned value remains the report (or manifest), not a fitted object or reconstructed chain. verify_hash = false skips byte checks; version, figure inventory and safe-path checks still apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_tables","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"load_fit_report_tables(directory; verify_hash = true,\n    return_manifest = false)\n\nLoad a fit-report table export written by save_fit_report_tables. By default this verifies the table manifest hash and each table file hash, then returns a vector of JSON-loaded table records. Set return_manifest = true to inspect the export manifest instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_fit_report_tables"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report(path, report; overwrite = false, label = nothing,\n    require_complete = false)\nsave_fit_report(path, fit; overwrite = false, label = nothing,\n    require_complete = false, kwargs...)\n\nWrite a fit_report bundle to a JSON export record at path. Passing a fit object first builds fit_report(fit; kwargs...). The saved record includes the original report content hash and a JSON-payload hash that load_fit_report verifies by default. Set require_complete = true to reject a report containing any captured section error before writing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_dossier","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report_dossier(path, dossier; overwrite = false, label = nothing,\n    require_complete = false)\n\nWrite a fit_report_dossier to a JSON export record. The export stores both the dossier content hash and a JSON-payload hash so load_fit_report_dossier can verify the saved payload. Set require_complete = true to reject a dossier containing any report-section error before writing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_dossier"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_dossier_markdown","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report_dossier_markdown(path, dossier; overwrite = false,\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report dossier\", max_rows = 6,\n    include_empty = false, label = nothing, require_complete = false)\n\nWrite a Markdown review draft for a fit_report_dossier payload and return an export record with dossier and Markdown content hashes. Set require_complete = true to reject an incomplete dossier before writing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_dossier_markdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_bundle","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report_bundle(directory, report; overwrite = false,\n    label = nothing, title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report\",\n    max_rows = 6, include_empty = false, require_complete = false)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(directory, fit; overwrite = false,\n    label = nothing, title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report\",\n    max_rows = 6, include_empty = false, require_complete = false,\n    figures = nothing, seed = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nWrite a portable fit-report bundle directory containing a JSON report export, JSON table files, a Markdown review draft, and a bundle manifest.json with the nested content hashes. report may be the in-memory fit_report payload or a JSON-loaded payload from load_fit_report. Passing a fit object first builds fit_report(fit; kwargs...). Set require_complete = true to reject captured section errors before creating the bundle directory.\n\nWith CairoMakie loaded, pass a fit and select optional figures, for example figures = (posterior = (block = :rater,), predictive = (;)). Supported keys are posterior, diagnostics, predictive, and stable-MFRM-only wright and pathway. Each value is a NamedTuple of that plot's selection/size options. The bundle adds PDF/SVG figures, their captions in Markdown, and JSON numerical inputs. No fitting occurs. Summary-only reports cannot supply figures: reload the saved fit with load_fit_cache.\n\nSet shared posterior_lower/posterior_upper central bounds (default 0.025/0.975), predictive_interval (0.9), and ndraws or draw_indices on this call, not inside plot options. Posterior, Wright and both axes of Pathway figures use those posterior bounds and all retained draws; predictive figures consume the report's exact replicated category-summary rows. seed defaults to 1 for figure bundles and controls local posterior/prior predictive RNGs; custom rng/prior_predictive_rng keywords are rejected there. Other report-only calls retain their RNG behavior. Figure bundles keep the plotting diagnostic settings: stable defaults or the stored generalized settings. Conflicting report diagnostic overrides are rejected.\n\nSaved fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fits support posterior, diagnostics and predictive figures with their stored diagnostic settings. They additionally accept prior and prior_predictive figure keys when include_prior_predictive = true. Set prior_predictive_ndraws (default 100) and prior_interval (default 0.95) on the bundle call. Prior figures use the report's exact prior summaries; no second simulation occurs during rendering. Their plots use model coordinates in unit logits; seed defaults to 1 for both figure and report-only bundles. These saved-result outputs remain experimental; estimate with BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit.\n\nAll optional figures render before destination exports are written. A figure bundle uses manifest schema v2, with hashes for every PDF, SVG and JSON input; report and table schemas are unchanged. Figure-free bundles retain v1. Current readers accept both; older readers reject v2 rather than silently skipping its figure checks. As with ordinary bundles, overwrite = true replaces export files, without deleting unrelated files. Only manifest-listed files are verified.\n\n\n\n\n\nsave_fit_report_bundle(directory, fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit;\n    view = :public, figures = nothing, seed = 1, kwargs...)\n\nSave Markdown, JSON and tables; optional figures adds editable PDF/SVG and their numerical inputs. Available kinds are posterior, diagnostics, predictive, prior and prior_predictive. Prior figures require include_prior_predictive = true. Set intervals, draw selection and seed on this call so report and figures agree; figure-specific overrides are rejected. All figures are prepared before replacing a bundle. Requires CairoMakie only when figures are requested. No posterior sampling occurs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_markdown","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report_markdown(path, report; overwrite = false,\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report\", max_rows = 6,\n    include_empty = false, label = nothing, require_complete = false)\nsave_fit_report_markdown(path, fit; overwrite = false,\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM fit report\", max_rows = 6,\n    include_empty = false, label = nothing, require_complete = false,\n    kwargs...)\n\nWrite a Markdown review draft for a fit_report payload and return an export record with report and Markdown content hashes. Passing a fit object first builds fit_report(fit; kwargs...). Set require_complete = true to reject captured section errors before writing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_markdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_tables","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"save_fit_report_tables(directory, report; overwrite = false, label = nothing,\n    require_complete = false)\nsave_fit_report_tables(directory, fit; overwrite = false, label = nothing,\n    require_complete = false, kwargs...)\n\nWrite every tabular row field from a fit_report payload into a directory of portable JSON table files plus manifest.json. The returned manifest records the exported table filenames, row counts, and table content hashes. report may be an in-memory fit_report NamedTuple or a JSON-loaded report payload from load_fit_report. Passing a fit object first builds fit_report(fit; kwargs...). Set require_complete = true to reject captured section errors before creating the export directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_fit_report_tables"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.related_software_capability_matrix","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"related_software_capability_matrix(; view = :full)\n\nReturn the related-software positioning matrix. The matrix compares Facets, TAM, mirt, sirt, immer, brms/Stan-style workflows, and BayesianMGMFRM.jl across model coverage, estimation style, rater/facet support, multidimensional support, Bayesian workflow support, diagnostics and sensitivity coverage, and report-artifact support.\n\nThis is a scope-governance artifact, not validation evidence and not a superiority claim. Overlap comparisons against R packages or Facets remain a post-v0.2.0 known-truth simulation task where model targets genuinely overlap. The compatibility default view = :full retains the original governance record. Use view = :public for a reader-facing capability matrix that keeps the substantive comparison axes and external sources without local workflow fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.related_software_capability_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit; scale = :model, interval = 0.95,\n    block = nothing, dimension = nothing, parameters = nothing,\n    max_parameters = 60, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nDraw posterior medians and central credible intervals from an existing MFRM, experimental GMFRM, or experimental fixed-Q MGMFRM fit. Load CairoMakie first. The returned CairoMakie Figure is editable and can be displayed or saved with save(\"posterior.pdf\", figure) or save(\"posterior.svg\", figure). No fitting, random draw selection, or report construction is performed.\n\n:model uses model coordinates, including derived constraints and fixed identification values. Diamonds marked \"fixed\" have no credible interval. :raw uses the existing posterior_summary coordinates; for MFRM these are already identified model coordinates, with fixed/derived coordinates omitted. The numerical summary functions retain their existing defaults.\n\nSelect a model block (such as :person, :rater, or :item), an MGMFRM dimension index or label, or exact parameters names in the desired order. Blocks and dimensions have separate axes. More than max_parameters selected coordinates raises an error rather than silently dropping rows; select a subset or explicitly increase the limit. size = (width, height) overrides automatic figure sizing. The diagnostic note always covers the whole fit.\n\nThis optional entry point is qualified, not exported. Generalized figures retain the experimental status of their fitted models.\n\nSaved fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fits also support this entry point. They use only scale = :model in unit logits, including fixed Q coefficients and reconstructed constraints; dimensions accept stored labels. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; kwargs...)\n\nPlot named dimensions, estimated positive loadings/consistencies or population rho from a saved result. Use block = :latent_correlation for rho or block = :person, dimension = \"label\" for abilities. Supports scale = :model (default) or :raw; raw correlation is Fisher z. Intervals use all retained draws and the note retains whole-fit warnings. Requires using CairoMakie. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior(check; interval = 0.95, block = nothing,\n    dimension = nothing, parameters = nothing, max_parameters = 60, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nPlot parameter medians and central prior intervals from a fixed-coefficient MFRM Experimental.prior_predictive_check result. Load CairoMakie first; returns an editable Figure for display or PDF/SVG saving. No posterior fitting or additional random simulation occurs. Select named dimensions or parameter blocks as in plot_posterior. Correlated models support block = :latent_correlation. Locations and reconstructed steps use unit logits; rho uses its correlation scale. Diamonds are fixed coefficients/baseline steps, not estimated precision. check.parameter_summary contains 95% numerical intervals for all coordinates. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(fit; scale = :raw, block = nothing,\n    dimension = nothing, parameters = nothing, max_parameters = 12,\n    bins = 20, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nDraw retained-chain traces and pooled-rank histograms directly from an MFRM, experimental GMFRM, or experimental fixed-Q MGMFRM fit. Load CairoMakie first; returns its editable Figure, supporting display and PDF/SVG save. No sampling, thinning, warmup-draw reconstruction or random tie-breaking occurs.\n\nThe default :raw shows the stored computational coordinates; :model uses identified/transformed coordinates, including fixed and derived constraints. Selection uses the same block, dimension and exact parameter names as BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior, with a smaller default limit of 12. All chains and retained iterations are shown, using their stored identities.\n\nRanks are pooled across chains separately for each coordinate, with average ranks for ties. Each chain histogram is normalized to sum to one; the gray reference is the pooled histogram, including its tie pattern. bins is a positive integer, capped at the total draw count. Rank comparison is unavailable for a single chain, fixed values, or constant sampled values. Matching rank histograms alone do not establish convergence.\n\nParameter labels show existing rank-normalized R-hat and bulk/tail ESS where available. Derived coordinates without stored diagnostic rows are explicitly unavailable. The note retains whole-fit diagnostics, including unselected parameters, and per-chain sampler counts/coverage. Generalized diagnostic settings are those stored with the fit; older incompatible records retain the existing diagnostic-contract rejection. Warmup parameter draws are not stored; inspect sampler_diagnostics(fit; phase = :warmup) for separate warmup summaries. This optional entry point is qualified, not exported.\n\nFor saved fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fits, the default and only scale is :model (unit logits). Stored diagnostic thresholds are preserved; warmup summaries are available in fit_report(fit).warmup. With between-item Q, view = :location shows each dimension's finite-panel ability mean, item mean and their difference. Select a dimension by index or label, or block = :person_mean, :item_mean, or :person_minus_item_mean. The default view = :parameters retains the individual coordinates. Both views show the separate location-check status alongside the whole-fit assessment. These equally weighted panel means are not population-mean parameters; within-item and mixed Q do not support the location view. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; kwargs...)\n\nTrace/rank plots in model coordinates (default) or scale = :raw. Select a block, named dimension or exact parameters as in plot_posterior. Derived steps without stored convergence diagnostics are labelled unavailable; whole-fit warnings remain visible. Only view = :parameters is supported. Requires CairoMakie. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(fit; interval = 0.9, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, seed = 1, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nCompare observed category proportions with posterior predictive replicated proportions for an MFRM, experimental GMFRM, or experimental fixed-Q MGMFRM fit. Load CairoMakie first. Returns its editable Figure, supporting display and PDF/SVG save. No MCMC or refitting is performed.\n\nThe check replicates the existing rating rows, conditional on their fitted persons, items and raters. Every declared score category remains visible, including categories with zero observed ratings. Diamonds show observed proportions, circles show replicated means, and bars show pointwise central predictive intervals. These are intervals for replicated category proportions, not parameter intervals, simultaneous bands, or Monte Carlo error bars.\n\nBy default, generate one replicated dataset for every retained draw in stored order. ndraws instead samples that many draw indices uniformly with replacement; draw_indices supplies exact ordered indices (repeats allowed). Pass only one of these selection options. The number of replications, distinct retained draws used and selection policy appear in the figure. interval changes the summary, not the replicated data.\n\nEach call creates a local MersenneTwister from the integer seed (default 1), without advancing the global RNG. Reuse the same fit, seed and selection options in the same Julia/package environment to regenerate the same result after load_fit_cache. Numerical results are available through posterior_predictive_check(fit; rng = MersenneTwister(seed), ...) followed by predictive_check_summary(...; interval).\n\nSaved fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fits also support this plot. For their numerical output, use fit_report(fit; seed, ndraws, draw_indices, predictive_interval = interval).posterior_predictive, with the same selection options and seed.\n\nThe whole-fit MCMC diagnostic note remains visible. This same-data check does not establish convergence or performance for new persons, items or raters. This optional entry point is qualified, not exported. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(check::NamedTuple; interval = 0.9, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nPlot observed category proportions alongside prior-predictive means and central intervals from a prior_predictive_check result, including experimental models. Load CairoMakie first; returns an editable Figure for display or PDF/SVG saving. Uses all the already generated replications without further random draws. The numerical values are predictive_check_summary(check; interval) rows with statistic = :category_proportion. Ratings are a comparison, not an update to the prior. Prior plausibility does not establish posterior convergence, recovery or performance for new persons, items or raters. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit;\n    interval = 0.9, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, seed = 1, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nPlot conditional existing-row category proportions with pointwise predictive intervals and whole-fit warnings. A local seed reproduces draw selection and replications after reload; it leaves the global RNG unchanged. Requires CairoMakie. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit; facets = :all, include_thresholds = true,\n    interval = 0.95, person_display = :intervals, bins = nothing,\n    max_levels = 60, size = nothing, annotation_io = stdout)\n\nDraw a Wright map from a stable MFRM rating-scale or partial-credit fit. Load CairoMakie first; returns its editable Figure for display and PDF/SVG save. Experimental GMFRM/MGMFRM fits are unsupported. All retained draws are used without refitting, random selection, or recentering.\n\nFacet panels share a vertical logit scale. Higher person ability increases adjacent-category log odds; higher rater severity or item difficulty decreases them. Points show medians and bars show central credible intervals. Diamonds identify fixed references or hard anchors; their external uncertainty is excluded.\n\nSet person_display = :histogram for horizontal person-frequency bars on the same vertical scale, retaining item/rater intervals. Each person contributes one posterior median; these shrunken estimates are not a population density, pooled posterior draws, or individual uncertainty intervals. bins accepts a positive bin count or strictly increasing finite edges covering every median. By default use min(20, ceil(Int, sqrt(n_persons))) equal-width bins over the median range; equal medians use a range of median ± 0.5 logits. Bins are left closed/right open, except the last includes its upper edge. Exact edges and counts accompany the external note and report figure JSON; all person rows remain in that JSON. Histogram mode requires the person facet; explicit bins require histogram mode. With unequal widths, compare bar lengths (counts), not areas or density. The default :intervals retains the individual view.\n\nThe optional boundary panel shows item difficulty plus category step, summed draw by draw with their joint uncertainty. These are adjacent-category equal-probability boundaries at zero rater severity, not expected-score half-points or 50% marginal category probabilities. A fixed step does not make the boundary fixed when the item is estimated. Whole-fit MCMC warnings remain visible; the map is not a convergence or model-fit check.\n\nfacets accepts :all, a single :person, :rater or :item, or an ordered tuple of these facets. include_thresholds = true adds boundaries for all items, independently of facet selection. Original level/category labels and order are preserved. More than max_levels individual positions raises an error: narrow facets, omit boundaries, or increase the limit and Figure size explicitly. People aggregated in a histogram do not count toward this individual-mark limit. No people or other selected positions are silently omitted. Reuse the same options after load_fit_cache to regenerate the map. wright_map_data returns its numerical inputs. This optional entry point is qualified, not exported. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_pathway","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_pathway(fit; facets = :all, metric = :infit,\n    interval = 0.95, min_n = 1, max_levels = 60, size = nothing,\n    annotation_io = stdout)\n\nDraw a Pathway map for a stable unidimensional MFRM rating-scale or partial-credit fit. Load CairoMakie first; returns an editable Figure for display or PDF/SVG saving. This qualified entry point is not exported. Other model families and multidimensional fits are unsupported until their fit estimands are defined.\n\nChoose metric = :infit or :outfit mean square. Facet panels share horizontal fit and vertical logit axes. facets accepts :all, one of :person, :rater, :item, or an ordered tuple. Positions retain their identified signs without recentering: higher ability increases, and higher severity/difficulty decreases, adjacent-category log odds. Points are marginal medians; horizontal and vertical bars are separate central credible intervals, not a joint credible region. Set interval = 0.99 for 99% intervals on both axes. All retained draws are used; no refitting, random selection or extra draws occur. diagnostic_map_data with matching facets, metrics, interval and min_n returns the numerical rows.\n\nDiamonds mark fixed reference/anchor locations; their fit statistic can still be uncertain and retain a horizontal interval. External anchor uncertainty is excluded. Labels include the number of contributing ratings; a trailing * marks a numerical flag detailed in the external note. Tiny predictive variances are clipped in the existing fit statistic; inspect flagged rows before use. Missing/nonfinite summaries (including levels below min_n) raise an error, never silently omit levels. More than max_levels selected levels raises an error; narrow facets or explicitly increase the limit and figure size.\n\nThe line at 1 is a mean-square reference, not an acceptance threshold. This is a same-data residual diagnostic, not ZSTD, a convergence check or model-selection evidence. Whole-fit MCMC warnings remain in the note, even for selected facets. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO to save the text, or nothing to embed it. Keep the note with exported images. Reuse the options after load_fit_cache to reproduce the figure, or use save_fit_report_bundle(...; figures = (pathway = (metric = :outfit,),)) for PDF/SVG, numerical JSON and external notes. Report bundles use their central posterior_lower/posterior_upper for both axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_pathway"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface(fit::MGMFRMFit; item, rater,\n    dimensions = (1, 2), x = range(-3, 3; length = 41),\n    y = range(-3, 3; length = 41), fixed_abilities = Dict(),\n    category = :all, interval = 0.9, draw_indices = nothing)\n\nSummarize a conditional item response surface from an existing independent fixed-Q MGMFRM fit, without fitting or requiring a renderer. Select item and rater by their stored labels. The two ability dimensions accept indices or stored labels; specify every remaining dimension in fixed_abilities. Axes are abilities on the fitted model's scale, not estimated person scores.\n\nBy default, category = :all returns response probabilities for all stored category values. With category = nothing, the surface is the expected rating using the actual numeric category levels. A stored category value instead selects its response probability. Each grid point is evaluated for every selected joint posterior draw before taking its mean and central credible interval. These intervals describe uncertainty in a probability or conditional expectation, not in an individual future rating, and are pointwise rather than simultaneous bands. No averaging over raters, ability populations, or unplotted dimensions occurs.\n\nThe result contains x, y, mean, lower, upper, interval_width, labels, selection metadata and the whole-fit diagnostic note. Arrays use [x,y,k] indexing for :all, with k indexing category_levels, and [x,y] otherwise. All retained draws are used unless unique draw_indices are supplied; the indices are retained in the result. No random subsampling occurs. The default ability range is a display choice, not evidence of data support there. This qualified, unexported entry point retains MGMFRM's experimental status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(fit::MGMFRMFit; item, rater,\n    size = nothing, azimuth = 1.275pi, elevation = pi/7, annotation_io = stdout, kwargs...)\n\nDisplay editable 3D posterior-mean category probability wireframes, with a fixed color and legend entry for each stored rating value. Axes use numbered abilities (θ₁, θ₂, …), independently of stored dimension names. Companion heatmaps show each category's pointwise central credible interval width on a shared scale. Select a numeric category for one filled probability surface, or category = nothing for an expected-rating surface. Load CairoMakie first. All other keywords are forwarded to BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface. The figure and accompanying text retain the whole-fit diagnostic note and the selected item, rater, dimensions, fixed abilities, interval and draw count. azimuth and elevation control the static viewing angle in radians. Save with CairoMakie's save. This does not refit, simulate future ratings, or add a report-bundle section. The default annotation_io = stdout writes the title and complete note outside the image. Pass a writable IO stream to save that text, or nothing for the previous embedded format. The editable Figure return is unchanged; keep the external note with the exported image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface"},{"category":"function","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(fit; lower = 0.025, upper = 0.975,\n    intervals = (0.66, 0.9, 0.95), reference = 0.0,\n    rope = nothing, rope_probability_threshold = 0.95)\n\nSummarize stored model-scale draws from an experimental GMFRMFit or MGMFRMFit. Call this qualified function for transformed parameters such as positive item discrimination and rater consistency. posterior_summary(fit) summarizes their raw computational coordinates instead.\n\nReturn a vector of named-tuple rows with parameter names, means, medians, standard deviations, central credible intervals, probability of direction relative to reference, and optional ROPE probabilities. lower and upper are quantile probabilities; intervals contains central interval masses. Set rope = r for [-r, r] or rope = (lower, upper) for explicit bounds.\n\nRows cover the stored direct coordinates. The fixed first and reconstructed final category-step rows are additionally displayed by BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit; scale = :model). This summary uses the existing fit and preserves its experimental scope and fitted prior.\n\nrows = BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(fit_result)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary"},{"category":"type","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.GMFRMFit","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"GMFRMFit\n\nExperimental scalar GMFRM fit result returned only by BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) (or the legacy fit(spec; experimental = true) spelling) for the one-dimensional rater-consistency experimental configuration with thresholds = :partial_credit, discrimination = :rater, and no anchors or fitted DFF terms. Raw draws are stored in draws, constrained direct draws in direct_draws, and observation-ordered direct pointwise log likelihoods in direct_pointwise_loglikelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.GMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-fitting-artifacts/#BayesianMGMFRM.MGMFRMFit","page":"Fitting and Artifacts","text":"MGMFRMFit\n\nGuarded experimental MGMFRM fit result returned by BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) (or the legacy fit(spec; experimental = true) spelling) for the fixed-Q confirmatory candidate with dimensions >= 1, thresholds = :partial_credit, the generic compatibility selector discrimination = :none, and no anchors or fitted DFF terms. The kernel still contains Q-masked item-dimension discrimination parameters; :none means that the broader generic discrimination selector is not used. Raw draws are stored in draws, constrained direct draws in direct_draws, and observation-ordered direct pointwise log likelihoods in direct_pointwise_loglikelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.MGMFRMFit"},{"category":"section","location":"api-experimental/#Experimental-Model-API","page":"Experimental Models","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThese entries describe the callable experimental model types and functions. Their availability does not establish scientific support beyond the documented model and prior configurations. Read the experimental model guide for equations, identifying constraints, prior scales, fitting examples and limitations. For saved prediction comparison and named recovery, use the separate prediction and recovery candidate reference.","title":"Experimental Model API"},{"category":"module","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental","page":"Experimental Models","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental\n\nNamespace for multidimensional MFRM and generalized models available in limited, experimental configurations but are not part of the stable MFRM fitting contract.\n\nUse BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.preview, BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check, BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit, and BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.cached_fit instead of adding experimental = true to the stable entry points. The legacy keyword remains available for source compatibility for GMFRM/MGMFRM. Fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM uses Experimental.fit and manual save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache, and both prior-predictive entries; preview and automatic cache entries in this namespace still accept only GMFRM/MGMFRM.\n\nThe narrower BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate entry point exposes a density-and-gradient target only. It has no fit result or cache path through that density-only entry. Use correlated(spec) with an explicit GeneralizedPrior for the separate, limited correlated MGMFRM fitting and manual-cache workflow. The density target's companion functions expose the transformed correlation state and finite-difference gradient checks. Inspect BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract for the exact accepted configurations and constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of experimental scalar GMFRM fitting. Inspect estimates with posterior_summary and sampling quality with diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MGMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of experimental fixed-Q MGMFRM fitting. Inspect estimates with posterior_summary and sampling quality with diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MGMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MultidimensionalMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of experimental fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fitting. Use Experimental.fit(spec) to estimate and save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache to save and reopen a result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MultidimensionalMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMSpec","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Two-dimensional MFRM specification with estimated population correlation. Construct with correlated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of experimental correlated MFRM fitting. direct_posterior_summary and plot_posterior(...; block = :latent_correlation) report rho on its correlation scale. Reports default to reader-facing output; diagnostics must be checked before interpreting estimates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMSpec","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Two-dimensional between-item MGMFRM specification with estimated positive loadings, rater consistency and population correlation. Construct with correlated(spec; lkj_eta = 2) and explicitly supply GeneralizedPrior to fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Experimental correlated MGMFRM result. Supports raw/direct summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, metadata, full fit artifacts and manual save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache. Direct summaries and default MCSE report rho; raw summaries report Fisher z. Supports category probabilities and posterior prediction for existing rating rows, reader-facing reports, tables and optional CairoMakie figures. Report bundles preserve model identity, diagnostic warnings and figure inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeablePrior","page":"Experimental Models","text":"ExchangeablePrior(; rater_kernel_sd, person_sd = 1.5, item_sd = 1.0, step_sd = 1.0)\n\nPrior for fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM with exchangeable, zero-sum rater severities. Pass the same prior to prior_predictive_check and fit, with an independent specification or correlated(spec), on either backend. All scales must be finite and positive. The rater scale is required: for R raters, marginal SD is rater_kernel_sd * sqrt((R-1)/R) and pairwise contrast SD is sqrt(2) * rater_kernel_sd. It is not the independent-free-coordinate rater_sd of MFRMPrior; choosing the same number does not match those priors. Ability, item and free-step scales keep their MFRMPrior meanings. Correlated abilities additionally use the LKJ shape specified by correlated. Scales are fixed inputs, not estimated hyperparameters. Unsupported for scalar MFRM, GMFRM and MGMFRM. The default prior remains MFRMPrior().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeablePrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of Experimental.fit(...; prior = ExchangeablePrior(...)) for independent or correlated multidimensional MFRM. Supports summaries, diagnostics, report bundles, plots, and save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache. Reports default to reader-facing output and retain the selected prior and sampler warnings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated","page":"Experimental Models","text":"correlated(spec; lkj_eta = 2)\n\nEstimate population correlation between the two named ability dimensions of a fixed-coefficient MFRM or an estimated-loading MGMFRM. For MFRM, pass the returned specification to Experimental.fit with backend = :advancedhmc (Julia) or :cmdstan and prior = MFRMPrior(...) or ExchangeablePrior. The input specification is copied and retains its independent-model meaning.\n\nRequires family = :mfrm, exactly two dimensions, partial-credit thresholds, between-item Q with at least two pure items per dimension, and observations in both dimensions for every person. Anchors and fitted bias terms are unavailable. Q coefficients and rater consistency are fixed at one; locations use unit logits and zero-centered priors. Direct ability pairs have covariance person_sd^2 * [1 rho; rho 1]. lkj_eta is a fixed positive integer LKJ shape on rho; it is not a standard deviation. Sampling uses rho = tanh(z) with the transformation Jacobian. A custom init ends in Fisher z, not rho.\n\nUse prior_predictive_check before fitting, and save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache to save and reopen MFRM results. Automatic request caching is unavailable.\n\nFor family = :mgmfrm, the same 2D pure-Q and observation-coverage restrictions apply, with estimated positive active loadings and product-one rater consistency. Pass an explicit prior = GeneralizedPrior(...) to Experimental.fit; there is no implicit prior choice for this combined model. Locations/loading scales are prior-anchored and the response equation retains its literal 1.7 multiplier. Both backends return CorrelatedMGMFRMFit, supporting raw/direct summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, existing-row prediction, full fit artifacts and manual save/reload. Pass the same explicit prior to prior_predictive_check before fitting. Reports, exports and optional CairoMakie figures preserve the model, prior, diagnostic warnings and conditional prediction target after reload.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.slope_restriction","page":"Experimental Models","text":"slope_restriction(spec; item_slopes, rater_consistency)\n\nSpecify a matched one-dimensional, item-step MGMFRM with item slopes :fixed_one or :estimated_positive and rater_consistency=:fixed_one. Both keywords are required. The input is copied. Its response equation is log(P(Y=k)/P(Y=k-1)) = 1.7*(a[i]*theta[p]-b[i]-r[j]-t[i,k]). The threshold owner, coefficient placement and literal 1.7 scale do not change. Fixed-one slopes map to unit-logit PCM only after rescaling locations, steps and their prior SDs by 1.7; this is not the existing scalar rater-step GMFRM.\n\nPass the result and an explicit NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(prior_model=:exchangeable, ...) to fit, prior_predict or prior_predictive_check. All six prior inputs remain required; log-consistency SD is inactive, and log-discrimination SD is also inactive for fixed item slopes. Fixed blocks have no sampled coordinates or density factors. These inputs do not act as near-zero-variance priors.\n\nThis first route supports Julia backend=:advancedhmc, raw coordinates and manual save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache. It returns NormalizedMGMFRMFit with the restriction bound to target identity, reduced raw coordinates and fixed coefficients reconstructed in model summaries. Inspect the order with surface_contract(model; prior).raw_parameter_names before supplying init. Summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, existing-level prediction, reports and common K-fold refit planning retain the policy and numerical warnings. CmdStan fitting, other sampling coordinates, source priors, correlations, higher dimensions, anchors and automatic caches are unavailable through this wrapper. Supported operations do not establish recovery, SBC or model-selection performance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.slope_restriction"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Experimental prior-scale type for GMFRM and MGMFRM fits. Its six standard deviations apply to independent normal priors on raw unconstrained coordinates, not directly to transformed model parameters. For an explicit correlated MGMFRM specification, ability pairs are conditionally bivariate normal with these marginal SDs and the specification's LKJ correlation; the other raw-coordinate priors retain their meanings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior","page":"Experimental Models","text":"NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(; prior_model, person_sd, rater_sd, item_sd,\n    log_discrimination_sd, log_consistency_sd, step_sd, source_rater=nothing)\n\nExplicit normalized prior for independent fixed-Q MGMFRM with estimated positive loadings and rater consistency. All six scales and prior_model are required. Use :exchangeable for symmetric centered blocks, or :source with an explicit source_rater ID for the reference prior with shifted log-consistency means. Rater, log-consistency and item-step scales are kernel SDs: a block of length n has marginal SD tau*sqrt((n-1)/n) and pairwise contrast SD sqrt(2)*tau. Other scales are SDs of independent normal raw coordinates. No scientific scale or default is selected by this constructor. This is distinct from the fixed-coefficient MFRM ExchangeablePrior and the raw GeneralizedPrior.\n\nUse with fit, prior_predict and prior_predictive_check. Correlated models and automatic request caching are unavailable; use manual fit caches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Result of Experimental.fit(spec; prior=NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(...)). Also represents slope_restriction fits; inspect fit_metadata for the saved coefficient policy. Supports summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, existing-row prediction, manual fit caches, reports and optional figures. Its saved prior retains distribution, kernel scales and the source rater identity. Scientific acceptance is separate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract","page":"Experimental Models","text":"surface_contract()\nsurface_contract(family)\nsurface_contract(correlated_spec)\n\nReturn the machine-readable stability boundary for the experimental namespace. The zero-argument form describes the executable configurations and constraints for independent fixed-coefficient MFRM and both generalized families. Pass :mfrm, :gmfrm or :mgmfrm for one family contract, or a specification returned by correlated for its correlation model contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_contract","page":"Experimental Models","text":"free_latent_correlation_2d_contract()\n\nReturn the experimental capability contract for the two-dimensional free latent-correlation density. The surface provides diagnostic operations only: it has no fit result or cache integration and does not alter the identity-correlation MGMFRM fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.preview","page":"Experimental Models","text":"preview(spec)\n\nCompile an inspectable design for a representable GMFRM or MGMFRM specification without fitting it. A successful preview does not imply that the configuration is executable; inspect surface_contract or model_equation(spec).experimental_fit_available before fitting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.preview"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predict","page":"Experimental Models","text":"prior_predict(spec; ndraws = 1000,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nGenerate score replications from the declared prior of a supported specification. Fixed-coefficient MFRM uses MFRMPrior() in unit logits; pass correlated(spec) to use its joint ability/LKJ prior. Either MFRM specification also accepts prior = ExchangeablePrior(rater_kernel_sd = ...). GMFRM/MGMFRM use GeneralizedPrior() on raw coordinates. Correlated MGMFRM requires an explicit GeneralizedPrior and uses its joint ability/LKJ prior, including the literal 1.7 response multiplier. Rows are independent replications of the existing rating design; this operation does not fit a posterior. Use a local MersenneTwister for reproducibility without advancing the global RNG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predict"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check","page":"Experimental Models","text":"prior_predictive_check(spec; ndraws = 1000,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), min_category_probability = 0.01,\n    prior_warning_probability = 0.95, wide_facet_range_fraction = 0.8)\n\nGenerate prior parameter draws and replicated scores for a supported model. Fixed-coefficient MFRM uses MFRMPrior(); correlated(spec) also samples its LKJ correlation and conditional bivariate ability prior. The result includes the selected prior; pass prior = ExchangeablePrior(rater_kernel_sd = ...) to give all raters a common marginal severity distribution. It also includes free-coordinate draws, reconstructed model_coordinates, and a parameter_summary with central 95% prior intervals. Fixed Q coefficients, fixed marginal scales and reconstructed sum constraints retain their fitting meaning. Generalized models instead separate raw and constrained direct draws under GeneralizedPrior(). Correlated MGMFRM requires the explicit prior used for fitting; its last raw column is Fisher z and its last direct column is rho. It draws the normalized LKJ prior on rho and conditional bivariate abilities; the other free raw coordinates retain their declared normal priors.\n\nAll results work with predictive_check_summary(check) and BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(check); fixed-coefficient MFRM also supports BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior(check). Plotting requires CairoMakie. Observed ratings serve only as a comparison; they do not update the prior. Simulations reuse the supplied persons, items, raters and rating rows, not new facet levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate","page":"Experimental Models","text":"free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate(spec; lkj_eta = 2)\n\nConstruct a log-density target for an exactly two-dimensional, fixed simple-structure Q MGMFRM. lkj_eta must be a positive integer. This function does not fit the model or integrate with the fit cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_state","page":"Experimental Models","text":"free_latent_correlation_2d_state(candidate, raw_params)\n\nReturn the transformed correlation state for a raw parameter vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_diagnostics","page":"Experimental Models","text":"free_latent_correlation_2d_diagnostics(spec, raw_params; kwargs...)\n\nCompare automatic and central-difference gradients and verify that introducing the correlation coordinate leaves the fixed-Q MGMFRM response likelihood unchanged. The returned reader-facing projection describes the experimental density evaluation, gradient checks, and likelihood-consistency check. The function is diagnostic only and does not run MCMC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"fit(spec; kwargs...)\n\nFit a supported multidimensional MFRM or generalized specification experimentally. Callers should not pass an experimental keyword. Family-specific structural constraints are validated before numerical execution. Pass correlated(spec) to estimate population correlation for two between-item MFRM dimensions; both backends then return CorrelatedMFRMFit when using MFRMPrior. Supported configurations accept backend = :advancedhmc or :cmdstan. For an MGMFRM correlated(spec), an explicit prior = GeneralizedPrior(...) is required. Both backends return CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, existing-row prediction and manual fit caches are available. Reports and optional CairoMakie figures preserve the saved prior and diagnostic warnings; automatic request caching remains unavailable.\n\nFor independent fixed-Q MGMFRM, explicitly pass NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(...) to use normalized exchangeable/source priors and return NormalizedMGMFRMFit. Both backends support manual caches and saved-result reports/figures. This prior requires all six scales and, for :source, a distinguished rater ID. AdvancedHMC also accepts sampling_coordinates = :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset; CmdStan requires :raw. Inspect coordinates with fit_metadata(result).sampler_controls. Automatic request caching is unavailable.\n\nThe independent MGMFRM kernel also accepts dimensions=1 with an explicit all-active, one-column Q. This is an item-step generalized model for comparison with higher-dimensional MGMFRM, not the existing rater-step scalar GMFRM. It retains the 1.7 likelihood constant, coefficient placement and prior rules. Matching the model structure does not establish predictive superiority or calibration. The correlated wrapper still requires two dimensions.\n\nAn unwrapped independent MGMFRM with GeneralizedPrior accepts sampling_coordinates = :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset with AdvancedHMC. This rotates person coordinates and shifts item coordinates while preserving the complete joint prior and likelihood. Initial values and jitter use raw coordinates; returned draws and diagnostics also retain their raw/model meanings. The default is :raw. Automatic request caching distinguishes the optional coordinates; default cache keys are unchanged. The option does not apply to correlated specifications or fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM.\n\nAn unwrapped fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM (family = :mfrm, dimensions >= 2, fixed q_matrix, partial-credit thresholds) has independent abilities. Use MFRMPrior for independent normal priors on free unit-logit coordinates. Q coefficients and rater consistency are fixed; latent correlation is identity and locations are prior-anchored. With MFRMPrior, both backends return MultidimensionalMFRMFit, with warmup sampler statistics recorded by default (record_warmup = true). Save and reload with save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache; automatic request caching is unavailable. Shared sampler controls include ndraws, warmup, chains, seed, init, step_size, target_accept, max_depth, and stored diagnostic thresholds. CmdStan additionally accepts cmdstan_path and cmdstan_cache_dir. For exchangeable zero-sum rater severities, explicitly pass prior = ExchangeablePrior(rater_kernel_sd = ...). Both independent and correlated specifications then return ExchangeableMFRMFit, with the same summary, report, plotting and manual cache operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit_cache_key","page":"Experimental Models","text":"fit_cache_key(spec; backend = :advancedhmc, kwargs...)\n\nReturn the deterministic cache key for an experimental generalized fit request. The namespace records the experimental identity and selects the generalized backend unless the backend is overridden explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit_cache_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api-experimental/#BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.cached_fit","page":"Experimental Models","text":"cached_fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc, kwargs...)\n\nRun or load an experimental generalized fit through the package cache while keeping its experimental identity explicit in the cache contract. The generalized backend is selected by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.cached_fit"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Getting-Started-with-Many-Facet-Measurement","page":"Getting Started","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nSee the Visualization Gallery for Wright Maps, Pathway Maps and MGMFRM response surfaces with worked reading guidance.\n\nWhen several raters score people's work, a low score can have several explanations: the performance was weaker, the task was harder, or the rater was more severe. Many-facet models describe these influences together. They estimate differences and their uncertainty under explicit assumptions about the ratings and their design.\n\nThis guide is for readers who can interpret a table of scores but are new to many-facet models or Bayesian analysis. Start here to choose a question and understand the data. Continue to How Rating Models Work for a worked probability calculation, then Reading Results and Comparing Models for interpretation. Julia is needed for running the examples, but not for reading the explanations.\n\nBefore running an analysis, check the available model and prior routes and what their current evidence permits. An implemented function and a scientifically supported conclusion are separate milestones; the guide identifies both.","title":"Getting Started with Many-Facet Measurement"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Start-with-a-concrete-question","page":"Getting Started","text":"Suppose students give short presentations, several tasks elicit their work, and raters score each response from 0 to 2. Higher categories represent better performance. You might ask:\n\nHow do two students differ after accounting for which tasks and raters they encountered?\nHow much more severe is one rater than another, on the model's rating scale?\nDo reasoning and communication need separate ability estimates?\nDoes allowing different item sensitivities improve predictions of held-out ratings?\n\nThese are different questions. A model that predicts scores well need not estimate every individual parameter precisely. Decide which quantities will answer your question before choosing a model or comparing fit statistics.\n\nHere, person identifies whose performance is assessed, item identifies the task or scored component, and rater identifies who assigns the score. These influences are called facets. What counts as an item depends on the study: a task–criterion combination may be an item, but that coding alone does not model dependence between scores on the same response.","title":"Start with a concrete question"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Understand-one-row-of-data","page":"Getting Started","text":"Use one row per observed rating. For example:\n\nPerson Item Rater Score Response\nP1 I1 R1 1 P1-I1\nP1 I1 R2 2 P1-I1\nP1 I2 R1 0 P1-I2\nP2 I1 R2 1 P2-I1\n\nThe first two rows describe two ratings of the same response. They are not two independent performances. A response identifier helps preserve that relationship when checking the data or splitting them for prediction. It does not, by itself, add a response effect to the fitted model. The table is only an illustration of row meaning, not an adequate analysis dataset.\n\nCategories are ordered outcomes. Coding them 0, 1 and 2 does not assume equal distances on an underlying ability scale. Declare the complete intended category order, including a category that happens not to occur in a small sample. An unobserved rating is missing information; it must not be entered as score zero.\n\nThe design also matters. If one group of people is rated only by R1 and another only by R2, with no linking observations or justified anchors, severity and group differences may not be separable from the ratings. More MCMC draws cannot create that missing link. Connectedness is necessary in many designs but does not guarantee useful precision or remove bias from selective rater assignment. See Data Validation for coverage, linking and missingness checks.","title":"Understand one row of data"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Choose-assumptions-before-choosing-a-model-name","page":"Getting Started","text":"Three choices are easy to confuse: the number of abilities, whether item/rater multipliers are estimated, and whether ability dimensions are correlated. They are separate modeling decisions.\n\nQuestion Relevant model choice What the choice adds\nIs one ability sufficient for the intended interpretation? One-dimensional MFRM Ability, item difficulty, rater severity and category steps, with fixed slopes.\nShould items differ in sensitivity and raters in the concentration of their category probabilities? Scalar GMFRM Positive item discrimination and rater consistency, within its one-dimensional response equation.\nAre distinct abilities needed with fixed item–dimension coefficients? Multidimensional MFRM A specified mapping from items to dimensions; active coefficients remain one.\nAre distinct abilities and varying active loadings needed? MGMFRM A specified zero/nonzero loading pattern, estimated positive active loadings and rater consistency.\nShould the population association between dimensions be estimated? An explicitly correlated model Correlation is an additional assumption and parameter, not an automatic consequence of using several dimensions.\n\nMFRM stands for many-facet Rasch model, GMFRM for generalized many-facet Rasch model, and MGMFRM for multidimensional generalized many-facet Rasch model. A more flexible model requires more information and stronger attention to priors and identification. The table is a way to formulate alternatives, not a rule that the most flexible model should win.\n\nThe implemented families also differ in who owns the category steps and in their scale conventions. In particular, the scalar GMFRM uses rater-specific steps, whereas MGMFRM uses item-specific steps. Comparing these configurations does not isolate the effect of dimensionality alone. The theory guide explains these differences explicitly.\n\nFor a multidimensional model, the Q matrix declares which dimensions enter each item. For example, two reasoning items and two communication items give:\n\nItem Reasoning Communication\nI1 1 0\nI2 1 0\nI3 0 1\nI4 0 1\n\nZero excludes a dimension. In MGMFRM, one permits a loading to be estimated; it does not fix that loading to one. Each row here uses one dimension, called a between-item structure. Several ones in a row allow several abilities to enter that item's predictor. Choose and justify Q from the intended measurement structure; the current interface does not discover an unknown Q automatically.\n\nSeveral active dimensions within one item are called within-item multidimensionality. This structure is distinct from a compensatory or non-compensatory response rule, and from correlation between abilities. See the worked explanation before interpreting these labels.","title":"Choose assumptions before choosing a model name"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Check-what-this-package-can-currently-fit","page":"Getting Started","text":"The stable fitting workflow covers one-dimensional MFRM with rating-scale or partial-credit steps. GMFRM, multidimensional MFRM and MGMFRM are experimental. Estimated ability correlation is available only through the documented, restricted two-dimensional between-item models. A three-dimensional model with independent abilities and a model estimating three-dimensional correlations are different capabilities; the latter is not a supported fitting route.\n\nThe common prediction/comparison APIs described in this development manual are an isolated, unreleased candidate. Their presence does not establish that any model is scientifically preferable. Read Scope and Releases for the exact restrictions and use documentation matching your installed revision.\n\nJulia versus CmdStan is a computation choice. MFRM versus GMFRM versus MGMFRM is a model choice. Changing the backend should preserve the intended probability model, priors and constraints; matching a backend name or random seed does not establish that those ingredients match.","title":"Check what this package can currently fit"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Follow-one-complete-analysis-path","page":"Getting Started","text":"State the quantity of interest. For example, the ability difference between P1 and P2 in communication, with a scientifically meaningful scale.\nValidate the ratings and their design. Inspect category use, facet coverage, links, missingness and shared responses.\nSpecify the model and priors. Check what each parameter means, how the origin and units are defined, and what simulated ratings from the prior look like.\nFit and inspect computation. Use multiple chains and review diagnostics and Monte Carlo error for the quantities you intend to report.\nExamine the substantive answer. Read uncertainty intervals, predictive checks and sensitivity results. Use held-out predictions when the question concerns predictive performance.\nSave and reproduce the result. Save the fitted object, model specification, data provenance and analysis environment; regenerate summaries and figures from the saved fit.\n\nTo try data checking and model specification first, without sampling or writing files, use the Inspect Data Without Fitting example. For the subsequent fitting example, use a project checkout with its dependencies installed and run this command from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. examples/minimal.jl\n\nThis uses artificial data and only 50 warmup plus 50 retained draws per chain. It demonstrates data validation, fitting, diagnostics and save/reload. Those settings are not a sample-size or MCMC recommendation, and warnings should be read rather than treated as successful inference. The example walkthrough explains its output files and optional plots. The Bayesian Workflow gives the fuller analysis sequence, including prior checks and sensitivity analysis.","title":"Follow one complete analysis path"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Use-Julia-help-and-examples","page":"Getting Started","text":"Julia has built-in help for functions, types and modules. From the repository root, start an interactive session with julia --project=. and load the package:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\n\nAt the beginning of the Julia prompt, press ?. The prompt becomes help?>; type FacetData and press Enter. Its example constructs a complete small dataset, checks it and creates a model specification without fitting. Press Backspace at an empty help prompt to return to julia>.\n\nIf you use R In Julia\n?function_name Type ?FacetData, ?mfrm_spec or ?fit at the Julia prompt.\nPackage-level help Type ?BayesianMGMFRM for this package's starting workflow.\nSearch help by a word In help mode, enter a quoted word such as \"severity\"; this searches loaded documentation.\nA longer details section Type ??fit from the Julia prompt to include its extended help.\nexample(function_name) There is no standard one-to-one command. Read/copy the documented example or run an example script supplied by the package.\n\nThe ? shortcut is REPL syntax; do not paste ?fit into a .jl script. Use @doc fit to retrieve its documentation in ordinary Julia code or a notebook. This retrieves help, including its full detail; it does not execute examples. See the official Julia help-mode guide and documentation guide.\n\nThe manual's API pages render the package's docstrings. The short REPL help and the web reference therefore share their source; the introductory chapters add the conceptual explanation and reading order. Blocks marked as doctests are examples whose displayed results can be checked during documentation builds. Merely opening help does not run those checks.\n\nFor an executable first example in this development checkout, run:\n\njulia --project=. examples/inspect_ratings.jl\n\nOr, in a Julia session that has loaded this same package revision:\n\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(BayesianMGMFRM), \"examples\", \"inspect_ratings.jl\"))\n\nBoth forms print the rating counts, category order, validation issues and model equation. They run no MCMC and write no files. include executes a script; other examples such as minimal.jl do fit models and save results, so read their description before running them. New development examples may be absent from an older installed release.\n\nYou can also download the no-fit example from this preview and run include(\"inspect_ratings.jl\") in a Julia session with BayesianMGMFRM loaded.\n\nOne important default is easy to miss: fit(spec) currently selects backend = :julia, meaning random-walk Metropolis. The minimal fitting example explicitly chooses backend = :advancedhmc for Julia NUTS. A backend called “Julia” is not itself a sampler algorithm, and the default chains = 1 is not sufficient for between-chain convergence checks.","title":"Use Julia help and examples"},{"category":"section","location":"learning-guide/#Check-your-understanding","page":"Getting Started","text":"If two people received the same average score but faced different raters, their adjusted ability estimates need not be equal. If a model reports finite intervals despite a weak design, inspect how the priors identify and regularize the parameters. If a richer model predicts better, ask whether the improvement answers your intended prediction question and whether its ability estimates are interpretable and precise enough for the proposed use.\n\nContinue with How Rating Models Work, or go directly to Reading Results and Comparing Models if you are already comfortable with probability models.","title":"Check your understanding"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Visualization-Gallery","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nA useful plot answers a question. Start with location, then examine response patterns, and finally ask how a multidimensional model translates abilities into rating probabilities.\n\nQuestion Display What the axes mean\nWhere are people, items and raters on the measurement scale? Wright Map Separate facets share a vertical logit scale.\nWhich response patterns deserve a closer look at a given location? Pathway Map Infit on the horizontal axis; facet location on the vertical axis.\nHow do two abilities change the probability of a rating? MGMFRM response surface Two ability axes and a vertical probability axis.\n\nThe Wright and Pathway illustrations use deterministic artificial draws, not fitted posteriors. They demonstrate the output and its reading conventions, not uncertainty calibrated by a study. The MGMFRM figures reuse a saved experimental fit to simulated ratings. None of these pictures establishes a recommended model. Select a figure to inspect it at full resolution. Titles and notes now appear outside the images, matching the default plotting behavior. Each figure has a separate downloadable text note; keep it with the image when sharing results.\n\nTo change an interval from 90% or 95% to 99%, follow the interval-level examples. They also explain ETIs, HDIs and the precision of interval endpoints.","title":"Visualization Gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Wright-Map:-locate-the-facets","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"Figure 1\n\nFacet Locations in an Artificial MFRM Illustration\n\n(Image: Wright Map with person, rater, item and adjacent-category boundary panels sharing a logit scale.)\n\nNote. This illustration uses 80 deterministic artificial draws, not a fitted posterior. Circles and bars summarize medians and central 95% intervals of those inputs; diamonds denote fixed references or anchors. External anchor uncertainty is excluded. All panels retain their original logit coordinates. The boundary panel uses zero rater severity and adjacent-category equality. The artificial draws trigger numerical MCMC warnings; they do not represent an estimation run.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note\n\nRead this map from left to right:\n\nPerson ability: higher locations increase the odds of a higher rating.\nRater severity and item difficulty: higher locations decrease those odds. The minus signs in the headings describe these effects; the plotted estimates have not been sign-reversed.\nItem plus category step: at zero rater severity, a person at a boundary has equal probabilities for the two adjacent categories. With three or more categories, those two probabilities need not each be 50%.\n\nWith an actual fit, circles mark posterior medians and bars mark central 95% credible intervals. Diamonds mark fixed references or anchors, whose external uncertainty is excluded. The illustration uses the same summary machinery on artificial inputs. Horizontal spacing simply separates labels. For boundaries, item difficulty and the step are added within each draw before summarization.\n\nThe shared scale can reveal how item locations relate to person locations and how raters differ in severity. It cannot show that an item measures the intended construct, that an anchor is correct, or that the chains converged. The current renderer supports stable unidimensional MFRM, including RSM and PCM. A single Wright axis does not summarize all abilities of MGMFRM.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nfit_result = load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result; interval = 0.95)\nsave(\"wright-map.svg\", figure)\n# The title and note appear in Julia output by default.\n\nSee the Wright Map reference for selection and anchor details. The Facets manual describes the traditional shared-scale display; its expected-score columns are different from the adjacent-category boundaries used here.\n\nFigure 2\n\nPerson Distribution Beside Item and Rater Locations\n\n(Image: Wright histogram with 80 artificial person medians on a vertical logit scale beside rater and item interval panels.)\n\nNote. A separate artificial example uses 80 people, four items and three raters, with 40 deterministic draws per parameter. Each person contributes one median to the histogram; the bars count 80 people, not 3,200 person-by-draw values. Bins are 0.5 logits wide from −3 to 3, left closed/right open with the final upper edge included. Points and intervals in the other panels summarize the artificial inputs; diamonds identify fixed references. The figure does not show individual person uncertainty or uncertainty in the bin counts. These inputs are not estimated posterior evidence; their numerical diagnostic warnings are retained in the full note.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note · Numerical inputs\n\nThe histogram makes a larger cohort readable without crowding the figure with person labels. Compare its vertical extent with the item and rater positions. The horizontal scale is now a person count, while horizontal spacing in the item and rater panels still just separates labels. With an actual fit this would be a distribution of posterior medians affected by prior shrinkage, not an estimated population density or a distribution of all MCMC draws. Use the individual-interval view in Figure 1 when uncertainty about a person matters; changing bin widths can also change the apparent shape.\n\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result;\n    person_display = :histogram, bins = 12, include_thresholds = false)\nsave(\"wright-histogram.svg\", figure)\n\nThe FACETS frequency-column example motivates the layout. This option preserves the package's Bayesian quantities and its existing individual-interval default. All persons are counted even when their number exceeds the default limit of 60 individual positions; that limit still applies to item, rater and boundary marks. See Person histogram overview for explicit edges, full-roster report output and saved-fit reproduction.","title":"Wright Map: locate the facets"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Pathway-Map:-connect-location-with-response-patterns","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"Figure 3\n\nLocation and Infit in an Artificial MFRM Illustration\n\n(Image: Pathway Map with separate person, rater and item panels, infit on the horizontal axis, location on the vertical axis, and marginal interval bars.)\n\nNote. This illustration uses the same 80 artificial draws as Figure 1. Points are marginal medians; horizontal and vertical bars show separate central 95% intervals, not a joint 95% region. Infit = 1 is a reference, not an automatic acceptance threshold. The artificial inputs are not posterior evidence. With a fitted model, review sampler diagnostics and predictive checks before interpretation.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note\n\nThis is a Pathway-style display using posterior infit mean squares. The traditional Bond-and-Fox teaching approach combines location, fit and precision; implementations also use standardized infit t-statistics. The axes and intervals here have different definitions, so t-statistic cutoffs such as −2 and +2 do not apply.\n\nThe vertical position retains the meaning of the corresponding Wright panel. The horizontal position adds infit mean square, an information-weighted summary of squared standardized residuals. Values above the reference of 1 indicate more residual variation than the model reference; values below 1 indicate less. Infit emphasizes ratings with more model information. Outfit, available from the same data function, is more sensitive to unexpected ratings with small predicted variance.\n\nFor example, a high rater location with infit near 1 would suggest severity without the same signal of excess residual variation. A similar location farther to the right would prompt inspection of that rater's response patterns. These are reading examples, not findings from the artificial figure.\n\nHere points are marginal medians. Horizontal and vertical bars summarize separate 95% marginal intervals; their cross is not a joint 95% region. The dashed line is a reference, not an automatic pass/fail cutoff. Posterior residual summaries reuse observed data, and their finite-sample behavior depends on the design. Interpret them alongside counts, predictive checks and the substantive rating context. A flagged point does not by itself establish bias, justify removing a rater, or choose MFRM over GMFRM.\n\nThe package supplies diagnostic_map_data; the figure is an example using CairoMakie, rather than a new package plotting API. Download the Pathway Map example, save it beside your analysis, and use a stable MFRM cache:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\ninclude(\"pathway_map.jl\")\nfit_result = load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\")\nsave(\"pathway-map.svg\", pathway_map(fit_result))\n\n# Inspect the exact numerical rows and their flags before interpreting them.\nrows = diagnostic_map_data(fit_result; metrics = :infit,\n    include_thresholds = false, interval = 0.95)\n\nThe example uses every retained draw and does not fit a model. Review sampler diagnostics separately; missing summaries produce an error rather than omitted points. See Reading Results for the surrounding workflow.","title":"Pathway Map: connect location with response patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#MGMFRM-in-3D:-abilities-become-probabilities","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"Figure 4\n\nRating Probabilities Across Two MGMFRM Abilities\n\n(Image: MGMFRM category response surfaces for item I1 and rater R1, with two ability axes and category probability as height; four heatmaps show pointwise interval widths.)\n\nNote. Item I1 and rater R1, using all 16,000 saved joint draws from one experimental fit to simulated ratings. Height shows posterior mean category probability; the companion heatmaps show 90% pointwise interval widths. Q excludes the second ability for this item, so the surfaces are flat in that direction. There are no additional fixed ability dimensions. The saved fit had no warnings under its recorded MCMC thresholds. These intervals concern conditional probabilities, not future individual ratings; display ranges do not establish data support.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note\n\nThis figure reuses 16,000 retained joint draws from one saved experimental MGMFRM fit to simulated ratings. Its four colors identify ratings 1–4. Moving along the first ability changes those probabilities: the lowest category loses probability, the highest gains probability, and intermediate categories can rise and fall. At each ability pair, all category probabilities sum to 1.\n\nThe surface is flat along the second ability because this item's Q row excludes that dimension. This is a model restriction, not evidence that the second ability is unimportant for every item. The heatmaps show widths of 90% pointwise credible intervals for the probabilities, on a shared color scale. They do not show probability height, future-rating intervals, simultaneous bands or MCSE.\n\nEach grid point averages probabilities evaluated at the joint draws. In general, that differs from evaluating the model once at posterior-mean parameters. The selected rater's severity and consistency remain in the calculation. No new sampling was performed to produce this gallery.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"mgmfrm-fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(restored;\n    item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", dimensions = (1, 2),\n    x = range(-2, 2; length = 21), y = range(-2, 2; length = 21),\n    category = :all, interval = 0.90)\nsave(\"mgmfrm-response-surface.svg\", figure)\n\nThree display axes are not three latent dimensions. The height here is a probability. For an independent model with more than two abilities, the existing surface function requires values for every undisplayed ability and shows a conditional slice. A three-ability person scatterplot would instead require three latent axes, uncertainty displays and identifiable dimension meanings. It would not establish support for estimating a free correlation matrix with three or more dimensions; that fitting extension remains unavailable.\n\nThis is a static figure. The current CairoMakie interface can change the viewing angle or select one category; it does not provide browser rotation. See 3D item response surfaces for those controls. The saved fit had no warnings under its recorded MCMC thresholds, but one figure and one fit do not establish recovery, calibration, mixed-Q performance or model-selection validity.\n\nSee Titles and notes outside the image for saving the note to a file or selecting the previous embedded layout.","title":"MGMFRM in 3D: abilities become probabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Compare-items-and-raters","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"Figure 4 shows how to read all categories for one item–rater pair. To see how that response changes across the instrument, compare all five items at one fixed rater, then all five raters at one fixed item. These two comparisons reuse the same saved fit. They are not separate fits or new simulations.\n\nThe following panels select the highest rating, category 4, so that each panel contains one surface. Both figures use the same ability range (−2 to 2), 21 × 21 grid, viewing angle and 0–1 probability scale. Height and color encode the same posterior mean probability. The I1–R1 reference appears in both figures. Category 4 is one part of the response distribution; return to Figure 4 or select another category to inspect the others.\n\nFigure 5\n\nItem Comparison at Rater R1\n\n(Image: Five category-4 probability surfaces for items I1 through I5 with rater R1 fixed. I1 and I2 depend on the first ability; I3, I4 and I5 depend on the second.)\n\nNote. All five items from the same experimental fit to simulated ratings; rater R1 is fixed. Each surface averages category-4 probabilities across all 16,000 retained joint draws. Height and color share the 0–1 probability scale. Active dimensions are prescribed by Q: I1–I2 use θ₁, and I3–I5 use θ₂. The other axis is flat by construction. These images show posterior means only; the downloadable data retain 90% pointwise credible intervals. No additional ability dimensions are fixed. Display ranges do not establish data support.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note · Numerical data\n\nStart with the panel's Active label. I1 and I2 respond along θ₁, whereas I3, I4 and I5 respond along θ₂. Within an active direction, compare where the surface rises and how steeply. Item differences can reflect difficulty, category steps and loadings, as well as Q. This is not a ranking of difficulty on a single ability axis: the items do not all measure the same ability. I2's category-4 mean probability stays above 0.947 throughout this displayed grid. Its shallow-looking surface reflects probabilities already close to 1 in this range; that is different from excluding an ability through Q.\n\nFigure 6\n\nRater Comparison at Item I1\n\n(Image: Five category-4 probability surfaces for raters R1 through R5 with item I1 fixed, all using the first ability and the same 0–1 probability scale.)\n\nNote. All five raters from the same experimental fit to simulated ratings; item I1 is fixed. Each surface averages category-4 probabilities across all 16,000 retained joint draws. The ability grid, viewing angle and 0–1 probability scale match Figure 5. Item I1 excludes θ₂, so every panel is flat along that axis. These images show posterior means only; the downloadable data retain 90% pointwise credible intervals. Rater differences include severity and consistency. They do not establish bias or rater quality.\n\nPDF · SVG · Full text note · Numerical data\n\nHere the item is held constant, so every panel has the same active dimension. Differences show how the fitted rater changes the probability of assigning the highest category at a given ability. Both severity and consistency enter the calculation. Do not infer a reliable between-rater difference from the distance between mean surfaces, or from overlap of separate marginal intervals. A probability contrast requires evaluating the difference at matched ability points within each joint draw and summarizing those differences. For example, at θ₁ = θ₂ = 0, the mean category-4 probability is approximately 0.93 for R1 and 0.34 for R4. These are conditional example probabilities from one simulated dataset, not a rater-quality ranking.\n\nThe saved fit had no warnings under its recorded MCMC thresholds. That does not establish calibration or adequate precision for every contrast. For a particular pair, plot_response_surface displays the pointwise interval-width heatmaps shown in Figure 4. The numerical downloads use rows for x and columns for y, and include each panel's mean, lower/upper endpoints and interval width. Their intervals describe conditional probabilities, not future individual ratings, simultaneous bands or intervals for differences.\n\nDownload the comparison example to create these editable figures from a two-ability MGMFRM cache. This example calls the existing numerical function; it adds no new fitting or plotting API. The first supplied item and rater are the references, and the supplied order sets the panel order. Titles and notes remain outside the images by default.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\ninclude(\"response_surface_variants.jl\")\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"mgmfrm-fit.jls\")\ncomparison = response_surface_variants(restored;\n    items = [\"I1\", \"I2\", \"I3\", \"I4\", \"I5\"],\n    raters = [\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\", \"R5\"], category = 4)\nsave(\"item-comparison.svg\", comparison.items)\nsave(\"rater-comparison.svg\", comparison.raters)\n# Pointwise intervals are also available in comparison.item_data/rater_data.","title":"Compare items and raters"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Choose-the-plot-after-the-question","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"Use the Wright Map for locations, the Pathway Map for diagnostic follow-up, and the response surface for model-implied probabilities. Read each together with its uncertainty and source. Then return to model comparison and simulation: attractive or smooth figures cannot replace predictive evaluation, recovery or SBC.","title":"Choose the plot after the question"},{"category":"section","location":"visualization-gallery/#Prior-predictive-sensitivity","page":"Visualization Gallery","text":"The prior sensitivity guide compares category use, every item mean and every rater mean across explicit loading priors. Its worked example shows why matching the overall average can still conceal a category-use mismatch. Predictive intervals describe replicated score statistics; the adjacent posterior ridge views describe parameter draws.","title":"Prior predictive sensitivity"},{"category":"section","location":"data-validation/#Long-Format-Data-and-Pre-Fit-Validation","page":"Data Validation","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.jl starts from long-format rating data: one row per rating event. The data and validation layer is intentionally sampler-free. Its purpose is to make common weak or non-estimable many-facet design patterns visible before a sampler is called.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\n\nratings = (\n    examinee = [\"E1\", \"E1\", \"E1\", \"E1\", \"E2\", \"E2\", \"E2\", \"E2\"],\n    rater = [\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\"],\n    item = [\"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\", \"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\"],\n    group = [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"B\", \"B\", \"A\", \"A\"],\n    score = [0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0, 2],\n)\n\ndata = FacetData(ratings;\n    person = :examinee,\n    rater = :rater,\n    item = :item,\n    score = :score,\n    category_levels = 0:2,\n    group = :group,\n)\n\nvalidation = validate_design(data; bias = [(:rater, :group)])\nvalidation.passed || error(validation)\nspec = mfrm_spec(data; thresholds = :partial_credit, validation_report = validation)\n\nFacetData records deterministic integer indexes and stable label maps for persons, raters, items, the intended consecutive integer score scale, and optional metadata roles. Pass category_levels when the form defines that scale. If the keyword is omitted, the backward-compatible default is the contiguous integer range from the observed minimum through maximum. validate_design returns a ValidationReport with machine-readable ValidationIssue.code values and human-readable messages. Current checks cover:\n\nobserved and skipped score categories;\nall-minimum/all-maximum person patterns and constant-score raters with at least two ratings;\nsingleton person, rater, item, and optional metadata levels;\nconnectedness of the person-rater-item graph;\nrank of the current reference-constrained minimal location design;\nitem/category support warnings for weakly informed partial-credit thresholds;\nempty, sparse, and potentially confounded DFF cells for requested bias terms.","title":"Long-Format Data and Pre-Fit Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"data-validation/#Ordinal-Response-Pattern-Stress","page":"Data Validation","text":"Use ordinal_response_pattern_audit before fitting when category use or boundary responses are part of the design risk:\n\naudit = ordinal_response_pattern_audit(data)\naudit.status\naudit.category_scale.intended_levels\naudit.category_scale.observed_levels\naudit.category_scale.unobserved_interior_categories\naudit.category_scale.unobserved_endpoint_categories\naudit.flags.extreme_person_levels\naudit.flags.constant_rater_levels\n\nIf a five-category dataset contains scores 1, 2, 4, and 5, FacetData retains the contiguous 1:5 range and the audit reports category 3 as an unobserved interior category. This is not a missing response row and is not automatically a fit error; its adjacent step transitions can be weakly separated and belong in prior-sensitivity and category-use checks. Literal missing score values are different: the current missing_policy = :error rejects them during data construction.\n\nA person rated 5 on every observed response supplies one-sided information about ability, and dimension-specific Q coverage must also be inspected. A rater assigning 1 on every observed response can weakly separate severity from the generalized rater-consistency parameter, depending on assignment overlap. Both patterns produce pre-fit warnings when the level has at least two ratings, but neither is converted into an infinite parameter or an automatic scientific failure. Proper priors regularize these weakly informed parameters; report the affected levels and examine sensitivity to prior regimes. If the entire dataset contains only one score category, the ordered- response likelihood is not estimable and validate_design returns an error.\n\nThe ordinary FacetData constructor infers the scale from the observed minimum through maximum unless category_levels is supplied. For example, category_levels = 0:4 preserves five intended categories even when the realized data contain only scores 1 through 4. validate_design then returns the warning code :unobserved_declared_endpoint, and the response-pattern audit separates the intended, observed, interior-unobserved, and endpoint- unobserved categories. The likelihood, predictions, refit subsets, model and fit metadata, and serialized fit cache retain the declared scale. The package does not silently collapse it; deciding whether a category is structurally possible or should be collapsed remains an analysis decision.","title":"Ordinal Response-Pattern Stress"},{"category":"section","location":"data-validation/#Clustered-Responses-and-Testlet-Identity","page":"Data Validation","text":"When multiple criteria, items, or raters refer to the same response, map the source columns explicitly:\n\nclustered_data = FacetData(clustered_ratings;\n    person = :examinee,\n    rater = :rater,\n    item = :criterion,\n    score = :score,\n    response_id = :essay_id,\n    testlet_id = :prompt_id,\n    occasion = :occasion,\n)\n\nresponse_id denotes one globally unique scored response. It must map to one person, one testlet, and, when recorded, one occasion. If response identifiers are unique only within person, construct a composite identifier before calling FacetData. testlet_id is separate from the existing task role: the package does not silently reinterpret task metadata as a testlet. All three fields are metadata in the current likelihood and add no fitted parameter.\n\nUse testlet_design_check to examine the intended clustered effect:\n\ndesign_check = testlet_design_check(clustered_data;\n    target = :scalar_shared_cluster,\n    independent_ratings_declared = true,\n)\n\nThe check separates identifier validity, structural identification, the scope of the named candidate, the frozen structural profile, current fitting support, and pair-family-specific diagnostic support. The fields structurally_eligible_for_candidate, structural_profile_met, and current_fit_supported are deliberately distinct; the last remains false for every clustered effect in this release. An :ok status means only that the unchanged structural screen is met. :unsupported_candidate distinguishes a current candidate-shape limitation, such as repeated responses in the scalar shared-cluster target, from :underidentified; :error indicates inconsistent response identity or duplicate response-rater-item keys. Any threshold override is labelled support_profile = :custom_unvalidated. Declaring independent ratings records a design fact supplied by the analyst; it does not verify independence from the scores.\n\nThe target = :scalar_shared_cluster structural screen requires one response per person-by-testlet, multiple indicators per response, multiple testlets per person, multiple persons per testlet, and connected ordinary and person-by-testlet graphs. Repeated responses are evaluated separately because stable person-by-testlet variation and response-specific variation cannot be distinguished from one occasion. Halo support requires the multiple-indicator rater-response cells to co-occur within independently multiply rated responses; a graph joined only by one shared response is rejected by the frozen screen. Rater-by-task support requires ordinary, rater-task, and person-task connectivity, replicated cells, multiple raters per task, and shared person or response links. Fixed-Q MGMFRM support additionally requires the person-testlet base graph, a connected Q-by-testlet graph, within-testlet Q contrasts, and no perfect dimension-testlet alignment. diagnostic_pair_support reports support separately by testlet for the contract's applicable pair families; it is not a local-independence result.\n\nlocal_dependence_contract records the provisional matching, duplicate, adjusted-Q3, FDR, global maximum-statistic, and conditional versus marginal predictive-check rules. It keeps three estimands distinct: item Q3 when each response-item has one rating and each response has one rater, item Q3 within a common response-by-rater unit, and rater-pair association on a common response-by-item unit. It never silently averages multiple raters or repeated responses; cross-rater cross-item pairing is also unavailable until its independence and weighting rules are predeclared. Estimation and adjusted-Q3 centering are stratified by testlet_id. Pair support is evaluated draw by draw from the joint validity mask, with both an absolute draw minimum and an eligible-draw fraction; undefined or sparse correlations remain report-only. The named :ld0_v1 thresholds are frozen but uncalibrated; threshold sensitivity must use profile = :custom_unvalidated.\n\nlocal_dependence_summary now evaluates those three pair families for fitted MFRM and guarded GMFRM/MGMFRM objects. It reports raw and adjusted-Q3- style draw summaries for item pairs, same-response/same-criterion rater-pair association, common units and distinct common responses, family-by-testlet support graphs, paired posterior predictive tail fractions, within-family BH values, and one all-family maximum-statistic reference. Draw indices must be distinct; ndraws uses sampling without replacement. A rater pair supported by many criteria from only one response is marked as single-response concentration and cannot justify a halo interpretation. Pair rows require at least one common unit; zero-overlap combinations are retained in aggregate family counts and testlet-stratified support graphs rather than materialized as quadratically many empty rows. family_status and testlet_status distinguish global family support from a sparse individual testlet; single-rating applicability is also testlet-specific. Candidate-pair rows, shared-unit links, positive-pair-by-draw cells, pair/common-unit-by-draw cells, and predictive cells have separate resource counts and fail with an actionable error before large work begins. The package still does not enable FDR/FWER decision labels, declare local dependence, identify its mechanism, or fit a testlet random effect.","title":"Clustered Responses and Testlet Identity"},{"category":"section","location":"data-validation/#Model-Specification-and-Design-Inspection","page":"Data Validation","text":"Use validation_suggestions to convert validation issues into machine-readable next-step suggestions:\n\nvalidation_suggestions(validation)\n\nmfrm_spec currently supports thresholds = :rating_scale and thresholds = :partial_credit. The default family = :mfrm, dimensions = 1, and discrimination = :none configuration is the fit-supported minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM slice. family = :gmfrm and family = :mgmfrm can be declared for manifest and constraint review, but they have estimation_status = :specified_only and are rejected by getdesign unless preview = true is requested. Preview designs expose parameter names and block ranges for design review, but are not accepted by fit unless they are one of the guarded experimental generalized candidates.\n\nUse model_ladder to inspect the package's fit-supported and specified-only model ladder, and constraint_table to inspect constraints, transforms, prior-block declarations, DFF validation-only rows, and multidimensional Q-mask gauge declarations.\n\nThe stable MFRM slice also accepts exact individual rater/item hard anchors. Each declaration needs a supported block, explicit observed level, finite non-Boolean logit value, and type = :hard. The anchor replaces that block's default first-level-zero gauge and is omitted from the sampled/prior vector. Multiple exact anchors may be combined across rater and item blocks, but they do not waive the ordinary connectedness/rank validation gate and do not by themselves establish common-response linking. Group anchoring of disconnected subsets is outside the stable numerical scope. Soft anchors and malformed or duplicate hard-anchor declarations remain specified_only and fail closed at getdesign.\n\nFor fixed-Q MGMFRM work, call q_matrix_validation before or after mfrm_spec. It reports binary-mask schema checks, empty item rows, empty dimensions, duplicate or aliased dimension columns, fixed cross-loading policy, the generic structural rank allowed by the Q zero pattern, person-specific dimension support, positive-loading/pure-item warnings, the default standard-normal identity-correlation prior anchor, and dimension-specific person-rater-item subgraph coverage. Invalid fixed-Q specs throw an actionable error that points back to this manifest.\n\nInspect the identification field returned by q_matrix_validation(spec) to keep three claims separate:\n\nlikelihood_support.q_full_column_structural_rank asks whether some loading matrix with the declared zero pattern can generically have full column rank;\nlikelihood_support.person_rows asks the same question for the items actually observed for each person, so an incomplete row is reported as relying on the population prior for at least one ability direction; and\nprior_anchor records the design-reference standard-normal ability prior and fixed identity correlation, rather than likelihood-only identification. An actual experimental fit can use a different person_sd; its diagnostics and artifacts record that resolved prior. See prior and coordinate conventions.\n\nguarded_fit_structure_ready retains the current experimental pre-fit boundary. conservative_stable_structure_ready is stricter: it additionally requires full person-level dimension support, at least one pure item per dimension, and connected dimension-specific facet graphs. It is a sufficient structural screen only; it does not replace recovery, sensitivity, or external validation.\n\nFor a specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM, use getdesign(spec; preview = true) to inspect the source-aligned generalized blocks without enabling fitting. GMFRM previews expose item-discrimination, rater-consistency, and rater-step blocks. MGMFRM previews expose person-by-dimension, item-dimension-discrimination, rater-consistency, and item-step blocks. The separate guarded generalized fit paths use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) and are limited to the one-dimensional rater-consistency GMFRM candidate and the fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM candidate with dimensions >= 1 and an explicit Q. The one-column restriction retains the MGMFRM equation and item-owned steps; it is distinct from the scalar rater-step GMFRM.\n\nUse design_row_table when you need row-level compiler evidence. The table shows the facet labels, identified parameter indexes, source-step path, and preview generalized parameter indexes touched by each observed rating. For specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM specs, call design_row_table(spec; preview = true); this remains an inspection path and does not make those likelihoods fit-ready.\n\nUse linear_predictor_table when you need the same compiler evidence for every response category, not only the observed category. This table is the denominator-level review surface for checking category-specific location multipliers, step paths, item-discrimination blocks, rater-consistency blocks, and item-dimension-discrimination blocks. Inspection does not extend the configurations accepted by the experimental fitting API.\n\nFor the current fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM slice, use linear_predictor_values with a parameter vector to add numeric eta, row log-denominator, and category log-probability values to the same row-by-category structure. Numeric values remain disabled for specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM previews.\n\nPass the validation_report from validate_design, or pass the same bias terms to mfrm_spec, when you want the DFF cell evidence retained in the spec. Supplied validation reports are accepted only for the same FacetData; if bias or min_cell_count is passed again, the validation options must also match.\n\nFor optional parameter inspection, compile the stable specification:\n\ndesign = getdesign(spec)\n\nThe design contains parameter names and block ranges. Without declared hard anchors, it fixes the first rater and item levels as references; threshold steps use a sum-to-zero constraint. Ordinary fitting can call fit(spec) directly without this separate compilation step.\n\nFacetData, FacetSpec, and FacetDesign contain mutable Julia collections, so construction alone is not a permanent immutability guarantee. Numerical, cache-request, and design-manifest entry points therefore recheck the current data signature and compiler-derived spec/design fields. Use design_identity to perform this check explicitly and obtain the canonical SHA-256 identity of the encoded data, model specification, parameter order, block ranges, and identification declarations:\n\nidentity = design_identity(design)\nidentity.algorithm   # :sha256\nidentity.value       # canonical semantic design fingerprint\n\nIf the underlying data, Q matrix, parameter names, block ranges, or identification declarations were changed after validation/compilation, the entry point fails and asks for a new FacetData, validation report, specification, or compiled design. fit and MFRMLogDensity retain a validated deep snapshot rather than the caller's mutable design object; compare designs with design_identity(...).value, not object identity (===). For cross-process provenance, use ordinary portable facet labels such as strings, symbols, integers, and tuples of those values. A custom label type whose repr embeds an object address cannot provide a portable fingerprint.\n\nUse model_manifest to capture the current data/spec/design provenance contract for reports and future cached fits:\n\nmodel_manifest(data; view = :public)\nmodel_manifest(spec; view = :public)\nmodel_manifest(design; view = :public)\n\nThe ordinary model-fitting API supports the minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM configuration; guarded experimental generalized fits are opt-in through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) for the narrow scalar GMFRM and fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM candidates described above.","title":"Model Specification and Design Inspection"},{"category":"section","location":"data-validation/#Reporting-Data-Before-Fitting","page":"Data Validation","text":"These reporting helpers expose fit-independent data for Quarto tables and figures without adding a plotting dependency:\n\ncoverage = coverage_summary(spec)\nheatmap_data = coverage_matrix(data; rows = :rater, columns = :person)\noverlap = rater_overlap(data; unit = :person_item)\nlinking = anchor_linking_summary(spec; unit = :person_item, view = :public)\nrating_design = rating_design_check(spec; unit = :person_item, view = :public)\nthresholds = threshold_map_data(design; params = zeros(length(design.parameter_names)))\n\ncoverage_summary returns long-form category counts, facet-level counts, and compact facet summaries. coverage_matrix returns a facet-by-facet count matrix for heat maps. rater_overlap returns pairwise overlap counts and Jaccard overlap for the chosen rated unit. With clustered metadata, units also include :response_id, :testlet_id, :person_testlet, and :response_item. The two testlet-based units are marked as descriptive coverage: sharing only a testlet label does not establish shared-response rater linking, so anchor_linking_summary and rating_design_check reject them as linking units. The response-based units are explicitly marked as common-response linking candidates. anchor_linking_summary combines declared hard/soft anchor rows, anchor target checks, rater overlap connectedness, and optional anchor-axis sensitivity coverage; it is a diagnostic report, not an anchor refit or linking-constant estimator. rating_design_check packages the observed rating-graph components, weak rater links, anchor coverage, complete-grid coverage, repeated ratings, sparse person-rater-item cells, optional time/order metadata, and nonignorable rater assignment limitation into report rows. Because the current FacetData contract stores observed complete long-format rows rather than an external planned-design table, structural versus accidental missingness is reported as not identifiable from the observed data alone. The same review is threaded into model_manifest and fit_report.\n\nThree names in this diagnostic require careful interpretation. The legacy optional_time_order_fields row only reports whether categorical occasion metadata exist; it does not verify within-rater sequence, timestamps, or randomized presentation. nonignorable_assignment_flagged = true is a standing limitation warning, not a data-driven finding that assignment was nonrandom. The sparse_person_rater_item_blocks row counts observed cells below the requested repetition threshold; it is not the overall fraction of a planned rating grid that was sampled.\n\nLikewise, anchor_coverage checks declared hard/soft parameter-anchor targets. It does not count common responses used to link raters. When reporting linking, distinguish the proportion of unique person–item targets scored by at least two raters, the proportion deliberately shared by designated linking raters, and the corresponding share of all rating events. A design can double-score every target without a special all-rater common set. Controlled benchmarks with reference information are another distinct quantity.\n\nthreshold_map_data returns rating-scale or partial-credit threshold-step metadata, including derived sum-to-zero steps when a parameter vector is supplied.","title":"Reporting Data Before Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#Prediction-and-Recovery-Candidate-API","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThese qualified functions are available in this isolated candidate, which has not been merged or released. Use BayesianMGMFRM. when calling them. They consume existing data specifications, fit caches or evaluation records; they do not automatically fit candidate models or choose a winning model or design.\n\nThe saved prediction and recovery guide connects these operations to saved-fit examples, model/dimension/Q choices, diagnostic warnings, failure accounting and interpretation limits. Existing exported comparison and vector-truth recovery APIs retain their meanings.","title":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate API"},{"category":"section","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#Existing-row-prediction-and-comparison","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"These methods evaluate fitted levels. WAIC and raw importance-sampling LOO are training-data approximations; they are not heldout K-fold scores, reference-validated PSIS or new-level prediction. See each function's supported inputs and uncertainty limits.","title":"Existing-row prediction and comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#Known-level-K-fold-preparation-and-scoring","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"Prepare the same declared split for each candidate model, then supply the matching saved training fits. The complete planned fold roster determines missing/failed status. Aggregation optionally estimates first-order MCMC error (mcse=:delta) conditional on fixed data/splits, with explicit independence assumptions for combining fold/model errors. It does not estimate data-sampling K-fold SE or execute refits. See the worked numerical-error guide.","title":"Known-level K-fold preparation and scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#Named-truth-and-independent-dataset-summaries","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"Truths must use the model's native scale and identifying constraints. Structural constants do not count as estimated coverage. Summaries of fixed-truth replications require the full dataset roster and a documented independence declaration before reporting replication SE; this neither verifies generation nor performs SBC.","title":"Named truth and independent-dataset summaries"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.prediction_observations","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"prediction_observations(fit; dataset_id, observation_ids,\n    ndraws=nothing, draw_indices=nothing, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n    include_probabilities=true)\n\nExtract conditional predictions for ALL original fitted rows of the eight supported fit types. Supply persistent observation IDs in that fit's row order; row numbers and response-group IDs are not automatically promoted to outcome IDs. dataset_id names the shared source dataset. IDs and native labels/scores are bound together, including optional metadata, in a row-order-independent hash.\n\nReturns draws × observations pointwise log likelihood, optional draws × observations × categories probabilities, selected chain/iteration IDs, model scale/constraints/prior metadata and diagnostics. Selection preserves duplicates. Probabilities and likelihood share one stable logit evaluation. Setting include_probabilities=false avoids allocating the category array. No prior or Jacobian enters the likelihood. Diagnostic warnings are retained, not filtered.\n\nThis does not accept new rows, folds, weights, new facet levels, or a replacement prediction target. The result is training-row, existing-level conditional prediction. It is not a fitted-CV result or a scientific acceptance decision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.prediction_observations"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.observation_alignment","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"observation_alignment(reference, candidate)\n\nReturn candidate column indices in reference outcome order after checking source dataset, outcomes, categories and conditional training target. Different latent models may align; this is not permission to compare their parameter values, an automatic comparison-axis choice, or a WAIC/LOO ranking function. Arrays are not copied. Draws from different fits need not have the same counts or RNG streams.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.observation_alignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.prediction_criteria","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"prediction_criteria(prediction; criteria=(:waic, :raw_loo), ...)\nprediction_criteria(fit; dataset_id, observation_ids, criteria=..., ...)\n\nCompute native criteria once from the common full retained log-likelihood matrix. Allowed names are :waic, :rawloo (native loo), and :hillsmoothedloo (native psisloo). The latter is the repository's Hill-tail smoother, not an assertion of equivalence to the reference loo package. No refit or ranking is performed.\n\nAll original chains and draws are required. Retain sampling and criterion warnings, including affected observation IDs. Native pointwise SE is not MCMC MCSE or a cluster/replication SE; native importance ESS is not autocorrelation adjusted. The LOO target omits one rating with specification/level maps fixed, not a person, response group, or new-level prediction task.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.PredictionObservationAdapter.prediction_criteria"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.compare_predictions","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_predictions(models::Pair...;\n    comparison_axis, criterion=:waic, baseline=nothing, se_unit=:none,\n    dataset_id=nothing, observation_ids=nothing, ...)\n\nCompare two or more supported fits, or two or more prediction_observations bundles, on the same original rating outcomes. For fits, supply dataset_id and unique observation IDs: a vector shared by all fits, or a dictionary from model labels to vectors in each fit's row order. For bundles omit both keywords. All original draws/chains are used; different fit draw counts are permitted.\n\nDeclare comparison_axis=:model (same D/Q), :dimensions (D and Q may differ), or :q_matrix (same D). Family, priors, scales and correlation metadata are retained; an axis label alone does not isolate a causal effect of that choice. Allowed criteria are :waic, :raw_loo, and :hill_smoothed_loo; the last is the package's non-reference-validated Hill smoother, not validated PSIS.\n\nRows retain input order. ELPD differences are model minus the declared baseline (first model by default), not an automatically selected winner. se_unit=:none leaves difference SE missing; :observation assumes exchangeable rating units; :person sums differences within person before computing the approximate SE. The latter is one-way clustering under an exchangeable-person assumption, not a correction for arbitrary crossed rater/item dependence. These SEs are neither MCMC MCSE nor repeated-data simulation uncertainty. One unit gives missing SE.\n\nWarnings, problem observation IDs and model identities remain in each row. This is an existing-level rating-omission approximation, not refitted K-fold, new-person/rater prediction, scientific acceptance or automatic model selection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_predictions"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_refit_plan","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"prediction_refit_plan(model, plan; prior, dataset_id, observation_ids)\n\nPrepare all K-fold training requests without sampling. model is a FacetSpec or a supported Experimental.correlated(spec) model; prior is explicit. The existing K-fold partition and known-level checks run before any request is returned. Model, Q, dimension names, prior and supported covariance are retained. Persistent unique rating IDs are supplied in the source specification's row order.\n\nEach folds entry exposes model and prior for fit (scalar MFRM) or Experimental.fit (other supported models), and is bound to the full source, split, training target and heldout order. Use prediction_fold_observations to score its independently fitted training result. No full-data fit, posterior initialization, backend choice or sampler control is inferred. Specify priors before examining heldout outcomes; this API cannot detect prior/model tuning.\n\nOnly existing person/rater/item/category levels are supported. Optional grouping metadata do not become fitted effects. This is not an automatic refit executor, LOO repair, or a new-level marginal prediction API. Use prediction_kfold and compare_kfold_predictions to aggregate and compare the saved heldout results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_refit_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_fold_observations","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"prediction_fold_observations(training_fit, request; include_probabilities=true)\n\nScore one prepared K-fold request using all original retained training-fit draws and chains. Reject changed requests and fits for different training observations, model, prior or covariance. Saved records undergo their native integrity checks. Heldout native labels are mapped to the actual training parameter order; the existing likelihood kernel is shared with prediction_observations.\n\nReturns heldout draws × rows log likelihood, optional category probabilities, persistent IDs, source/split/target bindings and unfiltered sampling diagnostics. Correlated draws already contain the joint ability transform; it is not applied again. Heldout results have a distinct schema and cannot be passed to training-row WAIC/LOO. No convergence qualification, fit execution receipt, or predictive comparison is inferred from a matching target. Legacy fixed-MFRM v1 records are not migrated into canonical refit targets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_fold_observations"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold(prepared, scores; failures=Pair[],\n    mcse=:none, independent_fold_rng=false)\n\nAggregate prediction_fold_observations results against the original complete prediction_refit_plan. Results may arrive in any fold order. Explicit failures are fold ID => nonempty reason pairs; absent results remain :missing. Duplicate, foreign or edited records are rejected. All retained draws/chains are used.\n\nEach rating's score is log(mean(exp(loglikelihood))) over its training posterior. Scores are additive over ratings, even for grouped splits; this is not a joint group predictive density. Any missing/failed fold leaves the full ELPD missing. Diagnostic warnings retain all scores and mark the result as a warning. No folds are discarded. Data-sampling SE and repeated-data uncertainty are not computed. This descriptive result neither qualifies convergence nor selects a model.\n\nSet mcse=:delta to add folds[i].mcmc_error and total mcmc_error for the finite-MCMC error conditional on these data and this split. For each fold, linearize sum(log(mean(exp(loglikelihood)))) using joint draws across all heldout ratings, then reuse the autocorrelation-aware mean MCSE. Require at least two chains and ten draws per chain; these are computational minima, not sufficient-precision criteria. Degenerate first-order influence is missing, not zero error. Warnings, draw concentration and curvature-review requirements remain explicit; the estimate does not account for finite-MCMC bias.\n\nTotal MCSE requires the caller's independent_fold_rng=true declaration that fold fits used independent random streams. It is unrelated to independence of the overlapping training datasets. Detectable reused sample hashes are rejected; the declaration is not verified from seeds or hashes. Without it, fold MCSEs remain available but total MCSE is missing. Default mcse=:none preserves the previous result structure. No posterior interval, precision gate or ranking is created.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold_predictions","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold_predictions(models::Pair...; comparison_axis, baseline=nothing,\n    independent_model_rng=false)\n\nCompare named prediction_kfold results on the identical source and K-fold split. Declare :model (same D/Q), :dimensions (D/Q may differ), or :q_matrix (same D). Input order is retained; differences are model minus baseline (first by default). IDs align the individual rating scores. If any model is incomplete, all contrasts remain missing. Diagnostic warnings remain visible and never remove a fold/model. No automatic ranking, winner, weights or data-sampling SE is produced. Fold dependence is not approximated by treating folds as independent replications.\n\nWhen any input requests prediction_kfold(...; mcse=:delta), rows retain its mcmc_error and add difference_mcmc_error. Difference MCSE requires available total MCSE for both models and independent_model_rng=true, declaring independent MCMC random streams between model fits. Known sample reuse is rejected; the caller remains responsible for the independence assumption. Within-model fold independence must also have been declared during aggregation. No MCMC row pairing or independence of rating outcomes is inferred. The baseline minus itself has exactly zero computational error when the comparison is complete; other missing MCSEs remain missing. All-data/fold sampling uncertainty and finite-MCMC bias remain separate and unestimated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold_predictions"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit)\n\nList supported named model-coordinate quantities of a saved fit, including dimension labels, units, structural constants and native derived quantities. All eight supported fit types use their existing report/plot reconstruction; canonical fixed-MFRM records are required. No plotting dependency is needed. Use these exact names as keys for recovery_evaluation. This is an inventory, not raw sampler coordinates or a mapping between different latent models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_evaluation","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_evaluation(fit, truth::AbstractDict;\n    dataset_id, observation_ids, interval=0.95)\n\nEvaluate named simulation truths for one dataset, using all retained draws and chains. Keys are exact names from recovery_quantities; nonempty subsets are allowed. Truths must already use the fitted model's scale, labels and identifying constraints. No truth centering, latent alignment or across-model mapping is inferred. This function cannot establish that the supplied values generated data.\n\nReuses native bias/interval recovery arithmetic and MCMC MCSE. Sampling warnings are retained. Estimated quantities have central-interval coverage; structural constants have covered=missing, coverage_applicable=false and a separate fixed-value/truth agreement flag. Their point intervals are not calibration evidence. Interval-endpoint MCSE is neither a coverage-decision error bound nor simulation replication uncertainty, and no precision threshold is applied.\n\nReturns a source-bound, content-hashed record with per-quantity rows and explicit single-dataset scope. This neither fits/generates data nor pools replications, performs joint-prior SBC, compares models, or establishes design adequacy. Existing vector-truth parameter_recovery methods are unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_evaluation"},{"category":"function","location":"api-prediction-recovery/#BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_replications","page":"Prediction and Recovery Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_replications(evaluations;\n    planned_dataset_ids, parameters, scenario_id, dataset_independence, failures=Pair[])\n\nSummarize sealed recovery_evaluation records for one declared fixed-truth design scenario. Declare the full dataset roster and exact focal quantity names before examining results. Each quantity receives one observation per dataset; quantities, persons, chains and folds are never pooled as independent replications. Changed model/prior/scale/interval contracts and varying focal truths are rejected.\n\nfailures maps planned dataset IDs to nonempty reasons. Missing results, failures, missing quantities, fixed constants and warnings remain in the output ledger. all_completed includes numerical warnings; without_warnings excludes sampling warnings, unavailable focal MCSE and any fixed-truth mismatch. The latter is a descriptive subset, not a precision-qualified or scientifically accepted result. Structural constants receive neither recovery summaries nor coverage credit.\n\nSupply dataset_independence=(status=:declared_independent, evidence=\"...\"), or :unresolved with an explanation. The declaration concerns independent datasets from the same scenario, not independent chains. Replication SE is sample SD / sqrt(n) only for declared independence with at least two retained datasets. Reused evaluated evidence suppresses SE despite a declaration. RMSE uses the first-order delta method; zero MSE leaves its SE unavailable. Zero empirical variance is explicitly flagged.\n\nCoverage bounds assign unresolved outcomes both 0 and 1 in the planned denominator; they are not confidence intervals. No MCMC error correction, coverage-decision error bound, seed/generator/design verification, SBC or design adequacy decision is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_replications"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Scope-and-Releases","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.jl follows a conservative release policy: a model family is documented as supported only when its likelihood, parameterization, fitting path, diagnostics, and user-facing examples are covered together.","title":"Scope and Releases"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Current-Support","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"Model surface Status Notes\nOne-dimensional MFRM with rating-scale or partial-credit steps Supported Available through the documented design, fitting, diagnostic, and reporting workflow.\nFixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM Experimental Experimental.fit on Julia or CmdStan; fixed Q coefficients, unit logits and identity latent correlation. Manual caches and report/figure bundles are available.\nTwo-dimensional correlated MFRM Experimental Experimental.correlated(spec) then Experimental.fit; between-item fixed Q, at least two pure items per dimension and both dimensions observed for each person. Julia/CmdStan, manual caches and report/figure bundles are available.\nFixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM with an exchangeable rater prior Experimental Explicit Experimental.ExchangeablePrior with independent abilities or the admitted 2D correlated specification. Julia/CmdStan return Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit. The rater kernel SD is supplied, not estimated; this prior is not accepted by scalar MFRM, GMFRM or MGMFRM.\nScalar GMFRM: item discrimination × rater consistency Experimental Enter through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental; the documented structural restrictions remain mandatory.\nFixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM with the raw prior Experimental Experimental.fit with explicit GeneralizedPrior; one or more dimensions with an explicit fixed Q mask, estimated positive active loadings and rater consistency, and independent ability priors.\nFixed-Q MGMFRM with a normalized prior Experimental Experimental.fit with explicit NormalizedMGMFRMPrior, prior_model=:exchangeable or :source, all six scales and a source-rater ID when required. Independent abilities; Julia/CmdStan, manual caches and reports. This selects a different prior target, not a relabeling of a raw fit. Correlated fitting and automatic request caching are not connected for this prior.\nTwo-dimensional correlated MGMFRM Experimental Between-item Q, estimated positive loadings/consistency, Experimental.correlated(spec) and explicit GeneralizedPrior. Julia/CmdStan, prior/conditional posterior prediction, summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, manual caches and report/figure bundles are available. Scientific prior/domain acceptance remains open.\nBroader generalized discrimination structures Not supported No stable fitting claim is made.\nExploratory loading patterns or higher-dimensional correlation estimation Not supported for fitting MGMFRM retains a fixed Q pattern and independent abilities unless explicitly wrapped for the admitted 2D correlation model. Both correlated models above require exactly two dimensions.\nGroup and differential facet functioning effects Not supported for fitting Design validation may describe these terms, but estimation is not yet exposed.\nTestlet, response-cluster, and rater-halo effects Not supported for fitting Explicit identifiers, structural checks, and residual/predictive summaries are available. These report-only summaries provide no calibrated decision or mechanism classification.\n\nExperimental features may change in a minor release and should be used with sensitivity checks. They must not be described as stable equivalents of external software or as evidence for broader MGMFRM support. The namespace is an explicit experimental stability boundary, not a maturity claim. BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract() records its exact current configurations and constraints. The historical experimental = true keyword is a compatibility route and does not define the forward-looking API.\n\nFor their exact parameter meanings and runnable entry points, see exchangeable rater priors, normalized MGMFRM priors and the normalized fitting example. The two uses of \"exchangeable\" above are different prior types for different models. Neither estimates a population rater SD from the data.","title":"Current Support"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#common-operation-support","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"The candidate connects eight result types to common saved-result operations: MFRMFit, GMFRMFit, MGMFRMFit, Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit, Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit, MultidimensionalMFRMFit, Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit and Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit. An operation being available does not establish that every model is appropriate for the same research question. Unsupported combinations remain unsupported.\n\nOperation Available route Boundary to check\nFit, diagnostics and numerical precision fit for scalar MFRM; Experimental.fit for the other routes; diagnostics and posterior_mcse Use the model's required prior/specification and backend restrictions. Inspect MCSE for the actual quantities and interval endpoints you report.\nCompare predictions of existing ratings Qualified compare_predictions using the same data and persistent rating IDs Descriptive WAIC/raw IS-LOO; optional approximate SE has declared unit assumptions. Hill smoothing is not reference-validated PSIS. No automatic winner.\nCompare held-out predictions prediction_refit_plan, separately fitted training models, prediction_fold_observations, prediction_kfold, compare_kfold_predictions Known facet levels only; complete split and failure accounting. Optional first-order MCMC error from saved scores; combined errors require explicit independent simulation streams. Data-sampling K-fold SE and an automatic resumable multi-model refit runner are not supplied.\nCompare one versus multiple dimensions under aligned response/prior conventions matched_dimension_refit_plans in the example, then the common refit/score route Experimental MGMFRM with item-owned steps and one active Q loading per item; joint predictive priors still differ. This is not a scalar-GMFRM equivalence or model-choice validation.\nEvaluate known-truth recovery recovery_quantities, recovery_evaluation, recovery_replications Truths must match model coordinates and constraints; independent datasets define replications. This does not generate data, perform SBC or select a collection design.\nReport categories, reliability and agreement fit_report(...; reporting=(...)) with category_report, posterior_reliability and rater_agreement Explicit observation IDs, selected reliability cohorts and response matching. Finite-cohort reliability is not conventional Strata; exact/adjacent agreement is not ICC or Krippendorff alpha.\nDescribe observed-score reliability BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha(data; item, level, ...); optional reporting.alpha One explicit item and nominal/ordinal measurement level. No fit required; singleton exclusions and undefined values remain visible. No CI or severity adjustment; a different observed-score ICC operation is listed below.\nDescribe observed-score ICC BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation(data; item, model, definition, unit, score_scale, ...); optional reporting.icc Complete crossed ratings on one item, explicit equal-gap scores and random/fixed, agreement/consistency, single/average choices. Point estimates only; no missing-data model, CI/SE or severity adjustment.\nSave and reconstruct results save_fit_cache / load_fit_cache, report bundles Use canonical records for fixed-Q reports/recovery. Legacy v1 refusal remains explicit. Manual caches do not imply automatic request caching or general cross-version Julia serialization.\nReport conditional ability precision and classification risk BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification and opt-in common fit_report section Explicit people in one dimension, cutoff and probability rule; joint posterior risk and numerical MCSE. No automatic standard, multidimensional composite, ranking or known-truth error-rate guarantee.\nReport rank uncertainty within a selected cohort BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking and optional common report section One fitted ability dimension, explicit direction, joint-draw midranks and optional top-k tie bounds. No automatic selection, multidimensional aggregate, simultaneous ordering or population rank guarantee.\nExport figures and facet diagnostics Model-specific documented plotting and diagnostic routes Common reporting does not extend every scalar diagnostic or plot. Wright/Pathway plots and assignment-adjusted DFF screening retain their documented scalar-MFRM scope; fitted DFF is not supplied.\n\nStart with the saved comparison and recovery workflow and integrated reporting example. The combined saved workflow accepts all eight types in a common row order and can include explicit per-model category, agreement and reliability requests before saving/reopening reports. Use all original retained chains/draws when required by those operations; warnings and unavailable results are part of the output. A complete report means its requested sections executed, not that inference or model selection was accepted.","title":"Common operations and model-specific limits"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#current-scientific-evidence","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"The current mathematical and computational checks, recovery studies and user workflow examples answer different questions. Their results must not be pooled into one \"validated model\" label. In particular, the completed normalized-prior recovery assessment concerns a fixed pure-Q, independent two-dimensional design with 50 persons, five items, five raters and four categories. It used 32 independent paired blocks across two truth scenarios and three loading-prior widths. The 192 fits are not 192 independent replications of each condition. That study does not validate all eight result types, arbitrary Q or correlated/3D models.\n\nQuestion Current evidence and permitted interpretation Remaining boundary\nDoes the implementation evaluate the declared model? Matched Julia/Stan density and gradient checks support the checked targets and coordinates. Finite checks are not a general identification theorem or a posterior-precision guarantee.\nDid numerical estimation work in the recovery assessment? 184 of 192 fits met that assessment's numerical qualification; eight retained diagnostic failures. Successful diagnostics do not establish recovery or calibration. Other designs/backends have their own evidence.\nAre the parameter intervals calibrated? Recovery and failure summaries are available for the declared conditions. In the R1 stress condition, I1's 90% interval covered its true loading in only 1 of 32 blocks at log-loading prior SD 0.25, despite all 32 fits qualifying numerically. General 90% coverage and joint-prior SBC calibration are not established. The wider priors are not automatically accepted alternatives.\nHow well are unused ratings predicted? The scoring path and pilot are checked; the fresh main assessment is still incomplete. Final predictive means/contrasts and their precision remain open. Existing-rating WAIC/LOO is a different target; new-level prediction needs separate evidence.\nWhich prior should be the default? Saved-fit sensitivity shows how named estimates and contrasts change across explicit priors. No scientific default follows solely from mixing, RMSE or predictive ranking. Inspect prior implications and the relevant inferential target.\nCan a researcher reproduce the workflow? Eight result types have a saved workflow and short actual-fit operability checks, including report/cache reconstruction. Short fits do not establish adequate inference. Final scope, independent scientific review, unfamiliar-user reproduction and release acceptance remain open.\n\nSee the saved sensitivity illustration for the observed prior dependence and the results guide for interpreting comparisons. Research use should state the actual model, prior, prediction target, diagnostics and unresolved limitations. An experimental API is not a recommendation for every study that can supply its inputs.","title":"What the current evidence permits"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#What-Changes-When-You-Choose-MGMFRM","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"Suppose several raters score responses on criteria intended to measure two abilities. The analysis must separate ability differences from item difficulty and rater severity. A fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM assigns each active item-dimension link a coefficient of one. MGMFRM additionally estimates how strongly each active dimension affects an item's ratings and how sharply each rater's category probabilities concentrate. Choose between these models according to that question and the rating design; the additional parameters are assumptions to investigate, not an automatic improvement.\n\nFor the independent fixed-Q MGMFRM:\n\nComponent Estimated or specified? Interpretation\nQ pattern and active loadings The zero/nonzero pattern is supplied; each active positive loading is estimated. Fixed Q does not mean fixed coefficients. A zero excludes a dimension from that item. Multiple active dimensions enter an additive weighted sum.\nAbilities and item difficulties Person abilities and item difficulties are estimated; the ability prior mean is zero and person_sd is supplied. The prior SD is not estimated population variability or an individual's posterior uncertainty. Separate ability/item locations depend on the declared priors.\nRater severity and consistency Both are estimated subject to zero-sum severity and product-one consistency constraints. Severity shifts the rating distribution. Consistency multiplies the category logits; larger values concentrate probability around the most favored category or tied categories, holding other parameters fixed. It is not a test-retest reliability coefficient or a measure of accuracy against an external standard.\nCategory steps Free item-specific steps are estimated; the baseline step is zero and the last nonbaseline step is reconstructed to make their sum zero. Category use can differ by item. The steps for an item are shared across raters and dimensions.\nDependence between dimensions Ability prior correlation is fixed to identity. This is a population-prior assumption, not a finding that posterior estimates or observed scores are independent. Estimating correlation requires the separate admitted two-dimensional correlated model.\n\nThe response multiplier is fixed at 1.7 in MGMFRM; fixed-coefficient MFRM uses unit logits. Raw ability or difficulty estimates from the two models therefore cannot be compared as though the scale and prior were unchanged. The equations and experimental guide give the parameter and prior conventions. In particular, the compatibility value discrimination = :none does not disable the estimated loadings in an MGMFRM specification.\n\nRead constraints together with priors. With more than two raters, equal SDs on free coordinates in the raw GeneralizedPrior do not give every constrained rater the same marginal prior. The last sorted rater is reconstructed from the others; changing which rater occupies that position can change the prior and posterior. Likewise, finite posterior intervals for separate ability and item locations do not establish likelihood identification. The location explanation shows which joint shift leaves rating probabilities unchanged and how to inspect its mixing without changing the model. The explicitly normalized exchangeable prior removes the raw rater-label asymmetry by changing the prior target; it does not by itself establish likelihood identification or calibrated inference.","title":"What Changes When You Choose MGMFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Interpreting-Support-and-Versions","page":"Scope and Releases","text":"“Supported” describes the documented implementation boundary, not proof of convergence or validity for a new dataset. Experimental availability does not establish recovery, robustness, or external-software agreement. Known-truth simulation and protocol helpers have their own research API reference; their presence does not change the fitting boundary above.\n\nThe registered release remains the default installation. Development versions may contain unreleased behavior and should be pinned to an explicit revision when used in reproducible work.\n\nRead the manual and examples for the installed revision. Development documentation can describe features absent from an earlier registered release.\n\nFor reporting scope and current limitations of bias, separation/reliability, Strata and score agreement, see the MFRM reporting guide. In particular, the historical posterior separation screen is not a validated FACETS or EAP reliability estimator; it should not be promoted by a Strata transformation.","title":"Interpreting Support and Versions"},{"category":"section","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#Workflow-and-Diagnostics-API","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThe Saved-fit Reporting API groups the common reliability, agreement and category reports.\n\nFor owner-aware category summaries across the eight common saved-fit types, use BayesianMGMFRM.category_report. Its expected-use posterior intervals differ from the replicated-use intervals below. Optional profiles specify the item, rater, plotted dimension and every fixed other ability. Request it through fit_report(...; reporting=(; category=(...), ...)) to add the distinct category_report section; the historical section below keeps its original interpretation.\n\nFor a fitted minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM model, category-use and pairwise rater diagnostics can be requested without changing the fitted data or model:\n\ncategories = category_functioning_summary(\n    fit_result;\n    ndraws = 200,\n    min_count = 5,\n)\n\nraters = rater_homogeneity_summary(\n    fit_result;\n    ndraws = 200,\n    severity_rope = 0.10, # use a substantively declared margin\n)\n\ncategories.usage_rows and categories.threshold_rows retain predictive and step-order review flags, but never collapse categories automatically. raters.contrast_rows computes paired severity differences; a positive severity_a - severity_b means rater A is more severe. Coordinate rows identify the default fixed reference, declared hard anchors, and estimated severities. A two-fixed-coordinate contrast is an exact constant with zero uncertainty draws, a not-applicable interval type and interval probabilities, and deterministic sign/ROPE probabilities. A one-fixed-coordinate contrast has posterior uncertainty only from the estimated side. A ROPE is not assumed unless the analysis supplies one.\n\nfit_report includes both summaries by default for stable MFRM fits: category_functioning.usage_rows, category_functioning.threshold_rows, and rater_homogeneity.contrast_rows are available in structured, public, Markdown, JSON, and table outputs. Category review flags become one aggregate report warning and never cause automatic recoding. Set include_category_functioning = false or include_rater_homogeneity = false for an independent opt-out. Generalized fits leave both stable-only report sections unrequested by default and return unsupported if either is requested explicitly. A one-rater stable fit returns zero contrast rows with contrast_availability = :not_applicable_single_rater; the empty pair set is not labelled connected evidence of homogeneity. Empty row fields survive public JSON, table, and bundle round trips. Markdown omits empty previews unless include_empty = true is requested.\n\nFor PCM reports using an explicitly declared category scale, unobserved endpoints remain present as skipped category-use rows and do not reduce the item-specific threshold-row universe.","title":"Workflow and Diagnostics API"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; view = :full, split_chains = true,\n            rhat_threshold = 1.01, ess_threshold = 400)\n\nReturn a single diagnostic surface for the current minimal Bayesian fitting path. The result includes chain-level sampler rows from sampler_diagnostics, parameter-level rows from mcmc_diagnostics, block-level rows from parameter_block_diagnostics, and a compact machine-readable summary with rank-normalized R-hat, bulk ESS, tail ESS, compatibility metrics, and pass/fail counts. For the AdvancedHMC-backed NUTS path, the summary also reports divergent-transition counts, max-tree-depth hits, and the minimum available E-BFMI estimate across chains. The fields n_e_bfmi_expected, n_e_bfmi_available, n_e_bfmi_unavailable, and e_bfmi_complete distinguish that compatibility minimum from an all-chain E-BFMI gate. A chain is unavailable when any retained energy value is missing or non-finite.\n\nGeneralized diagnostic surfaces also include direct constrained rows. Coordinates fixed by a declared zero-dimensional raw transform remain visible with diagnostic_status = :structurally_fixed and quality_gate_applicable = false; they do not enter convergence extrema or failure counts. Constrained coordinates reconstructed from estimable raw coordinates remain quality-gate applicable.\n\nThe surrounding version-1 diagnostic payload is discriminated by its nested diagnostic_contract. Rows without :rank_normalized_rhat_bulk_tail_ess_v1 are pre-modern compatibility records and must not be relabeled as modern evidence. Under the modern contract, flag aliases rank_normalized_flag; use classical_compatibility_flag when the previous classical result is specifically required.\n\nThe compatibility default view = :full returns the complete diagnostic surface. view = :public keeps reader-facing diagnostic results while carrying explicit family and stability fields in a compact portable schema.\n\n\n\n\n\ndiagnostics(fit::MGMFRMFit; include_location = false, view = :full, ...)\n\nReturn the saved parameter and sampler assessment. With include_location = true, also compute location_rows and location_summary for fitted-person ability means, item difficulties, loading-weighted ability means and their differences. These equally weighted finite-panel quantities distinguish movement in a common location from movement in the response-relevant differences. Active loadings are used at every draw, including within-item Q structures. They are not population means, a new centering constraint, or a replacement for the original summary. Both views retain these optional diagnostics; default output is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\ndiagnostics(fit::MultidimensionalMFRMFit; view = :full, ...)\n\nReturn parameter, sampler and warmup diagnostics using the saved fit's settings. For between-item fixed-coefficient MFRM (including correlated and exchangeable rater models), location_rows adds rank-normalized R-hat and bulk/tail ESS for each dimension's finite-panel ability mean, item mean and their difference. location_summary summarizes these additional checks; summary retains the parameter/sampler assessment. The means equally weight fitted facet levels and are not population-mean parameters. Within-item or mixed Q returns location_status = :unsupported with an explanation. No sampling is performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.sampler_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"sampler_diagnostics(fit; phase = :retained)\n\nReturn chain-level sampler diagnostics for a fitted minimal MFRM object. Rows include retained draw counts, warmup, step size, chain acceptance rate, finite log-posterior counts, and log-posterior summaries. This complements mcmc_diagnostics, which reports parameter-level R-hat and ESS. When the AdvancedHMC/NUTS backend is used, rows also include divergent-transition, tree-depth, step-size, and E-BFMI fields where available. For random-walk fits, n_nonfinite_proposals counts rejected non-finite proposals among retained iterations; these are not NUTS divergences. The count is missing for older fits without proposal telemetry and for other samplers.\n\nUse phase = :warmup for separate chain-level warmup counts from new fits. AdvancedHMC, Turing and CmdStan report divergences, maximum-depth hits and nonfinite log densities. Generalized GMFRM/MGMFRM fits support AdvancedHMC and CmdStan. Random walk reports n_nonfinite_proposals during burn-in; it does not adapt its proposal scale. Its NUTS event fields are missing because they do not apply. All rows report expected/observed iterations. coverage is :recorded, :not_run (zero warmup), or :not_recorded (history unavailable); unavailable counts are missing. Warmup events do not enter posterior summaries, R-hat/ESS or retained-draw warning flags. No warmup pass/fail rule is applied. Older Turing fits without an explicit sampler_controls.nadapts record have unavailable history even when their requested warmup was zero: that request did not reliably disable adaptation. These summaries survive save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache; warmup parameter draws are not retained. The default phase = :retained preserves the existing diagnostic rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.sampler_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.mcmc_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"mcmc_diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; split_chains = true,\n                 rhat_threshold = 1.01, ess_threshold = 400)\n\nReturn parameter-level convergence diagnostics for the current fitted draws. Rows include rank-normalized split R-hat, its bulk and folded components, bulk ESS, and tail ESS at the joint 10% tail probability. The compatibility fields rhat and ess retain the previous classical split R-hat and autocorrelation ESS values. The primary flag and rank_normalized_flag apply only the modern contract; classical_compatibility_flag records the previous diagnostic result without adding an undeclared quality gate.\n\nThese diagnostics require at least two independent chains. Rank-normalized ESS requires more than four draws per diagnostic chain after optional splitting; shorter runs return flag = :insufficient_draws. Non-finite or degenerate draws are never passed through as apparently usable diagnostics. Use sampler_diagnostics for backend-specific fields such as divergent transitions and tree depth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mcmc_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_block_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"parameter_block_diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; split_chains = true,\n                            rhat_threshold = 1.01,\n                            ess_threshold = 400)\n\nSummarize MCMC convergence diagnostics by identified parameter block. Rows aggregate the parameter-level R-hat and ESS diagnostics for blocks such as :person, :rater, :item, and :thresholds, while retaining block parameter names and machine-readable pass/fail counts. Empty blocks are returned with flag = :empty_block so reports can distinguish absent design terms from failed diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.parameter_block_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_mcse","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"posterior_mcse(fit; probabilities = (0.025, 0.5, 0.975),\n               parameter_space = :auto)\nposterior_mcse(draws; chains, parameter_names,\n               probabilities = (0.025, 0.5, 0.975),\n               parameter_space = :user_defined_estimand)\n\nEstimate Monte Carlo standard errors for posterior means, standard deviations, and requested quantiles. Fit-based calls use identified parameters for scalar MFRM fits and direct constrained parameters for generalized and fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM fits by default; pass parameter_space = :raw_unconstrained to inspect generalized sampling coordinates or multidimensional MFRM free coordinates.\n\nFor fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM (independent or correlated, with either rater prior), the default reconstructs the last rater and item steps, retains fixed coefficients, and reports rho rather than Fisher z. Direct rows include block, dimension label, fixed/derived status and their actual parameter_space: :unit_logit, :dimensionless or :correlation. Free-coordinate rows use :unit_logit and, for correlated fits, :fisher_z. These labels describe units, not likelihood identification. Saved records and chain/iteration ordering are validated before computing MCSE.\n\nThe matrix method supports derived estimands: arrange rows in contiguous chain blocks, transform each draw to the quantities of interest, and supply one name per column. MCSE measures simulation precision only. Every non-fixed row keeps convergence_review_required = true, and no universal precision threshold or scientific decision is applied. Calls with fewer than two chains or fewer than 10 retained draws per chain return typed unavailable rows instead of presenting short-chain MCSE as usable evidence. Numerically undefined standard-deviation MCSE is reported as missing with :mcse_unavailable; separately available mean and quantile MCSE are retained.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_mcse"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"posterior_summary(fit; lower = 0.025, upper = 0.975,\n    intervals = (0.66, 0.9, 0.95), reference = 0.0,\n    rope = nothing, rope_probability_threshold = 0.95)\n\nSummarize posterior draws for each parameter. Rows retain the legacy lower/upper interval columns and also include central credible intervals, probability of direction relative to reference, and optional ROPE/practical equivalence probabilities. Pass rope = r for a symmetric [-r, r] ROPE or rope = (lower, upper) for an explicit interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.pointwise_loglikelihood","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"pointwise_loglikelihood(design::FacetDesign, params)\n\nEvaluate the minimal additive RSM/PCM pointwise log likelihood for the identified parameter vector returned by getdesign. This helper is for design validation and does not apply Bayesian priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.pointwise_loglikelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.pointwise_loglikelihood_matrix","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"pointwise_loglikelihood_matrix(fit::Union{MFRMFit,GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit})\npointwise_loglikelihood_matrix(design::FacetDesign, draws;\n    parameter_space = :direct)\n\nEvaluate a draws-by-observations pointwise log-likelihood matrix for posterior summaries, posterior predictive checks, and future model-comparison helpers. For specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM preview designs, pass parameter_space = :raw when draws are raw unconstrained candidate coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.pointwise_loglikelihood_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.waic","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"waic(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nwaic(design::FacetDesign, draws)\nwaic(loglik::AbstractMatrix)\n\nCompute the widely applicable information criterion (WAIC) from posterior pointwise log-likelihood draws. The log-likelihood matrix must have dimensions draws-by-observations. The returned named tuple includes elpd_waic, p_waic, lppd, waic, standard errors, and pointwise components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.waic"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.waic_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"waic_diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; threshold = 0.4, only_flagged = false,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\nwaic_diagnostics(design::FacetDesign, draws; threshold = 0.4,\n    only_flagged = false, parameter_space = :direct)\nwaic_diagnostics(loglik::AbstractMatrix; threshold = 0.4,\n    only_flagged = false)\n\nReturn observation-level WAIC diagnostics. Rows include pointwise lppd, p_waic, elpd_waic, WAIC contribution, and a flag for observations whose p_waic exceeds threshold. When a FacetDesign or fit object is supplied, rows also include person, rater, item, score, category, and optional facet labels. Use parameter_space = :raw for raw GMFRM/MGMFRM preview-design draws. Use this helper to locate observations behind a WAIC :high_loglik_variance warning before interpreting model-comparison rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.waic_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.loo","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"loo(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), pareto_k_threshold = 0.7,\n    tail_fraction = 0.2, min_tail_draws = 5)\nloo(design::FacetDesign, draws; kwargs...)\nloo(loglik::AbstractMatrix; kwargs...)\n\nCompute a raw importance-sampling leave-one-out (LOO) estimate from posterior pointwise log-likelihood draws. The log-likelihood matrix must have dimensions draws-by-observations. The returned named tuple includes elpd_loo, p_loo, lppd, looic, standard errors, raw-importance effective sample sizes, and a Hill-estimated Pareto-k diagnostic for each observation.\n\nThis helper does not perform PSIS smoothing. Treat rows with pareto_k above pareto_k_threshold as unstable and prefer exact LOO, K-fold cross-validation, or model-specific follow-up before making strong model-comparison claims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loo"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.psis_loo","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"psis_loo(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), pareto_k_threshold = 0.7,\n    tail_fraction = 0.2, min_tail_draws = 5)\npsis_loo(design::FacetDesign, draws; kwargs...)\npsis_loo(loglik::AbstractMatrix; kwargs...)\n\nCompute a Pareto-smoothed importance-sampling leave-one-out (PSIS-LOO) estimate from posterior pointwise log-likelihood draws. The log-likelihood matrix must have dimensions draws-by-observations. The returned named tuple uses the same LOO schema as loo, with method = :pareto_smoothed_importance_sampling and psis_smoothing = true.\n\nThe smoother replaces the largest raw importance ratios with Pareto expected order statistics using the package's Hill-estimated tail shape, then applies the usual finite-draw importance-ratio truncation. Observations whose pareto_k exceeds pareto_k_threshold should still be treated as unstable and followed by exact LOO, K-fold cross-validation, or model-specific review before strong comparison claims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.psis_loo"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.loo_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"loo_diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; threshold = 0.7, only_flagged = false,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng(),\n    tail_fraction = 0.2, min_tail_draws = 5, psis_smoothing = false)\nloo_diagnostics(design::FacetDesign, draws; threshold = 0.7,\n    only_flagged = false, tail_fraction = 0.2, min_tail_draws = 5,\n    psis_smoothing = false, parameter_space = :direct)\nloo_diagnostics(loglik::AbstractMatrix; threshold = 0.7,\n    only_flagged = false, tail_fraction = 0.2, min_tail_draws = 5,\n    psis_smoothing = false)\n\nReturn observation-level LOO diagnostics. Rows include pointwise lppd, p_loo, elpd_loo, LOOIC contribution, importance-sampling effective sample size, a Hill-estimated pareto_k, and a flag for observations whose pareto_k exceeds threshold. When a FacetDesign or fit object is supplied, rows also include person, rater, item, score, category, and optional facet labels. Use parameter_space = :raw for raw GMFRM/MGMFRM preview-design draws. Set psis_smoothing = true to report diagnostics from psis_loo instead of raw importance-sampling loo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loo_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"loo_refit(data::FacetData, plan = loo_refit_plan(data);\n    thresholds = :partial_credit, prior = MFRMPrior(), kwargs...)\nloo_refit(spec::FacetSpec, plan = loo_refit_plan(spec);\n    prior = model default, experimental = false, return_fits = false, kwargs...)\nloo_refit(design::FacetDesign, plan = loo_refit_plan(design); kwargs...)\nloo_refit(fit::Union{MFRMFit,GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit},\n    plan = loo_refit_plan(fit.design); kwargs...)\n\nExecute exact leave-one-observation-out refits for a loo_refit_plan under the current fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM likelihood, or under the guarded experimental GMFRM/MGMFRM likelihood when experimental = true is supplied. Each plan row is refit on the complementary training observations, scored on its single heldout observation, and summarized with the same heldout log-score contract as kfold.\n\nBefore fitting, all plan rows must have disjoint heldout/training sets with training equal to the full heldout complement. Each heldout row occurs at most once; fold IDs, counts and scored-row labels must agree with the plan rows. Subset LOO follow-up and an empty no-flag plan remain supported. Training specs retain the declared dimension labels and item-specific Q rows. Plans are also checked with kfold_plan_diagnostics; rows with heldout-only person, rater, item, or score-category levels are rejected because the heldout observation cannot be scored against the training-fold parameter map. Optional facets are metadata in these supported likelihoods; their heldout-only labels are preserved without adding fitted effects. The general kfold_plan_diagnostics default still audits all facets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit_comparison","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"loo_refit_comparison(models::Pair...; plan = nothing, stat = nothing,\n    observations = nothing, threshold = nothing, only_flagged = true,\n    fold_ids = nothing, prior = nothing, return_refits = false,\n    return_fits = false, axis = :model, values = nothing, baseline = nothing,\n    kwargs...)\nloo_refit_comparison(model1, model2, ...; names = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nExecute exact leave-one-observation-out refits for multiple fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM candidates under a shared loo_refit_plan, then return K-fold-compatible comparison and sensitivity rows for the heldout exact-refit log scores. Pass an explicit plan, select observations, or pass a raw LOO stat to construct the shared follow-up plan from the first model. If a flagged-row plan contains no refits, comparison rows are empty and the returned warning is :no_refits_required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit_plan","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"loo_refit_plan(data::FacetData; observations = nothing, fold_ids = nothing)\nloo_refit_plan(data::FacetData, stat; threshold = nothing,\n    only_flagged = true, fold_ids = nothing)\nloo_refit_plan(spec::FacetSpec; kwargs...)\nloo_refit_plan(design::FacetDesign; kwargs...)\n\nConstruct a deterministic leave-one-observation-out refit plan. Each returned fold row holds out exactly one observation and lists the complementary training observations. Pass observations to plan exact-refit follow-up for a subset, such as flagged raw-importance LOO rows. When a raw LOO summary is supplied, only_flagged = true selects observations whose Pareto-k exceeds threshold; by default the threshold is read from the summary. This helper builds the refit plan only. It does not refit models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loo_refit_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold(fold_logliks; fold_ids = nothing, observation_indices = nothing)\nkfold(loglik::AbstractMatrix; fold_ids = nothing, observation_indices = nothing)\n\nSummarize heldout K-fold log predictive density from fold-specific refits. Each fold log-likelihood matrix must be draws-by-heldout-observations and contain posterior pointwise log-likelihood draws for observations that were not used to fit that fold. The returned named tuple includes elpd_kfold, kfoldic, standard errors, fold sizes, observation identifiers, and pointwise heldout components.\n\nThis helper does not refit models. Use kfold_plan to construct observation or grouped heldout folds, then use kfold to record exact heldout-refit or K-fold follow-up evidence after fitting each training fold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_diagnostics(stat)\nkfold_diagnostics(data::FacetData, stat)\nkfold_diagnostics(spec::FacetSpec, stat)\nkfold_diagnostics(design::FacetDesign, stat)\n\nReturn observation-level heldout K-fold rows from a kfold result. Rows include heldout index, observation identifier, fold, heldout ELPD, K-fold information-criterion contribution, method, prediction target, and flag. When FacetData, FacetSpec, or FacetDesign is supplied, observation identifiers must be valid data row indices and rows also include person, rater, item, score, category, and optional facet labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_plan","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_plan(data::FacetData; k, group_by = nothing, shuffle = false,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), fold_ids = nothing)\nkfold_plan(spec::FacetSpec; kwargs...)\nkfold_plan(design::FacetDesign; kwargs...)\n\nConstruct deterministic heldout-fold assignments for later K-fold refits. By default observations are assigned directly to balanced folds. With group_by = :person, :rater, :item, :category, or an optional facet such as :group, all observations for each level are kept in the same heldout fold.\n\nThe returned named tuple includes fold_rows, folds, and heldout_observation_indices; pass the latter two to kfold after fitting each training fold and collecting fold-specific heldout log-likelihood matrices. This helper builds the fold plan only. It does not refit models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_plan_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_plan_diagnostics(data::FacetData, plan; facets = :all)\nkfold_plan_diagnostics(spec::FacetSpec, plan; kwargs...)\nkfold_plan_diagnostics(design::FacetDesign, plan; kwargs...)\n\nCheck whether each training fold in a kfold_plan retains the levels needed to score its heldout observations. Rows are reported by fold and facet for person, rater, item, score category, and optional facets by default. A row with refit_blocker = true has heldout-only levels for that facet, so an external refit using only that training fold would need a fixed level map, pooled treatment, or a different fold assignment before heldout scoring. This is a level-availability audit, not a partition-integrity or source-data identity certificate. Automatic kfold_refit and loo_refit additionally check the complete partition and scoring metadata before starting any fits. Plan row indices remain positional and must refer to the supplied source data/order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_plan_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_refit","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_refit(data::FacetData, plan; thresholds = :partial_credit,\n    prior = MFRMPrior(), return_fits = false, kwargs...)\nkfold_refit(data::FacetData; k, group_by = nothing, shuffle = false,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), fold_ids = nothing, thresholds = :partial_credit,\n    kwargs...)\nkfold_refit(spec::FacetSpec, plan; prior = model default,\n    experimental = false, return_fits = false, kwargs...)\nkfold_refit(spec::FacetSpec; k, group_by = nothing, shuffle = false,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), fold_ids = nothing, kwargs...)\nkfold_refit(design::FacetDesign, plan; kwargs...)\nkfold_refit(fit::Union{MFRMFit,GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit}, plan; kwargs...)\n\nExecute heldout K-fold refits for a kfold_plan under the current fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM likelihood, or under the guarded experimental GMFRM/MGMFRM likelihood when experimental = true is supplied. Each fold is refit on its training observations, scored on all heldout observations in that fold, and summarized with the same heldout log-score contract as kfold.\n\nAll folds are validated before the first fit. Each training set must be the full heldout complement; K-fold holds out every observation exactly once, with at least two nonempty folds. Declared grouping units cannot span training and heldout. Fold IDs, counts, observation-to-fold maps and scored-row labels must agree with the fold rows, including their heldout order. Training specs retain dimension labels and remap Q by item label. Plans are also checked with kfold_plan_diagnostics; rows with heldout-only person, rater, item, or score-category levels are rejected because the heldout observations cannot be scored against the training-fold parameter map. Optional facets are metadata in these supported likelihoods, so group_by = :response_id or :occasion can keep whole rating events together without rejecting their heldout-only labels. Those labels are preserved for reporting and add no fitted effects. The general kfold_plan_diagnostics default still audits all facets. Scores remain pointwise marginal log scores; grouping the split does not turn their sum into a joint predictive score for a whole event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_refit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_refit_comparison","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_refit_comparison(models::Pair...; plan = nothing, k = nothing,\n    group_by = nothing, shuffle = false, rng = Random.default_rng(),\n    fold_ids = nothing, prior = nothing, return_refits = false,\n    return_fits = false, axis = :model, values = nothing, baseline = nothing,\n    kwargs...)\nkfold_refit_comparison(model1, model2, ...; names = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nExecute automatic heldout K-fold refits for multiple fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM candidates under a shared kfold_plan, then return both compare_kfold rows and baseline-relative kfold_sensitivity_comparison rows. Labels are supplied by pair keys or by names; unlabeled positional models use model_1, model_2, .... All models must use the same observation data and row order. Pass an explicit plan, or pass k plus the usual fold-planning keywords to build a shared plan from the first model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_refit_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"compare_kfold(stats...; names = nothing)\ncompare_kfold(models::Pair...)\n\nCompare heldout K-fold summaries returned by kfold. Rows are sorted by elpd_kfold in descending order and include differences relative to the best model, K-fold information-criterion differences, normalized expected-log-predictive-density weights, and a comparison contract requiring the same heldout observation order and fold assignment order across models.\n\nThis helper compares already computed K-fold summaries. It does not construct folds or refit models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.compare_models","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"compare_models(fits...; names = nothing, criterion = :waic,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\ncompare_models(models::Pair...; criterion = :waic, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nCompare fitted models with WAIC (criterion = :waic), raw importance-sampling LOO (criterion = :loo), or PSIS-smoothed LOO (criterion = :psis_loo). MFRM, scalar GMFRM, and experimental MGMFRM fit objects can be compared when they share the same observation data, row order, ordinal category levels, latent dimensionality, and fixed Q-matrix contract. PSIS-smoothed comparison rows keep criterion = :loo and are identified by method = :pareto_smoothed_importance_sampling and psis_smoothing = true. Rows are sorted by expected log predictive density in descending order. elpd_difference is relative to the best model and is therefore zero for the top row and non-positive for lower-ranked rows. relative_weight is a normalized Akaike-style weight computed from the selected expected log predictive density values. Each row carries the model family, thresholds, discrimination mode, dimensionality, Q-matrix, and data signature used by the compatibility check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.compare_models"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.sensitivity_comparison","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"sensitivity_comparison(fits...; names = nothing, axis = :model,\n    values = nothing, baseline = nothing, criterion = :waic,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\nsensitivity_comparison(models::Pair...; axis = :model, values = nothing,\n    baseline = nothing, criterion = :waic, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nReturn report-ready sensitivity comparison rows for already fitted candidate models. The function uses the same WAIC, raw-importance LOO, or PSIS-smoothed LOO scoring rows as compare_models, while applying sensitivity-specific compatibility checks. Additional columns identify the requested sensitivity_axis, the per-model sensitivity_value, the baseline model and value, and expected-log-predictive-density and information-criterion differences relative to that baseline.\n\nWhen values is omitted, values are inferred for common axes: :model, :model_family, :thresholds, :discrimination, :dimensions, :q_matrix, :estimation_status, :prior, :backend, and :sampler. Pass a vector, tuple, dictionary, or named tuple to values for custom axes such as :anchor, :dff, or externally defined prior regimes. baseline names the baseline model label and defaults to the first supplied model. Direct compare_models output still requires matching dimensions and Q-matrices; declared sensitivity_comparison rows allow dimensionality differences for axis = :dimensions, and fixed-Q differences for axis = :q_matrix or :dimensions, while retaining same-data and same-category checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.sensitivity_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.sensitivity_comparison_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"sensitivity_comparison_summary(rows; required_axes =\n    (:thresholds, :discrimination, :rater_pooling, :dff, :anchor,\n     :dimensions, :prior_regime), view = :full)\nsensitivity_comparison_summary(row, rows...; required_axes = ...)\n\nSummarize report-ready sensitivity comparison rows and check whether the declared sensitivity table covers the package's default critical-review axes: threshold family, discrimination on/off, pooled versus unpooled rater effects, DFF on/off, anchor choice, dimensionality, and prior regime. Rows are expected to come from sensitivity_comparison or an equivalent same-schema external comparison table.\n\nThe summary records observed axes, missing required axes, per-axis model and baseline coverage, criteria used, and whether every required axis has both a baseline and at least one candidate row. It checks declared comparison rows; it does not create refits, fit unsupported generalized/DFF/anchor models, or replace predeclared simulation and case-study protocols. view = :public returns the compact reader-facing projection; :full preserves the existing contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.sensitivity_comparison_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_sensitivity_comparison","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"kfold_sensitivity_comparison(stats...; names = nothing, axis = :model,\n    values = nothing, baseline = nothing)\nkfold_sensitivity_comparison(models::Pair...; axis = :model,\n    values = nothing, baseline = nothing)\n\nReturn report-ready sensitivity rows for supplied heldout K-fold summaries. This helper uses compare_kfold compatibility checks, then adds the declared sensitivity axis, per-model values, baseline label, baseline value, and baseline-relative heldout ELPD and K-fold information-criterion differences. It does not construct folds or refit models. For axes other than :model, pass explicit values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.kfold_sensitivity_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_likelihood_sensitivity","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"prior_likelihood_sensitivity(fit; prior_powers = (0.8, 1.0, 1.2),\n    likelihood_powers = (0.8, 1.0, 1.2), ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng(),\n    min_effective_sample_size = nothing)\nprior_likelihood_sensitivity(logprior_draws, loglikelihood_draws; kwargs...)\n\nCompute a local prior/likelihood power-scaling sensitivity grid by self-normalized importance reweighting of posterior draws. For each prior_power and likelihood_power cell, the helper estimates the shift in mean log prior and mean log likelihood relative to the baseline posterior (prior_power = likelihood_power = 1) and reports effective sample size diagnostics for the reweighted draws.\n\nThis helper does not refit models and does not replace predeclared simulation, exact LOO, or K-fold sensitivity work. Treat cells with :low_effective_sample_size as local-reweighting failures that need refits or a narrower sensitivity claim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_likelihood_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_predict","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"posterior_predict(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nGenerate replicated score matrices from posterior draws. The returned matrix has one replicated dataset per row and one rating observation per column, with entries on the original integer score scale.\n\n\n\n\n\nposterior_predict(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; kwargs...)\n\nSimulate scores for the existing rating rows from joint posterior draws, using the draw-selection controls of predictive_probabilities. Entries use the saved integer category labels; rows are replicated datasets. Use a local seeded RNG to reproduce both draw selection and score simulation after reloading a fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_predict"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_predictive_check","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"posterior_predictive_check(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nGenerate posterior predictive replicated scores and compact observed-vs- replicated summaries for the minimal MFRM design. The summary currently covers overall mean score, category proportions, person-level mean scores, rater-level mean scores, item-level mean scores, optional facet mean scores, and grouped DFF-cell and observed sparse-design-block mean-score rows for predictive_check_summary(...; include_grouped = true).\n\n\n\n\n\nposterior_predictive_check(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit; kwargs...)\n\nCompare observed and replicated score summaries for the existing rating design. Uses the selection and RNG controls of predictive_probabilities and returns resolved draw indices, model/prior identity and stored sampling-quality status. predictive_check_summary(check; include_grouped = true) includes observed design-block summaries. Same-data agreement does not establish convergence, parameter recovery or predictive performance for new facet levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_predictive_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predict","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"prior_predict(spec_or_design; prior = MFRMPrior(), ndraws = 1000,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nGenerate replicated score matrices from prior draws for the minimal MFRM design. The returned matrix has one replicated dataset per row and one rating observation per column, with entries on the original integer score scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predict"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predictive_check","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"prior_predictive_check(spec_or_design; prior = MFRMPrior(), ndraws = 1000,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), min_category_probability = 0.01,\n    prior_warning_probability = 0.95, wide_facet_range_fraction = 0.8)\n\nGenerate prior predictive replicated scores and compact observed-vs-replicated summaries for the minimal MFRM design. The returned object includes the prior parameter draws used to generate replicated_scores and prior-implication diagnostics for category use and facet-score ranges. It also includes grouped DFF-cell and observed sparse-design-block mean-score summaries for predictive_check_summary(...; include_grouped = true).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.prior_predictive_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_check_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_check_summary(check; interval = 0.9, include_grouped = false)\n\nSummarize a prior_predictive_check or posterior_predictive_check result as rows with observed values, replicated means, replicated intervals, and tail probabilities. The current summary covers overall mean score, category proportions, person-level mean scores, rater-level mean scores, item-level mean scores, and optional facet mean scores. Set include_grouped = true to append DFF-cell and observed sparse-design-block mean-score rows from checks generated by current prior_predictive_check or posterior_predictive_check objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_check_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_check_plot_data","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_check_plot_data(summary_rows)\n\nReturn plotting-ready rows from predictive_check_summary. Suggested mappings are y = observed, ymin/ymax = replicated_lower/replicated_upper, and replicated_mean as the model-implied center for each statistic/level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_check_plot_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_probabilities","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_probabilities(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\npredictive_probabilities(design::FacetDesign, draws)\n\nReturn observation-level category probabilities for posterior or supplied parameter draws. The returned array has dimensions draws-by-observations-by- categories, with categories ordered as fit.design.spec.data.category_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\npredictive_probabilities(fit::Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit;\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nCategory probabilities conditional on each retained joint draw for the existing rating rows. The array is draws by observations by categories, in the saved category order. With no selection all draws are used; ndraws samples with replacement, while draw_indices preserves the requested order and duplicates. Abilities already incorporate population correlation; it is not applied again. New persons, items and raters are outside this prediction target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_probabilities"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.expected_scores","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"expected_scores(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nexpected_scores(design::FacetDesign, draws)\n\nReturn observation-level expected scores for each posterior or supplied parameter draw. The returned matrix has dimensions draws-by-observations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.expected_scores"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_variances","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_variances(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\npredictive_variances(design::FacetDesign, draws)\n\nReturn observation-level conditional predictive score variances for each posterior or supplied parameter draw. The returned matrix has dimensions draws-by-observations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_variances"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_residuals","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_residuals(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\npredictive_residuals(design::FacetDesign, draws)\n\nReturn observation-level observed-minus-expected score residuals for each posterior or supplied parameter draw. The returned matrix has dimensions draws-by-observations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_residuals"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_standardized_residuals","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"predictive_standardized_residuals(\n    fit::MFRMFit;\n    ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(),\n    variance_tolerance = 1e-12)\npredictive_standardized_residuals(\n    design::FacetDesign,\n    draws;\n    variance_tolerance = 1e-12)\n\nReturn draw-specific Pearson score residuals for MFRM, GMFRM, or MGMFRM predictions. For observation n and parameter draw d, the reported value is (y[n] - E[y[n] | draw d]) / sqrt(Var[y[n] | draw d]). The result retains the draw-by-observation matrix in values, a same-shaped valid mask, draw indices, and exclusion counts. Predictive variances at or below variance_tolerance are excluded rather than clamped, preventing artificial extreme residuals. Non-finite predictive probabilities, expectations, variances, or standardized values raise ArgumentError; they are never counted as low-variance exclusions.\n\nThese residuals are a low-level diagnostic input. They do not by themselves establish local independence, a testlet effect, rater halo, or a universal Q3 cutoff. Use local_dependence_contract and testlet_design_check to inspect the planned matching and design requirements before forming pairwise dependence statistics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.predictive_standardized_residuals"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_contract","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"local_dependence_contract(; profile = :ld0_v1,\n    min_common_units = 20, min_eligible_draws = 100,\n    min_eligible_draw_fraction = 0.9,\n    pair_fdr_alpha = 0.05, global_fwer_alpha = 0.05,\n    variance_tolerance = 1e-12,\n    correlation_variance_tolerance = 1e-12)\n\nReturn the machine-readable LD0 diagnostic contract used to develop local- dependence summaries. The contract distinguishes single-rating item Q3, within-rater item Q3, and rater-pair estimands; fixes draw-specific support, duplicate rejection, adjusted-Q3 centering, predictive tail definitions, and pair weighting; and separates within-family FDR localization from one all-enabled-family maximum-statistic FWER decision. It deliberately provides no implicit aggregation over multiple raters or repeated responses.\n\nThe named :ld0_v1 numerical profile is frozen but provisional until calibrated by independent known-truth simulations. To explore other thresholds, pass profile = :custom_unvalidated; such a result is never decision eligible. This function does not compute Q3, declare local dependence, fit a testlet effect, or authorize a mechanism interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"local_dependence_summary(fit;\n    contract = local_dependence_contract(), interval = 0.95,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng(), max_pair_draw_cells = 2_000_000,\n    max_prediction_cells = 10_000_000,\n    max_common_unit_draw_cells = 2_000_000)\n\nReturn report-only residual-association summaries for a fitted MFRM, guarded GMFRM, or guarded MGMFRM model whose FacetData declares response_id and testlet_id. The summary keeps single-rating item pairs, within-rater item pairs, and rater pairs on the same response and criterion separate. It reports draw-specific Pearson-correlation summaries, item-family adjusted-Q3-style summaries, paired posterior predictive tail fractions, within-family BH adjustments, support graphs, and one all-family maximum-statistic reference.\n\nPosterior draws are distinct and, when ndraws is supplied, sampled without replacement. Observed and replicated residual statistics use the same parameter draw and one replicated dataset per draw. Sparse or undefined pairs with at least one common unit remain in the pair rows with structured support reasons and missing evidence values; they are not converted to zero association. Zero-overlap combinations remain visible through family counts and testlet-stratified support graphs. Before large allocations, the materialized candidate-pair row guard bounds candidate-pair rows, max_pair_draw_cells bounds positive-overlap pair-by-draw cells, and max_common_unit_draw_cells bounds both candidate-pair/common-unit links and the pair/common-unit-by-draw correlation work; max_prediction_cells bounds draw-by-observation-by-category cells. Reduce ndraws or prespecify separate strata when a guard is exceeded.\n\nThis function provides no universal Q3 cutoff, calibrated FDR/FWER decision, or testlet, halo, rater-by-task, multidimensional, or temporal mechanism label. Posterior predictive tail fractions and BH-adjusted values are descriptive calibration-pending references, not decision p-values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.local_dependence_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.calibration_table","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"calibration_table(fit::MFRMFit; target = :expected_score,\n    category = nothing, bins = 10, interval = 0.9, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\ncalibration_table(design::FacetDesign, draws; target = :expected_score,\n    category = nothing, bins = 10, interval = 0.9)\n\nReturn binned observed-vs-predicted calibration rows from posterior draws. The default target = :expected_score compares observed scores with posterior expected scores. With target = :category_probability, the table compares the observed proportion of category with the posterior probability of that category; when category is omitted, the highest score category is used. Use category = :all to return one category-probability calibration block for each ordinal score category. Use target = :all to return expected-score rows plus all ordinal category-probability rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.calibration_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.calibration_plot_data","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"calibration_plot_data(calibration_rows)\n\nReturn plotting-ready rows from calibration_table. Suggested mappings are x = predicted_mean, y = observed_mean, with reference = predicted_mean for the ideal calibration diagonal. The predicted interval fields can be used as horizontal uncertainty bars.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.calibration_plot_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.wright_map_data","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"wright_map_data(fit::MFRMFit; facets = :all, include_thresholds = true,\n    interval = 0.95, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nwright_map_data(design::FacetDesign, draws; facets = :all,\n    include_thresholds = true, interval = 0.95)\n\nReturn plotting-backend-independent Wright-map rows for the current fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM design. Facet rows summarize posterior person, rater, and item measures on the logit scale. Threshold rows summarize item-step positions as item measure + threshold step, which puts category boundaries on the same latent scale as person measures. The returned rows are intended for Quarto, Makie, AlgebraOfGraphics, ggplot, or CSV workflows and do not commit the package to a specific plotting backend.\n\nHigher person measures increase adjacent-category log odds; higher item difficulty or rater severity decreases them. An item-step position is an adjacent-category equal-probability boundary at zero rater severity, not an expected-score half-point. Positions are summed draw by draw, preserving joint item/step uncertainty. Threshold-row status describes the step constraint; is_fixed and fixed_value describe the complete item-step position. A derived sum-to-zero step remains uncertain; even a fixed binary step has an uncertain position when its item is estimated. Nonfinite derived positions raise an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.wright_map_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.diagnostic_map_data","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"diagnostic_map_data(fit::MFRMFit; facets = :all, include_thresholds = true,\n    metrics = :all, interval = 0.95, min_n = 1, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\ndiagnostic_map_data(design::FacetDesign, draws; facets = :all,\n    include_thresholds = true, metrics = :all, interval = 0.95,\n    min_n = 1)\n\nReturn plotting-backend-independent rows that join Wright-map logit positions to posterior infit/outfit summaries. :pathway_fit rows are intended for pathway-map displays with x = fit_value and y = logit_position; threshold rows are returned as :threshold_position reference rows on the same logit scale. The rows are diagnostic screening data, not model-selection or fairness-claim evidence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.diagnostic_map_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.fair_average_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"fair_average_summary(fit::MFRMFit; by = :person, interval = 0.95,\n    min_n = 1, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nfair_average_summary(design::FacetDesign, draws; by = :person,\n    interval = 0.95, min_n = 1)\n\nReturn posterior fair-average expected-score summaries for a focal MFRM facet. Rows use an equal-weight balanced reference grid over the non-focal core facets, so by = :person averages each person over all observed raters and items, by = :rater averages each rater over all persons and items, and by = :item averages each item over all persons and raters. Rows include the observed mean score for the focal level, fair-average and expected-score intervals, the fair-minus-observed adjustment, and a caveat that the balanced grid is a reporting standardization rather than a population-weighted target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fair_average_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.dff_report","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"dff_report(fit::MFRMFit; terms = :validation, adjustment = :assignment, interval = 0.95,\n    min_n = 1, expected_score_practical_threshold = nothing,\n    logit_practical_threshold = nothing,\n    practical_probability_threshold = 0.8, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\ndff_report(design::FacetDesign, draws; terms = :validation,\n    adjustment = :assignment, interval = 0.95, min_n = 1,\n    expected_score_practical_threshold = nothing,\n    logit_practical_threshold = nothing,\n    practical_probability_threshold = 0.8)\n\nReturn screening rows for declared or ad hoc differential facet functioning (DFF) terms. The default terms = :validation uses DFF/bias terms retained in the FacetSpec by mfrm_spec(...; bias = ...) or a matching validation report. Each row is one cell of a two-facet term and reports observed mean score, posterior expected-score residuals, and a two-way interaction residual on both expected-score and local logit-approximation scales.\n\nPass expected_score_practical_threshold and/or logit_practical_threshold to add estimand-specific practical-magnitude probabilities and classification. Rows classify a DFF contrast as :practically_positive, :practically_negative, :practically_negligible, or :mixed when the corresponding posterior probability is at least practical_probability_threshold; otherwise the practical-magnitude status is :not_requested.\n\nThese rows are not fitted DFF model effects. The logit scale is a local screening approximation that divides expected-score residuals by posterior predictive variance, so results should be used for fairness review triage and sensitivity planning rather than confirmatory unfairness claims.\n\nBy default adjustment=:assignment removes the weighted least-squares additive focal/comparison surface separately from each draw's cell score residuals and local logit shifts. Weights are observed rating counts, not inverse variances or model/sampling weights. No missing cell is imputed. On observed cells, with indicator matrix X and count weights W, the residual is W^(-1/2) * (I-U*U') * W^(1/2) * y, where U spans W^(1/2)*X. Thus additive cell composition is removed even under unbalanced assignment. The target is the remainder under this observed allocation, not a balanced population contrast or a fitted interaction. Scores/draws remain jointly aligned.\n\nRows retain the complete cell roster, counts, additive rank, assignment component count, interaction degrees of freedom and cell_residual_fraction (one minus weighted projection leverage). A cell without independent interaction support has contrast_status=:no_independent_cell_contrast and NaN interaction summaries, not a zero effect. Disconnected components may support their own within-component contrasts; they do not support between-component comparisons. min_n flags cells without removing them from the projection. Floating-point rank/support tolerances are reported; cancellation at that tolerance is set to zero before directional summaries. This is not a practical-effect threshold.\n\nn_expected_score_dff_draws and n_logit_dff_draws report usable draw counts. If any observed cell has an undefined local logit shift, that whole term's logit projection is unavailable for that draw; summaries use finite draws and an incomplete-draw flag remains. Intervals condition on the observed scores and assignment and propagate base-model uncertainty only. They are neither repeated- sample error intervals nor calibrated multiplicity-adjusted tests. Practical probabilities describe the residual statistic, not a fitted bias parameter.\n\nFor reproduction only, adjustment=:observed_margins retains the historical row/column/grand-margin arithmetic and emits a warning. That arithmetic can retain additive composition under unbalanced assignment. fit_report forwards dff_adjustment and records it in report controls. A formal bias claim needs a defined contrast, adequate linking, and an appropriate fitted interaction model or separately validated conditional procedure. Neither mode establishes unfairness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.dff_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.separation_reliability_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"separation_reliability_summary(fit::MFRMFit; facets = (:person, :rater, :item),\n    interval = 0.95, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nseparation_reliability_summary(design::FacetDesign, draws;\n    facets = (:person, :rater, :item), interval = 0.95)\n\nReturn posterior separation and empirical reliability summaries for MFRM person, rater, and item measures. For each requested facet, rows summarize the posterior distribution of level dispersion, mean posterior error variance, error-adjusted dispersion, separation, and reliability. Reliability is computed as adjusted variance divided by adjusted-plus-error variance; use the included caveat flag because these rows are practical screening summaries rather than full generalizability-study coefficients.\n\nThis historical screen is not a validated FACETS separation estimator or EAP score-reliability estimator. It subtracts the average marginal posterior variance from the cross-level variance within each draw, truncates at zero, then summarizes the resulting ratios. Equal posterior means can therefore yield positive values; shared location uncertainty can change the result even when all pairwise contrasts stay fixed. The returned interval is a distribution of this screening statistic, not an established credible interval for a reliability coefficient. Do not convert these values into reportable Strata or interpret rater separation as score agreement. Use qualified BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability for an explicit finite-cohort target; its definition and return schema differ. This legacy call now warns but preserves historical numerical values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.separation_reliability_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.rater_diagnostics","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"rater_diagnostics(fit::MFRMFit; interval = 0.95, min_n = 1,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\nrater_diagnostics(fit::GMFRMFit; interval = 0.95, min_n = 1,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\nrater_diagnostics(design::FacetDesign, draws; interval = 0.95, min_n = 1)\n\nReturn one posterior diagnostic row per rater. Rows combine observed category use, score range and central-category use, posterior rater severity intervals, posterior residual summaries, and MFRM infit/outfit summaries where available. For the fit-supported minimal MFRM, a rater-consistency multiplier is not modeled and the generalized consistency fields are marked missing. For the guarded scalar GMFRM experimental fit object, the rater_consistency multiplier is summarized as the current positive source-equation parameter; infit/outfit statistics are not yet available for that experimental path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rater_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.rater_homogeneity_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"rater_homogeneity_summary(fit::MFRMFit; interval = 0.95,\n    severity_rope = nothing, rope_probability_threshold = 0.95,\n    overlap_unit = :person_item, min_shared_units = 1,\n    ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nrater_homogeneity_summary(design::FacetDesign, draws; kwargs...)\n\nReturn draw-wise pairwise rater-severity contrasts for the minimal MFRM. Each unordered pair reports severity_a - severity_b on the logit scale; a positive contrast means rater A is more severe. Each coordinate is labelled :reference_zero, :hard_anchor, or :estimated. A contrast between two fixed coordinates is an exact constant, not a posterior estimate: its interval and uncertainty statuses are explicitly not applicable and its sign/ROPE probabilities are deterministic. A contrast with one fixed coordinate has posterior uncertainty only from the estimated coordinate. Other rows include a central posterior interval, probability of direction, optional ROPE probabilities and practical-equivalence classification, and direct/network/disconnected overlap under the requested overlap_unit. This shared-unit overlap is reported separately from identification by the full reference-constrained additive MFRM location design. With the default overlap_unit = :person_item, overlap is a person-item proxy and is not proof that two raters scored the identical response when repeated responses or occasions exist. Use :response_id or :response_item with declared response identifiers for verified common-response linking. Raters linked through shared persons, shared items, or a connected facet network are not labelled unidentified merely because their requested shared- unit overlap graph is disconnected. A single-rater fit returns zero contrast rows and explicit :single_rater / :not_applicable_single_rater statuses; it is not reported as evidence of connected pairwise homogeneity.\n\nseverity_rope is deliberately nothing by default because no universal practical-equivalence margin exists. Supply a preregistered symmetric radius or two-value interval to request :inside_rope, :outside_rope, or :mixed classification. Bayes factors are not computed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rater_homogeneity_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.category_functioning_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"category_functioning_summary(fit::MFRMFit; interval = 0.95,\n    min_count = 5, min_proportion = 0.01,\n    order_probability_threshold = 0.8, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\ncategory_functioning_summary(design::FacetDesign, draws; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a versioned MFRM/RSM/PCM category-functioning bundle. usage_rows contain overall, rater-by-category, and item-by-category observed use together with posterior predictive replicated proportions and tail probabilities. threshold_rows contain posterior step intervals and draw-wise adjacent-step ordering probabilities for the shared RSM steps or item-specific PCM steps.\n\nFlags are diagnostic review prompts. This function never collapses, recodes, or refits score categories; a flagged row records that an explicit analysis decision is required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.category_functioning_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.residual_summary","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"residual_summary(fit::MFRMFit; by = :observation, interval = 0.95,\n    min_n = 1, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nresidual_summary(design::FacetDesign, draws; by = :observation,\n    interval = 0.95, min_n = 1)\n\nReturn posterior summaries of observed-minus-expected score residuals by observation or facet level. Rows include observed mean score, posterior expected-score intervals, residual intervals, mean absolute residual, RMSE, and a screening caveat flag. Use by = :person, :rater, :item, :category, or an optional facet name to aggregate rows; by = :observation returns one row per original response. Fitted-object methods are available for MFRM and guarded scalar GMFRM fit objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.residual_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_stats","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"fit_stats(fit::MFRMFit; by = :rater, method = :posterior,\n    interval = 0.95, min_n = 1, ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nfit_stats(design::FacetDesign, draws; by = :rater, interval = 0.95,\n    min_n = 1)\n\nReturn posterior summaries of observation-level infit and outfit mean-square statistics by facet level. This initial implementation uses posterior draws and the current minimal MFRM predictive variances; method = :posterior is the only supported method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_stats"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.facets_report","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"facets_report(args...; kwargs...)\n\nReport-oriented public name for facets_compatibility_stats. It returns the same explicitly approximate, unit-weighted posterior-mean plugin rows for MFRM/RSM/PCM fits and preserves the same rejection boundaries for non-unit weights and generalized fits. fit_stats remains the default posterior Bayesian diagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.facets_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-workflow-diagnostics/#BayesianMGMFRM.facets_compatibility_stats","page":"Workflow and Diagnostics","text":"facets_compatibility_stats(fit::MFRMFit; by = :rater,\n    point_estimate = :posterior_mean, min_n = 1, weighting = :unit,\n    zstd_cap = 9, variance_tolerance = 1e-12, ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\nfacets_compatibility_stats(design::FacetDesign, params; by = :rater,\n    min_n = 1, weighting = :unit, zstd_cap = 9,\n    variance_tolerance = 1e-12)\n\nReturn an explicitly approximate FACETS-compatible fit table for the fit-supported one-dimensional MFRM rating-scale or partial-credit model. The fit method plugs the selected draws' parameter-wise posterior means into the Rasch response probabilities. It then computes infit and outfit MnSq, the Wright–Masters fourth-moment degrees of freedom, and Wilson–Hilferty ZSTD values capped at +/-zstd_cap.\n\nThis function is separate from fit_stats: posterior infit/outfit summaries remain the default Bayesian residual diagnostic. Only unit-weighted observations are supported, and rows state that posterior uncertainty is not propagated and numerical equivalence to FACETS JML output is not claimed. GMFRMFit and MGMFRMFit inputs are rejected because generalized discrimination invalidates an unqualified FACETS-df interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.facets_compatibility_stats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"Overview","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThe reference is split by workflow area for easier navigation.\n\nFunctions below are grouped by the task they support. Stable fitting uses fit(spec); the two restricted generalized families use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec). Compatibility names remain callable for older code. Research helpers are labelled separately and do not establish that a model or diagnostic is validated for an analysis.\n\nThe optional, qualified BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior, BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics, BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive and BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright and BayesianMGMFRM.plot_pathway functions are documented under the fitting API. Load CairoMakie to enable their renderer; the Wright map accepts stable MFRM only.\n\nData and Design API\nFitting and Artifact API\nWorkflow and Diagnostics API\nSaved-fit Reporting Candidate API (qualified, isolated candidate)\nPrediction and Recovery Candidate API (qualified, isolated candidate)\nExperimental Model API\nValidation and Evidence API","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Bayesian-Fitting","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.jl fits identified MFRM/RSM/PCM designs and exposes the documented multidimensional and generalized configurations behind explicit experimental opt-in. Validate data before sampling; inspect the compiled design when reviewing identification or model structure. The minimal example runs the ordinary fit, diagnostics, figures and save/reload sequence.","title":"Bayesian Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Supported-MFRM/RSM/PCM-Fit","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"using BayesianMGMFRM\n\nratings = (\n    examinee = [\"E1\", \"E1\", \"E1\", \"E1\", \"E2\", \"E2\", \"E2\", \"E2\"],\n    rater = [\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\"],\n    item = [\"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\", \"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\"],\n    score = [0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0, 2],\n)\n\ndata = FacetData(ratings;\n    person = :examinee,\n    rater = :rater,\n    item = :item,\n    score = :score,\n    category_levels = 0:2,\n)\nvalidation = validate_design(data)\nvalidation.passed || error(validation)\n\nspec = mfrm_spec(data;\n    thresholds = :partial_credit,\n    validation_report = validation,\n)\n\nfit_result = fit(spec;\n    backend = :advancedhmc,\n    ndraws = 500,\n    warmup = 500,\n    chains = 4,\n    seed = 20260718,\n)\n\nThe rating-scale and partial-credit threshold regimes share the same workflow. fit(spec) constructs the design internally; use getdesign(spec) when you want to inspect it separately. Declaring category_levels preserves the form's intended threshold count when an endpoint is absent from the realized sample. Inspect ordinal_response_pattern_audit(data).category_scale and validation warnings before fitting; do not reduce the scale merely to remove an empty category. Inspect constraint_table, identification_declarations, and model_manifest when reviewing the parameterization.","title":"Supported MFRM/RSM/PCM Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Posterior-interval-figures","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Install the optional renderer once with Pkg.add(\"CairoMakie\"). It is not required for fitting, summaries, or saved reports. With the fit above:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit_result; block = :rater, interval = 0.95)\ndisplay(figure)\ncontent(figure[1, 1]).xlabel = \"Rater severity\"\nsave(\"rater-posterior.pdf\", figure)\nsave(\"rater-posterior.svg\", figure)\n\nThe returned Figure can be edited using CairoMakie. Points are posterior medians; bars are central credible intervals, not predictive intervals or Monte Carlo error bars. Fixed identification values are labelled and shown as diamonds without intervals. External anchor uncertainty is not propagated. The diagnostic note concerns the whole fit, including parameters outside the selection; an interval figure does not establish convergence.\n\nUse block = :person, :rater, :item, or :thresholds for MFRM, or choose exact names with parameters = [\"rater[R2]\", \"rater[R1]\"]. Names must match your data. Explicit names set the order within each axis. Blocks have separate axes; model-scale figures include fixed reference values and the derived last threshold. scale = :raw instead shows the coordinates in posterior_summary and does not change that function's defaults. A selection above 60 coordinates asks you to narrow it or set max_parameters explicitly; no rows are silently dropped. Use size = (900, 600) to set the figure dimensions.\n\nFigures can be regenerated from an existing fit cache without rerunning MCMC:\n\nsave_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\", fit_result)\nreloaded = load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(reloaded; block = :rater)\n\nThe same qualified function accepts the existing experimental generalized fit types. Their model coordinates use transformed draws; scale = :raw is an explicit computational view. MGMFRM accepts a dimension index or declared label, for example dimension = 2, and keeps dimensions on separate axes. Plotting does not expand the supported model configurations. The guarded examples demonstrate model-scale intervals, raw-coordinate diagnostics and named-dimension selection from saved GMFRM/MGMFRM fits, with either Julia or CmdStan estimation.","title":"Posterior interval figures"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#3D-item-response-surfaces-for-MGMFRM","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"For a saved independent fixed-Q MGMFRM fit, show the probability of each rating category as a function of two numbered abilities, for a selected item and rater:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\n\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"mgmfrm-fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(restored;\n    item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", dimensions = (1, 2),\n    x = range(-2, 2; length = 31), y = range(-2, 2; length = 31),\n    interval = 0.9)\ndisplay(figure)\nsave(\"category-response-surfaces.png\", figure)\nsave(\"category-response-surfaces.svg\", figure)\n\n# A single category, with the same color as in the all-category view.\ncategory_figure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(restored;\n    item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", category = 2, dimensions = (1, 2))\n# The expectation on the actual numeric rating scale is a separate quantity.\nexpected_figure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_response_surface(restored;\n    item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\", category = nothing, dimensions = (1, 2))\n\nThe default category = :all uses a distinct, fixed color and legend entry for each stored rating value. All probability wireframes share a vertical scale from 0 to 1; color identifies the category, not probability height. The mapping uses the full fitted category scale even when displaying only one category, so changing item or rater within the same fit preserves category colors. category = 2 means rating value 2, not the second category by position. Intermediate-category probabilities can rise and then fall with ability. For crowded wireframes, select a single category or change the viewing angle.\n\nAxes use θ₁, θ₂, and the actual selected dimension numbers. Stored dimension names remain available for selection and in the numerical result; they do not automatically become axis labels. A name such as “Technique” requires a substantive interpretation justified by the rubric and model. This display follows the numbered abilities and category-specific response surfaces in Uto (2021), Fig. 2. Here the curves are posterior means: the calculation evaluates each joint posterior draw and then averages the probabilities. It does not substitute posterior-mean parameters into the model. Curvature arises from the ordinal response link applied to weighted ability; it does not by itself imply an interaction between the two abilities.\n\nThe companion heatmaps show the width of each category's pointwise central credible interval on a shared continuous color scale. Their category titles match the wireframe colors. For category = nothing, the surface and its interval summarize expected rating instead. These intervals describe uncertainty in a response probability or conditional expectation, not an individual future rating, a simultaneous band over the grid, or MCSE. The whole-fit diagnostic warning remains visible. Neither a smooth surface nor a narrow interval establishes convergence or calibration.\n\nThe selected rater's severity and consistency remain part of the calculation. There is no implicit averaging over raters or a population of persons. If Q excludes an axis for this item, the surface is flat along that axis. With more than two dimensions, explicitly fix every remaining ability, for example dimensions = (1, 2), fixed_abilities = Dict(3 => 0.0) for a three-dimensional fit. Stored dimension names are also accepted as selectors. This produces a conditional slice, not a marginal surface. The grid is on the fitted ability scale; the default range −3 to 3 is a display choice and does not establish that observations support predictions throughout that range.\n\nFor numbers without installing a renderer, use the same arguments with BayesianMGMFRM.item_response_surface(restored; item = \"I1\", rater = \"R1\"). The result includes mean, lower, upper, and interval_width arrays, indexed as [x_index, y_index, category_index] for :all, and as [x_index, y_index] for a single category or expected rating. category_levels[category_index] gives the actual rating value. All retained draws are used by default. An explicit, unique draw_indices selection is recorded in the result and the figure's draw count; reducing it changes the numerical approximation. Grid size affects computation time.\n\nFigures are editable CairoMakie Figures. azimuth and elevation set the static 3D viewing angle in radians; this entry point does not provide browser rotation controls. It supports the independent MGMFRM fit type and does not extend fitting support or automatically add figures to report bundles.","title":"3D item response surfaces for MGMFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Titles-and-notes-outside-the-image","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"All plotting entry points now put the overall title and explanatory note outside the image by default. Axis labels, units, panel headings and keys remain in the figure. The default annotation_io = stdout prints the text in the Julia REPL or script output, and each call still returns an editable Figure. Save both parts for a report:\n\nopen(\"wright-map-note.txt\", \"w\") do note\n    figure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result; annotation_io = note)\n    save(\"wright-map.svg\", figure)\n    save(\"wright-map.pdf\", figure)\nend\n\nThe text contains the title, followed by Note. and the complete interpretation and diagnostic information. Use annotation_io = IOBuffer() to collect it in memory, or annotation_io = nothing to select the previous embedded format. With the default, a single main plotting panel starts at figure[1, 1]; do not reuse an old figure[2, 1] edit without checking the layout. The option applies to posterior, prior, diagnostic, predictive, Wright and response-surface plots, including the supported fixed-Q and correlated-result adapters. It changes presentation only, not model support, summaries or the retained draw selection.\n\nAPA figure guidance places the figure number and title above the image and any necessary note below it; the key belongs inside the image. Add the manuscript's figure number and format the title and note in your document. Check lettering at the final physical size, contrast and readable symbols before submission. Separate text and editable PDF/SVG output are useful building blocks, not an automatic APA-compliance guarantee. Always keep diagnostic warnings, interval definitions, conditioning and data provenance with the image. The gallery demonstrates this layout.","title":"Titles and notes outside the image"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Chain-diagnostic-figures","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Use the same optional renderer to inspect retained draws without reshaping them:\n\nchains = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(fit_result; block = :rater)\ndisplay(chains)\nsave(\"rater-chains.pdf\", chains)\nsave(\"rater-chains.svg\", chains)\n\nEach selected coordinate has a trace and a rank histogram. Chain colors and line styles agree across panels. The trace retains the stored chain and iteration identities; plotting does not thin, subsample, or refit. The default scale = :raw helps inspect computational coordinates. Use scale = :model for transformed parameters. block, dimension, and exact parameters names work as in the interval figure. The default limit is 12 selected coordinates; choose a subset or explicitly increase max_parameters.\n\nRanks are pooled across chains for each coordinate, with average ranks for ties. Each chain's bin fractions sum to one. The gray line is the pooled histogram, so ties remain visible instead of being randomly separated. Set bins = 10 to change the bin count (capped at total retained draws). Compare chain distributions alongside the trace and numerical diagnostics; similar rank histograms alone do not establish convergence. Single-chain, fixed-value, and constant-draw rank comparisons are explicitly unavailable or inapplicable. The bayesplot explanation of trace and rank plots provides further context for interpreting these views.\n\nParameter captions use existing rank-normalized R-hat and bulk/tail ESS rows. Derived constraint coordinates without such rows are labelled unavailable. The note retains whole-fit warnings and extrema even when only one parameter is shown, plus per-chain reported divergence counts, maximum-depth hits and E-BFMI. Missing or incomplete sampler statistics remain unavailable; these panels do not create missing telemetry. Experimental fits use their recorded diagnostic settings and the same compatibility checks as diagnostics.\n\nWarmup parameter draws are not stored and are excluded from these plots. Use sampler_diagnostics(fit_result; phase = :warmup) for separate adaptation summaries. A fit reloaded with load_fit_cache works with the same plotting call; the editable Figure and PDF/SVG workflow are unchanged.","title":"Chain diagnostic figures"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Posterior-predictive-figures","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Check whether the fitted model reproduces overall category use:\n\npredictive = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(fit_result; ndraws = 200, seed = 42)\ndisplay(predictive)\nsave(\"category-predictive.pdf\", predictive)\nsave(\"category-predictive.svg\", predictive)\n\nBlack diamonds show observed category proportions. Blue circles show the mean proportion across replicated datasets; bars show 90% pointwise central predictive intervals by default. Set interval = 0.95 to change their width. Categories with no observed ratings remain visible. The replication preserves the original rating rows and uses posterior draws of their fitted persons, items and raters; it does not generate new facet levels or refit the model.\n\nAn observed proportion outside its bar identifies a discrepancy to investigate. These bars describe replicated category proportions, rather than parameter uncertainty or Monte Carlo error. They are pointwise intervals, not a simultaneous band or an overall model-acceptance rule. Overall category use can conceal differences between raters or items. Inspect the fit's diagnostics and relevant facet summaries alongside this check. See the Stan guide to posterior predictive checks for the distinction between replicated data and parameter draws.\n\nWith no selection option, the figure uses every retained draw once, in stored order. ndraws = 200 samples 200 draw indices with replacement from the retained draws. Alternatively, pass draw_indices = [1, 4, 10] for exact ordered indices; repeated indices generate new responses from the same parameter draw. Supply only one selection option. The caption distinguishes replicated datasets from the number of distinct posterior draws used. Generating more replications does not add posterior draws. For large fits, ndraws bounds the number of replicated datasets held in memory.\n\nEach call uses a local MersenneTwister with seed = 1 by default and leaves the global RNG unchanged. Reuse the same fit, seed and selection options in the same Julia/package environment to replay the result. Changing only interval or Figure labels does not change the simulated scores. For matching numerical rows:\n\nusing Random\ncheck = posterior_predictive_check(fit_result; ndraws = 200, rng = MersenneTwister(42))\nrows = predictive_check_summary(check; interval = 0.9)\n\nThe figure displays the category-proportion rows from this summary. The same plotting call accepts load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\"); retain the seed and selection arguments with your analysis code. The returned native Figure can be edited and saved without simulating again. Whole-fit MCMC warnings remain in its caption. Stable MFRM and the existing experimental GMFRM/MGMFRM fits retain their respective support limits; this conditional check does not establish performance for new persons, items or raters.","title":"Posterior predictive figures"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Wright-map","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"For a stable MFRM rating-scale or partial-credit fit, compare persons, raters, items and category boundaries on one logit scale:\n\nwright = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result; interval = 0.95)\ndisplay(wright)\nsave(\"wright-map.pdf\", wright)\nsave(\"wright-map.svg\", wright)\n\nEach panel uses the same linked vertical axis. Higher person ability increases adjacent-category log odds; higher rater severity or item difficulty decreases them. The + and - panel headings identify those effects; plotted severity and difficulty retain their original signs. The figure uses the fit's identified coordinates without recentering. Horizontal positions identify levels in their original order; horizontal spacing has no measurement interpretation.\n\nCircles are posterior medians and bars are central credible intervals. Diamonds mark fixed references or hard anchors, with no interval; external anchor uncertainty is not propagated. The whole-fit diagnostic note remains visible. A visually plausible map does not establish convergence or model adequacy.\n\nThe boundary panel shows item difficulty plus category step, summed within each draw before computing intervals. This preserves joint item/step uncertainty. At zero rater severity, a person at that position has equal probabilities for the two adjacent categories shown in the label. Those probabilities need not each be 50% when other categories exist. These are neither expected-score half-points nor predictive intervals. For another rater, add that rater's severity to the boundary, propagating its uncertainty in any numerical analysis. Zero severity remains the figure's reference even when all raters have nonzero estimates or anchors. The Facets Wright-map documentation illustrates the facet-orientation convention; its expected-score scale column has a different interpretation from this boundary panel.\n\nfacets = (:person, :item) chooses panels and their order. Set include_thresholds = false to omit boundaries; otherwise boundaries for every item are included independently of facet selection. All declared category transitions remain present, even when a category has no observed scores. More than 60 individual positions raises an error. Narrow the facet selection, omit boundaries, or explicitly increase max_levels and adjust size = (1600, 900). No levels are silently dropped. GMFRM and MGMFRM fits are explicitly unsupported.\n\nThe map uses all retained draws without random selection or refitting. The same call accepts load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\"); the returned Figure is editable. wright_map_data(fit_result; interval = 0.95) supplies matching numerical rows. In threshold rows, status describes the step constraint while is_fixed and fixed_value describe the full item-step position. A derived step is uncertain; even a fixed binary step has an uncertain position when its item is estimated.","title":"Wright map"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Person-histogram-overview","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"To see where a cohort is located without labelling every person, use a horizontal histogram beside the existing item and rater panels:\n\nopen(\"wright-histogram-note.txt\", \"w\") do note\n    figure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result;\n        person_display = :histogram, bins = 10, annotation_io = note)\n    save(\"wright-histogram.svg\", figure)\nend\n\nEach person contributes one posterior median. The horizontal axis counts people; the vertical axis retains the common logit scale. This is a distribution of estimated person locations, which are affected by prior shrinkage. It is neither a population density nor a histogram of all posterior draws, and it does not show uncertainty about each person or the bin counts. Use the default person_display = :intervals to inspect individual credible intervals. This arrangement is inspired by the person-frequency column in FACETS Vertical=; it does not reproduce FACETS estimation or its expected-score scale column.\n\nbins can be a positive integer or an explicit vector of increasing finite edges, such as bins = [-3.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0]. Explicit edges must cover every person median; a narrow range raises an error rather than dropping people. Each bin includes its lower edge and excludes its upper edge, except the last includes its upper edge. With unequal widths, bar length still shows the count, not count per logit; compare counts rather than bar areas. Without bins, use min(20, ceil(Int, sqrt(n_persons))) equal-width bins over the median range. If all medians coincide, that range is expanded by 0.5 logits on each side. Binning is a display choice; inspect how a different width changes the apparent pattern before drawing conclusions about distribution shape.\n\nAll people in the person facet are included. Their aggregate count does not use the max_levels allowance for individually displayed positions; rater, item and boundary positions still do. Histogram mode requires the person facet. The full note records the exact edges, counts and diagnostic status. In report bundles, use figures = (wright = (person_display = :histogram, bins = 10),). The figure JSON retains data.histogram and all numerical data.rows, including every person's label and interval. Reusing the options after load_fit_cache reproduces the counts. See the gallery example.","title":"Person histogram overview"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Pathway-map","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"A Pathway map adds a response-fit axis to the facet locations. Use it to identify response patterns for closer inspection at a given ability, severity or difficulty:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nopen(\"pathway-map-note.txt\", \"w\") do note\n    figure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_pathway(fit_result;\n        facets = (:rater, :item), metric = :outfit, interval = 0.99,\n        annotation_io = note)\n    save(\"pathway-map.svg\", figure)\nend\n\nThe default metric = :infit shows an information-weighted residual mean square; :outfit gives each rating's squared standardized residual equal weight. The latter is more sensitive to unexpected ratings with low predictive variance. For each retained parameter draw, the calculation uses observed scores, their conditional expected scores and variances. The resulting fit statistics and facet locations are summarized with marginal medians and central intervals. Thus a cross shows two separate marginal intervals, not a joint credible region. The line at 1 is a model reference, not an automatic acceptance rule. These quantities are not standardized ZSTD and have no automatic −2/+2 cutoffs.\n\nAll selected panels share both axes. Original facet order and identified logit signs are retained. Diamonds mark fixed locations, which have no vertical uncertainty; a diamond can still have a horizontal fit interval because the other model parameters remain uncertain. External anchor uncertainty is excluded. Labels show each level's contributing rating count, n. Orange marks with * identify numerical flags, described by level in the external note. In particular, tiny conditional variances are clipped in the existing statistic. Inspect the numerical rows before interpreting these cases:\n\nrows = diagnostic_map_data(fit_result; facets = (:rater, :item),\n    metrics = :outfit, include_thresholds = false, interval = 0.99, min_n = 1)\n\nThe plot uses these rows' fit_median and logit_median, their lower/upper bounds, counts and flags. min_n is a minimum number of contributing ratings; if a selected level falls below it, missing summaries cause an error. Nonfinite summaries also fail explicitly. No levels or bad values are silently omitted. The default limit is 60 selected levels; narrow facets or explicitly increase max_levels and size. With crowded or coincident labels, select fewer facets and edit the returned Figure before publication.\n\nAll retained draws are used without random selection or fitting. Whole-fit MCMC warnings accompany every selection in the external note. Same-data residual screening does not establish convergence, bias or a preferred model family. Reusing identical options after load_fit_cache reproduces the numerical inputs. To export the figure, inputs and notes together:\n\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"pathway-report\", fit_result;\n    figures = (pathway = (facets = (:rater, :item), metric = :outfit),),\n    posterior_lower = 0.005, posterior_upper = 0.995)\n\nThese report bounds set 99% central intervals on both axes. Set interval bounds on the bundle call, not inside figures.pathway. The bundle includes PDF/SVG, full numerical JSON with fixed-location flags, and matching external titles/notes. Report predictive-draw options do not subsample the Pathway inputs.\n\nThis API supports stable unidimensional MFRM RSM/PCM. Generalized and multidimensional inputs raise an error. A person's pooled residual fit is not inherently dimension-specific; pure-Q subsets need an explicit conditional estimand, and mixed-Q responses have no unique attribution to one dimension. See the Pathway gallery for an artificial illustration and reading examples.","title":"Pathway map"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Fixed-coordinates-and-anchors","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"For a stable MFRM fit with exact item or rater hard anchors, inspect the public report directly:\n\nreport = fit_report(fit_result; view = :public)\nreport.fixed_coordinates.rows\nreport.fixed_coordinates.warning_rows\n\nThe rows include both default reference coordinates and declared hard anchors. Every row states that the coordinate is unsampled, prior-free, and not a posterior estimate. A hard-anchor fit contributes two baseline warnings to the structured report and to the prominent Warnings block in fit_report_markdown(report): one marks fixed coordinates as constants, and the other states that MFRMPrior remains zero-centered on free identified coordinates. Thus changing an anchor value can change prior and posterior predictions even when a likelihood-equivalent reparameterization exists. The fixed-value warning also states that uncertainty from an externally estimated anchor is not propagated into this fit. When two or more anchors occur in the same facet, a third warning states that within-facet contrasts have been fixed and require contamination or drift sensitivity; one rater plus one item anchor does not trigger that warning. Posterior-summary rows continue to contain only estimated coordinates.\n\nThe same stable MFRM report includes practitioner-facing category and rater sections by default:\n\nreport = fit_report(\n    fit_result;\n    view = :public,\n    category_functioning_min_count = 5,\n    rater_severity_rope = nothing,\n)\nreport.category_functioning.usage_rows\nreport.category_functioning.threshold_rows\nreport.rater_homogeneity.contrast_rows\n\nCategory flags are review prompts, not an automatic category-collapse, recoding, or refitting rule; any flagged rows contribute one aggregate warning. No universal severity ROPE is assumed for rater contrasts. The default rater_overlap_unit = :person_item is a proxy rather than proof that two raters scored the identical response when repeated occasions exist; use :response_id or :response_item with declared response identifiers when common-response linking is the intended claim. Each contrast labels its rater coordinates as reference_zero, hard_anchor, or estimated. A contrast between two fixed coordinates is an exact constant rather than a posterior estimate: n_uncertainty_draws is zero, the interval is not applicable, and its interval probability and quantile-probability fields are missing; sign/ROPE probabilities are deterministic. With one fixed coordinate, posterior uncertainty comes only from the estimated coordinate. Set include_category_functioning = false or include_rater_homogeneity = false to disable either section independently. For a one-rater fit, the rater section is still computed but contains zero contrast rows and reports not_applicable_single_rater; an empty comparison is not evidence of rater homogeneity. Generalized fits do not request these stable-only sections by default, and an explicit request returns unsupported. Public JSON, table, and bundle exports preserve the zero-row contrast table. Markdown keeps the section summary but omits empty table previews by default; use fit_report_markdown(report; include_empty = true) when an explicit zero-row table marker is required.\n\nWith thresholds = :partial_credit, a declared but unobserved endpoint remains in category usage rows as observed_flag = :skipped, while item-specific step rows continue to cover the complete declared scale. This is a review prompt, not authorization to narrow the scale or collapse the category.","title":"Fixed coordinates and anchors"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Backends-and-Sampler-Controls","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"fit supports:\n\nbackend = :julia for a simple random-walk Metropolis implementation;\nbackend = :advancedhmc for direct AdvancedHMC/NUTS sampling;\nbackend = :turing for the package target wrapped in Turing/NUTS;\nbackend = :cmdstan for stable MFRM/RSM/PCM and both guarded generalized configurations through an external CmdStan installation. Use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit for GMFRM/MGMFRM.\n\nRun cmdstan_backend_check() to inspect CmdStan, stanc, make, and C++ compiler availability without compiling a model. The first fit for a supported family compiles its package-owned Stan model into a temporary machine-local cache. It uses the same Julia raw/identified parameter order and prior scales, imports the standard CmdStan sampler columns, and checks generated pointwise log likelihoods against Julia at every retained draw. target_accept maps to CmdStan's adapt delta; thinning remains one. CmdStan failures raise CmdStanError instead of being converted to a missing result.\n\nSupply a new empty cmdstan_cache_dir for each CmdStan fit. Compiled-model reuse is currently disabled pending verified build evidence; a populated directory is preserved and rejected, as is a symlink root. The default directory cannot be reused after a build attempt leaves files. A fresh model directory does not isolate CmdStan's shared build products or verify build provenance.\n\nMAKE selects one executable name or path, not a shell command with arguments. Compilation requires MAKEFILES, MAKEFLAGS, and GNUMAKEFLAGS to be unset or exactly empty; even whitespace or parallel-build flags are rejected. The readiness check does not enforce this compile-time policy. See cmdstan_backend_check for discovery and toolchain details.\n\nFor both guarded generalized families, every retained raw draw is transformed through the Julia identification map before the common fit, diagnostics, and prediction interfaces are built. Unwrapped MGMFRM specifications remain fixed-Q with identity correlation. The explicit correlated MGMFRM uses a separate result type and currently supports summaries, diagnostics, MCSE and manual caches, plus prior and conditional existing-row posterior prediction. Reader-facing reports, tables and optional CairoMakie figures retain the saved prior, model identity and sampling warnings. cached_fit and parallel chain execution are not yet connected to CmdStan. CmdStan remains optional for package installation, and no backend is declared faster or more accurate without a same-target analysis.\n\nUse short runs only to verify wiring. For substantive work, choose the number of chains, warmup, retained draws, target acceptance, tree depth, metric, and initialization strategy before examining the results. Record a seed when exact replay is part of the analysis plan.\n\nThe runnable examples show stable and guarded fits with --cmdstan, including saved-fit reload and optional figures.\n\nSampler success is necessary but not sufficient. Review:\n\nsampler_diagnostics for acceptance and HMC warnings;\nmcmc_diagnostics for parameter-level R-hat and ESS;\nposterior_mcse for on-demand mean, SD, and quantile simulation error after convergence review;\nparameter_block_diagnostics for block-level summaries;\ndiagnostics for the compact overall status;\nprior and posterior predictive checks, calibration, and sensitivity results.\n\nVery short chains commonly produce unreliable R-hat and ESS values even when the example completes without an exception.\n\nInspect warmup separately from the retained posterior draws:\n\nsampler_diagnostics(fit_result)                  # retained draws (default)\nsampler_diagnostics(fit_result; phase = :warmup) # warmup events by chain\n\nNew AdvancedHMC, Turing and CmdStan fits record warmup iteration counts, divergences, maximum-tree-depth hits and nonfinite log densities. Generalized GMFRM/MGMFRM fits support AdvancedHMC and CmdStan. Random-walk fits record n_nonfinite_proposals during burn-in, with no proposal-scale adaptation. Their divergence, tree-depth and nonfinite-log-density fields are missing because those NUTS statistics do not apply. The rows distinguish coverage = :recorded, :not_run when warmup = 0, and :not_recorded for fits without recorded history. Unavailable counts are missing. Warmup events do not contribute to posterior summaries, R-hat/ESS or retained sampler warning flags, and have no automatic pass/fail rule.\n\nTuring adapts for exactly warmup transitions per chain, independently of ndraws; warmup = 0 disables adaptation. The initial state and every adapting transition are excluded from posterior draws. Older Turing fits without sampler_controls.nadapts used an automatic adaptation length tied to ndraws and could include adapting transitions. Their warmup history is reported as :not_recorded, even if zero warmup was requested. Re-estimate those fits for analysis under the corrected schedule. Turing cache request keys distinguish the corrected controls; cached_fit(...; refresh = true) replaces an older cache through a new fit. Manual loading preserves historical draws and metadata.\n\nsave_fit_cache preserves these compact summaries and load_fit_cache restores them; warmup parameter draws are not saved. Existing caches remain readable. For GMFRM/MGMFRM, coverage = :recorded confirms these chain-level totals; it does not mean that an iteration-by-iteration adaptation history is available. The fit cache does not retain the adapted mass matrix or warmup step-size history. sampler_controls.step_size is the requested initial step size; the retained sampler_stats rows contain the step sizes actually used during sampling. A cache refresh collects the same supported summaries, not the missing adaptation history or metric.\n\nFor future AdvancedHMC and CmdStan comparisons, the repository provides an opt-in research helper for independent fixed-Q MGMFRM. From the repository root, use the same fit options as the planned ordinary fit:\n\ninclude(\"scripts/mgmfrm_adaptation_record.jl\")\nfitted = MGMFRMAdaptationRecord.fit_recorded(\"adaptation-run-001\", spec;\n    fit_options...)\n\nfit_options is a named tuple of ordinary Experimental.fit keywords, including the prior, seed, warmup/retained lengths and other controls chosen for the study. The helper returns the ordinary fit and creates a new directory containing fit.jls and adaptation.json; an existing directory is an error. This is a repository helper, not an exported package API or an automatic cache refresh. AdvancedHMC supports raw and :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset sampling coordinates; CmdStan supports raw coordinates. Both support unit, diagonal and dense metrics. Adapted metric recording needs CmdStan 2.34 or later. Correlated models and private sampler overrides are outside the helper's scope.\n\nThe JSON records actual chain starts before and after the coordinate transform, every warmup and retained transition's sampler statistics, the retained kernel, coordinate order, model/data/prior identity, controls, environment/source hashes and the saved fit's SHA-256. AdvancedHMC copies inverse mass matrices when they change. iteration counts from one through warmup and retained sampling together; phase marks their boundary. For AdvancedHMC, metric_used and metric_after are one-based indices into metrics. The former was used by that transition; the latter follows its adaptation update and is used by the next transition. The row's step_size was used by the completed transition, so it must not be paired with metric_after as though both preceded adaptation. Dense matrices are JSON arrays of rows; diagonal/unit metrics are diagonal vectors, all in sampling_names order. Rotated person coordinates are numbered means/contrasts, not individually labelled person abilities. Item offsets are distinguished from raw item parameters. The prior remains defined on the original raw coordinates. The input signature is a decimal string; other integers outside the exact Float64 integer range, including large seeds or nested identifiers, are also decimal strings to prevent precision loss in JSON consumers.\n\nWith backend = :cmdstan, the helper also preserves the native chain CSV files (including warmup parameter rows), input/init JSON and adapted metric JSON, each with a hash. CmdStan compilation uses a new compile subdirectory unless the caller supplies a different fresh cmdstan_cache_dir. The record retains actual per-chain seeds, command arguments and executable hashes. The official metric JSON output is requested with save_metric=1 and 18 significant digits; rounded CSV metric comments are not treated as the exact adapted matrix. When warmup is zero, adaptation is disabled and the adapter supplies no metric file, so the record explicitly identifies the metric as the command's default identity. Dense metric entries are preserved as written, including tiny floating-point asymmetry. Parsing checks approximate symmetry with relative tolerance sqrt(eps(Float64)), positive definiteness using the lower triangle, and records the maximum asymmetry; it does not silently symmetrize the saved matrix.\n\nCmdStan's warmup metric history is unavailable: warmup metric_used and metric_after are null, while retained rows reference the final metric. Energy errors and is_accept remain unavailable. stan_lp is the native lp__ value, which omits constants; the record's log_density remains null rather than representing it as the Julia-evaluated density saved in the fit. The original CSV is copied before result parsing, including partial output when a sampling command fails. Saving it does not make a failed attempt complete.\n\nA completed recording requires status = \"complete\"; this describes recording success, not convergence or calibration. Caught errors preserve a failed record and rethrow the error. Abrupt process termination may leave only the initial started record. AdvancedHMC warmup positions, tree states and RNG checkpoints are not recorded, so this is not an exact mid-chain restart facility. It cannot recover missing history from an old fit. Its additional records must be included explicitly in any new study specification; it does not change frozen cohorts.\n\nTo inspect a completed record without fitting, use the repository review helper:\n\ninclude(\"scripts/mgmfrm_adaptation_review.jl\")\nreview = MGMFRMAdaptationReview.review(\"adaptation-run-001\"; tail_fraction=0.2)\nreview.chains[1].late_warmup\n\nFor a JSON file, run julia --project=. scripts/mgmfrm_adaptation_review.jl adaptation-run-001 review.json. The command refuses an existing output file. Input records and caches are read only. As with load_fit_cache, use trusted local Julia caches. The review requires a complete v1 record and checks the cache hash before loading it, then compares model/data/prior identity, controls, coordinate order, initial-state transforms, chain layout, retained statistics, compact warmup counts and metric timing. CmdStan CSV/data/init/metric files are hash-checked and reparsed; their retained parameter values are compared with the cache. Historical environment/source hashes remain provenance and need not match the current checkout. This checks saved correspondence, not authenticity of an arbitrarily edited bundle or the availability of its original executable.\n\nEach chain reports whole warmup, its final ceil(warmup * tail_fraction) iterations, and retained sampling separately. The default final 20% is a descriptive window, not a claim that backend adaptation schedules align. Summaries include numerical errors, maximum-depth hits, step sizes and acceptance statistics, with separate counts for unavailable and nonfinite values. CmdStan density uses stan_lp; acceptance statistics are not proportions of states that moved. Missing warmup metric history remains null for update counts, even when warmup was zero. Zero warmup has empty windows, not a successful adaptation verdict. Final metric summaries refer to the recorded sampling coordinates. No warmup threshold, convergence verdict, or calibration acceptance is assigned; incomplete records and inconsistent bundles raise an error.\n\nTuring temporarily buffers warmup transitions to produce the counts, then keeps only the chain summaries. Recording preserves the corrected adaptation schedule, retained draws and RNG stream. A corrected Turing cache created before recording was added remains reusable with unavailable history; refresh it only if that history is needed. cached_fit can reuse an older cache with unavailable warmup history; request refresh = true to collect it in a new fit. CmdStan fits can be saved/loaded explicitly, but automatic cached_fit remains limited to its supported Julia backends.\n\nFor random-walk fits, sampler_diagnostics(fit) reports n_nonfinite_proposals: proposals with non-finite parameters or log density are rejected while retaining the last valid state. The count covers retained iterations and is distinct from NUTS divergences. It is missing for older fits without this telemetry and for other samplers. Such proposals trigger a sampler warning in diagnostics(fit). Use phase = :warmup to see the separate burn-in count. Warmup-only rejections do not trigger retained-draw warnings. Random walk stores only one warmup counter per chain; recording preserves retained draws and the RNG stream.\n\nFailures during chain initialization, sampling and retained-output validation identify the backend, chain and phase. Unexpected exceptions retain their original cause and backtrace; initialization and output validation errors keep their ArgumentError type with context added to the message. CmdStan per-chain command and parser failures preserve the existing CmdStanError.stage and .reason categories. A failed chain aborts the fit; completed chains are not returned as a successful partial fit. The sampling stage covers the library sampling call, including warmup and retained transitions; an exception does not by itself identify the exact iteration or whether adaptation had finished. If fitting fails inside cached_fit, no new result is saved. With refresh = true, the previous cache remains available unchanged.\n\nThe package's gradient, sampler and CmdStan adapters rethrow Julia InterruptException, OutOfMemoryError and StackOverflowError as received. fit_report also propagates these exceptions with the default section-error capture policy.\n\nOn POSIX systems, each CmdStan build or sampling command runs in its own process group. The adapter terminates remaining group members and waits for its direct child on completion or interruption. This includes compiler and wrapper descendants that stay in the group. On other systems, cleanup covers the direct child only.\n\nJulia's SIGINT policy is preserved. Capturable interrupts propagate as InterruptException; default script SIGINT exits and explicit exit() calls use an exit hook to clean up active commands. To catch SIGINT yourself in a script, call Base.exit_on_sigint(false) before fitting. No completed command is kept in the exit-cleanup registry.\n\nProcesses that create another session or process group escape this cleanup. Forced Julia termination that bypasses exit hooks, such as SIGKILL, also bypasses cleanup.\n\nfit_metadata and diagnostics retain their complete existing payload with the default view = :full. For a reader-facing structured result, request view = :public. The public form identifies its schema, model family, and stability level in a compact portable payload. Experimental fit metadata reports the fitted configuration as estimation_status = :experimental. Public generalized diagnostics retain raw-space, constrained-space, and combined convergence metrics, parameter names, and scientific warning counts, while omitting initialization identity hashes and repository artifact paths. The public form is intended for tables and reports, while the full form remains available when the complete fitting record is required.","title":"Backends and Sampler Controls"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Experimental-Generalized-Fitting","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Generalized fitting is deliberately outside this stable fitting surface. Use the Experimental Generalized Models page for the scalar item-discrimination × rater-consistency GMFRM and fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM contracts, examples, and migration guidance.\n\nBoth configurations require thresholds = :partial_credit. Both configurations require no anchors and no fitted DFF terms. Scalar GMFRM uses discrimination = :rater; fixed-Q MGMFRM uses the compatibility selector discrimination = :none. Use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior to vary the independent normal scales on raw unconstrained coordinates. Direct-scale generalized priors remain unsupported. Run BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check before fitting to inspect score, category-use, and facet-range implications. The legacy fit(spec; experimental = true) form remains source-compatible, but BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit is the canonical entry point for new work.","title":"Experimental Generalized Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Predictive-Checks-and-Model-Comparison","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Use prior_predictive_check before interpreting a fit and posterior_predictive_check afterward. calibration_table provides expected-score and category-probability calibration rows.\n\nWAIC, LOO, PSIS-LOO, and K-fold summaries require a clearly stated prediction target and compatible observations across compared models. Treat Pareto-k and held-out diagnostics as part of the result. A numerical ranking alone is not a scientific superiority claim.\n\nAutomatic kfold_refit and loo_refit validate every fold before starting the first fit. Training observations must be exactly the heldout complement, without overlap. K-fold must hold out each observation once; LOO may target a subset or require no refits when no observations are flagged. Declared split groups stay together, and fold IDs, counts and scored-row labels must agree with the actual heldout rows and their order. Inconsistent edited or loaded plans are rejected before sampling. Training specifications preserve dimension names and match Q rows by item label. Training data retain the source order of the observed person/rater/item levels, so removing or reordering rows does not silently move reference-level raw-coordinate priors to another label. Absent fitted levels still remain absent and cannot be scored by this path.\n\nkfold_plan_diagnostics checks level availability. Its passed flag by itself does not certify a valid partition or matching source dataset. Plan row indices are positional: supply the data and row order used to define the plan. This validation does not add new-level prediction or extend automatic refitting to recorded normalized/correlated MGMFRM or fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM results. Those common-refit connections remain separate work.","title":"Predictive Checks and Model Comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Reports-and-Reproducibility","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"fit_report collects metadata, diagnostics, design checks, posterior summaries, predictive results, calibration, stable-MFRM category/rater practitioner rows, and optional comparison rows. It also includes a separate warmup table with chain-level adaptation counts and recording status. Retrieve it with fit_report_rows(report, :warmup); public reports, Markdown, JSON tables and report bundles include it automatically. The Markdown explanation distinguishes observed counts, no requested warmup, and unavailable history. Missing counts remain JSON null and blank Markdown cells. A computed warmup section means reporting succeeded; it does not apply a warmup quality threshold or change retained sampler warnings. Older reports without this section remain readable as saved. The complete version-1 payload retains its fields for machine compatibility. Use fit_report(fit; view = :public) or fit_report_public for a portable report shared with readers. fit_report_markdown applies the same projection. It preserves user-supplied person, rater, item, parameter, category, and dimension labels, and its JSON-normalized content hash remains stable after a save/load round trip. Markdown tables use a common column order before and after reload: identifiers, sampler coverage, estimates and uncertainty first, then other columns by name. Report dossiers use the same order. JSON table fields and values are unchanged; JSON object key order is not a display-order contract. Report dossiers saved by v0.1.0 remain readable; loading converts them to the same portable form before rendering or resaving.\n\nUse fit_artifact for a hash-checked fit artifact and cached_fit when cache identity is explicitly part of the workflow. The compatibility default view = :full is the complete reproduction archive and can contain repository- and environment-specific metadata; use fit_artifact(fit; view = :public) when sharing a reader-facing artifact. The same distinction applies to model_manifest and fit_reproduction_manifest: retain the full view for a private reproduction record and request view = :public for a portable public projection. A cache hit should be accepted only when model, data, prior, initialization, backend, and sampler controls match the requested fit.\n\nsensitivity_comparison_summary also accepts view = :public for compact reader-facing sensitivity results.","title":"Reports and Reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Reports-with-figures","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Load a saved fit and select the figures to include in the existing bundle:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\")\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"analysis-report\", restored;\n    view = :public,\n    require_complete = true,\n    figures = (\n        posterior = (block = :rater,),\n        diagnostics = (block = :rater,),\n        predictive = (;),\n        wright = (;),\n    ),\n    ndraws = 200,\n    seed = 42,\n)\n\nThis example selects a stable MFRM fit. It writes the ordinary JSON report and tables, plus figures/posterior.pdf, .svg, .json and corresponding files for the other selected figures. fit_report.md embeds the SVGs, explains the uncertainty and diagnostics, and links to PDFs and numerical inputs. Each PDF contains one figure; this does not create a combined PDF manuscript. Figure numbers, titles and notes are written outside the image in the Markdown report. Each numerical-input JSON also retains the exact figure_title, caption and annotation_text; report bundles do not depend on captured terminal output.\n\nEach figure entry accepts its usual selection and size options. Choose the shared posterior_lower/posterior_upper central bounds (default 0.025/0.975), predictive_interval (0.9), and ndraws or draw_indices on the bundle call. Posterior and Wright figures use those posterior bounds. Predictive figures use the exact category-summary rows already computed for the report, including unobserved declared categories. Their intervals describe replicated category proportions rather than uncertainty about a parameter. The local seed (default 1) controls predictive simulation; use it instead of rng keywords for a figure bundle. Rendering does not run MCMC or draw another predictive sample. Diagnostic settings match the standalone plots; contradictory report threshold overrides are rejected.\n\nFor GMFRM or MGMFRM, omit wright. An MGMFRM selection can be posterior = (block = :person, dimension = \"communication\"), using the fit's actual dimension label; the diagnostic entry accepts the same selection. Generalized posterior figures default to model coordinates and diagnostics to raw coordinates. The plotted subset and whole-fit warning remain visible.\n\nRead or verify the output without CairoMakie:\n\nreport = load_fit_report_bundle(\"analysis-report\"; require_complete = true)\nmanifest = load_fit_report_bundle(\"analysis-report\"; return_manifest = true)\n\nFigure bundles use manifest v2 and verify PDF, SVG and numerical-input hashes. The JSON report/table formats and figure-free v1 bundles remain unchanged. Older package readers reject v2. Numerical-input JSON includes the selected coordinates and, for diagnostic figures, their retained values and chain/ iteration IDs. Keep this in mind when sharing row-level or person-labelled outputs. Only files listed in the manifest are verified.\n\nKeep the saved fit and analysis settings to regenerate a bundle in another session. A summary-only JSON report cannot recreate traces; passing one with figures gives an error directing you to the saved fit. To edit a native Figure, use the existing plot_* functions on that fit, then save the edited figure separately. See figure editing.","title":"Reports with figures"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#FACETS-Compatible-Descriptive-Rows","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"facets_report and facets_compatibility_stats return a separately labelled posterior-mean plugin summary for supported MFRM/RSM/PCM fits. The rows use unit weights, Wright–Masters fourth-moment degrees of freedom, and a capped Wilson–Hilferty transformation. They are approximate, do not propagate full posterior uncertainty, and do not claim numerical equivalence with FACETS. Generalized fits are rejected.","title":"FACETS-Compatible Descriptive Rows"},{"category":"section","location":"fitting/#Interpretation-Boundary","page":"Bayesian Fitting","text":"Diagnose rating-design support before interpreting facet differences.\nSeparate statistical uncertainty from practical magnitude.\nTreat DFF output as screening unless a fitted, identified effect model is explicitly supported.\nDo not generalize fixed-Q results to exploratory multidimensional models.\nReport sampler warnings, prediction targets, prior choices, and unsupported model features alongside substantive results.","title":"Interpretation Boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"api-data-design/#Data-and-Design-API","page":"Data and Design","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nFunctions that expose both detailed compatibility records and concise reader-facing records use a view keyword. Existing calls retain view = :full. For reports or shared structured output, use view = :public:\n\nlayout = fit_ready_parameter_layout(spec; preview = true, view = :public)\ndomain_rows = domain_compilation_summary(spec; preview = true, view = :public)\nladder = model_ladder(view = :public)\nrating_check = rating_design_check(spec; view = :public)\nsoftware = related_software_capability_matrix(view = :public)\n\nThe public parameter-layout and domain rows report stability, fit_available, entrypoint, and claim_scope directly. They preserve raw and constrained parameter names, blocks, transforms, constraints, priors, and fixed-Q information. Stable MFRM/RSM/PCM fitting uses fit(spec); the limited scalar-GMFRM and fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM subsets use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec). A preview layout by itself is not a claim that every represented generalized configuration can be fitted.","title":"Data and Design API"},{"category":"section","location":"api-data-design/#Compatibility-diagnostic-names","page":"Data and Design","text":"rating_design_audit, model_surface_audit, and testlet_design_audit are retained at package root for source compatibility in the 0.1.x line. New reader-facing workflows should use rating_design_check, model_surface_check, and testlet_design_check. Retention does not make the detailed audit payloads a new stable extension surface.","title":"Compatibility diagnostic names"},{"category":"module","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM","page":"Data and Design","text":"BayesianMGMFRM\n\nAnalyze ratings while accounting for person ability, item difficulty and rater severity, with Bayesian uncertainty estimates and diagnostic checks.\n\nStart with FacetData to describe one observed rating per row, then validate_design to inspect the data and mfrm_spec to choose a model. fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc, chains = 4) runs Julia NUTS; its draw counts and priors still need to suit the analysis. Read diagnostics, posterior_summary and posterior_mcse before interpreting a fitted result. Use save_fit_cache and load_fit_cache to revisit it without fitting again. Stable fitting covers one-dimensional MFRM. GMFRM and multidimensional models use the restricted BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental entry points.\n\nAt the Julia prompt, type ?FacetData for a complete data example, ?fit for fitting help, and ??fit for detailed controls. In a script or notebook, @doc FacetData retrieves the documentation. The manual's Getting Started guide introduces the model choices; the repository's examples/inspect_ratings.jl runs a first data-and-model example without sampling or writing files.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe stable fitting surface supports the documented MFRM/RSM/PCM configurations. BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) additionally supports fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM in unit logits, a limited partial-credit scalar GMFRM configuration and a fixed-Q, identity-correlation confirmatory MGMFRM configuration. Explicit normalized-prior MGMFRM and two-dimensional correlated MFRM/MGMFRM configurations also have experimental fit results, diagnostics, parameter MCSE, and manual save/reload operations. See Experimental.fit for required priors and supported configurations. Unsupported generalized options are rejected before numerical execution. Computational availability does not establish replicated recovery, calibrated intervals, or a scientific model-selection recommendation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM"},{"category":"type","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.FacetData","page":"Data and Design","text":"FacetData(table; person, rater, item, score, group = nothing, task = nothing,\n          form = nothing, occasion = nothing, response_id = nothing,\n          testlet_id = nothing, category_levels = nothing,\n          missing_policy = :error)\n\nPrepare a table of observed ratings for analysis. Each row is one person's score on one item from one rater. Pass column names as Julia symbols, such as person = :student; the example below uses a named tuple of column vectors. Use category_levels to preserve the complete intended consecutive integer score scale, even if a category was not observed. Missing ratings are not score zero. By default, missing required values are rejected, and missing_policy = :error is the only supported policy. Decide how to handle missing ratings before constructing the table, preserving a record of any exclusions.\n\nReturn a FacetData object. data.n is the retained number of rating rows, and data.person_levels, data.item_levels, data.rater_levels and data.category_levels give their labels and ordering. Creating the object does not validate the measurement design or estimate parameters; call validate_design next.\n\nExamples\n\nThis artificial panel illustrates input syntax, not an adequate study size. It requires only this package and runs no sampler.\n\njulia> using BayesianMGMFRM\n\njulia> ratings = (student = [\"P1\", \"P1\", \"P1\", \"P1\", \"P2\", \"P2\", \"P2\", \"P2\"],\n                  item = [\"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\", \"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\"],\n                  rater = [\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\"],\n                  score = [0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0, 2]);\n\njulia> data = FacetData(ratings; person = :student, item = :item,\n                       rater = :rater, score = :score, category_levels = 0:2);\n\njulia> (data.n, length(data.person_levels), length(data.category_levels))\n(8, 2, 3)\n\njulia> report = validate_design(data); report.passed\ntrue\n\njulia> spec = mfrm_spec(data; validation_report = report);\n\njulia> (spec.family, spec.dimensions, spec.thresholds)\n(:mfrm, 1, :partial_credit)\n\nreport.passed means no blocking design errors were found; inspect report.issues for warnings. It does not establish model adequacy or precision. See also mfrm_spec and facet_response_table.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe object encodes labels as deterministic integer indexes. Optional columns are stored as indexed metadata; supplying them does not add fitted model terms. Use response_id for a globally unique scored response and testlet_id for its declared task or item-cluster identity. occasion remains categorical metadata; its encoded index must not be interpreted as elapsed time or row sequence. By default, the ordinal scale is the contiguous integer range from the minimum through maximum observed score. Pass category_levels, such as 0:4, to preserve an intended consecutive integer scale when the realized data omit an endpoint or another structurally possible category.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.FacetData"},{"category":"type","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.ValidationIssue","page":"Data and Design","text":"ValidationIssue\n\nMachine-readable issue produced by validate_design, with code, severity, a message, and optional context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.ValidationIssue"},{"category":"type","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.ValidationReport","page":"Data and Design","text":"ValidationReport\n\nStructured result returned by validate_design, including pass/fail status, category and facet counts, graph components, item/category and boundary-pattern warnings, requested DFF cell counts, and data signatures used to prevent stale report reuse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.ValidationReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.FacetSpec","page":"Data and Design","text":"FacetSpec\n\nMachine-readable many-facet measurement specification produced by mfrm_spec. The stable compiler and fit entrypoint support the minimal one-dimensional MFRM/RSM/PCM slice. The same object can hold GMFRM/MGMFRM configuration intent for design inspection. A narrower scalar-GMFRM and fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM subset is available through the explicitly limited BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) entrypoint; broader represented configurations remain inspection-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.FacetSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.FacetDesign","page":"Data and Design","text":"FacetDesign\n\nInspectable design object with deterministic parameter names and block ranges. Stable MFRM/RSM/PCM designs use either default first-level references or declared exact individual hard anchors for rater and item blocks, plus sum-to-zero threshold steps. GMFRM/MGMFRM preview designs expose their declared raw and constrained parameter layouts for inspection; only the narrow subset accepted by BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) is numerically available, and that limited entrypoint does not make the preview compiler a stable generalized fitting API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.FacetDesign"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.validate_design","page":"Data and Design","text":"validate_design(data::FacetData; bias = Tuple{Symbol,Symbol}[], min_cell_count = 2)\n\nCheck whether the observed ratings have design problems that should be resolved before fitting. Review category use, sparse or extreme response patterns, links between persons, raters and items, and the ability to separate their effects.\n\nReturn a ValidationReport. report.passed is false when blocking errors exist; warnings can remain when it is true. Each entry in report.issues has a severity, code and explanatory message. A passing report is not evidence of adequate sample size, correct model assumptions or MCMC convergence.\n\nExamples\n\nAfter constructing data with the complete example in FacetData:\n\nreport = validate_design(data)\nreport.passed\n[(issue.severity, issue.message) for issue in report.issues]\n\nResolve errors and review warnings before calling mfrm_spec.\n\nExtended help\n\nChecks include category coverage, singleton and extreme response patterns, person-rater-item connectedness, minimal location-design rank, item/category support, and optional DFF/bias cell counts. min_cell_count controls sparse-cell warnings; it is not a universal adequacy threshold or a way to repair missing links.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.validate_design"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.validation_suggestions","page":"Data and Design","text":"validation_suggestions(report::ValidationReport)\nvalidation_suggestions(issue::ValidationIssue)\n\nReturn machine-readable next-step suggestions for validation issues. Suggestions are intentionally conservative and are meant to guide design repair or model simplification before fitting more complex MFRM/GMFRM/MGMFRM specifications.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.validation_suggestions"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.ordinal_response_pattern_audit","page":"Data and Design","text":"ordinal_response_pattern_audit(data_or_spec_or_design;\n                               minimum_pattern_observations = 2)\n\nReturn a sampler-free audit of category gaps and constant or boundary response patterns by person, rater, and item. The audit distinguishes inputs rejected by the current fitting interface (only one category in the whole dataset) from supported stress conditions that may be weakly informed. A rejection does not establish posterior impropriety under an explicitly declared scale and proper priors. The legacy :structural_error status denotes this input boundary, not a theorem about all Bayesian ordered-response models.\n\nBy default, FacetData infers the category scale as the contiguous integer range between the observed minimum and maximum. Pass category_levels to the constructor when an intended endpoint or other structurally possible category may be absent from the realized data. The audit then reports intended, observed, interior-unobserved, and endpoint-unobserved categories separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.ordinal_response_pattern_audit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.mfrm_spec","page":"Data and Design","text":"mfrm_spec(data::FacetData; thresholds = :partial_credit,\n          family = :mfrm, dimensions = 1, discrimination = :none,\n          q_matrix = nothing, dimension_labels = nothing,\n          bias = Tuple{Symbol,Symbol}[], anchors = NamedTuple[], min_cell_count = 2,\n          validation_report = nothing)\n\nDescribe which model to fit after resolving blocking data-validation errors. Return a FacetSpec; this function does not estimate parameters or run MCMC. The default is a one-dimensional MFRM with item-specific partial-credit steps. thresholds = :rating_scale instead shares the step offsets across items.\n\nExamples\n\nUsing data from the complete example in FacetData:\n\nspec = mfrm_spec(data; thresholds = :partial_credit)\nmodel_equation(spec)\nconstraint_table(spec)\n\nThe equation and constraints describe the model you requested. Inspect its priors before fitting. Use fit for stable one-dimensional MFRM and BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit for admitted experimental models. Constructing a specification does not mean that every configuration is fit-supported.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe default family = :mfrm, dimensions = 1, and discrimination = :none path is the minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM slice supported by getdesign and fit, including valid exact individual rater/item hard anchors. Multidimensional MFRM with explicit Q and named dimensions supports partial-credit specification and getdesign(spec; preview = true) inspection. Estimate its fixed-coefficient unit-logit model through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit, using Julia or CmdStan and MFRMPrior. For a one-dimensional comparison using the MGMFRM equation, explicitly set family=:mgmfrm, dimensions=1, q_matrix=trues(n_items, 1) with partial-credit thresholds. This retains item-owned steps and the 1.7 scale constant; it is not the rater-step scalar GMFRM. Use the same explicit normalized prior form and scales in matched-dimensional comparisons, and inspect the full model contract. GMFRM/MGMFRM configurations can be represented for manifest and constraint review with estimation_status = :specified_only. The guarded generalized numerical path is narrower than this representation surface: it requires thresholds = :partial_credit, no anchors or fitted DFF terms, and the family-specific discrimination/Q contract documented by fit. Other specified-only configurations remain inspection-only and are rejected before numerical evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.mfrm_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.getdesign","page":"Data and Design","text":"getdesign(spec::FacetSpec; preview = false)\n\nCompile the identified additive MFRM/RSM/PCM parameter names, block ranges, and constraints. This inspection step is optional: fit(spec) compiles the stable specification internally. Without anchors, the first rater and first item levels are fixed to zero. An exact individual hard anchor replaces that default gauge for its block: anchored coordinates are fixed at their declared values and omitted from the sampled parameter vector. Rating-scale and partial-credit threshold steps use a sum-to-zero constraint.\n\nSpecified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM configurations are rejected by this stable compiler route so fitting code cannot silently use an unsupported likelihood. Set preview = true to compile an inspectable parameter blueprint. The documented partial-credit scalar GMFRM and fixed-Q, identity-correlation MGMFRM subsets are fit-ready only through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit; other preview configurations remain inspection-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.getdesign"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.design_identity","page":"Data and Design","text":"design_identity(design::FacetDesign)\ndesign_identity(spec::FacetSpec; preview = false)\n\nValidate that a specification still matches its original data-validation contract and that a design exactly matches the package compiler output. Return a canonical SHA-256 identity for the data, model specification, parameter order, block ranges, and identification declarations.\n\nThe identity is suitable for provenance comparisons and stale-object guards. It is not a hash of posterior draws or a substitute for a fit-cache identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.design_identity"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.constraint_table","page":"Data and Design","text":"constraint_table(spec_or_design)\n\nReturn machine-readable identification and transform declarations for a FacetSpec or FacetDesign. For specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM configurations, rows explain the declared constraint/gauge. The documented partial-credit scalar GMFRM and fixed-Q, identity-correlation MGMFRM subsets are executable only through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit; all other generalized configurations remain inspection-only. For a compiled FacetDesign, implemented rows also include parameter ranges and names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.constraint_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.identification_declarations","page":"Data and Design","text":"identification_declarations(spec_or_design)\n\nReturn machine-readable identification declarations for a FacetSpec or FacetDesign. Rows normalize constraint-table entries into reviewable components such as :sum_to_zero, :reference, :fixed, :geometric_mean_one, :hard_anchor, :soft_anchor, and :multidimensional_gauge.\n\nFor a compiled FacetDesign, rows also include parameter ranges and names when the declaration maps to an explicit parameter block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.identification_declarations"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_ladder","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_ladder(; view = :full)\n\nReturn the package's machine-readable model ladder. Rows distinguish the implemented minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM fitting slice, guarded experimental generalized fit surfaces, and broader specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM configurations. The ladder is documentation data: it is used to keep claims about fitting support separate from claims about representable specification intent. The compatibility default view = :full retains the original ladder rows. Use view = :public for rows with explicit stability, fit entrypoint, and claim scope fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_ladder"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_manifest","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_manifest(data_or_spec_or_design_or_fit; view = :full)\n\nReturn a serializable, report-ready manifest for FacetData, FacetSpec, FacetDesign, or a fitted MFRM/experimental generalized object. The manifest records facet roles, level maps, category scale, validation status, threshold structure, deterministic parameter names, block ranges, identification rules, and data signatures. The compatibility default view = :full retains complete compatibility metadata, including detailed provenance references, and is intended for private reproduction records. Use view = :public when sharing reader-facing output; that projection keeps scientific model and parameterization metadata in a concise form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_manifest"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_equation","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_equation(spec_or_design)\n\nReturn a source-traced mathematical contract for the specification. The result records the intended likelihood family, a compact adjacent-category logit kernel, primary-source references, required parameter blocks, identification restrictions, and implementation gaps.\n\nFor GMFRM/MGMFRM specifications, fit_ready continues to describe the stable compiler route and remains false. experimental_fit_available separately records whether the documented limited configuration can be executed through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit. The implementation-gap rows retain the broader literature-alignment work that is outside that limited route; implementation_gap_scope identifies that stable-compiler and broader-model boundary explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_equation"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_family_contract","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_family_contract()\nmodel_family_contract(spec_or_design)\n\nReturn a machine-readable overview of the model families, or the exact branch contract implied by a FacetSpec or FacetDesign.\n\nThe contract keeps the source's multidimensional classification separate from the algebraic dimension aggregator, distinguishes between-item, within-item, and mixed fixed-Q structures, names the PCM/GPCM-form category kernel and step owner, and records whether each branch is stable, guarded, specified-only, or blocked. It describes implementation scope; it does not fit a model, validate scientific performance, or promote an experimental branch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_family_contract"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_surface_check","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_surface_check(spec_or_design; preview = nothing, view = :full)\n\nCheck how each declared model block maps to parameters, constraints, priors, report labels, and execution availability. Use view = :public for the reader-facing projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_surface_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.q_matrix_validation","page":"Data and Design","text":"q_matrix_validation(data::FacetData; family = :mgmfrm, dimensions = nothing,\n                    q_matrix = nothing, dimension_labels = nothing,\n                    cross_loading_policy = :confirmatory_fixed)\nq_matrix_validation(spec::FacetSpec; cross_loading_policy = :confirmatory_fixed)\nq_matrix_validation(design::FacetDesign; cross_loading_policy = :confirmatory_fixed)\n\nValidate the fixed confirmatory Q-matrix contract used by guarded MGMFRM specifications. The returned manifest records binary mask schema checks, item/dimension coverage, duplicate or aliased dimension columns, fixed cross-loading policy, the generic structural rank of the Q zero pattern, person-specific dimension support, simple loading anchors, the standard-normal identity-correlation prior anchor, and dimension-specific person-rater-item subgraph coverage. Error rows make passed = false; warning rows remain actionable review evidence without rejecting the guarded fixed-Q spec. identification.conservative_stable_structure_ready is a stricter sufficient promotion screen; it is not by itself evidence of empirical recovery or stable public readiness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.q_matrix_validation"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.fit_ready_parameter_layout","page":"Data and Design","text":"fit_ready_parameter_layout(spec_or_design; preview = false, view = :full)\n\nReturn deterministic parameter names and block ranges for each compiled likelihood layout currently represented by the package. The fit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM path reports direct parameter blocks. Specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM preview designs report experimental raw and constrained fit-ready blocks, including raw-to-constrained transform rows, without enabling broad public generalized fitting.\n\nFor specified-only FacetSpec values, pass preview = true, matching getdesign. The compatibility default view = :full retains the complete compiler record. Use view = :public for a reader-facing projection that states stable or experimental fit availability directly while retaining the scientific raw/constrained parameter layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.fit_ready_parameter_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.domain_compilation_summary","page":"Data and Design","text":"domain_compilation_summary(spec_or_design; preview = false, view = :full)\n\nReturn a review table showing how domain options were compiled into the current design contract. Rows cover likelihood family, additive/location blocks, discrimination or loading blocks, scoring vectors, constraints, priors, fixed Q-masks, validation requirements, DFF/bias validation terms, and anchors.\n\nFit-supported MFRM/RSM/PCM designs report fit_ready = true and public_fit = true. Specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM previews report the experimental parameterization without enabling broad generalized fitting. For specified-only FacetSpec values, pass preview = true, matching getdesign. The compatibility default view = :full retains the complete compiler record. Use view = :public for rows that state stable or experimental fit availability directly, omit compiler-control fields, and redact non-scientific anchor metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.domain_compilation_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.design_row_table","page":"Data and Design","text":"design_row_table(spec_or_design; preview = false)\n\nReturn one row of machine-readable design metadata per observed rating. The table records the facet level indexes and labels, identified parameter indexes and names touched by the row, the source-step path up to the observed category, and preview-only generalized blocks such as item discrimination, item-dimension discrimination, and rater consistency when a specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM design is compiled with preview = true.\n\nFor a FacetSpec, specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM configurations are rejected unless preview = true, matching getdesign. The returned table is a compiler inspection aid; it does not make specified-only likelihoods fit-ready.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.design_row_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.linear_predictor_table","page":"Data and Design","text":"linear_predictor_table(spec_or_design; preview = false)\n\nReturn one row per observed rating and response category describing the source-level linear-predictor components used by the current design compiler. Rows include the category-specific location multiplier, facet parameter indexes, source-step path, and generalized item/rater blocks when a specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM design is compiled with preview = true.\n\nThe table is a compiler-inspection artifact for checking denominator terms and source-equation alignment. It does not make specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM likelihoods fit-ready.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.linear_predictor_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.linear_predictor_values","page":"Data and Design","text":"linear_predictor_values(spec_or_design, params; preview = false)\n\nReturn one row per observed rating and response category with numeric linear-predictor values for the fit-supported minimal MFRM/RSM/PCM design. Rows include the same compiler metadata as linear_predictor_table, plus identified person/rater/item values, the additive location value, threshold-step values, their sum, the category score eta, the row log denominator, and the category log probability.\n\nNumeric values are deliberately not implemented for specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM previews. The limited BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec) entrypoint performs its own guarded generalized evaluation, but it does not extend this stable compiler-inspection helper to generalized parameter vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.linear_predictor_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.initial_params","page":"Data and Design","text":"initial_params(design_or_spec_or_target; value = 0.0)\n\nReturn a parameter vector filled with Float64(value). The converted value must be finite. For a design or specification, entries follow getdesign(...).parameter_names; log-density targets use their own parameter ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.initial_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.loglikelihood","page":"Data and Design","text":"loglikelihood(design::FacetDesign, params)\nloglikelihood(spec::FacetSpec, params)\nloglikelihood(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nloglikelihood(fit::Union{GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit}; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nEvaluate the total minimal additive MFRM/RSM/PCM log likelihood for the identified parameter vector returned by getdesign, without Bayesian priors. For fit objects, return one total log-likelihood value per selected posterior draw. Guarded generalized fits use their stored constrained direct log-likelihood values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.loglikelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.logposterior","page":"Data and Design","text":"logposterior(design::FacetDesign, params, prior = MFRMPrior())\nlogposterior(spec::FacetSpec, params, prior = MFRMPrior())\nlogposterior(fit::Union{MFRMFit,GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit}; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nEvaluate the log posterior for the minimal additive MFRM/RSM/PCM design: the sum of loglikelihood(design, params) plus logprior(design, params, prior) on the identified parameter vector. For fit objects, return the stored log posterior for each selected posterior draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.logposterior"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.logprior","page":"Data and Design","text":"logprior(design::FacetDesign, params, prior = MFRMPrior())\nlogprior(spec::FacetSpec, params, prior = MFRMPrior())\nlogprior(fit::MFRMFit; ndraws = nothing, draw_indices = nothing,\n    rng = Random.default_rng())\nlogprior(fit::Union{GMFRMFit,MGMFRMFit}; ndraws = nothing,\n    draw_indices = nothing, rng = Random.default_rng())\n\nEvaluate the independent normal log prior for the identified minimal MFRM parameter vector returned by getdesign. For fit objects, return one log-prior value per selected posterior draw. Guarded generalized fits report the raw-prior contribution implied by their stored log posterior minus direct log likelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.logprior"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.threshold_map_data","page":"Data and Design","text":"threshold_map_data(spec_or_design; params = nothing)\n\nReturn long-form threshold-step metadata for rating-scale or partial-credit threshold maps. When params is supplied, the returned rows include the identified threshold-step value, including the sum-to-zero derived step. Without params, value is missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.threshold_map_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.facet_response_table","page":"Data and Design","text":"facet_response_table(data::FacetData; observations = nothing)\nfacet_response_table(spec::FacetSpec; kwargs...)\nfacet_response_table(design::FacetDesign; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a role-normalized long-format response table from encoded FacetData. The table is a named tuple with person, rater, item, and score vectors, plus optional facet vectors such as group, task, form, occasion, response_id, or testlet_id when present. Pass observations to materialize a selected row order, for example a training or heldout row set from kfold_plan.\n\nThe returned table uses role names rather than the original input column names, so it can be passed back to FacetData(table; person = :person, rater = :rater, item = :item, score = :score, category_levels = data.category_levels, ...) when a role-normalized split is desired. Preserve category_levels explicitly when the selected rows may omit an intended category.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.facet_response_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.coverage_summary","page":"Data and Design","text":"coverage_summary(data_or_spec)\n\nReturn fit-independent coverage summaries for long-format rating data. The result is a NamedTuple containing rating counts, category counts, long-form facet-level counts, compact facet summaries, and validation status when a FacetSpec or FacetDesign is supplied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.coverage_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.coverage_matrix","page":"Data and Design","text":"coverage_matrix(data_or_spec; rows = :rater, columns = :person)\n\nReturn a facet-by-facet rating-count matrix for heat maps and design coverage checks. rows and columns may be required facets (:person, :rater, :item) or optional metadata roles present in FacetData, such as :group, :task, :form, :occasion, :response_id, or :testlet_id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.coverage_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.rater_overlap","page":"Data and Design","text":"rater_overlap(data_or_spec; unit = :person_item)\n\nReturn pairwise rater-overlap data for coverage and linking plots. unit controls what counts as a shared rated unit and may be :person, :item, :person_item, :task, :person_task, :response_id, :testlet_id, :person_testlet, or :response_item. Metadata-based units require the corresponding optional role in FacetData. Rows for :testlet_id and :person_testlet are explicitly marked as descriptive coverage and are not eligible for rater-link connectivity. Rows for :response_id and :response_item are marked as common-response linking units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rater_overlap"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.anchor_linking_summary","page":"Data and Design","text":"anchor_linking_summary(data_or_spec; unit = :person_item,\n    min_shared_units = 1, sensitivity_rows = nothing, view = :full)\n\nReturn a compact anchoring and rater-linking diagnostic summary. The summary combines declared hard/soft anchor rows from a FacetSpec or FacetDesign, pairwise rater-overlap connectivity for the chosen linking unit, and an optional anchor-axis sensitivity coverage check from sensitivity_comparison_summary.\n\nThis is a report and design-review helper. It does not fit hard anchors, turn soft anchors into priors, estimate linking constants, or create sensitivity refits. Use it to document whether declared anchors are internally consistent and whether raters are connected strongly enough for a planned analysis. unit = :testlet_id and :person_testlet are descriptive coverage units and are rejected here; use rater_overlap to inspect them without a linking claim. The compatibility default view = :full retains the complete design-review record. Use view = :public for a concise diagnostic projection without workflow-control fields or attached sensitivity records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.anchor_linking_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.anchor_refit_plan","page":"Data and Design","text":"anchor_refit_plan(spec_or_design; require_provenance = true)\n\nReturn machine-readable declaration validation for anchor-constrained re-estimation. The accepted declaration scope is deliberately narrow: explicit individual hard anchors on rater severity or item difficulty in the minimal Bayesian MFRM/RSM/PCM family. Each candidate anchor is checked for a finite value, an observed level, duplicate or conflicting declarations, and typed source, version, model, estimator, SHA-256, source-scale, and sign provenance. Boolean values and finite numbers that cannot be represented as finite Float64 coordinates are rejected. require_provenance = false permits absent provenance, but any provenance field that is supplied must still satisfy the fail-closed field contract.\n\nThe version-1 source_scale and sign fields describe the already transformed anchor value in destination logit coordinates. Original source scale/sign and the applied transform must be preserved in the artifact named by source_hash. Only the lowercase SHA-256 string format is checked; source bytes are not verified by this declaration check.\n\nAn explicit hard anchor must not also declare scale, sd, or prior_scale. A soft anchor on the current first-level-zero rater/item reference is rejected because its prior would be constant under that gauge; an anchor-aware estimator must reparameterize the reference or transform the source anchor to an identified contrast.\n\nThis function performs no fit. Semantically valid individual hard anchors are accepted by the stable mfrm_spec/getdesign/fit route; provenance remains an optional audit layer and is not a numerical fitting prerequisite. Use require_provenance = false for a distribution-oriented declaration check that does not require source hashes or external artifact metadata. Input data must already pass the ordinary connectedness and rank gates: individual parameter anchors do not create observed graph links, and group-anchored alignment of disconnected components is not implemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.anchor_refit_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.facets_bridge_bundle","page":"Data and Design","text":"facets_bridge_bundle(spec_or_design;\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM FACETS overlap bridge\",\n    include_original_labels = false)\n\nCompile a portable, unanchored FACETS input bundle for the fit-supported one-dimensional MFRM/RSM/PCM model. The bundle contains a manual-syntax control file, rating data, generated ASCII identifiers, category and observation maps, SHA-256 manifests, and a Windows runner. It never launches FACETS and never includes an executable or licence. Retain the returned host_preflight bundle ID and runner/verifier hashes through a separate trusted channel; transferred scripts are not their own trust anchor.\n\nOriginal labels are omitted by default; unsalted deterministic hashes of their canonical representations remain in id_map.tsv. This is pseudonymization, not anonymization: guessable labels can be dictionary matched and equal canonical labels can be linked across bundles. Set include_original_labels = true only when the transfer host is authorized to receive those labels. Row-level ratings are always present and should be handled as sensitive data. Both declared category endpoints must occur globally; PCM items must also contain both endpoints so the external category universe cannot silently narrow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.facets_bridge_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.conquest_bridge_bundle","page":"Data and Design","text":"conquest_bridge_bundle(spec_or_design;\n    title = \"BayesianMGMFRM ConQuest overlap bridge\",\n    include_original_labels = false)\n\nCompile a portable, unanchored ACER ConQuest input bundle for the fit-supported one-dimensional MFRM/RSM/PCM model. Sparse ratings remain one row per observed rating; ConQuest's documented pid mapping links rows belonging to the same person. The generated command uses MML with Gauss-Hermite integration. The bundle includes a Windows verifier/runner and a macOS verifier/runner; neither path supplies ConQuest itself.\n\nRepeated person-item-rater cells, rater-item generalized items without both declared category endpoints, and PCM category holes are rejected because they would need a more explicit external model contract. This function does not run ConQuest and does not include an executable or licence. Canonical-label hashes are unsalted pseudonyms, not an anonymization mechanism; row-level ratings remain sensitive. Retain the bundle ID and platform-specific verifier/runner hashes from host_preflight through a separate trusted channel before transfer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.conquest_bridge_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.save_external_bridge_bundle","page":"Data and Design","text":"save_external_bridge_bundle(directory, bundle; overwrite = false)\n\nWrite a bundle returned by facets_bridge_bundle or conquest_bridge_bundle to a transfer directory. Existing files are protected unless overwrite = true. Overwrite accepts only an unexecuted directory whose existing paths are declared by the new bundle; results must be absent or empty. Every written input is verified before the function returns. Writing to a fresh directory is preferred because guarded overwrite is not an atomic recovery mechanism for an interrupted filesystem write.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.save_external_bridge_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.validate_external_bridge_bundle","page":"Data and Design","text":"validate_external_bridge_bundle(directory; expected_bundle_id = nothing)\n\nVerify the manifest hash, full manifest-contract identity, normalized relative paths, and SHA-256/byte length of every input in a saved external-software bundle. Undeclared entries outside results/ are rejected. Pass the bundle_id retained on the originating machine as expected_bundle_id when validating a directory returned from another host. The returned host_preflight contains runner/verifier hashes for an independent comparison before any transferred script is executed.\n\nThis checks transfer integrity only. It does not execute FACETS or ConQuest and does not validate returned estimates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.validate_external_bridge_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_conquest_parameter_export","page":"Data and Design","text":"load_conquest_parameter_export(path; expected_sha256 = nothing)\n\nRead ConQuest's documented text export parameters format as unique positive parameter-number/value pairs. A pair may be followed by one well-formed, single-line /* ... */ source comment; its trimmed content is returned as source_comment, without treating it as a resolved parameter identity. The parser rejects headers, malformed rows, duplicate parameter numbers, nonfinite values, lines longer than 4096 bytes, and exports above 1,000,000 pairs. It streams the source bytes while hashing and parsing, but the returned row collection is necessarily proportional to the number of parameter pairs. It does not infer semantic item, rater, or step labels from positional parameter numbers.\n\nPass the SHA-256 recorded by external_bridge_result_receipt as expected_sha256 when reading a transferred file later.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_conquest_parameter_export"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.load_conquest_semantic_parameters","page":"Data and Design","text":"load_conquest_semantic_parameters(directory, spec_or_design;\n    expected_bundle_id,\n    software_version,\n    executable_sha256,\n    executed_at_utc)\n\nResolve the parameter identities in a returned ConQuest bridge bundle for the version-specific, three-category, one-dimensional additive MFRM/RSM/PCM contract. The function verifies the complete input bundle, constructs a receipt from the current returned-file snapshot, requires an exact match between the supplied specification and the hash-bound control, rating, identifier-map, category-map, and observation-map inputs, and then jointly checks the parameter comments and exported design matrix.\n\nThe returned rows are ConQuest source-gauge values. Under lconstraints=items, the last rater and item values and the last shared or item-specific step are reconstructed as the negative sum of the corresponding free values. No global sign reversal is applied: raters use severity_positive, items use difficulty_positive, and steps enter as subtractive transitions. Empty conquest_labels.txt output is allowed because semantic identity comes from the jointly verified bundle inputs, comments, and design matrix.\n\nThis first adapter is deliberately restricted to ConQuest 5.47.5 and exactly three ordered categories, the executed RSM/PCM fixture boundary currently available in the package. It does not validate convergence, align values to the package's first-reference gauge, construct a fit parameter vector or anchors, permit a numerical-comparison claim, or claim estimator/software equivalence. Use load_conquest_parameter_export when only the raw numbered values are needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.load_conquest_semantic_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.external_bridge_result_receipt","page":"Data and Design","text":"external_bridge_result_receipt(directory;\n    expected_bundle_id,\n    software_version,\n    executable_sha256,\n    executed_at_utc)\n\nVerify a bundle returned from a licensed execution host. The function first rechecks the retained bundle_id and every input, then requires all declared outputs, no undeclared result files or directories, a zero process exit code, regular non-symlink files, no execution lock, and no recognized fatal marker. Every returned file is streamed once and bound to its byte length and SHA-256 digest. Fatal-marker inspection and ConQuest parameter-row validation run in that same streaming pass. Raw output content is not retained except for the strictly bounded process-exit record.\n\nThe software version, executable digest, and execution time are operator- supplied metadata; this function cannot authenticate the executable. A valid receipt records reported completion but does not independently verify that the external executable ran. It also does not validate convergence, semantic parameter identities, gauge alignment, numerical agreement, or product equivalence. Those comparison claims remain disabled until version-specific output samples and adapters are checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.external_bridge_result_receipt"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.rating_design_check","page":"Data and Design","text":"rating_design_check(data_or_spec_or_design; unit = :person_item,\n    min_shared_units = 1, min_sparse_cell_count = 2, view = :full)\n\nCheck the observed rating design before fitting. The result describes graph connectivity, overlap, coverage, repeated ratings, sparse cells, optional time/order fields, and assignment limitations. Use view = :public for the reader-facing projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rating_design_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.testlet_design_check","page":"Data and Design","text":"testlet_design_check(data_or_spec_or_design; kwargs...)\n\nCheck whether clustered-response metadata support the requested testlet or local-dependence design. The result separates structural identification, candidate scope, current fitting support, and calibration status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.testlet_design_check"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.rating_design_audit","page":"Data and Design","text":"rating_design_audit(data_or_spec_or_design; unit = :person_item,\n    min_shared_units = 1, min_sparse_cell_count = 2, view = :full)\n\nReturn a report-ready audit of the observed rating design before fitting. Rows summarize person-rater-item graph components, rater overlap strength, anchor coverage, complete-grid coverage for required facet combinations, repeated ratings, sparse observed person-rater-item blocks, optional time/order fields, and the interpretation limitation caused by unmodeled rater assignment.\n\nThe current FacetData contract stores complete observed long-format rows but does not store an external planned-design table. Consequently the audit counts unobserved complete-grid cells and explicitly marks structural versus accidental missingness as not identified from the observed data alone. unit = :testlet_id and :person_testlet are descriptive coverage units and cannot be used for the audit's rater-link connectivity decision. The compatibility default view = :full retains the complete audit record. Use view = :public for a reader-facing projection with a public nested anchor summary and without model-compiler status fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rating_design_audit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.model_surface_audit","page":"Data and Design","text":"model_surface_audit(spec_or_design; preview = nothing, view = :full)\n\nReturn a machine-readable audit table for the current model surface. Rows trace each parameter or validation block from the source-equation symbol to the direct interpretation, raw coordinate, constraint, prior scale, report label, and current status policy.\n\nThe compatibility default view = :full retains additional provenance fields. Use view = :public for a concise reader-facing result. Public rows keep model execution availability (execution_status, fit_available, and fit_entrypoint) separate from each block's declaration_status.\n\nFor specified-only GMFRM/MGMFRM specs, preview defaults to true so the audit can inspect the represented source-aligned blocks without enabling broad public fitting. Passing a compiled FacetDesign audits that design directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.model_surface_audit"},{"category":"function","location":"api-data-design/#BayesianMGMFRM.testlet_design_audit","page":"Data and Design","text":"testlet_design_audit(data_or_spec_or_design;\n    target = :scalar_shared_cluster,\n    support_profile = :ld0_structural_v1,\n    min_indicators_per_response = 2,\n    min_testlets_per_person = 2,\n    min_persons_per_testlet = 2,\n    independent_ratings_declared = false,\n    max_materialized_pair_rows = 200_000,\n    max_pair_common_unit_links = 10_000_000)\n\nAudit the clustered-rating design encoded by response_id and testlet_id metadata before fitting a local-dependence or testlet extension. The audit checks globally unique response nesting, duplicate rating keys, response and person-by-testlet replication, mechanism-specific graph connectivity, rater-by-response halo support, repeated-response support, rater-by-task crossing, and fixed-Q dimension-by-testlet coverage when applicable. Before quadratic item/rater pair tables are expanded, an O(n) preflight bounds their total row count with max_materialized_pair_rows and the total pair by common-unit incidence count with max_pair_common_unit_links. The graph bridge check uses a linear-time undirected-graph traversal.\n\nThe top-level status is target-specific. :ok means only that the unchanged frozen structural profile is met; it does not show that a cluster effect exists. :warning records a weak, custom, or not-yet-fit-supported candidate, :unsupported_candidate distinguishes a current candidate-shape limitation from structural underidentification, :underidentified blocks the requested structure, and :error denotes an invalid identifier contract. structurally_eligible_for_candidate, structural_profile_met, and current_fit_supported are intentionally separate. mechanism_claim_eligible remains false until the diagnostic and model families have been calibrated under independent known-truth generators. The current audit targets are :scalar_shared_cluster, :stable_person_testlet, :rater_response_halo, :rater_task, and :mgmfrm_testlet_separation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.testlet_design_audit"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Saved-fit-Reporting-Candidate-API","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"These qualified candidate functions summarize the eight common saved-fit types. Posterior summaries consume original retained draws and preserve numerical warnings and source identity. The separate observed-score alpha and ICC operations consume ratings and also accept FacetData without a fit. No fitting is performed. Their uncertainty definitions differ: a finite-cohort reliability functional, matched-response score agreement, category use/steps/conditional curves, binary classification risk, and finite-cohort ranks answer distinct questions.\n\nRead the interpretation guide and the saved-fit examples before reporting their values. The standalone functions can also be requested together through fit_report(...; reporting=(...)); see the integrated example below. The ordinary MFRM diagnostics, including the historical replicated-category summary, remain in the Workflow and Diagnostics API.","title":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate API"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Reopen-an-analysis-or-share-its-results","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"Choose the saved format for the task:\n\nTask Saved format and limitation\nRecompute summaries or request another analysis from retained draws Use save_fit_cache / load_fit_cache in the original Julia and package environment. Keep the Julia version (VERSION), Project.toml and Manifest.toml with the cache. A .jls cache uses Julia's native serialization; package support for two Julia versions does not guarantee that their caches are interchangeable.\nRead or share already computed results Use save_fit_report_bundle / load_fit_report_bundle. The bundle contains portable JSON reports and tables, with Markdown for reading. It preserves the reported values, warnings and identities; it does not reconstruct a fitted object or supply draws for a new analysis.\n\nFor example, a cache written with Julia 1.12 can be rejected by Julia 1.10 with Cannot read stream serialized with a newer version of Julia. Reopen that cache in its original environment and export a report bundle there. Renaming the file does not convert its format. An inability to read this binary cache is separate from the package's ability to run an analysis under Julia 1.10.\n\nKeeping the Julia version alone is not sufficient. A fresh project can resolve newer dependency versions even when BayesianMGMFRM itself is pinned. Recomputed diagnostics may then differ at floating-point precision and fail the native cache's exact diagnostic checks. Preserve and restore the complete original Project.toml and Manifest.toml; do not bypass integrity checks or refit merely to read saved results. Export a portable report in the original environment when the recipient only needs the computed results.","title":"Reopen an analysis or share its results"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#One-report-with-explicit-analysis-choices","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"The optional reporting named tuple is shared by all eight supported saved-fit types. Omitting it preserves the previous report behavior. Choose sections by supplying category=(...), agreement=(...) and/or a reliability tuple/vector of named cohorts. The optional classification=(;parameters, cutoff, pass_probability, interval=0.90) adds a binary decision section; omitting it preserves the previous report shape. A missing legacy section is :not_requested; no cohort, target or response matching is silently inferred.\n\nOptional alpha=(item=\"fluency\", level=:ordinal, response_ids=performance_ids) adds observed-score alpha with the same dataset/rating identities. It uses no posterior draws and supplies no SE or interval; see its API below.\n\nOptional icc=(item=\"fluency\", model=:two_way_random, definition=:agreement, unit=:single, score_scale=:interval, response_ids=performance_ids) adds intraclass_correlation. It requires a complete response-by-rater rectangle for the selected item and an explicit equal-gap score assumption. There is no silent complete-case deletion. The observed-data section supplies no SE or interval; the existing fit diagnostics remain visible in their own section.\n\nreporting = (;\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    category=(;interval=0.99),\n    agreement=(;response_ids=performance_ids, interval=0.99, se_unit=:person),\n    reliability=[(;id=\"Ability A\", parameters=person_names_A, target=:centered)])\n\nreport = fit_report(fit_result; reporting, view=:public,\n    include_posterior_predictive=false, include_artifact=false,\n    include_waic=false, include_loo=false)\nreport.category_report.usage_rows\nreport.posterior_reliability.rows\nreport.rater_agreement.rows\nfit_report_health(report)\n\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"assessment-report\", report; require_complete=true)\nrestored = load_fit_report_bundle(\"assessment-report\"; require_complete=true)\n\nrating_ids are persistent unique IDs in fitted-row order. performance_ids identify actual shared performances; existing fitted response metadata may be used by omitting that vector. The category and agreement sections share the same dataset/rating identities. When normalized/correlated MGMFRM or multidimensional MFRM reports also request predictive criteria, their top-level dataset_id and observation_ids must match these identities.\n\nperson_names_A must contain exact estimated location-coordinate names for one ability dimension. Inspect BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit_result) and choose the cohort deliberately. Add a separate named cohort for another dimension; select target=:absolute or :centered to match the question. Optional normalized weights and the coordinate roster remain in the results. Fixed anchors are not valid members of a reliability cohort.\n\nEach category/agreement options tuple forwards the corresponding standalone API's keywords, except their shared dataset/rating IDs. For example, category options can contain profiles, and agreement options can choose an adjacent-category tolerance. A reliability cohort accepts id, parameters, target and optional weights. All three sections use every original retained draw, regardless of ndraws or draw_indices used for the report's other prediction summaries.\n\nNew section names are category_report, posterior_reliability and rater_agreement. The historical ordinary-MFRM category_functioning section keeps its different predictive-replication interpretation and existing defaults. The new flattened rows are intended for tables; result (or reliability results) retains detailed quantities, MCSE, source identities and assumptions. source_result_hash identifies the original standalone API result before report projection. It is not a hash of the projected result; the full report and its public/bundle exports have their own integrity records.\n\non_section_error=:capture retains section failures and successful neighboring sections. Failed reliability cohorts stay listed alongside successful cohorts. on_section_error=:throw raises the underlying error. require_complete=true rejects captured failures; it does not establish convergence, MCSE adequacy, measurement validity or the availability of every optional quantity. Unknown request keys, inconsistent IDs and ambiguous cohort names fail before reporting. Sampling, numerical, category-review and no-overlap warnings remain visible in Markdown and table/JSON exports. No new model fitting is performed.","title":"One report with explicit analysis choices"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Finite-cohort-posterior-reliability","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"","title":"Finite-cohort posterior reliability"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Common-response-score-agreement","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"","title":"Common-response score agreement"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Observed-score-Krippendorff-alpha","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"This operation takes FacetData and needs no fit. In an existing fit report, request reporting=(dataset_id=\"study\", observation_ids=rating_ids, alpha=(item=\"fluency\", level=:ordinal, response_ids=response_ids)). The optional krippendorff_alpha section preserves observed disagreement, expected disagreement, category margins and excluded singleton IDs through public JSON/Markdown/table bundles. Omission leaves existing report fields unchanged. Its descriptive status does not certify the fitted model.","title":"Observed-score Krippendorff alpha"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Owner-aware-category-functioning","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"","title":"Owner-aware category functioning"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Conditional-ability-precision-and-classification","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"To include this result in the same public report and bundle, add classification=(;parameters=person_names_A, cutoff=study_cutoff, pass_probability=study_probability_rule, interval=0.99) to reporting. It needs no observation IDs of its own. posterior_classification.rows contains individual results and posterior_classification.cohort_rows contains the four cohort summaries; both are exported as tables. This option also works in each model's reporting_by_model entry in saved_prediction_workflow. The required rules must be justified on each model's fitted ability scale; sharing their numeric values across models does not establish scale equivalence.\n\nInvalid coordinates/rules follow the same section-error and require_complete policies as other reports. Captured errors remain errors on public export and reload. Read the decision interpretation before presenting a probability as evidence for a pass/fail decision.","title":"Conditional ability precision and classification"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Finite-cohort-posterior-ranking","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"Add ranking=(;parameters=person_names_A, direction=:higher, top_k=3, interval=0.99) to reporting for a cohort with at least three people. posterior_ranking.rows exports each person's rank summary and top-k probability bounds, with cohort size, direction, interval and tie policy included in the table. Full MCSE/status details, diagnostics and source identity remain in posterior_ranking.result. The same request works in the per-model reporting_by_model entries of saved_prediction_workflow.\n\nOmitting ranking or supplying nothing preserves the preceding report shape. An invalid cohort or exceeded max_rank_cells follows the existing captured-error and require_complete policy. A complete report still may contain unavailable MCSE. The ranking interpretation guide explains pointwise intervals, cohort dependence, ties and what ranks omit.","title":"Finite-cohort posterior ranking"},{"category":"section","location":"api-saved-reporting/#Observed-score-intraclass-correlation","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"icc = BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation(data;\n    item=\"fluency\", model=:two_way_random, definition=:agreement,\n    unit=:single, score_scale=:interval,\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    response_ids=performance_ids)\nicc.icc\nicc.status\nicc.anova_rows\nicc.unit_rows\n\nHere N is the number of response units, K the number of raters, and MS_response, MS_rater and MS_error are the two-way ANOVA mean squares. The numerator in all four point formulas is MS_response - MS_error:\n\nDefinition and unit Denominator\nAgreement, single MS_response + (K-1)*MS_error + K*(MS_rater-MS_error)/N\nAgreement, average MS_response + (MS_rater-MS_error)/N\nConsistency, single MS_response + (K-1)*MS_error\nConsistency, average MS_response\n\nThe residual combines response-by-rater interaction and measurement error; with one rating per cell these components cannot be separated. Negative component differences are not set to zero. A negative ICC with a positive denominator is retained; a zero or negative denominator returns missing ICC with a distinct status, retaining any finite raw_ratio for inspection. That raw ratio is not an admissible reliability result. Constant scores everywhere therefore do not produce an informative coefficient. Near-zero denominators can make ratios unstable; inspect the ANOVA table rather than interpreting a cutoff mechanically.","title":"Observed-score intraclass correlation"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability(fit; parameters, target, weights=nothing)\n\nSummarize finite-cohort posterior reliability from an existing supported fit. Select at least two estimated location coordinates by exact names from recovery_quantities(fit), all in the same person/item/rater block and dimension. Fixed anchors, loadings, thresholds and mixed dimensions are rejected. Optional positive weights follow parameters order and are normalized to sum to one; the default gives each selected level equal weight, not each observed rating.\n\nThe required target=:absolute uses locations in the fitted coordinate system. target=:centered subtracts the selected cohort's weighted mean within each joint draw, retaining covariance and removing common-location uncertainty. Subset selection and weights therefore define both the cohort and its reference.\n\nFor selected means mᵢ, posterior variances vᵢ, and normalized weights wᵢ, return B = sum(wᵢ*(mᵢ-sum(wⱼ*mⱼ))^2), W = sum(wᵢ*vᵢ) and B/(B+W). This is the between-level fraction of variance of a weighted mixture of the selected posterior marginals. Both level and retained-draw moments use normalized probability weights (no Bessel correction). It is a posterior functional estimated by pooled complete chains, not a distribution of reliability over draws. No CrI, FACETS separation/Strata, population, retest or new-design reliability is inferred. Zero total variance returns missing; empirical zero posterior variance is flagged, never treated as proof that the sampler resolved a parameter exactly.\n\nmcse uses the first-order influence of the ratio with the existing chain-aware asymptotic-variance estimator. It is numerical error, not a credible interval. It is unavailable at zero between/within variance, for short/single chains or degenerate influence. Finite-draw bias and convergence are not certified by MCSE. Sampling warnings, exact selected coordinates, model/prior identity and source hash status are retained. Shared calibration uncertainty is included only to the extent represented in the saved joint draws; fixed calibration cannot be restored. High rater reliability means distinguishable severity, not score agreement.\n\nThis qualified candidate entry supports the eight common saved-fit types and preserves each model's support limits. It does not replace the historical separation_reliability_summary numerically or validate its old interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement(fit; dataset_id, observation_ids,\n    response_ids=nothing, interval=0.95, adjacent=1, se_unit=:none,\n    max_prediction_cells=20_000_000, max_rater_pairs=10_000)\n\nCompare observed and model-expected agreement for the same response and item. Uses the eight common saved-fit types, all original retained draws and category probabilities. Supply unique rating observation_ids in fitted-row order and globally unique response identities through fitted response_id metadata or an explicit response_ids vector (nonempty strings or integers). Repeated response IDs across raters/items are expected; one ID must belong to one person and one combination of all recorded optional metadata. Supplied and fitted IDs must agree. Duplicate response-item-rater rows are rejected. Matching by person-item alone is never inferred, and omitted context cannot be verified from the scores.\n\nReturn overall, per-rater and every unordered fitted-rater-pair row, retaining zero-overlap rows and unpaired observation IDs. Each common response-item and unordered rater pair contributes one opportunity. Overall weights are equal per opportunity, not per person, response or rater pair. Per-rater totals count its incident opportunities, so their denominators sum to twice the overall denominator. The opportunity ledger preserves both rating IDs and the response/item/person.\n\nexact uses the original category labels. adjacent includes exact agreement and categories at most the declared number of ordinal positions apart. It does not realign a rater's lowest observed PCM category as some FACETS reports do. For each joint posterior draw, expected exact agreement is sum_k p_A(k)*p_B(k); adjacent agreement sums the corresponding category pairs. Conditional independence is assumed given that draw. Averaging separately by rater before multiplying would lose shared-parameter dependence and is not used.\n\nIntervals are central posterior intervals for the conditional expected rate on these fitted responses and raters, not posterior predictive intervals for realized agreement, population CIs or evidence for a dependence mechanism. The mean observed-minus-expected discrepancy is descriptive, not a p-value. MCMC MCSE for expected means/quantiles is reported separately; short/degenerate draws retain unavailable status. If the tolerance spans the whole category scale, the adjacent probability is structurally one and labeled accordingly.\n\nse_unit=:person optionally adds a ratio-linearization SE for observed rates with independent, exchangeable person clusters, preserving all within-person opportunities together. It conditions on these raters/items and does not handle arbitrary crossed dependence or sampling-design weights. Default :none reports no observed-rate SE. Fewer than two persons is unavailable; zero empirical cluster variance is flagged. No observed CI, kappa, automatic cutoff or reliability correction is computed. Resource limits reject oversized extraction/rosters before allocating the predictions; they do not subsample or omit pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha(data::FacetData; item, level,\n    dataset_id, observation_ids, response_ids=nothing,\n    max_category_pairs=1_000_000)\n\nCompute observed-score Krippendorff alpha for one explicitly selected item (use its label, not its index). level is required: :nominal or :ordinal. No model fit or posterior draws are used. Integer category codes and their order come from data.category_levels; nominal codes are labels, not distances. Ordinal distances use pairable category frequencies, not squared score gaps.\n\nUnique rating IDs are in data-row order. Supply globally unique response IDs explicitly or through response_id metadata, as in rater_agreement. A response must identify the same person and recorded context. Duplicate response-item-rater rows are rejected; repeated occasions need separate response IDs. Items are never pooled. This checks recorded identity, not construct validity.\n\nMissing ratings are absent rows (FacetData rejects literal missing scores). Units with one rating are listed but excluded from both disagreements and the category margins. Entirely unobserved units cannot be counted from these data. Each usable unit with m ratings contributes its ordered pairs weighted by 1/(m-1), giving total coincidence mass m, rather than equal weight per pair. Returns alpha, observed/expected disagreement, coincidence/distance matrices, marginal counts and a unit ledger with included, singleton and other-item IDs. Zero expected disagreement and no pairable units yield missing with distinct statuses. Negative alpha is retained; no cutoff or clipping is applied.\n\nThe result is descriptive: no confidence/credible interval, SE, bootstrap, missingness correction, severity adjustment, ICC or population guarantee. Ordinal distance matrices are specific to these pairable marginal frequencies. max_category_pairs bounds each dense K-by-K matrix before allocation; there is no dense rater-pair roster. Use fit_report(...; reporting=(dataset_id=..., observation_ids=..., alpha=(item=..., level=:ordinal, response_ids=...))) to include this observed-data result in existing report bundles.\n\nReference: Krippendorff, Computing Krippendorff's Alpha-Reliability (2011, literature updated 2013), sections C and D.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.krippendorff_alpha"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.category_report","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.category_report(fit; dataset_id, observation_ids,\n    interval=0.95, min_count=5, min_proportion=0.01,\n    order_probability_threshold=0.8, profiles=(),\n    max_prediction_cells=20_000_000, max_curve_cells=20_000_000)\n\nSummarize category use and correctly owned steps across the eight common saved-fit types, using every original retained draw. Supply persistent, unique rating IDs in fitted-row order. Canonical fixed-MFRM records are required. No fitting, response simulation, category sorting/collapse or random draw selection occurs.\n\nusage_rows retain every declared category overall, per item and per rater, observed counts/denominators, actual contributing threshold groups and the posterior distribution of the conditional expected proportion. Counts give equal weight to ratings, not persons, raters or likelihood/survey weights. This is the observed allocation, not balanced standardization. Its intervals are not predictive intervals for realized category use; a discrepancy is not a p-value. The existing MFRM category_functioning_summary keeps its predictive-replication semantics and unchanged numerical implementation.\n\nthreshold_rows identify shared, item- or rater-owned centered transitions, excluding dummy reference-category zeros. They include posterior SD, equal-tailed intervals, joint-draw ordering probabilities, gap summaries and chain-aware MCSE. Posterior SD is not a repeated-sampling SE; empirical constant draws/indicators are not automatically treated as structural constants or certain ordering. Flags prompt review and never establish a reason to merge categories.\n\nOptional profiles are named tuples with unique string id, exact stored item and rater labels, dimension (index or label), strictly increasing finite ability_grid, and fixed_abilities=Dict(other_dimension=>value, ...) specifying ALL other dimensions. Profiles are slices, not integrations over other abilities or raters. curve_rows give conditional category probabilities at the grid points; crossing_rows give draw-wise ability values where adjacent categories are equally probable. These probabilities need not each be 0.5. Steps, crossings and expected- score half-points differ. Inactive dimensions retain flat curves and no unique crossing. The scalar GMFRM's item coefficient multiplies the whole contrast, whereas MGMFRM loadings multiply abilities; native scale conventions are retained.\n\nAll intervals are pointwise, conditional on this fitted model and its calibration policy; they are not simultaneous bands, new-rater guarantees or calibration evidence. Fixed anchors omit external calibration uncertainty. A profile grid is a display choice, not evidence of empirical support. Structural constants and unavailable numerical summaries stay explicit. Sampling warnings, source identity, model/step contracts, complete chain layout and a content seal are retained. Resources exceeding the explicit budgets fail instead of subsampling. This qualified candidate API is also available through the optional fit_report(...; reporting=(; category=(...), ...)) request.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.category_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification(fit; parameters, cutoff,\n    pass_probability, interval=0.90)\n\nReport conditional ability precision and binary classification risk from saved joint draws. parameters selects one or more distinct estimated person locations by exact names from recovery_quantities(fit), in one dimension and scale. Fixed anchors, other facets and mixed dimensions are rejected. The finite cohort is exactly these people, each counted once, independent of their rating counts.\n\nBoth rules are required: qualification means ability >= cutoff in the fitted coordinate system; classify a person as :pass when the saved estimate of that probability is >= pass_probability (strictly between zero and one). Equality belongs to the qualifying/passing side. The package does not choose a substantive standard or infer loss costs. No centering, scale linking or multidimensional composite is performed. For dimensions with different interpretations, call separately with justified cutoffs; these calls do not define a conjunctive rule.\n\nEach row retains posterior mean/SD, pointwise equal-tailed CrI (interval=0.99 requests 99%), probability of qualification, the specified decision and its posterior error probability. SD describes uncertainty conditional on this model, prior, data and calibration; it is not repeated-sampling SE or MCMC error. cohort reports expected false-pass/false-fail/total counts and the probability of any error, calculated within each joint draw to retain posterior dependence. Decisions are held fixed at the reported choices for these calculations.\n\nMean MCSE accounts for chain autocorrelation. Short/single chains and constant sampled events have unavailable MCSE, not proof of certainty. A count is known zero only when the decision rule leaves no people eligible for that error type. Inspect event MCSE and convergence before interpreting decisions near the chosen probability boundary; no precision threshold or automatic refit is applied. The reported MCSE conditions on the displayed decisions and does not quantify their possible changes across finite MCMC runs.\n\nThese are posterior risks for the selected people, not known-truth error rates, population sensitivity/specificity, future-score predictions or design guarantees. Shared calibration uncertainty is included only where represented in the joint draws. Source identity and sampling warnings are preserved for all eight common fit types. This qualified candidate is not statistical acceptance of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking(fit; parameters, direction,\n    top_k=nothing, interval=0.90, max_rank_cells=20_000_000)\n\nSummarize posterior ranks of an explicit finite cohort from saved joint draws. Select at least two distinct estimated person coordinates by exact names from recovery_quantities(fit), in one ability dimension and scale. Fixed anchors, other facets and mixed dimensions are rejected. direction=:higher puts the largest value first; :lower puts the smallest first. Rank 1 is always first. No direction, composite ability, population or selected winner is inferred.\n\nRank people within each original retained draw, preserving posterior dependence. Exact numeric ties receive the average of their occupied positions (midrank). Each row reports mean rank and its chain-aware MCSE, rank SD, median and a pointwise equal-tailed interval (interval=0.99 requests 99%). Interval endpoints use the empirical inverse CDF without interpolation, so belong to the observed rank support. They need not enclose exactly the requested mass, especially with ties or few draws. These are marginal rank intervals, not a simultaneous ordering or a credible interval for the mean rank. Interval/quantile MCSE is not computed.\n\nOptional top_k is an integer from 1 to the cohort size. top_k_probability contains lower, upper and boundary_tie_probability summaries, each with a posterior mean and numerical MCSE. A person's best position is one plus the number strictly ahead; their worst position includes all equals. The lower probability counts draws with worst position <= k; the upper counts draws with best position <= k. The difference is the probability of a tie crossing the selection boundary. These are bounds over unspecified tie-breaking rules, not a credible interval. With no boundary ties they coincide. No random tie-breaking or arbitrary input-order preference is introduced. top_k == n_people is the structural event that everyone is included, with probability one and MCSE zero.\n\nMean MCSE reuses the classification report's autocorrelation-aware estimator. Constant sampled ranks/events and short/single chains retain unavailable MCSE, not certainty. Diagnostics and source identity remain attached. Ranks depend on the selected cohort; they discard the magnitude of ability differences and are not ranks of posterior means, future-score ranks, practical-equivalence tests, known-truth rank recovery or calibrated selection guarantees. No all-pairs matrix, whole-order probability or multidimensional aggregate is computed.\n\nThe algorithm uses O(draws * people * log(people)) sorting and O(draws * people) working storage. max_rank_cells bounds the selected draw-by-person matrix before allocation; it is not a bound on the already loaded fit's memory. This qualified candidate supports the eight common saved-fit types without refitting and does not establish their scientific acceptance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking"},{"category":"function","location":"api-saved-reporting/#BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation","page":"Saved-fit Reporting Candidate","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation(data::FacetData; item, model,\n    definition, unit, score_scale, dataset_id, observation_ids,\n    response_ids=nothing)\n\nCompute a two-way ANOVA, observed-score ICC for one explicit item label and a complete response-by-rater rectangle. Every response must have exactly one rating from the same set of at least two raters; at least two responses are required. Incomplete designs are rejected, without deletion or imputation. Response/rating identities follow rater_agreement. Items are not pooled.\n\nAll choices are required. model=:two_way_random treats raters as a sample from a rater population; :two_way_mixed targets this fixed panel. definition is :agreement (systematic rater differences count) or :consistency (additive rater differences are removed). unit=:single concerns one rating; :average concerns the arithmetic mean of all K recorded raters. The model choice changes generalization, not the formula for a given definition/unit.\n\nscore_scale=:interval explicitly declares that equal gaps between the integer score codes have substantive meaning. This is a user assumption, not a property established by FacetData or the ICC. Nominal/ordinal scale choices are rejected; consider krippendorff_alpha for nominal/ordinal agreement instead.\n\nReturn the coefficient, numerator/denominator, ANOVA sums of squares, degrees of freedom and mean squares, response/rater ledger and identities. Negative values with a positive denominator are retained, not clipped. A nonpositive denominator yields missing with an explicit status; any finite raw ratio is retained for inspection, not interpreted as reliability. Repeated responses from one person are counted and disclosed; there is no independence or population guarantee.\n\nThis is an unadjusted observed-score method-of-moments point estimate, independent of model draws. No SE, confidence/credible interval, significance test, one-way ICC, missing-data mixed model or latent-trait reliability is provided. Fit-report integration uses reporting=(dataset_id=..., observation_ids=..., icc=(item=..., model=:two_way_random, definition=:agreement, unit=:single, score_scale=:interval, response_ids=...)). No MCMC is run.\n\nReferences: Shrout & Fleiss (1979), doi:10.1037/0033-2909.86.2.420; Liljequist et al. (2019), doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0219854; the psych package ICC documentation for the McGraw-Wong random/mixed interpretation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.intraclass_correlation"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Experimental-Models","page":"Experimental Models","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.Experimental contains multidimensional MFRM and generalized models executable only in the limited configurations documented below. They are not part of the stable MFRM fitting contract, and their availability does not imply broader GMFRM or MGMFRM support.","title":"Experimental Models"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Boundary","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use Scope and Releases to choose among the eight documented result types and their prior routes. The current evidence summary separates implementation checks, recovery findings and unresolved scientific acceptance.\n\nThe fitting entry currently admits five configurations:\n\nfixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM with at least two dimensions, fixed Q coefficients, item-specific partial-credit steps and identity latent correlation;\ntwo-dimensional between-item MFRM with fixed coefficients and an estimated population correlation, selected with Experimental.correlated(spec);\none-dimensional source-aligned scalar GMFRM with positive item/task discrimination multiplied by positive rater consistency and rater-specific partial-credit steps;\nfixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM with one or more dimensions, item-specific partial-credit steps, and fixed identity latent correlation; an explicit one-column Q selects the unidimensional restriction of this kernel;\ntwo-dimensional between-item MGMFRM with estimated positive loadings, rater consistency and population correlation, selected with Experimental.correlated(spec) and an explicit GeneralizedPrior. Its workflow covers summaries, diagnostics, MCSE, prior/conditional posterior prediction, manual fit caches and report/figure bundles.\n\nThe matched-dimension example aligns one-dimensional and between-item MGMFRM response/prior conventions. Its one-dimensional model differs from the rater-step scalar GMFRM above; see the equations.\n\nAll five reject anchors and fitted DFF terms. Broader discrimination, rating-scale generalized kernels, exploratory or rotated loadings, and higher-dimensional correlation estimation remain outside the fitting boundary.\n\nThe compatibility selector discrimination = :none on MGMFRM means that no broader generic discrimination family is selected. The experimental kernel still estimates positive item-by-dimension discriminations at the active cells of the fixed Q-matrix.\n\nLikewise, the GMFRM compatibility selector discrimination = :rater does not mean that item/task discrimination is absent. Its source-aligned kernel uses the effective multiplier item_discrimination[i] * rater_consistency[r]. Each rater has one step vector that is shared across items and persons on the direct parameter scale. With K categories, the first step is fixed to zero, K - 2 steps per rater are free, and the final step is reconstructed so the remaining steps sum to zero. The effective response-scale spacing also depends on the item-by-rater discrimination product.\n\nInspect the executable contract before building an experimental workflow:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\nusing Random\n\ncontract = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract()\ncontract.families.mfrm\ncontract.families.mgmfrm\ncontract.families.gmfrm\ncontract.candidate_surfaces.mgmfrm_free_latent_correlation_2d","title":"Boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Matched-fixed-and-estimated-item-slopes","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use Experimental.slope_restriction to compare item slopes while keeping the threshold owner, coefficient placement, scale constant and shared priors fixed. This first implementation uses one ability dimension, item-specific steps and rater consistency fixed at one in both models. It does not switch to the rater-step scalar GMFRM.\n\nE = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental\nbase = mfrm_spec(data; family=:mgmfrm, dimensions=1,\n    thresholds=:partial_credit, q_matrix=trues(length(data.item_levels), 1))\nfixed = E.slope_restriction(base;\n    item_slopes=:fixed_one, rater_consistency=:fixed_one)\nfree = E.slope_restriction(base;\n    item_slopes=:estimated_positive, rater_consistency=:fixed_one)\nprior = E.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(; prior_model=:exchangeable,\n    person_sd=0.8, item_sd=0.9, rater_sd=0.6, step_sd=0.7,\n    log_discrimination_sd=0.3, log_consistency_sd=0.4)\n# Illustrative prior inputs, not a recommendation for your data.\ncheck = E.prior_predictive_check(free; prior, ndraws=100)\nlayout = E.surface_contract(free; prior)\n# Choose and check an adequate sample budget for the actual application.\nfit = E.fit(free; prior, backend=:advancedhmc,\n    chains=4, warmup=1000, ndraws=1000, seed=4137)\ndiagnostics(fit)\nposterior_mcse(fit)\nsave_fit_cache(\"estimated-slopes.jls\", fit)\nreopened = load_fit_cache(\"estimated-slopes.jls\")\nfit_metadata(reopened).loading_policy  # :estimated_positive\n\nThe adjacent-category log odds are 1.7*(a[i]*theta[p] - b[i] - r[j] - t[i,k]). Fixed-one slopes give a PCM likelihood after rescaling every location, step and corresponding prior SD by 1.7. Setting a=1 alone does not match the package's unit-logit MFRM prior. The intercept b[i] and the ratio b[i]/a[i] are different quantities. Proper zero-centered priors anchor location and scale; this is not likelihood-only identification.\n\nFixed coefficients are removed from the sampled vector and reconstructed as one in model summaries. Their zero-width intervals express an imposed constraint; they do not demonstrate estimation precision. MCSE and diagnostic outputs distinguish structurally fixed values. The required log-consistency SD is inactive for this wrapper, and the log-discrimination SD is also inactive when item slopes are fixed. Reports mark these inputs inactive. Changing an inactive input does not change the posterior density, although the declared input is retained in the saved model record. Do not use a near-zero prior SD as a replacement for a fixed coefficient.\n\nRaw initial values and saved draws contain only free parameters, in layout.raw_parameter_names order. The saved target identity includes the coefficient policy. Manual caches and report bundles preserve it after reload; a fixed-slope result is rejected when supplied to a free-slope refit request. Both models use NormalizedMGMFRMFit, so distinguish them by metadata, not by result type. Existing unwrapped normalized MGMFRM keeps its original meaning.\n\nFor predictive model comparison, prepare the same split with BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_refit_plan(fixed, split; prior, dataset_id, observation_ids) and the corresponding call for free. Fit every training request, score with prediction_fold_observations, aggregate with prediction_kfold and compare with compare_kfold_predictions(...; comparison_axis=:model). Keep failed folds and sampling warnings. Full-data estimates or a short example fit do not establish which model predicts better.\n\nThe callable matched-slope workflow exercises fitting, saving and reopening both policies. This API currently supports Julia/AdvancedHMC and raw coordinates. CmdStan fitting, transformed sampling coordinates, source priors, correlations, higher dimensions, anchors and automatic request caching are rejected. Separate native Stan density checks support the equations; they are not a public CmdStan fitting route. Small pilots remain numerically unqualified and do not establish recovery, SBC calibration, a default coordinate choice or empirical model-selection performance.","title":"Matched fixed and estimated item slopes"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Settings-and-help","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Keep model choices, prior assumptions, computational controls and prediction targets explicit. The current interfaces accept ordinary keyword arguments; the common prediction and recovery APIs remain an isolated candidate, not a new universal configuration object. The following separates choices that change the target from choices that control computation or presentation.\n\nChoice Current entry and meaning What to check\nData/model FacetData, mfrm_spec: roles, category order, thresholds, dimensions and Q; Experimental.correlated where supported Q rows follow data.item_levels. Metadata such as task does not silently add a fitted effect. A changed model needs its own evidence\nPrior An explicit supported prior, such as Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior with all scales Free-coordinate SD, zero-sum kernel SD and transformed-effect SD differ. Fixed input scales are not learned population variances; a value in an example is not a recommended default\nComputation Experimental.fit: backend, seed, chains, warmup, retained draws, target acceptance, depth and supported sampling coordinates Record settings and actual diagnostics/MCSE. More draws do not repair a changed target. Current normalized CmdStan requires raw coordinates; the transformed-coordinate comparison is future work\nPrediction/evaluation Native prediction methods; opt-in common adapter with dataset/observation IDs and explicit criterion names Known fitted rows, heldout rows and unknown levels are different targets. Common criteria require all retained draws; a plotting subset cannot be passed as a full likelihood sample\nOutput save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache, fit_report and save_fit_report_bundle Preserve model/prior metadata and warnings. Report completeness is not convergence or scientific acceptance; automatic request caching has narrower type coverage than manual save/reload\n\nIn Julia's help mode, inspect ?BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit and ?BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior. In a script or notebook:\n\n@doc BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit\n@doc BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior\n@doc posterior_mcse\n\nStart with the normalized-prior walkthrough for fitting, diagnostics and saved reports, and the saved prediction and recovery guide for common comparison, K-fold scoring, named recovery and their limitations. These guides describe current behavior. Recommendations about scientifically supported priors, prediction domains and final API stability await the corresponding evidence and review; documentation of existing behavior need not wait for those decisions.","title":"Settings and help"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Fixed-coefficient-multidimensional-MFRM","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use mfrm_spec(data; family = :mfrm, dimensions = 2, q_matrix, dimension_labels, thresholds = :partial_credit) followed by BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc) or backend = :cmdstan. Q rows follow data.item_levels. Multiple active cells in a row give an additive, compensatory within-item sum. Q is fixed, not estimated. Review q_matrix_validation(spec) for coverage and identification warnings, including dimensions with no single-loading indicators; an admitted Q is not evidence of statistical identification or recovery.\n\nMFRMPrior sets independent zero-centered normal priors on the free unit-logit coordinates. Person and item locations are prior-anchored; rater severities sum to zero. Active Q coefficients and rater consistency are one; latent correlation is identity. Item steps have a first zero step, K - 2 free steps and a final step reconstructed so the remaining steps sum to zero. This declared free-coordinate density needs no Jacobian adjustment and has no 1.7/1.702 multiplier.","title":"Fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Prior-scales,-facet-labels-and-locations","page":"Experimental Models","text":"With the default MFRMPrior, rater_sd and step_sd describe the free-coordinate standard deviations. They are not common marginal standard deviations of all reconstructed effects. With R raters, the first R-1 severities have prior variance rater_sd^2, while the last has variance (R-1)*rater_sd^2. For three raters, their prior SDs are therefore rater_sd, rater_sd, and sqrt(2)*rater_sd. The last severity is negatively correlated with every free severity.\n\nThe reconstructed rater is data.rater_levels[end]; facet levels are sorted by label. Reordering data rows preserves that choice. Renaming rater IDs can change it and, when R>2, can change the prior and posterior even if the rating design and likelihood are otherwise equivalent. Preserve facet IDs when reproducing an analysis. This default prior is not exchangeable over all raters when R>2; a zero-sum constraint alone does not provide exchangeability.\n\nFor each item's K stored step contributions, the first is zero, K-2 are independent normals with SD step_sd, and the last is their negative sum with variance (K-2)*step_sd^2. Both steps are zero for binary responses. The nonbaseline steps are dependent; their prior does not require ordered thresholds. Response categories still have their declared order, so permuting step positions generally changes response probabilities.\n\nPerson and item locations are prior-anchored. Adding the same dimension vector c to each person's abilities and adding dot(Q[i,:], c) to each item's difficulty leaves the likelihood unchanged. The zero-centered priors determine locations along that direction. Fixed Q coefficients do not remove this location freedom, and finite posterior intervals do not by themselves establish identification from the data. person_sd is a fixed population-prior scale; the correlated model below estimates rho while keeping both marginal ability SDs fixed. These conventions apply to both backends.\n\nFor a between-item model (one active dimension per Q row), inspect the means of the fitted persons and items alongside their difference:\n\nchecks = diagnostics(fit_result)\nchecks.location_rows      # named dimensions, rank-normalized R-hat, bulk/tail ESS\nchecks.location_summary   # separate from the parameter/sampler summary\n\nusing CairoMakie\nfig = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(fit_result; view = :location, dimension = 2)\nsave(\"location-diagnostics.pdf\", fig)\n# Include the same figure in a saved report bundle:\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"report\", fit_result;\n    figures = (; diagnostics = (; view = :location, dimension = 2)))\n\nEach mean equally weights the fitted facet levels, regardless of how many ratings they received. These are finite-panel summaries, not estimated population means. The ability-minus-item mean cancels a joint shift of ability and item locations. It can mix well even when the separate means mix slowly; review both before interpreting location estimates. This calculation imposes no centering constraint on the model.\n\nfit_report(fit_result) includes diagnostics.location_rows, an explanation and a warning when these additional checks need review. They also appear in public, Markdown, JSON and table exports. The existing parameter/sampler summary is preserved, so inspect location_summary as well. All retained draws and the saved diagnostic thresholds are used, including after load_fit_cache, for either backend. Within-item and mixed Q return location_status = :unsupported with a reason; view = :location rejects those models. Individual-coordinate diagnostics remain available.","title":"Prior scales, facet labels and locations"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Exchangeable-rater-prior","page":"Experimental Models","text":"When every rater should have the same prior severity distribution regardless of their ID, explicitly select Experimental.ExchangeablePrior. For example:\n\nprior = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeablePrior(\n    rater_kernel_sd = 0.4, person_sd = 0.7, item_sd = 0.6, step_sd = 0.5)\ncheck = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check(spec;\n    prior, ndraws = 1000, rng = MersenneTwister(42))\nfit_result = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec; prior, backend = :advancedhmc)\nsave_fit_cache(\"exchangeable-fit.jls\", fit_result)\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"exchangeable-fit.jls\")\n\nThe same prior supports correlated(spec) and backend = :cmdstan. The full zero-sum severity vector has covariance rater_kernel_sd^2 * (I - 11'/R). Every rater's marginal SD is rater_kernel_sd * sqrt((R-1)/R); any pairwise contrast has SD sqrt(2) * rater_kernel_sd. Thus with three raters and kernel SD 0.4, each marginal SD is about 0.327 and contrast SD about 0.566. Kernel SD is required and is not MFRMPrior.rater_sd; copying that value selects a different prior, rather than matching the old marginal or contrast variances.\n\nThe free severities are dependent under this normalized joint prior. No extra Jacobian is needed for the deterministic last-rater reconstruction. Ability, item and free-step prior meanings and location constraints stay as described above. For correlated abilities, the LKJ prior and Fisher-z Jacobian also apply. These scales remain fixed inputs, not learned variance components.\n\nBoth backends return Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit, supporting the same summaries, diagnostics, plots and manual cache operations. Reports default to reader-facing output and preserve kernel, marginal and contrast scales. Old saved fits retain their original priors; selecting this prior requires a new fit. Scalar MFRM, GMFRM and MGMFRM do not accept it.","title":"Exchangeable rater prior"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Fitting-and-saved-results","page":"Experimental Models","text":"With MFRMPrior, both backends return Experimental.MultidimensionalMFRMFit. Warmup telemetry is recorded by default; set record_warmup = false to omit it. The common default is 100 warmup and 100 retained draws per chain, with two chains. These are computational defaults, not evidence of adequate MCMC precision.\n\nUse diagnostics(fit), posterior_summary(fit) and BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(fit) for free and reconstructed coordinates; fit_metadata(fit) records the prior, backend and target identity. save_fit_cache/load_fit_cache and save_fit_report_bundle support this result, including named-dimension figures. See the runnable example. Automatic request caching and Experimental.preview remain limited to GMFRM/MGMFRM. Prior prediction also supports this MFRM model; inspect its design with getdesign(spec; preview = true). Stable fit(spec) does not accept it.\n\nAfter reviewing convergence, quantify the Monte Carlo error of reported values directly from the fit or a reloaded cache:\n\nprecision = posterior_mcse(fit_result; probabilities = (0.025, 0.5, 0.975))\nability_precision = filter(row -> row.block == :person && row.dimension == 1, precision)\n\nThis works for independent and correlated models, either rater prior, and both backends. The default includes reconstructed rater severities and item steps, with block, dimension_label, fixed and derived fields for selection. Locations use unit logits, fixed coefficients are dimensionless, and estimated rho uses the correlation scale. Use parameter_space = :raw_unconstrained for free coordinates; the correlation coordinate is then Fisher z. Fixed quantities have zero MCSE; fewer than two chains or fewer than ten draws per chain leave nonfixed MCSE unavailable. An available MCSE estimates computation error, not posterior uncertainty or convergence, and applies no universal precision cutoff. User-defined contrasts still use the documented matrix method.","title":"Fitting and saved results"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Correlated-ability-dimensions","page":"Experimental Models","text":"When the two abilities may be related in the population, select that model explicitly. Starting from a two-dimensional MFRM spec as above:\n\nmodel = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated(spec; lkj_eta = 2)\nBayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract(model)\nresult = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(model;\n    backend = :advancedhmc, prior = MFRMPrior(), seed = 42)\ndiagnostics(result) # Review R-hat, ESS and sampler warnings before interpretation.\nBayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(result)\nsave_fit_cache(\"correlated-fit.jls\", result)\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"correlated-fit.jls\")\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"correlated-report\", restored)\n# With CairoMakie loaded:\n# BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; block = :latent_correlation)\n\nChoose backend = :cmdstan for the same model on CmdStan. With MFRMPrior, both routes return Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit. The original spec still denotes independent abilities. This model requires between-item Q, at least two pure items per dimension, and observations in both dimensions for every person. These checks do not establish adequate information or recovery for a particular dataset.\n\nEach person's directly estimated ability pair has a bivariate normal prior with covariance person_sd^2 * [1 rho; rho 1]. The marginal standard deviation remains a fixed input in either prior constructor. lkj_eta is a fixed positive integer shape for the LKJ prior on rho, not a standard deviation. Sampling uses Fisher z with rho = tanh(z) and includes log(1-rho^2) exactly once as the transformation Jacobian. The covariance determinant belongs to the normal density. All other location, step, loading and rater-consistency conventions above remain in effect.\n\nposterior_summary reports the sampled Fisher-z coordinate; direct_posterior_summary and correlation figures transform each draw to rho before computing intervals. This population correlation is distinct from dependence among posterior draws. Reports and saved report bundles default to view = :public; view = :full retains additional reproducibility information. The report includes rho, its interval and its diagnostics without manual draw reshaping. Automatic request caching is unavailable.","title":"Correlated ability dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Inspect-priors-before-fitting","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use the same MFRMPrior for prior inspection and subsequent fitting. Both the independent spec and the correlated model above are supported:\n\nusing Random\nprior = MFRMPrior(person_sd = 0.7, rater_sd = 0.4, item_sd = 0.6, step_sd = 0.5)\nprior_check = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check(model;\n    prior, ndraws = 1000, rng = MersenneTwister(42))\nprior_check.parameter_summary  # model-scale medians and central 95% prior intervals\npredictive_check_summary(prior_check; interval = 0.9)\nprior_check.implication_diagnostics  # category use and facet-score ranges\n\nusing CairoMakie\nsave(\"ability-prior.pdf\", BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior(prior_check;\n    block = :person, dimension = 2))\nsave(\"correlation-prior.svg\", BayesianMGMFRM.plot_prior(prior_check;\n    block = :latent_correlation)) # correlated model only\nsave(\"prior-predictive.pdf\", BayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(prior_check))\n\nThe figures reuse the simulated draws; no posterior sampling or manual draw reshaping is needed. For the correlated model, rho is sampled from its LKJ prior and each person's ability pair is sampled conditional on that rho, with fixed marginal person_sd. Rater and item-step sum constraints are reconstructed draw by draw. Locations and steps use unit logits; correlation uses its own scale. The seed is controlled by the supplied RNG and is reproducible in the same Julia/package environment.\n\nAssess whether the simulated ratings and facet differences are plausible for the measurement context before choosing prior scales. The observed scores are shown for comparison and do not update the prior. Replications use the supplied rating rows and facet levels; they do not predict newly introduced persons, items or raters. Plausible prior predictions do not establish identification, posterior convergence or parameter recovery.","title":"Inspect priors before fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Include-priors-in-a-saved-fit-report","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Reload either fixed-coefficient MFRM fit and request prior summaries explicitly. The saved model, prior scales and LKJ shape determine the simulation; no posterior fitting occurs and no prior specification needs to be repeated:\n\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"correlated-fit.jls\")\nreport = fit_report(restored; include_prior_predictive = true,\n    prior_predictive_ndraws = 1000, prior_interval = 0.95,\n    predictive_interval = 0.9, seed = 42)\nreport.prior_predictive.parameter_rows\nreport.prior_predictive.correlation_rows # empty for independent dimensions\n\nusing CairoMakie\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"report-with-priors\", restored;\n    include_prior_predictive = true, prior_predictive_ndraws = 1000,\n    prior_interval = 0.95, predictive_interval = 0.9, seed = 42,\n    figures = (prior = (block = :person, dimension = 2),\n               prior_predictive = (;), posterior = (block = :person, dimension = 2),\n               predictive = (;)))\nload_fit_report_bundle(\"report-with-priors\") # verifies tables, text and figure files\n\nUse prior = (block = :latent_correlation,) for the correlated model's rho figure. prior_interval controls central parameter prior intervals; predictive_interval controls prior and posterior rating-prediction intervals. The default prior draw budget is 100; specify a larger value when needed for more precise simulation summaries. Prior and posterior simulation use separate local RNGs initialized from seed, so changing the prior budget does not change posterior prediction or MCMC diagnostics. Figures use the report's exact summaries; rendering does not simulate again. The report distinguishes prior intervals from posterior credible intervals, and preserves MCMC warnings. Set include_posterior_predictive = false to omit posterior prediction when only the prior check is needed. Without include_prior_predictive = true, the prior section is not requested and prior figure requests are rejected.","title":"Include priors in a saved-fit report"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Dimension-aggregation-and-item-structure","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Keep author terminology, response algebra, and item structure separate. Uto (2021), Section 5 calls the model non-compensatory, but Equations 5–6 and Appendix 1 use an additive weighted ability sum. The cited Yao and Schwarz (2006) publisher abstract explicitly describes its multidimensional partial-credit model as compensatory. This is a terminology discrepancy, not a reason to replace the published likelihood with a probability product.\n\nOur algebraic reading of Equation 6, with categories indexed from 1, is\n\nu_ij=sum_d=1^Da_idtheta_jdqquad\neta_ijrk=17alpha_rleft(k-1)(u_ij-beta_i-beta_r)\n -sum_m=2^kd_imrightqquad\nP_ijrk=frace^eta_ijrksum_h=1^Ke^eta_ijrh\n\nThe empty step sum is zero, so the first logit is zero. For a fixed item with two positive loadings, replacing abilities by (theta_1 + t, theta_2 - a_i1/a_i2 * t) leaves u_ij and the entire category probability vector unchanged. For example, loadings (2, 1) make abilities (0, 2) and (1, 0) indistinguishable on that item. Thus the conditional response equation has compensatory tradeoffs. Other items with different loadings, the ability prior, and the full posterior need not be unchanged. This result is not evidence of global nonidentification or practical validity.\n\nIn the visually checked Appendix 1 Stan code (printed page 451), also checked against the published Stan implementation:\n\nOperation Index/role\ndot_product of item discrimination and person ability Multiply each loading by its ability, then sum over dimensions\n1.7 * trans_alpha_r Scalar rater multiplier on the category logits\ncumulative_sum of steps and category scores 0:(K-1) Accumulate category steps, not dimensions\nReciprocal of prod(alpha_r) Reconstruct the first rater consistency so the full product is one; an identification constraint\ncategorical_logit Softmax normalization over response categories\n\nThe package Stan kernel uses an explicit loading-sum loop and reconstructs the last rater's log-consistency as the negative sum of the free log-consistencies. It has the same response algebra under the Q restriction, not a demonstrated match of the complete source prior. A product of unnormalized exponentials (exp(sum(z)) = prod(exp(z))) or likelihoods across observations is not a product of dimension-specific success probabilities. For the latter distinction, Bolt and Lall (2003), Equations 1–2 contrast a compensatory logistic weighted sum with a non-compensatory product of component success probabilities; that binary comparison is not an implemented alternative ordinal kernel here.\n\nWithin/between instead describes which dimensions an item measures (ACER ConQuest manual, Section 2.8.3). The source Appendix estimates a positive discrimination for every item-dimension cell, without a Q mask: for D > 1, it is within-item. A small positive loading is not a structural zero. In this package, one active cell per Q row gives between-item structure; rows with multiple active cells are within-item. The contract calls a combination of pure and cross-loaded rows mixed; this is a finer subdivision of what the cited manual calls within-item at test level. These labels do not establish non-compensation or identify the model by themselves.\n\nThe contract's source_classification preserves the author's wording; algebraic_aggregation records the weighted sum. Its unresolved operational_compensation_status is not evidence against the conditional identity above or a certification of a practical decision rule.","title":"Dimension aggregation and item structure"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Workflow","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Specifications continue to use the common domain-language constructor. Design preview, fitting, and cached fitting then cross the explicit namespace boundary:\n\nspec = mfrm_spec(data;\n    family = :mgmfrm,\n    dimensions = 2,\n    thresholds = :partial_credit,\n    discrimination = :none,\n    q_matrix = Bool[1 0; 0 1],\n)\n\ndesign = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.preview(spec)\n\nfit_result = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec;\n    backend = :advancedhmc,\n    ndraws = 500,\n    warmup = 500,\n    chains = 4,\n    seed = 20260722,\n)\n\nThe guarded fit accepts a typed sensitivity prior. Its scales are standard deviations of independent zero-centered normal priors on the raw unconstrained coordinates; they are not priors on the transformed direct parameters:\n\nunit_raw_prior = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior(;\n    person_sd = 1.0,\n    rater_sd = 1.0,\n    item_sd = 1.0,\n    log_discrimination_sd = 1.0,\n    log_consistency_sd = 1.0,\n    step_sd = 1.0,\n)\n\nprior_check = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check(spec;\n    prior = unit_raw_prior,\n    ndraws = 500,\n    rng = Random.MersenneTwister(20260813),\n)\n\nsensitivity_fit = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec;\n    prior = unit_raw_prior,\n    backend = :advancedhmc,\n    ndraws = 500,\n    warmup = 500,\n    chains = 4,\n    seed = 20260814,\n)\n\nThe same resolved scale values enter experimental fit-cache keys. Direct-scale generalized priors remain unsupported because they would require a separately specified transform and change-of-variables policy. The prior-predictive result contains raw and constrained direct parameter draws, replicated scores, and implication diagnostics. Use it before fitting, then use actual refits—not only importance reweighting—to assess posterior sensitivity.\n\nFor independent MGMFRM, the ability-scale row in diagnostics and fit_report records the actual person_sd in row.prior.sd; fit_artifact also exposes it as ability_prior.sd. SD 1 retains the :standard_normal_by_dimension label; other scales use :normal_by_dimension. These describe independent Normal(0, person_sd) priors conditional on fixed hyperparameters, not posterior uncertainty or proof of identification. Fixed identity correlation does not imply unit covariance when person_sd != 1. Fit artifacts and report Q sections mark their embedded design/source-reference prior and gauge metadata with design_prior_scope = :source_reference_not_resolved_fit_prior. The design manifest itself is unchanged, preserving existing fit-cache keys. Pointwise/scale checks without a prior report the scale as missing.\n\nFor these generalized fits, the posterior density is defined with respect to the free raw coordinates. Exponentiating a sampled log-loading or log-consistency, or reconstructing a constrained coordinate, adds no Jacobian term to that target. Both Julia and the package's Stan models use this convention; Stan samples an unconstrained beta vector. The fit's log_posterior therefore remains a raw-coordinate density even when you inspect direct_draws. Summaries and plots of transformed draws need no density adjustment. Expressing a density in different coordinates does: for a free positive coordinate a = exp(z), log p_a(a) = log p_z(log(a)) - log(a). This distinction follows the Stan change-of-variables rule.\n\nWith CairoMakie, generalized fits support experimental posterior intervals, chain diagnostics and conditional predictive plots for the original rating rows. See the plotting guide for coordinate scales, dimension labels, editing and PDF/SVG export. BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright supports stable MFRM only and rejects generalized fits.\n\nEqual raw scales do not imply exchangeable constrained priors. For MGMFRM, the last sorted rater severity is the negative sum of the other R - 1 coordinates. With raw variance s², its variance is (R - 1)s², whereas each free severity has variance s². The same asymmetry holds for log-consistency; the final reconstructed item step also has a different induced variance when there are more than three categories. Consequently, renaming raters so that a different rater occupies the reconstructed position can change the posterior target even when ratings and matched likelihoods are unchanged. This matters especially when the data weakly constrain a rater; it is not a data-exclusion rule or evidence that a particular rater is unreliable.\n\nSetting every raw scale to one is a sensitivity setting, not replication of the complete prior in Uto (2021), Appendix 1. For example, that code applies the severity normal density to the full constrained rater vector, including its reconstructed coordinate; the default raw-prior path applies it only to the free coordinates. Matching the response equation or the two package backends therefore does not establish source-posterior equivalence. The explicit normalized source and exchangeable MGMFRM routes below are separate targets. Experimental.ExchangeablePrior above remains fixed-coefficient MFRM-only. Choosing scientific scales and replacing the raw default remain separate decisions.","title":"Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#MGMFRM-prior-choice-and-comparable-scales","page":"Experimental Models","text":"The current raw prior, normalized exchangeable reference and normalized source reference are distinct probability distributions, even with equal numerical scale settings:\n\nPrior Rater/step structure Role\nRaw GeneralizedPrior Independent free normals, with the last coordinate reconstructed Public experimental MGMFRM workflow and the existing computational reference; retain IDs and the declared prior.\nNormalized exchangeable reference Symmetric zero-sum severity, log-consistency and item-step blocks Explicit NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(prior_model=:exchangeable, ...); candidate when rater IDs carry no prior scientific distinction.\nNormalized source reference Same severity/step blocks; log-consistency additionally distinguishes an explicit rater Explicit NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(prior_model=:source, source_rater=..., ...); comparison against a declared source target.\n\nUse Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior to carry one explicit prior through prediction, fitting, manual caches and reports. Specify all six scales and the prior family; the constructor does not select scientific widths. This applies to fixed-Q MGMFRM with estimated loadings and identity latent correlation. The fixed-coefficient ExchangeablePrior keeps its separate MFRM meaning. The normalized-prior walkthrough provides a self-contained fit → diagnostics → save/reload → report script, with optional figures. Its illustrative scales and short sampling budget are not scientific recommendations; diagnostic warnings remain visible.\n\nFor the source reference, supply prior_model=:source and source_rater equal to a rater ID in the data. That ID changes the log-consistency prior mean. Reports preserve the distribution, kernel/marginal/contrast scales, source identity and model scope. With CairoMakie loaded, optional bundle figures posterior, diagnostics, predictive, prior and prior_predictive use these same saved draws and prior; prior figures require include_prior_predictive=true.\n\nBoth Julia/AdvancedHMC and CmdStan use the existing normalized targets. CmdStan retains its K≥3 boundary and requires sampling_coordinates = :raw. AdvancedHMC also accepts :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset, as used in the walkthrough; the default remains :raw. The complete normalized prior and likelihood are preserved. Initial values and jitter use raw coordinates, as do stored samples and parameter diagnostics. Inspect fit_metadata(restored).sampler_controls.sampling_coordinates for the explicit transformed route; raw records may omit this field. Correlated MGMFRM and automatic request caching are not connected for this prior. Existing private v1/v2 sample records keep their formats and identities; the public result and manual fit cache have separate types/schemas. Existing raw fits keep their meaning. Numerical and workflow checks do not establish scientific acceptance or validate every mixed-Q design.\n\nFor a symmetric zero-sum block of length n, kernel SD tau gives marginal SD tau*sqrt((n-1)/n) and pairwise contrast SD sqrt(2)*tau. The raw free SD sigma instead gives marginal SD sigma for free entries and sigma*sqrt(n-1) for the last entry. Contrast variances are 2sigma^2 between free entries and (n+2)sigma^2 when the last entry is involved.\n\nChoosing tau=sqrt(2)*sigma matches the raw prior's average marginal variance and average pairwise contrast variance. For n>2 it does not match all individual distributions or covariances. Using the same numerical scale matches the free-to-free contrast variance, where such a pair exists, while changing the other contrasts. State what is held equal before attributing posterior changes to symmetry alone. Here n=R for rater blocks and n=K-1 for item steps.\n\nFor the source reference, write ell=log(gamma) and let q denote the explicitly distinguished rater. Its mean is tau^2*(1/R-e_q), with covariance tau^2*(I-11'/R). Thus ell_q-ell_j has mean -tau^2 for j≠q, whereas the exchangeable reference has mean zero. The source rater is not fixed at consistency one. A relabeling must carry that rater's identity along with the observations; choosing a different distinguished rater changes the prior.\n\nWhen no rater has a scientifically distinguished prior role, exchangeability is the preferred structure for a future scientific candidate. It does not determine the SDs or establish calibration. For an exchangeable prior, a declared 95% range [-delta,delta] for one severity difference gives tau=delta/(sqrt(2)*z_0.975). A range [1/C,C] for one consistency ratio gives tau=log(C)/(sqrt(2)*z_0.975). These specify individual prior contrasts, not a simultaneous guarantee or a recommended practical tolerance. Use prior predictions to inspect the resulting rating distributions before fitting.\n\nInspect category probabilities as well as expected scores. With four categories, zero location and consistency one, steps (-1,0,1) and (1,0,-1) both give expected score 2.5, but their total endpoint probabilities are approximately 0.1545 and 0.8455. Smaller step variance need not reduce endpoint mass when other facets vary. Concentration in one category, total endpoint probability and entropy answer different questions; none alone is a prior acceptance rule.\n\nScore direction also matters. Negating abilities, item locations and severities, and replacing each step vector by its negative reverse, reverses category probabilities while preserving positive loadings and consistencies. The centered normalized step prior preserves this transformation. For K>=4 the raw free-step prior generally does not: (1,1,-2) and its reflected (2,-1,-1) have equal full squared norms but different free-coordinate normal densities. Consequently, the raw prior can give different prior-predictive probabilities to the two middle categories. When neutrality to score direction is intended, state that requirement alongside rater exchangeability. This is a score relabeling, not an additional fixed-label likelihood equivalence or a silent change to GeneralizedPrior.\n\nAbility units need the same care. Scaling abilities by t without changing the response model requires dividing loadings by t and transforming their priors together. A zero-mean log-loading prior then has mean -log(t) in the new coordinates. GeneralizedPrior exposes SDs, with log-loading means fixed at zero, so changing only person_sd is a prior-sensitivity change, not merely a unit conversion. See Estimands, origins and units.\n\nThe normalized Julia reference density also handles binary responses, where there is no free step density. The current MGMFRM CmdStan model requires at least three categories; algebra for K=2 does not extend that backend's fitting boundary.","title":"MGMFRM prior choice and comparable scales"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#MGMFRM-sampler-defaults-and-backends","page":"Experimental Models","text":"If sampler counts are omitted, both guarded families currently use 100 warm-up iterations and retain 100 draws per chain across two chains. Thus warm-up is 50% of the 200 iterations per chain and is discarded before the returned draw matrix is constructed. This is a computational default, not analysis guidance. The explicit 500 warm-up plus 500 retained draws in the example is also only an example; choose and report a larger budget when diagnostics or the inferential target require it. The stable MFRM fit entry point has a separate default of 1,000 warm-up plus 1,000 retained draws per chain.\n\nBoth guarded configurations accept either backend = :advancedhmc or backend = :cmdstan. Each CmdStan route uses a package-owned Stan model, samples the same raw-coordinate prior, applies the Julia identification transform, and checks Stan's generated pointwise log likelihood against Julia at every retained draw. An unwrapped MGMFRM specification retains fixed Q and identity correlation; the explicit correlated workflow below has a separate result type. Experimental cached_fit remains AdvancedHMC-only for the unwrapped specifications. CmdStan is an optional external runtime; inspect it with cmdstan_backend_check() before requesting that backend.\n\nThe older fit(spec; experimental = true) form remains available during the migration, but new code should not depend on it. Passing experimental inside the namespace is rejected because the namespace itself is the opt-in.","title":"MGMFRM sampler defaults and backends"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Sampling-coordinates-for-independent-MGMFRM","page":"Experimental Models","text":"To separate common ability/item location movement during sampling, an unwrapped fixed-Q MGMFRM can use orthogonal person coordinates and loading-weighted item offsets with AdvancedHMC:\n\ncoordinates = :orthogonal_person_mean_item_offset\ncontrols = (; chains = 4, warmup = 1000, ndraws = 1000, seed = 42, step_size = 0.03)\nfit_result = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec;\n    sampling_coordinates = coordinates, controls...)\ncheck = diagnostics(fit_result; include_location = true)\ncheck.summary\n\nThe person coordinates include an estimated finite-panel mean in each dimension and orthogonal contrasts. The item coordinate subtracts the loading-weighted person mean from difficulty. The transform has unit absolute Jacobian, and the sampler evaluates the complete original joint prior and likelihood after mapping back to raw coordinates. It does not fix the person mean to zero or introduce independent priors on the item offsets. Positive loadings use the active cells of the admitted fixed Q, including within-item and mixed structures.\n\ninit and init_jitter keep their raw-coordinate meanings. Returned draws, summaries, prediction and parameter diagnostics retain their existing parameter names and scales. The selected coordinates are recorded in fit_result.sampler_controls.sampling_coordinates for GeneralizedPrior, or fit_metadata(fit_result).sampler_controls.sampling_coordinates for NormalizedMGMFRMPrior; manual save/reload preserves that record. Inspect the full parameter and sampler diagnostics: a different coordinate choice does not guarantee better mixing or establish model adequacy.\n\nWith the default raw GeneralizedPrior, automatic caching accepts the same option (NormalizedMGMFRMPrior uses manual save/reload only):\n\ncached_result = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.cached_fit(spec;\n    cache_path = \"mgmfrm-location.jls\",\n    sampling_coordinates = coordinates, controls...)\n\nUse the same explicit sampler controls when matching a direct fit to a cache request. Different coordinate choices have different computation/cache keys, while the model, prior and stored parameter spaces retain their identities. Requesting :raw from a file created with the alternative coordinates, or the reverse, raises a cache-key mismatch; choose a separate path or explicitly use refresh = true to replace it. Repeating the same request reloads the saved fit.\n\nThe default sampling_coordinates = :raw preserves existing behavior and cache keys, including when explicitly supplied. The alternative is available only for independent fixed-Q MGMFRM with backend = :advancedhmc. CmdStan, correlated specifications and other model families do not support this coordinate option.","title":"Sampling coordinates for independent MGMFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Location-diagnostics-for-fixed-Q-MGMFRM","page":"Experimental Models","text":"For an independent fixed-Q MGMFRMFit, inspect finite-panel locations when item difficulties or abilities mix slowly:\n\ncheck = diagnostics(fit_result; include_location = true)\ncheck.location_rows\ncheck.location_summary\ncheck.summary # original parameter/sampler assessment, including all warnings\n\nAt each retained draw, these rows calculate the equally weighted ability mean over the fitted persons in each dimension. For each item they report its difficulty, the loading-weighted ability mean sum(a[i,d] * mean(theta[:,d])) over its active Q cells, and difficulty minus that weighted mean. The quantities precede multiplication by the response-scale constant and rater consistency. Using the actual draw-specific loadings matters: simply subtracting an unweighted ability mean does not cancel a location shift in MGMFRM.\n\nAdding a vector c to every person's abilities and adding sum(a[i,d] * c[d]) to each item difficulty preserves response probabilities. The derived difference also stays unchanged, for between-item and within-item Q structures. The declared priors still distinguish those shifted parameter vectors. Consequently, the additional R-hat and ESS rows can reveal slow movement in a common location; they do not impose centering, establish likelihood identification, or change the original diagnostic decision. These means describe the fitted panel, not extra population-mean parameters. Good diagnostics for a derived difference do not clear a warning on the full fit.\n\nThe calculation uses the saved diagnostic thresholds, validates the stored chain order and reconstructs model coordinates from raw draws. It works after load_fit_cache and is available in both view = :full and view = :public. The default include_location = false preserves existing diagnostic and report output. This option currently applies to MGMFRMFit; the separate correlated MGMFRM result type has not acquired this option.","title":"Location diagnostics for fixed-Q MGMFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Correlated-MGMFRM:-explicit-fitting-and-saved-results","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use this model when the two named ability dimensions should have an estimated population correlation while item loadings and rater consistency are also estimated. The Q pattern must be fixed and between-item, with at least two pure items per dimension and observations on both dimensions for every person. Within-item Q, additional dimensions, anchors and fitted bias terms are unavailable. The runnable example constructs its own small rating panel and checks save/reload. Its --cmdstan option selects the corresponding backend.\n\nmodel = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated(spec; lkj_eta = 2)\nprior = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior(\n    person_sd = 0.7, rater_sd = 0.4, item_sd = 0.6,\n    log_discrimination_sd = 0.3, log_consistency_sd = 0.2, step_sd = 0.5,\n)\nresult = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(model;\n    prior, backend = :advancedhmc, ndraws = 100, warmup = 100, chains = 2, seed = 92141,\n)\nfit_metadata(result)\ndiagnostics(result)\nsampler_diagnostics(result; phase = :warmup)\nBayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(result) # includes rho\nposterior_mcse(result)                         # rho MCSE from transformed draws\nposterior_summary(result)                      # raw coordinates, including Fisher z\nsave_fit_cache(\"correlated-mgmfrm-fit.jls\", result)\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"correlated-mgmfrm-fit.jls\")\nposterior_mcse(restored)\n\nHere spec must be an admitted two-dimensional family = :mgmfrm specification; the original remains an independent model. The scales and small sampler budget above illustrate the API, not analysis recommendations. Inspect Experimental.surface_contract(model) for the exact operation boundary. An explicit GeneralizedPrior is required for both prior prediction and fitting this combined model.\n\nFor pure item i, the adjacent-category logit is 1.7 * gamma_r * (a_i,d(i) * theta_p,d(i) - b_i - r_r - s_i,h). Active loadings are positive, rater consistencies have product one, severities sum to zero, and nonbaseline steps sum to zero. Ability pairs are jointly normal conditional on rho with covariance person_sd^2 * [1 rho; rho 1]; other free raw coordinates retain the selected normal priors. LKJ eta is a fixed integer shape from 1 to 10,000, not a standard deviation. Its prior is declared on rho and rho = tanh(z) contributes its Jacobian exactly once. A supplied init vector ends in Fisher z. Priors anchor locations/scales that the conditional likelihood alone does not identify; successful sampling does not resolve that distinction.\n\nJulia and backend = :cmdstan return Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit. Raw/direct summaries, MCSE, diagnostics, metadata, full fit_artifact and manual fit caches preserve the prior and z/rho meanings. diagnostics retains the fitted thresholds; MCSE applies no precision acceptance margin. The dedicated fit cache validates the sample, artifact and target even with verify_hash = false. Existing independent MGMFRMFit objects and caches retain their interpretation. Use trusted caches within a compatible Julia environment.\n\nReports, exported tables and optional CairoMakie figures are available; automatic request caching remains unavailable for this result type. Its numerical and short-run engineering checks do not establish convergence, posterior agreement, recovery or a scientifically accepted prior/domain.","title":"Correlated MGMFRM: explicit fitting and saved results"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Prior-and-conditional-posterior-prediction","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Use the same explicit prior to examine implied ratings before fitting:\n\nusing Random\nbefore = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check(model;\n    prior, ndraws = 1000, rng = MersenneTwister(41))\npredictive_check_summary(before; include_grouped = true)\n\nafter = posterior_predictive_check(result; rng = MersenneTwister(42))\npredictive_check_summary(after; include_grouped = true)\nprobabilities = predictive_probabilities(result; draw_indices = [1, 4, 1])\nscores = posterior_predict(result; draw_indices = [1, 4, 1], rng = MersenneTwister(43))\n\nThe prior check samples (rho + 1)/2 from Beta(eta, eta) and abilities from their joint normal prior. Its raw draws end in Fisher z, while its direct draws end in rho. Log-loadings and free log-consistencies follow the selected normal priors before transformation. Observed ratings provide the comparison, without updating the simulated prior. The existing plot_predictive(before) can display the generated category-proportion comparison when CairoMakie is loaded.\n\nPosterior prediction conditions on each joint draw of the existing abilities, loadings, difficulties, severities, consistencies and steps. Population rho affects their posterior distribution; applying it to sampled abilities again would change the model. Category probabilities retain the literal 1.7 factor. Their array is draws by observations by categories in the saved category order; score matrices use the original integer labels. With no selection all retained draws are used. ndraws selects with replacement; draw_indices preserves order and duplicates. Supply only one of those controls. A local seeded RNG reproduces selection and simulation after load_fit_cache in the same Julia environment.\n\nBoth checks concern the existing rating rows and facet levels. Posterior checks return the selected indices, prior/model identity and sampling-quality flag; sampling warnings remain relevant even when observed and replicated summaries agree. These checks describe same-data implications, not held-out accuracy, parameter recovery or prediction for new persons, items or raters. Those targets require separately specified validation and, for new levels, integration over their unknown effects.","title":"Prior and conditional posterior prediction"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Reports-and-figures-from-saved-results","page":"Experimental Models","text":"The report starts from the saved model and prior, retains sampling warnings, and separates raw coordinates from model-coordinate summaries. It labels ability/loading dimensions, reports rho intervals and MCSE prominently, and includes the reconstructed last-rater and item-step constraints. Q specifies structural zeros; an active Q entry is an estimated positive loading, not a coefficient fixed at one. Prior SDs and LKJ eta are fixed inputs; the last-rater and last-step priors are induced by the sum constraints. The report explains their rater-label dependence and the role of priors in setting location/scale.\n\nreport = fit_report(restored; include_prior_predictive = true,\n    prior_predictive_ndraws = 100, seed = 42, require_complete = true)\nprintln(fit_report_markdown(report))\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"correlated-report\", restored;\n    include_prior_predictive = true, prior_predictive_ndraws = 100,\n    seed = 42, require_complete = true)\nreopened = load_fit_report_bundle(\"correlated-report\"; require_complete = true)\n\nReports default to view = :public; view = :full retains reproduction details. Markdown previews selected rows, while JSON and table exports retain all rows. MCSE describes simulation precision and is distinct from credible intervals. Report completeness means no captured section errors; it does not mean that MCMC diagnostics passed or that every analysis is implemented. Unsupported analyses state their reasons. Optional prior checks use the saved prior without changing the posterior comparison. Failed requested sections remain visible; require_complete = true prevents exporting them as a complete report.\n\nusing CairoMakie\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; block = :person, dimension = \"communication\")\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; block = :latent_correlation)\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(restored; block = :latent_correlation)\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(restored; seed = 42)\n\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"correlated-report-with-figures\", restored;\n    include_prior_predictive = true, prior_predictive_ndraws = 100,\n    posterior_lower = 0.1, posterior_upper = 0.9, predictive_interval = 0.8,\n    seed = 42, require_complete = true,\n    figures = (posterior = (block = :item_dimension_discrimination, dimension = \"communication\"),\n        diagnostics = (block = :latent_correlation,), predictive = NamedTuple(),\n        prior = (block = :latent_correlation,), prior_predictive = NamedTuple()))\n\nUse the dimension labels in the fitted specification; communication belongs to the runnable example. Posterior/diagnostic figures default to model coordinates; scale = :raw shows Fisher z and the raw log coordinates instead. All retained draws enter parameter summaries and diagnostics. Only prediction uses optional ndraws or draw_indices. Figure bundles take their intervals, selection and seed from the report call and export PDF, SVG and numerical JSON. Prior figures use the report's already generated prior summaries. Reconstructed steps lacking stored convergence rows are labelled unavailable in trace/rank figures; fixed baselines are distinguished from estimated precision. No finite-panel location diagnostic view or Wright map is provided for this model.","title":"Reports and figures from saved results"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Two-dimensional-correlation-density","page":"Experimental Models","text":"The experimental namespace also provides a density for an exactly two-dimensional simple-structure Q design with at least two pure items per dimension and observations on both dimensions for every person. It appends one raw coordinate zρ to the existing parameter vector and sets ρ = tanh(zρ). Person abilities receive a bivariate normal prior with fixed marginal scale and correlation ρ; ρ receives a normalized two-dimensional LKJ prior including the tanh Jacobian. Existing response likelihood terms and all earlier raw coordinates remain unchanged.\n\ncorrelation_spec = mfrm_spec(data;\n    family = :mgmfrm,\n    dimensions = 2,\n    thresholds = :partial_credit,\n    discrimination = :none,\n    q_matrix = Bool[1 0; 0 1; 1 0; 0 1],\n)\n\ncandidate = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.\n    free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate(correlation_spec; lkj_eta = 2)\n\nraw = BayesianMGMFRM.initial_params(candidate; zrho = 0.0)\nstate = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.\n    free_latent_correlation_2d_state(candidate, raw)\ndiagnostics = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.\n    free_latent_correlation_2d_diagnostics(\n        correlation_spec,\n        raw;\n        finite_difference_coords = (1, 2, length(raw)),\n    )\n\nThis exactly two-dimensional implementation accepts positive integer lkj_eta values. Its name deliberately retains 2d: independently transforming pairwise correlations would not ensure a positive-definite matrix in higher dimensions, where an LKJ-Cholesky parameterization is required. This density-only candidate does not itself accept fitting or cache operations; use the explicitly wrapped specification above for the separate fitting workflow. Its public diagnostics evaluate the density and selected gradient coordinates only; they do not assess sampler convergence or response-level recovery.","title":"Two-dimensional correlation density"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#Stability-and-evidence-limits","page":"Experimental Models","text":"Experimental types, arguments, parameterizations, and report details may change in a minor release. A successful run demonstrates only that exact configuration. It does not establish broader source-equation coverage, identification robustness, known-truth recovery, predictive validity, sensitivity robustness, external-software agreement, construct validity, or real-data validation.\n\nTargeted Float64 tests exercise the stable-residual switch and extreme subnormal inputs, but they do not establish numerical stability for every dataset or sampler trajectory. At the smallest representable scales, gradient contributions may round to zero; users should treat this as a documented numerical limit rather than validation evidence.\n\nArtifact digests support transport and reproducibility checks, but a matching digest does not by itself establish scientific equivalence, execution authenticity, or external validation.","title":"Stability and evidence limits"},{"category":"section","location":"experimental/#API-reference","page":"Experimental Models","text":"<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MGMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.MultidimensionalMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMSpec\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMSpec\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeablePrior\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.surface_contract\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_contract\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.preview\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predict\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_candidate\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_state\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.free_latent_correlation_2d_diagnostics\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit_cache_key\"></a>\n<a id=\"BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.cached_fit\"></a>\n\nThe experimental API reference documents the model types, priors and callable functions. The model explanations and examples remain above.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#Bayesian-Workflow","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nA many-facet analysis is more than a sampling call. The rating design, identification constraints, priors, sampler behavior, predictive performance, and reporting scope all affect what can be interpreted.","title":"Bayesian Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#1.-Validate-the-Rating-Design","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"Create FacetData from long-format ratings and run validate_design. Review:\n\nperson, rater, item, and category coverage;\ndisconnected or weakly linked rating blocks;\nskipped or sparse categories;\nrepeated ratings and optional time or order fields;\nanchors and optional grouping variables;\nthe distinction between planned and accidental missingness.\n\ncoverage_summary, coverage_matrix, rater_overlap, anchor_linking_summary, and rating_design_check provide additional review rows. These checks do not make non-random rater assignment ignorable. The current optional occasion column is categorical metadata; it does not by itself encode exact within-rater order, timestamps, active duration, or randomized presentation. Likewise, declared parameter anchors and rater-linking summaries are not the same as controlled benchmark responses deliberately distributed across a rating sequence. Time-varying severity, fatigue, or learning claims therefore require a separate process-data and temporal-identification design.\n\nIf ratings share an answer, prompt, or item cluster, record response_id and testlet_id separately and run testlet_design_check. The check keeps ordinary rating-graph connectivity separate from person-by-testlet, rater-by-response, rater-by-task, and fixed-Q dimension support. Passing it establishes only conservative structural eligibility for a candidate that is not currently fit-supported, not the presence or interpretation of a clustered effect. Custom thresholds are explicitly marked unvalidated.","title":"1. Validate the Rating Design"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#2.-Inspect-the-Model-Before-Fitting","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"State the estimand first: for example, an ability difference within a named dimension, a rater-severity contrast, or predicted category proportions for a specified rating design. This determines which model assumptions, summaries, and precision checks matter. Use the model comparison to distinguish fixed coefficients from estimated loadings and fixed from estimated ability correlation.\n\nCreate an mfrm_spec and inspect:\n\nmodel_equation for the likelihood and source contract;\nconstraint_table and identification_declarations for the gauge and reference rules;\noptionally, getdesign for the identified parameter vector;\nmodel_manifest for a portable summary of data, model, and design.\n\nFor stable one-dimensional MFRM, call fit(spec) when no separate design inspection is needed. Multidimensional MFRM and GMFRM/MGMFRM use BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec). An estimated correlation requires BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.correlated(spec) first, within the documented two-dimensional restrictions. Specified configurations are not necessarily fit-supported. The support table in Scope and Releases governs whether a fitting call is available.","title":"2. Inspect the Model Before Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#3.-Check-Prior-Implications","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"Choose the prior for the actual model, and pass the same prior to the prior check and fitting call:\n\nModel Prior Prior check\nStable one-dimensional MFRM MFRMPrior prior_predictive_check\nFixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM, independent or correlated MFRMPrior, or the explicit BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.ExchangeablePrior alternative for rater severities BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check\nScalar GMFRM or independent fixed-Q MGMFRM BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check\nIndependent fixed-Q MGMFRM with normalized centered priors Explicit Experimental.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior; all six scales and the prior family are required Experimental.prior_predictive_check\nTwo-dimensional correlated MGMFRM An explicitly supplied BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.GeneralizedPrior; the specification also declares the LKJ correlation prior BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.prior_predictive_check\n\nLook for implausible score distributions, category use, or facet ranges before inspecting the observed-data posterior. Generalized prior SDs apply to raw unconstrained coordinates, including log-loadings and log-consistencies, not directly to the positive transformed parameters. person_sd fixes the marginal ability prior scale; it is not an estimated hyperparameter. The experimental prior check exposes raw/direct draws, replicated scores, and implication warnings. See prior conventions before choosing scales or comparing them across models. Posterior sensitivity requires actual refits under defensible alternatives; repeatedly summarizing one saved fit does not evaluate it.","title":"3. Check Prior Implications"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#4.-Fit-and-Diagnose","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"Use the fitting entry point for the selected model above. Set an integer seed when replay is required and record the sampler controls. Multiple chains are required for meaningful between-chain convergence checks.\n\nReview:\n\nsampler_diagnostics for chain and HMC behavior;\nmcmc_diagnostics for R-hat and ESS;\nparameter_block_diagnostics for block-level patterns;\ndiagnostics for the compact combined status.\n\nThe primary convergence fields are rank-normalized split R-hat, bulk ESS, and tail ESS. The historical rhat and ess fields remain available for compatibility. Use the rank-normalized fields for convergence review; at least two independent chains and enough finite, nondegenerate draws are required.\n\nFor guarded GMFRM/MGMFRM fits, inspect both raw unconstrained and direct constrained parameter rows: the gate fails if either applicable surface fails. A constrained coordinate fixed by a transform with zero raw dimension stays in the output with diagnostic_status = :structurally_fixed, flag = :structurally_fixed, and quality_gate_applicable = false. It is not used in extrema or failure counts. This exception does not apply to a reconstructed constrained coordinate that varies with free raw coordinates; that coordinate remains gated. The versioned diagnostic contract is part of generalized cache identity, so a cache written under the older provisional contract cannot silently supply a modern diagnostic status.\n\nThe sampler summary also reports E-BFMI availability for every expected chain. An unavailable energy diagnostic is missing evidence, not a passing check. Use sampler_diagnostics(fit_result; phase = :warmup) to inspect recorded AdvancedHMC or CmdStan adaptation events separately from retained posterior diagnostics.\n\nA completed run is not automatically a trustworthy run. Divergences, tree-depth saturation, low ESS, unstable R-hat, non-finite evaluations, or constraint failures require investigation.\n\nAfter that convergence review, use posterior_mcse to quantify the simulation error of reported means, standard deviations, and quantiles:\n\nmcse_rows = posterior_mcse(fit_result;\n    probabilities = (0.025, 0.5, 0.975),\n)\n\nFor GMFRM/MGMFRM, BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary and the default posterior_mcse summarize transformed model parameters. posterior_summary instead summarizes their raw coordinates, so match parameter names and scales before comparing intervals and MCSE. A credible interval describes parameter uncertainty conditional on the model and data; MCSE describes numerical uncertainty in an estimated posterior summary. A small MCSE does not imply a narrow credible interval, correct model assumptions, or accurate measurement.\n\nFor fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM, this call also supports correlated abilities and either rater prior, including reloaded fits from either backend. It reports reconstructed parameters and rho on their model scales by default; see multidimensional precision summaries for coordinate selection and fixed/short-chain statuses.\n\nFor a derived estimand, compute one value per posterior draw while preserving the contiguous chain blocks, place the values in matrix columns, and call the matrix method with chains and parameter_names. The function deliberately does not turn MCSE into a universal pass/fail threshold. Required precision depends on the reported estimand and substantive decision; MCSE also cannot repair or certify non-converged chains. The saved-fit contrast example implements this calculation for within-dimension ability differences, rater severity differences and log-consistency ratios, including optional practical ranges and the simulation error of their posterior probabilities.\n\nFor independent fixed-Q MGMFRM, optionally inspect diagnostics(fit_result; include_location = true) for common ability/item location movement. Its location rows supplement the original diagnostic assessment and are not automatically included in the ordinary report. The saved-fit walkthrough shows these checks, intervals and MCSE together without sampling again.","title":"4. Fit and Diagnose"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#5.-Examine-Predictions-and-Residuals","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"Use posterior_predictive_check, predictive_check_summary, and calibration_table to compare observed and replicated outcomes. predictive_residuals, predictive_standardized_residuals, residual_summary, fit_stats, and rater_diagnostics help locate misfit.\n\nThe generalized posterior predictions replicate the existing rating rows using the fitted persons, items, and raters. Agreement checks conditional model fit; it does not measure held-out accuracy or prediction for a new person, item, or rater. A category-proportion plot also cannot establish parameter recovery or interval coverage. Those questions need their own design and evidence.\n\npredictive_standardized_residuals reports draw-specific Pearson residuals and explicitly excludes rows with negligible predictive variance. Non-finite predictions are errors, not low-variance exclusions. This is a low-level input, not a test of local independence. The provisional local_dependence_contract separates single-rating item pairs, within-rater item pairs, and rater pairs; fixes draw-specific support, duplicate rejection, weighting, paired predictive tails, and multiplicity scopes; stratifies estimation by testlet; and forbids implicit rater aggregation or cross-rater cross-item pairing. Its decision labels remain disabled until known-truth calibration.\n\nWhen response_id and testlet_id are declared, use local_dependence_summary for the corresponding report-only pair summaries:\n\nld = local_dependence_summary(fit_result)\n\nThe function selects distinct posterior draws, generates one conditional replicated dataset from each selected draw, and applies the same matching and validity rules to observed and replicated standardized residuals. It keeps single-rater item pairs, within-rater item pairs, and rater pairs on the same response and criterion separate. Criterion-split scoring is not silently relabelled as single-rater Q3, and applicability is evaluated separately in each testlet so one criterion-split stratum does not suppress another valid single-rating stratum. Sparse or undefined pairs with at least one common unit remain structured pair rows with missing evidence values; zero-overlap combinations remain visible in family counts and testlet support graphs. Family-wide and testlet-specific support statuses are reported separately. Before large work or allocations, the API counts candidate-pair rows, shared-unit links, positive-pair-by-draw cells, pair/common-unit-by-draw cells, and draw-by-observation-by-category cells. Posterior predictive tail fractions, BH-adjusted values, and the all-family maximum statistic are calibration-pending references; none is a decision label or evidence for a specific mechanism.\n\nThese local-dependence summaries are uncalibrated diagnostic references. They provide no decision threshold or mechanism classification and do not make clustered effects available for fitting. The separate research helpers are documented in the Validation and Evidence API; running a simulation study is not a prerequisite for an ordinary analysis.\n\nObservation-row LOO does not validate prediction for a wholly unseen response whose shared effect was informed by other rows from that response.\n\nDFF rows are screening information unless the fitted model explicitly supports the corresponding identified effect. Statistical differences should be reported separately from practical magnitude and substantive interpretation.\n\nFor figures without assembling draw matrices, load CairoMakie and use:\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(fit_result; block = :rater)\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_diagnostics(fit_result; block = :rater)\nBayesianMGMFRM.plot_predictive(fit_result; ndraws = 200, seed = 42)\n\nThe first shows parameter uncertainty, the second retained-chain behavior, and the third category proportions under conditional replications of the fitted rows. BayesianMGMFRM.plot_wright(fit_result) additionally displays the stable MFRM measures and item-step boundaries on a shared logit scale. Generalized fits require dimension/scale interpretation and do not support a Wright map. See figures and export.","title":"5. Examine Predictions and Residuals"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#6.-Compare-Models-Carefully","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"WAIC, LOO, PSIS-LOO, and K-fold summaries require compatible observations and an explicit prediction target. Inspect pointwise influence, Pareto-k, and held-out diagnostics. Relative weights are not posterior model probabilities, and a ranking is not by itself a superiority claim.\n\nSensitivity work should cover defensible prior choices and any threshold, anchor, dimensionality, or Q-matrix decisions that could change the interpretation.","title":"6. Compare Models Carefully"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian-workflow/#7.-Report-the-Boundary","page":"Bayesian Workflow","text":"posterior_summary, fair_average_summary, separation_reliability_summary, wright_map_data, and other reporting helpers return table-oriented results where the chosen model supports them. In particular, the stable MFRM Wright map is unavailable for generalized fits. Use model-scale summaries for the named dimension instead. fit_report assembles the requested machine-oriented sections. Use fit_report(fit; view = :public) or fit_report_public for a reader-facing structured projection, and fit_report_markdown for a Markdown preview.\n\nThe top-level status describes model exposure (:supported or :experimental); it does not certify that every requested report section was computed. fit_report_health derives report-generation health from the section statuses. A captured status = :error section sets report_status = :incomplete, while :not_requested and :unsupported do not. Use require_complete = true on fit_report, report exporters, or fit_report_dossier when a failed requested section should stop export. Use on_section_error = :throw when the first failing section should abort immediately.\n\nReport completeness and diagnostic quality are separate: a complete report can preserve MCMC warnings or unsupported sections. Read the diagnostic assessment and the status of each section needed for the claim. For positive loadings or consistencies, a posterior probability above zero reflects the imposed positive support and is not evidence of an effect. Define meaningful comparisons before interpreting these rows. In MGMFRM, consistency one is the model's product-one reference, not a calibrated reliability threshold.\n\nSave the fit with save_fit_cache and reload it with load_fit_cache to regenerate tables and figures in the same environment without sampling again. Keep source data, analysis code, and environment/version information alongside portable reports; the serialized cache alone is not a durable analysis archive. Recorded-fit caches retain exact integrity hashes while allowing tiny roundoff differences in recomputed means and SDs. This neither changes the saved values nor establishes cross-version compatibility.\n\nA report should state:\n\nmodel family, threshold regime, dimensions, and constraints;\nrating-design limitations;\npriors and sampler controls;\nconvergence and predictive diagnostics;\nthe prediction target for model comparison;\nunsupported features and the limits of generalization.\n\nExperimental fixed-Q MGMFRM results must not be generalized to exploratory multidimensional models or freely estimated correlation structures.","title":"7. Report the Boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"<a id=\"保存済みモデルの予測比較・回復評価：隔離したAPI候補\"></a>\n<a id=\"保存済みモデルの予測比較・回復評価：隔離したAPI候補-1\"></a>","title":"Saved Prediction and Recovery"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Saved-Prediction,-Model-Comparison-and-Recovery","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nUse this workflow to compare predictions for the same ratings and evaluate named parameters against generating truths. The common operations cover eight fit types: scalar MFRM and GMFRM, raw and normalized MGMFRM, correlated MGMFRM, fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM, correlated MFRM and exchangeable-rater MFRM. Their model, prior and fitting restrictions remain different; see the scope and operation table.\n\nRun the examples with the candidate project loaded through julia --project=.. Saved-result evaluation does not fit a posterior. K-fold scoring additionally requires a fit trained separately on each fold's training data. The API reference documents arguments and return values.\n\nYour question Route What the output establishes\nHow do models predict the same observed ratings? compare_predictions Descriptive WAIC or raw IS-LOO comparison from fitted training data.\nHow do models predict ratings excluded from fitting? prediction_refit_plan → prediction_fold_observations → prediction_kfold → compare_kfold_predictions K-fold comparison of saved training fits, conditional on existing facet levels.\nHow close are estimates to generating truths? recovery_quantities → recovery_evaluation Named-quantity evaluation and MCMC MCSE for one dataset.\nWhat happens across independent datasets with the same design and truths? recovery_replications Quantity-specific summaries retaining every planned replication; replication SE requires evidence of dataset independence.\n\nThese functions do not select a model automatically, establish construct validity or recommend a sufficient number of persons or raters. Separate changes to fixed/estimated coefficients, dimensions/Q, covariance and priors before interpreting a comparison. The output retains these model contracts; it does not align ability components across models with different dimensions.\n\n<a id=\"保存から比較まで\"></a>\n<a id=\"保存から比較まで-1\"></a>","title":"Saved Prediction, Model Comparison and Recovery"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Compare-saved-fits","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Start with results saved by save_fit_cache(path, fit). Replace the example IDs with persistent rating IDs from your study. Each rating needs its own ID, including multiple ratings by one rater or of the same response. Matching row numbers alone does not establish matching data.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\ninclude(\"examples/saved_model_comparison.jl\") # Run from the candidate project root.\n\nresult = saved_model_comparison(\n    \"MFRM\" => \"mfrm.jls\",\n    \"GMFRM\" => \"gmfrm.jls\",\n    \"MGMFRM D2\" => \"mgmfrm-d2.jls\";\n    dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\",\n    observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    comparison_axis=:dimensions,\n    criterion=:waic,\n    baseline=\"MFRM\",\n    se_unit=:none,\n)\n\n[(; row.model, row.elpd_difference, row.se_elpd_difference,\n    row.sampling_warning, row.criterion_warning,\n    row.problem_observation_ids) for row in result.rows]\n\nFor fits already in memory, call BayesianMGMFRM.compare_predictions(\"MFRM\"=>fit1, \"GMFRM\"=>fit2; ...). For previously extracted BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_observations(...) bundles, omit the ID arguments: the bundles already retain them. Supply only fits or only prediction bundles, never a mixture.\n\nWhen row orders differ, pass observation_ids=Dict(\"MFRM\"=>ids1, \"GMFRM\"=>ids2, ...), with each vector in its fit's original row order. Scores, person/item/rater labels, additional facets, category definitions and the complete training-data binding are checked before alignment to the baseline model. Inputs are unchanged. Draws from different fits need not be paired; each fit uses all original chains and retained draws. Subsampled, reordered or resampled draw collections are rejected.\n\n<a id=\"比較軸・評価指標・不確実性\"></a>\n<a id=\"比較軸・評価指標・不確実性-1\"></a>","title":"Compare saved fits"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Comparison-axes,-scores-and-uncertainty","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Argument Meaning\ncomparison_axis=:model Require the same dimensions and Q. Inspect metadata for differences in family and other assumptions.\n:dimensions Allow dimensions and Q to differ while comparing predictions of identical observations.\n:q_matrix Allow Q to differ at the same dimension count.\ncriterion=:waic Compute the existing WAIC from original pointwise log likelihoods.\n:raw_loo Unsmoothed importance-sampling LOO; inspect weight instability.\n:hill_smoothed_loo The existing Hill smoother; not independently reference-validated PSIS.\nse_unit=:none Default: difference SE is missing; no inferential unit is silently selected.\n:observation Approximate SE treating ratings as exchangeable units.\n:person Sum ELPD differences within each person, then compute an approximate SE treating persons as exchangeable units.\n\nFor rating differences d_i, the observation-level SE is sqrt(N * var(d)). The person-level version uses person totals D_g and sqrt(G * var(D)). One unit returns missing. This is one-way aggregation, not a general correction for crossed item/rater dependence. Selecting a unit does not establish its exchangeability. Neither estimate is MCMC MCSE or the replication SE of a design simulation.\n\nelpd_difference is model minus baseline, so positive values favor that model on the selected score. Rows retain input order; no winner, automatic adoption or posterior model probability is returned. Do not decide from point-estimate rankings while uncertainty or warnings remain unresolved.\n\nAn axis label does not isolate the effect of changing that axis. For example, the scalar GMFRM has rater-owned steps, whereas fixed-Q MGMFRM has item-owned steps; slope and scale conventions also differ. Their predictive comparison can address the complete alternatives, but cannot attribute a difference solely to dimensionality. Review threshold ownership and the saved model_contract.\n\nThis route approximates omission of one rating with the specification and level sets fixed. It is conditional on existing levels, not leave-person/rater/item-out cross-validation, integration over new levels or refitted K-fold evaluation. Match the prediction and omission units to the question; see the loo FAQ.\n\n<a id=\"診断と互換性\"></a>\n<a id=\"診断と互換性-1\"></a>","title":"Comparison axes, scores and uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Diagnostics-and-compatibility","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Each row retains sampling warnings, criterion warnings, problem rating IDs, retained-draw counts, model information and saved-sample hashes where supported. Warned models are not dropped from the comparison roster. Even status=:descriptive does not establish scientific acceptance. Comparison MCMC MCSE, relative-efficiency correction and crossed-cluster adjustment are not computed. Predictive fit does not establish construct validity or interval coverage.\n\nSchemas are bayesianmgmfrm.prediction_comparison.v1, bayesianmgmfrm.prediction_observations.v1 and bayesianmgmfrm.prediction_criteria.v1. Re-extract earlier .prototype.v1 bundles from their saved fits with the candidate API. Existing compare_models, sensitivity_comparison, fit-cache formats and root exports retain their interfaces. Call candidate operations with the BayesianMGMFRM. prefix; their final acceptance remains open.\n\nThe available checks cover analytic scores, synthetic draws, persistence, invalid-input rejection and compatibility. They do not establish model-selection accuracy, empirical recommendations, study-design guarantees or release completion.\n\n<a id=\"MGMFRMと固定係数の多次元MFRMのレポート\"></a>\n<a id=\"MGMFRMと固定係数の多次元MFRMのレポート-1\"></a>","title":"Diagnostics and compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Reports-for-MGMFRM-and-fixed-coefficient-multidimensional-MFRM","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"The following saved-fit types accept the same WAIC and raw IS-LOO arguments in fit_report and save_fit_report_bundle. Existing diagnostics, prediction summaries and persistence routes are retained.\n\nResult type Model\nExperimental.NormalizedMGMFRMFit Normalized prior; identity population ability correlation.\nExperimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit Two-dimensional correlated MGMFRM.\nMultidimensionalMFRMFit Independent multidimensional MFRM with fixed Q coefficients and unit logits, using canonical records.\nExperimental.CorrelatedMFRMFit The correlated two-dimensional fixed-coefficient model.\nExperimental.ExchangeableMFRMFit Exchangeable rater prior with independent or admitted two-dimensional correlated abilities.\n\nFixed versus estimated coefficients, D/Q, covariance and rater priors remain separate model choices. Reports preserve their respective model, prior and scale records. Legacy fixed-Q v1 records can be read for common comparison and criteria, but public reports still reject them because their specification semantics differ. Do not migrate them by changing a format label.\n\nfit = load_fit_cache(\"mgmfrm.jls\")\nreport = fit_report(fit;\n    dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    include_waic=true, include_loo=true,\n    draw_indices=[1, 2, 2], # Used only for posterior-predictive display.\n    require_complete=true,\n)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"mgmfrm-report\", report; require_complete=true)\nrestored = load_fit_report_bundle(\"mgmfrm-report\"; require_complete=true)\n\nFor these five report types, include_waic and include_loo both default to false, producing :not_requested sections. Historical :unsupported sections remain readable. Requested criteria require a dataset ID and unique rating IDs; supplying those criterion IDs without requesting a criterion is rejected. The original three fit types retain their existing report keywords and defaults.\n\nWAIC/LOO use all retained draws and chains independently of draw_indices. Their rows contain total ELPD, approximate SE, draw counts and warnings; diagnostic_rows contain pointwise ELPD, diagnostics and rating IDs. problem_observation_ids and problem_rows identify criterion warnings, so a problem near the end of the full observation table remains visible in a preview. Markdown truncates previews to the requested row count; JSON and tables retain all rows. LOO settings are loo_threshold=0.7, loo_tail_fraction=0.2 and loo_min_tail_draws=5. Report LOO is unsmoothed. Changing a warning threshold does not improve precision or establish scientific acceptance.\n\nUse posterior_mcse(fit) for parameter-summary MCMC error. Normalized/correlated MGMFRM reports also contain direct_posterior.mcse_rows; that table was not added to fixed-coefficient MFRM reports. Criterion se_elpd is an approximate SE across observations, without MCMC or crossed-cluster correction. These interpretations and warnings survive public display, Markdown, JSON, tables and reload.\n\nWAIC and LOO are separate sections: an error in one does not erase the other. on_section_error=:capture retains an incomplete report; require_complete=true rejects that state. :computed and report_status=:complete describe report execution, not successful inference.\n\nexamples/saved_mgmfrm_report.jl generates these five report types without refitting. examples/normalized_mgmfrm.jl starts from synthetic data and saves rating IDs; its short MCMC is inadequate for inference. Existing numerical and figure checks remain evidence for their recorded scope. The common APIs below prepare refits and score/aggregate saved folds. Automatic multi-model refitting, new-level prediction, reference PSIS and model-selection validation remain open.\n\n<a id=\"K-foldの学習データを固定し、保存結果で保留データを採点する\"></a>\n<a id=\"K-foldの学習データを固定し、保存結果で保留データを採点する-1\"></a>","title":"Reports for MGMFRM and fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Prepare-K-fold-training-data-and-score-held-out-ratings","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"For prediction of unused ratings of known persons, raters and items, score each fold with posterior draws fitted only to its training data. This differs from training-data WAIC/LOO. New-person or new-rater generalization requires a separate prediction target and integration. Decide in advance whether all ratings of a response must be held out together.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, Serialization\n\n# Prespecify data/spec, prior, persistent rating_ids and the splitting rule.\nplan = kfold_plan(spec.data; k=5, group_by=:response_id)\nprepared = BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_refit_plan(spec, plan;\n    prior=MFRMPrior(), dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\", observation_ids=rating_ids)\nrequest = prepared.folds[1]\nserialize(\"fold-1-request.jls\", request)\n\n# Scalar MFRM example; use the sampler settings from the analysis plan.\ntraining_fit = fit(request.model; prior=request.prior,\n    chains=4, warmup=1000, ndraws=1000)\nsave_fit_cache(\"fold-1-fit.jls\", training_fit)\n\n# Resume with the same request and its matching saved training fit.\ninclude(\"examples/saved_fold_predictions.jl\")\nscored = saved_fold_predictions(\"fold-1-fit.jls\", deserialize(\"fold-1-request.jls\"))\nscored.pointwise_loglikelihood   # draw × heldout rating\nscored.observation_ids          # Persistent IDs in this column order.\nscored.sampling_quality         # Retains diagnostic warnings.\n\nPreparation/scoring themselves perform no fitting and need no full-data posterior. For GMFRM, MGMFRM, normalized MGMFRM, fixed-Q multidimensional MFRM and their admitted correlated/exchangeable-prior routes, explicitly supply the corresponding experimental model and prior, then use Experimental.fit(request.model; prior=request.prior, ...). Existing dimension, Q, prior and backend restrictions apply. The example's iteration counts do not guarantee precision.\n\nPreparation checks every fold and existing-level condition. Training data are the exact complement of held-out data, and every rating is held out once. FacetData(table) normally sorts levels; explicitly encoded inputs can use a different order. Training preserves the original level order so reconstruction of the last level does not move its induced prior to another label. Item/Q alignment, dimension names, prior scales and covariance are preserved. Grouping on metadata does not add a fitted effect.\n\nScoring detects altered requests and checks the actual training data, model, prior and correlation configuration. Full-data fits, different priors/LKJ settings and damaged samples are rejected. Already correlated draws are not transformed again. Legacy fixed-MFRM v1 records are not automatically migrated.\n\nAll retained draws/chains are used, including diagnostic warnings. include_probabilities=false avoids allocating category-probability arrays. Returned likelihoods exclude priors and Jacobians. source_binding joins source data to persistent IDs; split_binding binds the split. They agree across models/dimensions using the same data and split, while training-target identities also include the model/prior. These checks detect mismatches; they do not certify convergence or execution history. Held-out results are rejected by the training-data WAIC/LOO entry prediction_criteria.\n\nThere is no automatic all-model/all-fold refit runner. Aggregation below checks source, split and prediction target rather than matching only row counts or positions. It cannot detect whether held-out outcomes informed later model or prior tuning. Fitting and figure rendering are separate operations.","title":"Prepare K-fold training data and score held-out ratings"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Matched-dimensionality-comparison","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"To ask whether the same ratings benefit from multiple ability dimensions, compare the one-dimensional MGMFRM restriction with a between-item MGMFRM. Both use item-owned steps, the 1.7 response multiplier, positive loadings and rater consistency. The ordinary scalar GMFRM uses a different equation; see the distinction and prior limits.\n\nThe following prepares two models without running a sampler. Use your ingested data, persistent rating_ids, Q rows in data.item_levels order, and dimension names. Choose prior scales before inspecting held-out outcomes; the values below illustrate the arguments and are not calibrated defaults.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\nconst E = BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental\ninclude(\"examples/matched_dimension_refit_plans.jl\")\n\nprior = E.NormalizedMGMFRMPrior(prior_model=:exchangeable,\n    person_sd=1.0, rater_sd=0.5, item_sd=1.0,\n    log_discrimination_sd=0.3, log_consistency_sd=0.3, step_sd=0.7)\nsplit = kfold_plan(data; k=5, group_by=:response_id)\nplans = matched_dimension_refit_plans(data, split;\n    q_matrix=study_q, dimension_labels=study_dimension_names,\n    prior, dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\", observation_ids=rating_ids)\n\nThe comparison Q must have at least two columns and exactly one active entry per item. Both plans use the identical split and prior settings. The shared validator still checks level coverage and Q support. Grouping by response ID holds its ratings together; it does not add response dependence to the fitted model. This is prediction for existing persons, items and raters, not new-level prediction. A design that cannot retain every facet level in training is rejected.\n\nEach entry is a label paired with a prepared plan. Fit each plan's training requests using your chosen backend and justified numerical controls:\n\nlabel, prepared = first(plans)\nrequest = first(prepared.folds)\nfit = E.fit(request.model; prior=request.prior, backend=:advancedhmc,\n    chains=4, warmup=1000, ndraws=1000, seed=20261012)\nsave_fit_cache(\"one-dimension-fold-1.jls\", fit)\nscore = BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_fold_observations(fit, request;\n    include_probabilities=false)\nscore.sampling_quality\n\nRepeat for every request of both plans, keeping every fit, diagnostic warning and execution failure. The counts above are an example, not a precision assurance. Aggregate all folds with prediction_kfold, then call compare_kfold_predictions(\"One dimension\"=>one, \"Multiple dimensions\"=>many; comparison_axis=:dimensions) as described below. The complete-cache helper saved_kfold_comparison can also use these prepared plans.\n\nThis comparison keeps one loading per item and the same marginal prior rules, but changes person-level dependence through dimension sharing. Equal scale arguments do not equalize joint prior predictive distributions. Data-sampling K-fold SE remains unavailable; optional numerical MCSE is described below. A returned score difference alone does not establish dimensionality, construct validity or a reproducible advantage.\n\n<a id=\"保存した全foldの集約とモデル比較\"></a>\n<a id=\"保存した全foldの集約とモデル比較-1\"></a>","title":"Matched dimensionality comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Aggregate-all-saved-folds-and-compare-models","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Prepare each model with the same source data, persistent IDs and split. Save prepared with Serialization.serialize and reuse it when resuming. Once training fits are saved, the example scores held-out ratings and compares models:\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_kfold_comparison.jl\")\n\nresult = saved_kfold_comparison(\n    \"MFRM\" => (; prepared=mfrm_prepared,\n        caches=Dict(1=>\"mfrm-fold-1.jls\", 2=>\"mfrm-fold-2.jls\")),\n    \"MGMFRM D2\" => (; prepared=mgmfrm_prepared,\n        caches=Dict(1=>\"mgmfrm-fold-1.jls\", 2=>\"mgmfrm-fold-2.jls\"));\n    comparison_axis=:dimensions, baseline=\"MFRM\",\n)\n[(; row.model, row.elpd, row.elpd_difference, row.complete,\n    row.status, row.sampling_warning, row.se_elpd_difference) for row in result.rows]\nresult.rows[1].folds # Per-fold status, diagnostics, failures and saved-sample identity.\n\nThis example uses two folds; supply the cache mapping for every fold in your actual plan. Declare :model, :dimensions or :q_matrix as above. Inspect model_contract for covariance, coefficients, dimensions and priors instead of inferring differences from model names. Rows retain input order, differences are model minus baseline, and no automatic ranks, selected model or weights are returned.\n\nFor previously scored results, use BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold(prepared, scores; failures=...), then BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold_predictions(\"MFRM\"=>a, \"GMFRM\"=>b; comparison_axis=:model). Scores may arrive in any fold order. Persistent IDs align differently ordered ratings after checking scores, facet labels, categories and splits. All retained chains and draws are used.\n\nState Aggregation/comparison behavior\nAll folds present, no diagnostic warning Return complete ELPD and baseline differences; scientific acceptance remains separate.\nAll folds present, diagnostic warnings Retain those folds/models and descriptive values; the comparison is warning-bearing.\nResult not received Fold is :missing; total ELPD is missing, with available rating scores retained for inspection.\nDeclared execution failure failures=[fold_id=>\"failure reason\"] records :failed, distinct from missing.\nAny candidate model incomplete Retain completed-model scores but set every model contrast to missing.\nDuplicate, mismatched split/target or altered saved content Error; do not silently replace with missing.\n\nEach model in the saved example can include failures=[2=>\"storage failure\"]; exclude that fold from caches. Diagnostic warnings are not discarded as execution failures, and successful-fold sums are not treated as complete ELPD. diagnostics_ok reflects recorded flags, not a convergence/performance guarantee.\n\nRating i is scored by log(mean(exp(loglikelihood[s,i]))) over its training posterior draws s, not by mean log likelihood. Holding out groups still returns a sum of rating-wise marginal predictive scores, not the joint predictive density obtained by multiplying within-group likelihoods before averaging over draws.\n\nData-sampling SE and repeated-dataset uncertainty are not computed. Neither independent-fold SE nor simple rating-wise SE is silently substituted. MCMC MCSE is opt-in, as described below. Point differences alone do not establish a useful or reproducible advantage. This route excludes new-person/rater/item marginal prediction and adjustment for model tuning after inspecting held-out results.","title":"Aggregate all saved folds and compare models"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Numerical-error-of-the-K-fold-comparison","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Before spending more computation on a small score difference, ask whether its MCMC error is small enough for your intended numerical comparison. This is a question about approximating the fitted posterior, conditional on the observed data and the chosen split. It is separate from how scores would change with new data, a different split, or a different model/prior. The distinction between MCSE and data-sampling SE is also explained in the loo glossary.\n\nRequest mcse=:delta when aggregating the existing saved scores:\n\none = BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold(one_plan, one_scores;\n    mcse=:delta, independent_fold_rng=true)\nmany = BayesianMGMFRM.prediction_kfold(many_plan, many_scores;\n    mcse=:delta, independent_fold_rng=true)\ncomparison = BayesianMGMFRM.compare_kfold_predictions(\n    \"One dimension\"=>one, \"Multiple dimensions\"=>many;\n    comparison_axis=:dimensions, independent_model_rng=true)\n\n[(; row.model, row.elpd_difference,\n    numerical_error=row.difference_mcmc_error,\n    data_sampling_se=row.se_elpd_difference,\n    row.sampling_warning) for row in comparison.rows]\none.folds[1].mcmc_error # Includes draw count and concentration information.\n\nUse the true declarations only if fold fits and model fits actually used independent MCMC random streams, respectively. Reusing the same random stream, paired/common random numbers, or posterior draws does not satisfy this condition. Different seeds alone are not a proof of independence. Independence here concerns the simulations conditional on the data; training folds may still overlap. Detectable sample-hash reuse is rejected, but the API cannot certify how the random streams were generated. Without the declarations, the relevant combined MCSE remains missing and available fold-level results are retained.\n\nThe existing saved_kfold_comparison(...; mcse=:delta, independent_fold_rng=true, independent_model_rng=true) example forwards these options when starting from fit-cache paths. Omitting the options preserves the previous result structure and values. The result is serializable with Julia's Serialization; keep the source-bound prepared plans and score records together.\n\nFor a fold with draws s and held-out ratings i, define\n\nwidehatell_i=logleft(S^-1sum_s p_siright)qquad\nu_s=sum_ileft(fracp_siS^-1sum_t p_ti-1right)\n\nThe implementation evaluates the ratio using log probabilities and expm1, so very small category probabilities need not be exponentiated on their original scale. Applying the existing autocorrelation-aware mean MCSE to the joint influence sequence u gives a first-order delta approximation to the numerical error of the fold's sum of log predictive probabilities. Summing influences within each draw retains covariance between ratings. Adding separate rating-wise variances would generally give a different answer. The mean-MCSE calculation uses the installed MCMCDiagnosticTools implementation, with the package's existing split-chain convention. This is not PSIS or an importance-weight calculation.\n\nIndependent fold errors combine as the square root of the sum of their squared MCSEs. Independent model errors combine in the same way for a difference. This sums MCMC variances, not variances of fold scores across overlapping datasets. Naive fold-independence estimates of data-sampling uncertainty can be misleading; see Bengio and Grandvalet (2004).\n\nField or state Interpretation\nmcmc_error.value Estimated computational standard error of total ELPD, in natural-log units.\nmcmc_error.per_rating The same error divided by the number of scored ratings; not a rating-wise independence SE.\ndifference_mcmc_error Error of the reported model-minus-baseline difference, subject to the separate simulation-independence declaration.\n:first_order_approximation The estimate is available, but convergence, adequate mixing and the local linear approximation still need review.\nFewer than two chains or ten retained draws per chain MCSE is missing; these minima are computational guards, not evidence that ten draws suffice.\n:first_order_degenerate The influence is constant or numerically cancels. Higher-order error can remain; zero MCSE is not reported.\nmax_draw_probability_share Largest contribution of a single draw to any rating's estimated predictive mean. Concentration near one calls the approximation into question; no calibrated cutoff is imposed.\nMissing/failed fold or unavailable required MCSE Total/difference MCSE is missing. Scores, failures and diagnostics remain visible.\nBaseline minus itself Exactly zero computational error for this identity when the comparison is complete. It is not evidence of model adequacy.\n\nMCSE does not bound finite-MCMC bias, missed posterior modes, model misspecification or uncertainty from collecting data. For example, if two rating probabilities move in opposite directions, the first derivative of their summed log means can cancel while second-order variability remains. Small MCSE alone therefore does not justify selecting a dimension count, declaring equivalence, or ignoring diagnostic warnings. No automatic extension of a fit, precision threshold, confidence interval or model ranking is performed.\n\nPreparation and scoring use the unreleased v2 schemas, which retain the complete fold roster and content hashes. Rebuild older candidate preparations/scores from the same specification/split and rescore their matching saved fits; no new MCMC is required. Fit-cache formats and fold-request v1 remain unchanged. Hashes detect accidental modification; they do not authenticate execution history or prove absence of data leakage.\n\n<a id=\"設計シミュレーションのための共通回復評価\"></a>\n<a id=\"設計シミュレーションのための共通回復評価-1\"></a>","title":"Numerical error of the K-fold comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Evaluate-recovery-for-design-simulations","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit) and BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_evaluation(fit, truth; ...) match named model quantities to truths across the same eight fit types. They evaluate saved draws from one dataset, without generating data, fitting, aggregating independent replications, performing SBC or recommending sample sizes. Fixed-coefficient MFRM requires canonical records; legacy v1 is rejected.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, Serialization\ninclude(\"examples/saved_recovery_evaluation.jl\")\n\nfit = load_fit_cache(\"simulation-fit.jls\")\ncatalog = BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit)\n[(; r.parameter, r.block, r.dimension_label, r.parameter_space, r.fixed)\n    for r in catalog.rows]\n\n# A model-coordinate name => truth dictionary saved during generation.\n# Match generating and analysis targets in advance; do not use estimates as truth.\ntruth = deserialize(\"truth-model-coordinates.jls\")\nevaluation = saved_recovery_evaluation(\"simulation-fit.jls\", truth;\n    dataset_id=\"design-A-replication-001\", observation_ids=rating_ids, interval=.95)\nevaluation.rows\nevaluation.sampling_quality\n\nSpecify any nonempty subset of quantities. Unknown names, nonfinite truths, correlation truths outside [-1,1] and duplicate rating IDs are rejected. Rows retain model order rather than dictionary order. The catalogue includes supported reconstructed last severities/steps, correlations and structural constants. derived=missing means the existing route lacks derivation metadata, not that the quantity is an independent parameter.\n\nTruths must match the listed scale, facet labels and identification constraints. Fixed-coefficient MFRM retains unit logits; generalized models retain their own scales. Correlations are evaluated as rho, not Fisher z, and correlated ability draws are not transformed twice. Cross-dimensional alignment, cross-model rescaling and truth centering are not automatic. Matching names alone does not establish identical estimands: GMFRM has sum-zero item difficulties and free severities, whereas MGMFRM has sum-zero severities. GMFRM item discrimination and rater consistency are dimensionless, separate from location scales.\n\nOutput Meaning and limit\nposterior_mean, posterior_sd, posterior_lower/upper Summaries and the requested equal-tailed interval using all retained draws/chains.\nbias This posterior mean minus truth; not average bias across independent datasets.\nsquared_error, interval_width, covered One-quantity, one-dataset evaluations; coverage applies only to estimated quantities.\nfixed, coverage_applicable Constants have covered=missing and coverage_applicable=false; point intervals do not count as calibration.\nfixed_value_matches_truth Whether a constant equals its declared truth; disagreement is warned, not silently corrected.\nmcse Existing MCMC MCSE for the same quantity's mean, SD and interval endpoints; unavailable values retain their status and missing.\nsampling_quality, sampling_warning Original diagnostics; warnings do not erase evaluation values.\nsource_binding, model_contract, evaluated_draws_hash Data/ID, model/prior/scale and evaluated-draw identities.\n\nA constant's zero MCSE follows from its fixed model value, not precise estimation or model correctness. Endpoint MCSE does not certify a coverage classification, and this function applies no precision threshold. MCMC error differs from between-dataset replication uncertainty; see the Stan posterior-analysis guide.\n\nDo not count persons, items, chains or folds within one fit as independent simulation replications. Historical parameter_recovery_summary summarizes parameter rows descriptively, without replication SE; its vector-truth interface and meaning remain unchanged. Do not pass all new rows, including constants, into that old summary indiscriminately. Selecting coverage_applicable rows still does not turn them into independent replications.\n\nSBC needs a separate design generating parameters and data from the analysis model's joint prior across multiple datasets. Fixed-truth recovery is not SBC; see the Stan SBC guide. The API cannot authenticate generation or independence, so it retains generating_truth_verification=:not_established, independent_replications=:not_verified and sbc=:not_performed. The next operation reuses these evaluations. A general data-generation, fitting and between-design comparison runner is not implemented.\n\n<a id=\"固定した真値でのデータセット間の回復集計\"></a>\n<a id=\"固定した真値でのデータセット間の回復集計-1\"></a>","title":"Evaluate recovery for design simulations"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Aggregate-fixed-truth-recovery-across-datasets","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_replications matches saved recovery_evaluation results to a planned dataset roster and summarizes bias, mean absolute error, MSE, RMSE, coverage and mean interval width for each quantity. One dataset is one replication for that quantity. Persons, items, chains, folds and other parameter rows do not increase the replication count. Use separate scenario_id values for different sample sizes, allocations, analysis models, priors or truths.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, Serialization\ninclude(\"examples/saved_recovery_replications.jl\")\n# Each file contains that dataset's serialized recovery_evaluation.\nsummary = saved_recovery_replications([\"recovery-D1.jls\", \"recovery-D2.jls\"];\n    planned_dataset_ids=[\"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"],\n    parameters=[\"theta[P1]\"], # Use a name from the actual recovery_quantities result.\n    scenario_id=\"predeclared-fixed-design-A\",\n    dataset_independence=(status=:unresolved, evidence=\"Generation and seed independence remain unverified\"),\n    failures=[\"D3\" => \"Estimation interrupted\"])\n\nHere D1/D2 are completed, D3 failed and D4 is missing. Prespecify the roster and quantities before seeing results. Inferring the denominator from available files would exclude failures. Dataset IDs must be unique and failure reasons explicit. Duplicate/unplanned IDs, both a result and failure for one ID, and altered records are rejected. Output follows the planned roster regardless of input order.\n\nOutput Interpretation\nledger Every planned completed/failed/missing dataset, retaining completed evaluations, diagnostics and MCSE.\nsummaries[i].all_completed Descriptive results for all completed datasets containing that quantity, including numerical warnings.\nsummaries[i].without_warnings Excludes sampling warnings, unavailable quantity MCSE and fixed-value/truth disagreement; this is not passage of a precision threshold.\ndispositions Quantity-specific missingness, constants and warnings; datasets evaluating only other quantities remain in the denominator.\nn_datasets, n_unresolved, n_planned Used, unresolved and planned counts. Constants receive no recovery summary or coverage rate.\nall_planned_coverage_bounds With c covered, n used and N planned, [c/N, (c+N-n)/N]. This assigns every unresolved outcome 0 or 1; it is not a confidence interval.\n\nResults are conditional on completed/selected datasets. Favorable warning-free summaries do not establish performance over the entire planned design. Continuous performance over all planned datasets is unavailable when outcomes remain unresolved. Coverage bounds concern the computed 0/1 interval decisions and do not correct finite-MCMC endpoint error.\n\nOnly with evidence of independent datasets from the same generating condition, set dataset_independence=(status=:declared_independent, evidence=\"Reference to the actual generation plan and seed rule\"). For at least two used datasets, replication SE is the metric's sample SD (denominator n−1) divided by √n. RMSE SE uses SE(MSE)/(2√MSE); at zero MSE this first-order approximation returns missing. Empirical zero variance, including all-zero/all-one coverage, is labeled :empirical_zero_variance; zero reported SE is not evidence of zero uncertainty. These SEs do not sum the fits' MCMC MCSE. For separating questions, generating conditions, failures and simulation uncertainty, see Morris, White & Crowther (2019).\n\nReused sample records, or identical target/evaluated-draw pairs under different IDs, are retained in repeated_evaluation_evidence and suppress replication SE. This detects some duplicates; different hashes/IDs do not establish independence. The function rejects mismatched model/prior/scale/interval probability and changed truths for evaluated quantities. It cannot verify the full generating process, allocation, seed rule or every generating parameter. Check name/scale alignment in the study design. Distribution-generated assurance, paired condition contrasts, SBC and precision-based design acceptance are outside this operation.\n\n<a id=\"比較・診断・保存を一つの手順で確認する\"></a>\n<a id=\"比較・診断・保存を一つの手順で確認する-1\"></a>","title":"Aggregate fixed-truth recovery across datasets"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Connect-comparison,-diagnostics-and-persistence","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"examples/saved_prediction_workflow.jl connects all eight common fit types to comparison, diagnostics, parameter MCSE and public report save/reload. Scalar MFRM, scalar GMFRM and raw MGMFRM use the same example as the five recorded types above. Supply fits saved in the same observation order and their persistent rating-ID vector. For different row orders, use saved_model_comparison with its per-model ID dictionary and the individual report routes. Fixed-coefficient MFRM reports still require canonical records.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\ninclude(\"examples/saved_prediction_workflow.jl\")\n\nanalysis = saved_prediction_workflow(\n    \"Independent\" => \"normalized.jls\",\n    \"Correlated\" => \"correlated.jls\";\n    directory=\"comparison-reports\", # A new output directory.\n    dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\",\n    observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    comparison_axis=:model,\n)\nanalysis.comparison.rows\nanalysis.models[1].diagnostics\nanalysis.models[1].parameter_mcse\nanalysis.models[1].report[\"waic\"] # The report reloaded after saving.\n\nOutput directories are model-1, model-2, etc.; labels remain in the result. All comparisons, diagnostics and reports are computed before saving, so unsupported inputs do not leave partial reports. Existing destinations are not overwritten. This does not provide an all-model rollback after an external file-write failure.\n\nThe example accepts :waic or :raw_loo and leaves difference SE uncomputed. Each report requests both criteria regardless of the comparison criterion; require_complete=true stops before saving if either cannot be computed. Labels such as Independent/Correlated do not isolate a correlation effect when priors or other assumptions also differ. Inspect each model contract and retain warnings and unresolved decisions. This example performs neither fitting nor figure rendering.","title":"Connect comparison, diagnostics and persistence"},{"category":"section","location":"common_prediction_comparison/#Include-explicit-category,-reliability-and-agreement-choices","page":"Saved Prediction and Recovery","text":"Add reporting_by_model to the same call to carry the selected reporting sections through save/reload. Supply a dictionary containing every model label; use nothing for a model that needs no additional sections. Each nonempty choice uses the category, agreement and/or reliability options from integrated reporting. The example supplies the common dataset and rating IDs to these sections, so they cannot accidentally report a different row identity from the comparison. Do not repeat those IDs in the per-model options.\n\ncatalogue = BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(load_fit_cache(\"normalized.jls\"))\nfirst_dimension_persons = [q.parameter for q in catalogue.rows\n    if q.block === :person && q.dimension === 1 && !q.fixed]\ncohort = (id=\"Fitted person cohort, dimension 1\",\n    parameters=first_dimension_persons, target=:centered)\n\n# response_ids identify actual responses in the same row order as rating_ids.\n# Keep distinct repeated performances distinct; do not infer them from person alone.\nagreement = (response_ids=response_ids, interval=.95, se_unit=:person)\nchoices = Dict(\n    \"MFRM\" => (category=(interval=.99,), agreement=agreement),\n    \"MGMFRM D2\" => (category=(interval=.99,), agreement=agreement,\n        reliability=[cohort]),\n)\nanalysis = saved_prediction_workflow(\n    \"MFRM\" => \"mfrm.jls\", \"MGMFRM D2\" => \"normalized.jls\";\n    directory=\"comparison-with-reporting\", dataset_id=\"rating-study-v1\",\n    observation_ids=rating_ids, comparison_axis=:dimensions,\n    reporting_by_model=choices,\n)\nanalysis.models[2].report[\"posterior_reliability\"][\"rows\"]\nanalysis.models[2].report[\"rater_agreement\"][\"warning_rows\"]\n\nThis explicitly selects the fitted-person cohort on the first dimension and removes its draw-specific mean. It does not choose a population/design reliability target or combine dimensions. The 99% category intervals are pointwise ETIs; the 95% agreement intervals describe conditional expected agreement. Neither option changes the intervals in unrelated report sections or supplies calibrated coverage. Retain sampling warnings and unavailable MCSE.\n\nFor the original three fit types, this focused example omits the extra legacy category-functioning, rater-homogeneity and calibration sections. Request the common sections explicitly as above, or use fit_report separately for other native diagnostics. Native scalar/generalized WAIC/LOO tables retain their row indices; persistent IDs are in the common comparison and requested integrated sections. The underlying report schemas are not rewritten to look identical.\n\nUnknown/duplicate/missing model keys, misplaced identity options or failed requested sections stop before any report is written. Completed reports retain warnings: require_complete=true checks execution, not inferential adequacy. The function remains an example composed of existing public operations, with no automatic refitting, retries, model winner or new scientific acceptance.","title":"Include explicit category, reliability and agreement choices"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#How-Rating-Models-Work","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nA rating model assigns a probability to each possible score. Its parameters describe how those probabilities change with the person, item and rater. This page builds the equations from a three-category example, then explains what generalization, multiple dimensions and Bayesian estimation add. The notation follows the implemented families; the detailed Model Equations page records their source conventions.","title":"How Rating Models Work"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#From-adjacent-categories-to-probabilities","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"Write Y_pir for the rating of person p on item i by rater r. Let its categories be 0ldotsK-1. For the partial-credit MFRM, define\n\nx_pir=theta_p-b_i-s_r qquad\nlogfracP(Y_pir=k)P(Y_pir=k-1)=x_pir-d_ik\nquad k=1ldotsK-1\n\nSymbol Meaning Effect when other parameters are held fixed\ntheta_p Person ability Larger values favor higher scores.\nb_i Item difficulty Larger values favor lower scores.\ns_r Rater severity Larger values favor lower scores.\nd_ik Step offset for the transition from category k-1 to k A larger offset makes that transition harder.\n\nThe ratio compares two adjacent exact categories. It is not the odds of scoring at least k. At x_pir=d_ik, categories k-1 and k have equal probabilities, but either might be less probable than another category.\n\nRecover all category probabilities by accumulating the adjacent log odds:\n\nell_0=0 qquad\nell_k=sum_m=1^k(x_pir-d_im) qquad\nP(Y_pir=k)=fracexp(ell_k)sum_h=0^K-1exp(ell_h)\n\nSetting the first log weight to zero chooses a reference; it does not make the probability of category zero vanish. Dividing by the sum makes the probabilities add to one. Rating-scale MFRM shares the step offsets across items; partial-credit MFRM allows item-specific steps. Ordered score labels do not require fitted adjacent-category step offsets to be increasing. Their interpretation differs from cumulative cutpoints in an ordered-logistic model.","title":"From adjacent categories to probabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#Work-through-a-three-category-example","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"Consider categories 0, 1 and 2, with illustrative step offsets d_i1=-05 and d_i2=05. Set ability to 0.8 and item difficulty to 0.3. For rater severity 0.5, the net location is x=08-03-05=0. The adjacent log odds are 0.5 and -0.5, so the category log weights are (0050). Exponentiating and normalizing gives approximately (027410451902741).\n\nRater severity Net location x P(Y=0) P(Y=1) P(Y=2)\n1.5 -1 0.5741 0.3482 0.0777\n0.5 0 0.2741 0.4519 0.2741\n-0.5 1 0.0777 0.3482 0.5741\n\nA more severe rater lowers the score distribution while the person and item stay the same. These are conditional probabilities at chosen parameter values, not estimates from data or a claim that this particular prior or design is suitable.\n\nThis small Julia calculation reproduces the table without a package or MCMC:\n\nusing Printf\n\nfunction category_probabilities(x, steps)\n    logweights = vcat(0.0, cumsum(x .- steps))\n    weights = exp.(logweights .- maximum(logweights))\n    return weights ./ sum(weights)\nend\n\nsteps = [-0.5, 0.5]\nfor severity in (1.5, 0.5, -0.5)\n    x = 0.8 - 0.3 - severity\n    probability = category_probabilities(x, steps)\n    @assert isapprox(sum(probability), 1.0)\n    @assert all(isapprox.(log.(probability[2:end] ./ probability[1:end-1]), x .- steps))\n    @printf(\"severity=%4.1f  x=%4.1f  probabilities=(%.4f, %.4f, %.4f)\\n\",\n        severity, x, probability...)\nend\n\nSubtracting the maximum log weight before exponentiating preserves the probabilities and avoids unnecessarily large exponentials.","title":"Work through a three-category example"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#What-GMFRM-and-MGMFRM-change","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"The implemented scalar GMFRM uses the adjacent log odds\n\nlogfracP(Y_pir=k)P(Y_pir=k-1)\n=a_i c_r(theta_p-b_i-s_r-d_rk)\nqquad a_i0 c_r0\n\nHere a_i is item discrimination and c_r is rater consistency. Increasing their product multiplies all category log weights, concentrating probability on the most favored category or tied categories. It does not always move probability toward the highest score. Severity shifts where the distribution lies; consistency changes its concentration. Consistency is not a reliability coefficient, agreement percentage, or accuracy against an external standard. The steps d_rk belong to the rater in this scalar model.\n\nThe implemented MGMFRM instead uses\n\nlogfracP(Y_pir=k)P(Y_pir=k-1)\n=17c_rleft(sum_d=1^Da_idtheta_pd-b_i-s_r-d_ikright)\n\nThere are D named abilities for each person. A zero in Q fixes the corresponding a_id to zero; an active entry permits a positive loading. The steps now belong to the item. The factor 1.7 is part of this implementation's source equation; scalar GMFRM above and fixed-coefficient MFRM use unit scaling. Item loadings in MGMFRM multiply abilities inside the sum, whereas scalar GMFRM discrimination multiplies the whole bracket. Thus raw parameter values cannot be compared across these families as though their units and priors were identical.\n\nFor fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM, the adjacent log odds are sum_d Q_idtheta_pd-b_i-s_r-d_ik: active coefficients are one and there is no estimated consistency multiplier. This provides a distinct multidimensional alternative without estimating active loadings.\n\nIn MGMFRM, multiple active abilities enter an additive weighted sum. If an item uses reasoning and communication, a higher value in one can offset a lower value in the other. An interpretation requiring a person to exceed a separate minimum in both abilities would require a different response mechanism. The source discussion explains why terminology in an article must be read alongside its actual equation.","title":"What GMFRM and MGMFRM change"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#Compensatory-and-non-compensatory-response-rules","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"These words describe how abilities combine in the response equation. Imagine an item assessing reasoning and communication. In a compensatory model, stronger reasoning can offset weaker communication on that item, to an extent determined by its loadings. In a non-compensatory model, a weakness in a required ability limits performance even when another ability is strong. This need not be a literal pass/fail cutoff; the exact restriction depends on the response rule.\n\nFor the implemented MGMFRM, the relevant sum is u_i=a_i1theta_1+a_i2theta_2. With loadings (21), profiles (02) and (10) both give u_i=2. Holding this item's remaining parameters and the rater fixed gives exactly the same category probabilities. This is a conditional tradeoff on one item. Other items with different loadings can distinguish these people; their full posteriors need not be the same.\n\nFor contrast, a binary product rule could be P(Y=1)=sigma(theta_1)sigma(theta_2), where sigma(z)=1(1+e^-z). Holding the second ability fixed bounds success probability by sigma(theta_2), however large the first ability becomes. This illustrates a conjunctive restriction, but is not an ordinal model fitted by this package. No product, minimum or other operationally non-compensatory alternative is currently implemented.\n\nUto (2021) uses the term non-compensatory, while the published equation and Stan code aggregate abilities additively. We preserve the source terminology separately from this algebraic interpretation. Do not infer a conjunctive response mechanism from that label. The detailed source comparison links the article, original Stan code and contrasting response equations.","title":"Compensatory and non-compensatory response rules"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#Within-item-and-between-item-multidimensionality","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"These terms describe which abilities enter each item, rather than how those abilities combine. Here, within and between refer to items, not within-person versus between-person effects in a multilevel model.\n\nItem structure Example Q rows for reasoning and communication Reading\nBetween-item I1: (1,0); I2: (0,1) Each item measures one ability; different items address different abilities.\nWithin-item I3: (1,1) This item measures both abilities.\nMixed in the package contract I1: (1,0); I2: (0,1); I3: (1,1) Pure and cross-loaded items coexist.\n\nThese rows illustrate the structure, not a sufficient calibration design. The ACER ConQuest manual uses within-item at test level when any item measures several dimensions. Our contract additionally distinguishes a mixed collection of pure and cross-loaded items. A small estimated positive loading is not the structural zero declared by Q.\n\nA within-item model can use either a compensatory or a non-compensatory rule. In this package it uses the additive rule above. A between-item structure has no cross-ability tradeoff inside a single item, because that item uses only one ability; this does not make it a conjunctive model. Correlation between abilities is another separate choice: between-item does not mean uncorrelated, and within-item does not itself imply correlated population abilities.\n\nThe fixed-Q independent MGMFRM branch can represent cross-loaded items subject to its warning-bearing design and identification checks. Its current correlated 2D fitting route is restricted to between-item structure. In the gallery, each saved-fit item loads on only one dimension: its probability surface is flat along the other ability axis. A 3D display alone does not establish within-item measurement.","title":"Within-item and between-item multidimensionality"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#Why-origins,-units-and-priors-matter","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"In the simple MFRM equation, adding the same constant to every ability and item difficulty leaves theta_p-b_i unchanged. The ratings alone cannot choose that common origin. Reference constraints and priors define the coordinates in which estimates are reported. Zero is a reference on that scale, not absence of ability or severity in an absolute sense.\n\nEstimated loadings introduce further scale ambiguity: multiplying abilities by a positive constant and dividing their loadings by that constant preserves the weighted ability contribution. The MGMFRM ability prior fixes a population origin and marginal scale, while other constraints handle rater and step coordinates. That population convention does not force the observed persons' sample mean to zero or variance to one.\n\nA proper prior can give a finite posterior even where the likelihood alone does not distinguish parameters. Finite intervals therefore do not prove that the data identify every separate effect. Compare meaningful contrasts on a declared scale, examine prior sensitivity, and consult Estimands, origins and units for the less obvious consequences of unequal loadings.\n\nPrior SDs must also name their coordinate: an SD for a log loading is not an SD for a positive loading. A supplied ability prior SD is not an estimated population SD or a person's posterior standard error. The original generalized free-coordinate prior and the explicit normalized-prior alternatives can imply different distributions after constraints, including different treatment of raters. See Experimental Models before selecting their scales.","title":"Why origins, units and priors matter"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#What-Bayesian-estimation-computes","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"Let phi collect the unknown abilities, facet parameters and any estimated correlations. Bayesian inference combines a likelihood with a prior:\n\np(phimid y)propto p(ymidphi)p(phi)\n\nThe likelihood describes observed scores conditional on the parameters. The prior describes their distribution before using these scores. The posterior describes their uncertainty after combining those ingredients. MCMC provides dependent numerical draws intended to approximate that posterior; increasing the draw count improves computation only when the sampler is exploring it adequately.\n\nThe baseline likelihood multiplies rating probabilities conditional on the modeled parameters. This assumes conditional independence, not independence of the observed scores before accounting for shared persons, items and raters. Unmodeled variation shared by ratings of the same response can violate that assumption. Merely storing a response identifier does not repair the likelihood.\n\nPosterior prediction averages over parameter uncertainty:\n\nP(Y^mathrmnew=kmid y)\n=int P(Y^mathrmnew=kmidphi)p(phimid y)dphi\n\nIn practice, average the category probabilities over joint posterior draws. Plugging the posterior mean of each parameter into a nonlinear probability formula generally gives a different result. Prediction for an unseen person also needs that person's ability distribution; it is a different target from another rating of a person already represented in the fitted data.","title":"What Bayesian estimation computes"},{"category":"section","location":"rating-model-theory/#How-correlation-and-LKJ-fit-into-the-model","page":"How Rating Models Work","text":"For several abilities, a population prior can use boldsymboltheta_psim N(0SRS). The diagonal matrix S contains marginal SDs, while R contains correlations. Correlation describes population association between abilities, not agreement between raters or causation.\n\nAn LKJ prior has density p(Rmideta)proptodet(R)^eta-1 on valid correlation matrices. At eta=1 it is uniform over those matrices; for eta1 it favors matrices near identity. This does not mean all pairwise correlations are independently uniform. Its implications depend on dimension. Computations often use a lower-triangular Cholesky factor L with R=LL^top. Changing coordinates changes the density: the Cholesky density is generally not constant at eta=1. See the Stan correlation-distribution reference.\n\nThese formulas extend mathematically beyond two dimensions. The package's fitting interface currently admits only its specified two-dimensional correlated models; a mathematical generalization does not establish a fitted or validated higher-dimensional workflow. Identity population correlation likewise does not make every posterior estimate or observed score independent.\n\nContinue to Reading Results and Comparing Models to see which outputs answer which questions.","title":"How correlation and LKJ fit into the model"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nInstall the matching development preview first. Repository-relative commands below assume that your working directory is the source checkout. After Pkg.add, locate a shipped example instead with include(joinpath(pkgdir(BayesianMGMFRM), \"examples\", \"observed_icc.jl\")) after using BayesianMGMFRM; substitute the desired example filename. An example that accepts your own cache/output paths still needs those arguments.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Observed-score-ICC-without-fitting","page":"Examples","text":"The candidate's examples/observed_icc.jl compares four questions on the same three performances and two raters. Rater 2 always gives one point more.\n\ninclude(\"examples/observed_icc.jl\")\nresult = observed_icc_example()\n[(r.definition, r.unit, r.icc) for r in result.results]\n# (:agreement, :single,  2/3)\n# (:agreement, :average, 0.8)\n# (:consistency, :single,  1.0)\n# (:consistency, :average, 1.0)\n\nThe perfect consistency coefficient reflects the identical ordering and spacing once the additive rater offset is removed. It does not mean the raw scores agree. The average coefficient concerns the mean of these two ratings. The example explicitly assumes equal score gaps and treats the raters as a fixed panel. See Choosing an ICC for the design, scale and uncertainty limits. This example performs no model fitting.","title":"Observed-score ICC without fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Observed-score-alpha-without-fitting","page":"Examples","text":"The candidate's examples/observed_alpha.jl contains a complete small example:\n\ninclude(\"examples/observed_alpha.jl\")\nresult = observed_alpha_example()\nresult.ordinal.alpha\nresult.ordinal.observed_disagreement\nresult.ordinal.expected_disagreement\nresult.ordinal.singleton_observation_ids\n\nThe two coefficients use the same ratings but different declared measurement levels. Choose a level from the meaning of the categories, not whichever value is larger. See Observed-score alpha for the definition, exclusions and report integration.\n\nThe repository keeps runnable examples compact. These scripts are learning and verification examples rather than substantive analyses; they exercise the public surfaces described in the Bayesian Fitting page.","title":"Observed-score alpha without fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Inspect-Data-Without-Fitting","page":"Examples","text":"Start with examples/inspect_ratings.jl to learn the input and model workflow without running MCMC or creating files. From a repository checkout with its dependencies installed:\n\njulia --project=. examples/inspect_ratings.jl\n\nThe script constructs eight artificial ratings from two persons, two items and two raters on categories 0–2. It prints those counts, the complete category order, validation errors or warnings, and the equation for a one-dimensional partial-credit MFRM. A passing design check permits the next specification step; it does not make this tiny dataset adequate for inference.\n\nIn an interactive session, ?FacetData shows a complete input example, ?validate_design explains how to read its report, and ?mfrm_spec explains the model choice. ?fit gives a short fitting introduction; ??fit includes the full controls. The Julia help walkthrough explains the prompts and how these examples differ from R's example().","title":"Inspect Data Without Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Minimal-MFRM-Workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/minimal.jl runs a stable partial-credit MFRM analysis. From the repository root, after installing the project's dependencies:\n\njulia --project=. examples/minimal.jl\n\nThe script validates the ratings, constructs a specification and calls fit(spec; backend = :advancedhmc) for Julia NUTS. It prints the diagnostic flag, maximum R-hat, minimum bulk/tail ESS and posterior medians with 95% central credible intervals. It then saves the fit, reloads it and checks that posterior summaries are identical. Design inspection and manual cache keys are not required.\n\nThe two chains each use only 50 warmup and 50 retained draws. This is a short workflow demonstration, not a substantive analysis. Inspect any reported warnings with diagnostics(fit_result) when working interactively. A completed script or successful save/reload does not establish convergence or model validity.\n\nFor figures, install CairoMakie in the active Julia environment (for example, using Pkg; Pkg.add(\"CairoMakie\")), then run:\n\njulia --project=. examples/minimal.jl --plots\n\nThis mode loads CairoMakie before fitting and generates all figures from the reloaded fit. The default command does not load a plotting backend. Every run prints a new output directory under results/minimal/, retained after Julia exits. It contains:\n\nFile Contents\nfit.jls The fit, retained draws, model/data information and diagnostics\nrater-posterior.pdf Rater medians and credible intervals, including the fixed reference\nrater-chains.pdf Retained traces and rank histograms with diagnostic notes\ncategory-predictive.pdf Observed versus replicated category proportions, using seed 42\nwright-map.pdf, wright-map.svg Stable MFRM facet measures and category boundaries on a shared scale\n\nWith the default Julia backend, only fit.jls is written without --plots. In a later session, use the printed cache path to regenerate and edit a figure without running the script or MCMC again:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"results/minimal/<printed-directory>/fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; block = :rater)\ncontent(figure[1, 1]).xlabel = \"Rater severity (logits)\"\nsave(\"rater-posterior-edited.svg\", figure)\n\nReplace <printed-directory> with the actual directory name. The plotting guide explains selection and intervals; the reports guide covers the separate report, table and bundle APIs. To export selected figures with the same report, use the figure bundle workflow.\n\nWith CmdStan configured, add --cmdstan (it can be combined with --plots):\n\njulia --project=. examples/minimal.jl --cmdstan --plots\n\nThe same specification and workflow use backend = :cmdstan. Each CmdStan run gets a new cmdstan-build/ directory alongside its saved fit; compiled-model reuse is not attempted. See backend setup for runtime discovery and build requirements. Shared seeds do not imply identical draws across backends.","title":"Minimal MFRM Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Synthetic-paired-criterion-ratings","page":"Examples","text":"examples/synthetic_paired_ratings.jl generates one pronunciation example with artificial persons, words, raters and responses. Run it from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. examples/synthetic_paired_ratings.jl\n\nThe example has 12 persons, 37 words, 19 raters and two criteria: ease of understanding and weak accent. Both scores range from 0 to 8, with higher scores indicating more of the named property. The design includes repeated ratings of the same recording, unequal word coverage, two sparsely covered words and a small amount of missingness in the first criterion. No empirical file is read. These counts and generating constants illustrate a design, rather than a recommended sample size or estimates from real speakers.\n\nThe printed output directory contains ratings.json (observed long-form ratings), recordings.json (all possible person–word cells and their assignment), truth.json (generating effects, correlations, complete assigned ratings and category probabilities), and manifest.json (seeds, equation, counts and file checksums). Missing first-criterion scores leave the second criterion intact; absent recordings are not assigned score zero. An optional argument selects a new output directory; existing directories are not overwritten.\n\nPerson correlation is 0.6 and residual recording correlation is 0.25. Recording effects are shared across raters, rater severity varies by criterion, and steps are shared across words within each criterion. These are generating conditions, not fitting priors. The realized correlation among 12 persons will differ from the population correlation. The script draws no posterior samples: the current MGMFRM fitting interface does not represent this complete recording/criterion structure. Use the example to inspect known truth and data handling; it does not establish parameter recovery or conclusions about real speakers.","title":"Synthetic paired-criterion ratings"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Fixed-coefficient-multidimensional-MFRM","page":"Examples","text":"examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl fits two named dimensions through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit and saves the fit and a report. Use either backend, optionally adding figures with CairoMakie:\n\njulia --project=. examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl\njulia --project=. examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl --cmdstan --plots\njulia --project=. examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl --correlated --plots\njulia --project=. examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl --correlated --prior-only --plots\njulia --project=. examples/multidimensional_mfrm.jl --exchangeable --correlated --plots\n\nIts fixed Q assigns items 1–2 to reasoning and items 3–4 to communication. Active coefficients and rater consistency are one; person/item locations use zero-centered priors, rater severities sum to zero, and latent correlation is identity. MFRMPrior sets standard deviations on free unit-logit coordinates. The experimental guide explains the model and limits. Add --correlated to estimate population rho with an LKJ(2) prior on either backend; its figures show rho and its chains. Ability-pair marginal standard deviations remain fixed prior inputs. Add --exchangeable to use an exchangeable zero-sum rater prior with kernel SD 0.4 for either model/backend. This selects Experimental.ExchangeablePrior for both the prior check and the fit; the default remains MFRMPrior.\n\nBefore fitting, the script prints prior parameter and rating summaries. Add --prior-only to stop there. --plots also saves ability-prior and rating-prior figures, plus a correlation-prior figure when --correlated is selected.\n\nEach fitting run prints a new directory under results/multidimensional_mfrm/ containing fit.jls and report/. The script reloads the fit, checks its metadata and summaries, and reopens the report bundle, including prior parameter and rating summaries regenerated from the saved model. --plots adds prior figures and reasoning posterior and chain figures plus category predictive figures in PDF/SVG and their JSON inputs. Posterior figures use the reloaded fit; users need not reshape MCMC draws. The posterior intervals are central 90% intervals in unit logits. Fixed coefficients and derived coordinates are labelled; whole-fit diagnostic warnings remain visible. Report-only mode and subsequent bundle verification need no renderer.\n\nThe 50 warmup and 50 retained draws per chain are solely a workflow demonstration. A completed report does not establish convergence or scientific validity. To select another dimension later, load fit.jls and follow the saved-fit report example. Automatic request caching is unavailable for this model; manual save/reload is supported.","title":"Fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Guarded-Scalar-GMFRM-Workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/guarded_gmfrm.jl fits the experimental scalar GMFRM through BayesianMGMFRM.Experimental.fit(spec):\n\njulia --project=. examples/guarded_gmfrm.jl\n\nIts discrimination = :rater configuration estimates positive item discrimination and rater consistency, with rater-specific partial-credit steps. The script validates the ratings, prints model-scale posterior summaries and MCMC diagnostics, then saves and reloads the fit. It does not fit additional task effects, multidimensional ability, rating-scale generalized kernels or DFF terms. See the experimental model guide for the response equation, identification and raw-coordinate prior.","title":"Guarded Scalar GMFRM Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Guarded-Fixed-Q-MGMFRM-Workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/guarded_mgmfrm.jl uses the same experimental entrypoint for two named ability dimensions:\n\njulia --project=. examples/guarded_mgmfrm.jl\n\nQ rows follow data.item_levels; its columns follow the declared dimension labels. The script prints both orders and the matrix before fitting:\n\nItem reasoning communication\nI1 1 0\nI2 0 1\n\nThis is a confirmatory between-item example. Active positive item-by-dimension loadings and rater consistency are estimated even though the compatibility selector is discrimination = :none. Latent correlation is fixed to identity; item-specific partial-credit steps are used. The two-item toy dataset illustrates the API and is insufficient for substantive multidimensional conclusions. Exploratory loadings, free latent correlations, validated bifactor support and model-weight claims are outside this fitting route. The experimental model guide explains these restrictions.","title":"Guarded Fixed-Q MGMFRM Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Explicit-normalized-prior-MGMFRM-workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/normalized_mgmfrm.jl is a self-contained two-dimensional, fixed-Q example with estimated positive loadings and identity latent correlation:\n\njulia --project=. examples/normalized_mgmfrm.jl\n# With CairoMakie installed in the analysis environment:\njulia --project=. examples/normalized_mgmfrm.jl --figures\n\nIt specifies all six normalized exchangeable-prior scales, inspects prior rating implications, and fits with the explicit location coordinates. Two chains with 10 warmup and 12 retained draws demonstrate the interface; their warnings remain visible and these settings are insufficient for inference. The script prints model-scale summaries and Monte Carlo errors, saves to a new directory under results/normalized_mgmfrm/, and checks metadata, summaries and diagnostics after reloading fit.jls.\n\nThe optional person and loading-diagnostic figures use the reloaded fit and the named reasoning dimension. The report reader verifies the exported bundle; report completeness does not mean MCMC diagnostics passed. This workflow uses AdvancedHMC. See the explicit prior contract for the separate raw-coordinate CmdStan route and the meaning of the illustrative scales.","title":"Explicit normalized-prior MGMFRM workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Correlated-MGMFRM-Workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/correlated_mgmfrm.jl constructs a two-dimensional pure-Q panel, explicitly chooses GeneralizedPrior, simulates prior rating implications, fits the population correlation with estimated loadings/consistency, and prints diagnostics, rho precision and a conditional posterior-predictive comparison. It verifies the same MCSE, category probabilities and seeded score replications after save/reload, then saves and reopens a reader-facing report with tables.\n\njulia --project=. examples/correlated_mgmfrm.jl\n\nAdd --cmdstan to use the matching backend. The example uses only 10 warmup and 12 retained iterations in each of two chains; the resulting warnings must not be interpreted as inferential acceptance. Each run prints a new directory under results/correlated_mgmfrm/ containing fit.jls and a report/ bundle. Add --figures in an environment containing CairoMakie to include loading, rho-diagnostic, prior and predictive PDF/SVG figures with their numerical inputs. The result is Experimental.CorrelatedMGMFRMFit. Prediction concerns the existing rating design; agreement does not establish performance for new persons, items or raters. See the model and prior explanation.","title":"Correlated MGMFRM Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Saved-Generalized-Fits-and-Figures","page":"Examples","text":"This section applies to scalar GMFRM and independent fixed-Q MGMFRMFit results.\n\nBoth guarded scripts default to Julia AdvancedHMC/NUTS with two chains, each using 50 warmup and 50 retained draws. These are short workflow demonstrations; inspect the printed R-hat, ESS and diagnostic warnings before interpretation. They use the default generalized prior on raw computational coordinates. BayesianMGMFRM.direct_posterior_summary(fit_result) describes transformed model parameters; posterior_summary(fit_result) retains its raw-coordinate meaning.\n\nThe scripts check that model-scale summaries survive save/reload. Each run prints a retained directory under results/guarded_gmfrm/ or results/guarded_mgmfrm/. Without plotting, the default Julia run writes only fit.jls. After installing CairoMakie as described above, add --plots:\n\njulia --project=. examples/guarded_gmfrm.jl --plots\njulia --project=. examples/guarded_mgmfrm.jl --plots\n\nFigures are generated from the reloaded fit, without reshaping draw matrices:\n\nScript Model-scale intervals Raw-coordinate trace/rank diagnostics Predictive check\nGMFRM rater-consistency.pdf, .svg rater-chains.pdf category-predictive.pdf\nMGMFRM ability-posterior.pdf, .svg, with separate named dimensions reasoning-chains.pdf, selecting the reasoning dimension category-predictive.pdf\n\nThe predictive figures compare observed and replicated category proportions for the same fitted rating rows, using seed 42. Interval bars and predictive bars describe different uncertainties; none of these figures establishes convergence. Wright maps are available only for stable MFRM.\n\nTo change the dimension shown in a later session, replace the placeholder with the printed MGMFRM output directory and load the fit in the same Julia analysis environment:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM, CairoMakie\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"results/guarded_mgmfrm/<printed-directory>/fit.jls\")\nfigure = BayesianMGMFRM.plot_posterior(restored; scale = :model,\n    block = :person, dimension = \"communication\")\ncontent(figure[1, 1]).xlabel = \"Communication ability\"\nsave(\"communication-posterior.pdf\", figure)\nsave(\"communication-posterior.svg\", figure)\n\nThis does not rerun MCMC. For a saved GMFRM fit, select block = :rater_consistency and omit dimension. See the plotting guide for other selections and the reports guide for tables and bundles.\n\nBoth scripts also accept --cmdstan, with or without --plots, using the same specification and a fresh cmdstan-build/ directory alongside the fit. For example:\n\njulia --project=. examples/guarded_mgmfrm.jl --cmdstan --plots\n\nConfigure the runtime using the backend setup guide. A shared seed does not imply identical draws across backends. Switching backends preserves the experimental model restrictions.","title":"Saved Generalized Fits and Figures"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Review-a-saved-MGMFRM-fit-without-fitting","page":"Examples","text":"review_saved_mgmfrm.jl starts from an existing independent fixed-Q MGMFRM cache. Use the same Julia analysis environment that reads the cache, with CairoMakie and JSON3 available. JSON3 writes the diagnostic supplement. Find the stored dimension labels with fit_metadata(load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\"); view = :public).dimension_labels, then pass one of those labels:\n\nRunning the script without arguments or with --help prints usage without loading a cache or requiring the plotting packages.\n\njulia --project=. examples/review_saved_mgmfrm.jl \\\n  analysis-fit.jls results/saved_mgmfrm_review communication\n\nReplace communication with the actual label and choose a new output directory for each review. Missing or incompatible caches produce an error; this example does not call fit, cached_fit, or refresh a cache. The correlated MGMFRM result type has its own saved-result workflow.\n\nRead the output in this order:\n\nREADME.md shows the whole-fit and finite-panel location diagnostic status. Inspect both, including the separate warmup history in review.json. Good location-difference diagnostics do not clear whole-fit warnings.\nreport/fit_report.md contains model-scale 90% credible intervals and a posterior figure for all fitted persons in the selected dimension. The diagnostic figure shows the active loadings in that dimension, with all retained chains and the whole-fit warning. PDF, SVG and numerical inputs are saved under report/figures/.\nreview.json retains all model-scale posterior summaries and their mean, SD, median and interval-endpoint Monte Carlo errors. Match rows by parameter; credible intervals describe posterior uncertainty, while MCSE describes error in its numerical calculation. Unavailable MCSE remains unavailable, and this example applies no precision acceptance threshold.\nThe predictive figure compares category proportions on the existing rating rows with 200 posterior replications, using seed 42 and 90% predictive intervals. This does not assess held-out ratings or new persons, items or raters. Calibration, WAIC and LOO are not requested in this walkthrough.\n\nParameter summaries and MCSE use every retained draw. Prediction alone resamples 200 joint draws. The supplement is necessary because the ordinary independent-MGMFRM report does not automatically include location diagnostics or MCSE. It also records the input cache SHA256 and the analysis settings. The example verifies that the cache bytes are unchanged and reopens the report bundle to verify its manifest-listed files. README.md and review.json sit outside that bundle's verification scope.\n\nTo regenerate the same numerical figure inputs, rerun with the same cache, dimension, script and Julia environment, writing to a new directory. Report completeness means that the requested sections were produced; diagnostic warnings remain visible, and a complete report is not scientific acceptance.","title":"Review a saved MGMFRM fit without fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Compare-abilities-or-raters-in-a-saved-MGMFRM-fit","page":"Examples","text":"Use a joint posterior contrast to ask whether two fitted persons differ within one dimension, or whether two raters differ in severity or consistency. Subtract the two values at each retained draw: subtracting marginal interval endpoints or independently shuffling the columns loses their posterior dependence. For example, the variance of a difference includes minus twice the covariance, even when the population ability prior has identity correlation.\n\nsaved_mgmfrm_contrasts.jl provides an includable example for independent fixed-Q MGMFRM caches from either backend. It uses Statistics and the package's existing MCMCDiagnosticTools dependency; no renderer is needed. From the repository root:\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_mgmfrm_contrasts.jl\")\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"analysis-fit.jls\")\nability = saved_mgmfrm_contrast(restored; kind = :ability,\n    left = \"P1\", right = \"P2\", dimension = \"communication\")\nseverity = saved_mgmfrm_contrast(restored; kind = :severity,\n    left = \"R1\", right = \"R2\")\nconsistency = saved_mgmfrm_contrast(restored; kind = :log_consistency_ratio,\n    left = \"R1\", right = \"R2\", rope = (-log(1.25), log(1.25)))\nability.summary\nconsistency.summary.event_rows\n\nReplace the person/rater labels and dimension with those in your saved data. The example rejects unknown levels, a missing ability dimension, a dimension selector on rater parameters, self-comparisons, and altered chain ordering. It keeps all retained draws and the original fit's diagnostic assessment. These helpers are example code, not additional package fitting APIs.\n\nPositive contrasts mean that the left person has higher ability, the left rater is more severe, or the left rater has larger consistency. The consistency contrast is log(left) - log(right): zero means equal multipliers. Its ratio_interval exponentiates the log-scale median and interval endpoints; this is not a posterior mean ratio, and its MCSE remains on the log scale. Unrepresentable ratio endpoints are marked unavailable.\n\nAbility-difference magnitudes use the saved model's ability unit. A common origin shift cancels, but positive rescaling multiplies the difference and any nonzero ROPE bounds; the sign is invariant. Item-location differences require additional care when loadings differ: even their sign can depend on the ability origin. See Estimands, origins and units before interpreting contrasts across models or prior settings. Invariance of a contrast does not make its posterior independent of the chosen prior.\n\nsummary contains a central 90% credible interval by default, mean/SD and quantile MCSE, and rank-normalized split R-hat plus bulk/tail ESS for the contrast itself. Inspect those diagnostics as well as whole_fit_diagnostics; one contrast cannot clear warnings elsewhere. Short or constant sequences keep unavailable diagnostics and precision. No new acceptance threshold is applied.\n\nAn optional two-bound rope specifies a practically negligible range on the contrast's scale. The illustrative consistency bounds above correspond to ratios from 0.8 to 1.25; they are not a recommended tolerance. Justify bounds before inspecting the posterior, or omit them. Event rows report the posterior fraction above zero and, if requested, below/inside/above the ROPE, with MCSE computed from the ordered indicator draws. All-zero or all-one indicators retain unavailable MCSE: an observed fraction of zero or one is not proof of exact posterior certainty. No equivalence decision, multiplicity adjustment, or rater reliability classification is made. A contrast still depends on the fitted model, prior and rating design; it does not establish agreement on future scores.","title":"Compare abilities or raters in a saved MGMFRM fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Common-saved-fit-prediction-preview","page":"Examples","text":"The isolated common-API candidate also provides a saved comparison, diagnostics and reporting workflow. That guide distinguishes the candidate package APIs from the standalone preview below, lists supported result types and explains their warning/uncertainty limits.\n\nThe repository example examples/common_prediction_observations.jl reads a fit cache through the opt-in prototype common adapter. It works with eight result types: MFRMFit, GMFRMFit, MGMFRMFit, NormalizedMGMFRMFit, CorrelatedMGMFRMFit, MultidimensionalMFRMFit, CorrelatedMFRMFit and ExchangeableMFRMFit (experimental types use the Experimental namespace). This adapter is not yet included or exported by the package; use a repository checkout. Its availability does not establish scientific acceptance of those models.\n\njulia --project=. examples/common_prediction_observations.jl --help\njulia --project=. examples/common_prediction_observations.jl \\\n  results/my-analysis/fit.jls study-A/ratings-v1 results/my-analysis/observation-ids.txt\n\nReplace the paths and dataset version with your saved analysis. The cache must come from save_fit_cache, rather than a private samples.jls record. The ID file contains one unique, nonblank observation ID per line, in the original fit row order. Preserve IDs at data ingestion and carry them through filtering or reordering with the ratings; repeated person/item/rater combinations need distinct observation IDs. The adapter checks uniqueness, counts and bindings, but cannot discover whether a user supplied the wrong real-world ID-to-row map. Keep the ID file and dataset version alongside the fit cache.\n\nThe example writes no files and runs no sampler. It prints category order, array dimensions, model scale, WAIC/raw IS-LOO scores, sampling warnings and problem observation IDs. One retained draw illustrates the probability-array axes; it is not a posterior mean or a precision assessment. Criteria use all original draws and chains, without creating the full category probability cube. Warnings remain visible even when a numerical score is returned.\n\nFor use in a Julia session, run from the repository root:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\ninclude(\"scripts/prediction_observation_adapter.jl\")\nusing .PredictionObservationAdapter\n\nrestored = load_fit_cache(\"results/my-analysis/fit.jls\")\nids = readlines(\"results/my-analysis/observation-ids.txt\")\nevaluation = prediction_criteria(restored;\n    dataset_id=\"study-A/ratings-v1\", observation_ids=ids,\n    criteria=(:waic, :raw_loo))\nevaluation.sampling_warning\nevaluation.criterion_warnings\nevaluation.problem_observation_ids\nevaluation.uncertainty\n\n# Inspect the current prototype's arguments and output contract.\n@doc prediction_observations\n@doc prediction_criteria\n\nThe extraction target is the original training rows and existing facet levels; criterion aggregation gives equal weight to rating rows. This differs from the foundation study's heldout, globally person/dimension-weighted scoring. The raw IS-LOO target omits one rating with fixed specification/level maps. Neither operation predicts a new person/rater or performs grouped K-fold refits. Returned pointwise score SE is not MCMC MCSE, a cluster-adjusted SE or an independent-study replication SE. :hill_smoothed_loo remains a separate experimental option; reference PSIS equivalence is unverified. This example performs no model ranking.\n\nThe normalized-prior walkthrough above provides the earlier fit → diagnostics → save/reload → report/figure sequence. Its short run and prior values demonstrate syntax; they do not prescribe adequate draws or an accepted scientific prior.","title":"Common saved-fit prediction preview"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reliability-from-a-Saved-Fit","page":"Examples","text":"The candidate's examples/saved_posterior_reliability.jl reads an existing cache and calls BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability. It performs no fitting. Select estimated location names with BayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities, then declare whether absolute locations or deviations from the selected cohort's mean are the target. For multiple dimensions, make a separate call for each dimension.\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_posterior_reliability.jl\")\n# Replace these with exact names from your fit's inventory.\nresult = saved_posterior_reliability(\"my-fit.jls\";\n    parameters=[\"person[1]\", \"person[2]\"], target=:centered,\n)\nresult.reliability\nresult.mcse\nresult.sampling_warning\n\nThis is a finite-cohort posterior summary, with numerical MCSE where available. It is not a reliability CrI, inter-rater agreement, Strata or an assurance about a new rating design. Read the target and derivation before interpreting it. Save the selected names, weights and target with the fit so that another session can reproduce the same result.","title":"Reliability from a Saved Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Common-response-score-agreement-from-a-saved-fit","page":"Examples","text":"Use this when several raters scored the same performance and criterion. Retain the actual response IDs from data collection; a person can produce more than one performance on the same item. The example works with the same eight saved-fit types as the common prediction interface, including multidimensional models.\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_rater_agreement.jl\")\n\nresult = saved_rater_agreement(\"my-fit.jls\";\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    response_ids=performance_ids, adjacent=1, interval=0.99,\n    se_unit=:person)\nresult.overall.exact.observed_rate\nresult.overall.exact.expected_mean\nresult.overall.exact.expected_lower, result.overall.exact.expected_upper\nresult.overall.exact.observed_se\nresult.overall.exact.mcse\nresult.rater_pairs\n\nThe observed percentage, conditional model-expected rate, observed-rate cluster SE and numerical MCSE answer different questions. See the matching rules, denominators and uncertainty explanation. Zero-overlap pairs remain in the table. The function neither diagnoses copying or halo nor infers agreement on new performances. No new fit is performed.","title":"Common-response score agreement from a saved fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Category-use-and-conditional-curves-from-a-saved-fit","page":"Examples","text":"The qualified common report retains shared, item-owned or rater-owned steps as specified by the saved model. It does not change the threshold facet. For a one-dimensional fit, the saved-cache example can be called as follows:\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_category_report.jl\")\n\ncategories = saved_category_report(\"my-fit.jls\";\n    dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n    interval=0.99,\n    profiles=[(;id=\"selected-item-rater\", item=\"I1\", rater=\"R1\",\n        dimension=1, ability_grid=range(-3, 3; length=41))])\ncategories.step_contract\ncategories.usage_rows\ncategories.threshold_rows\ncategories.curve_rows\ncategories.crossing_rows\ncategories.sampling_warning, categories.mcse_warning\n\nUse the exact stored item and rater labels. Multidimensional fits require fixed_abilities=Dict(2 => 0.0, ...) for every dimension other than the plotted one. All retained draws contribute. The intervals are pointwise equal-tailed posterior intervals; the expected category proportion has no new-rating realization noise. Read the ownership, crossings and uncertainty explanation before interpreting these rows. This standalone example performs no fitting. The integrated example below adds explicitly requested sections to fit_report.","title":"Category use and conditional curves from a saved fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Integrated-saved-fit-report","page":"Examples","text":"For a comparison of several models, the combined saved workflow uses all eight common fit types, diagnostics, parameter MCSE and explicit per-model reporting choices in one save/reload path.\n\nexamples/saved_integrated_report.jl loads a trusted cache and writes a public JSON/Markdown/table bundle. Declare persistent rating IDs, actual shared-response IDs and a deliberate reliability cohort before calling it:\n\ninclude(\"examples/saved_integrated_report.jl\")\n\nrestored = saved_integrated_report(\"my-fit.jls\", \"assessment-report\";\n    reporting=(;\n        dataset_id=\"assessment-2026\", observation_ids=rating_ids,\n        category=(;interval=0.99),\n        agreement=(;response_ids=performance_ids, interval=0.99),\n        reliability=[(;id=\"Ability A\", parameters=person_names_A, target=:centered)]),\n    include_posterior_predictive=false, include_artifact=false,\n    include_waic=false, include_loo=false)\nrestored[\"category_report\"][\"usage_rows\"]\nrestored[\"posterior_reliability\"][\"rows\"]\nrestored[\"rater_agreement\"][\"rows\"]\n\nReplace these input vectors with the actual study identities and exact estimated coordinate names; another ability dimension requires its own named cohort. The example requires execution-complete sections when saving, while preserving MCMC and numerical warnings. It does not require that the underlying scientific claims have been accepted. See the request and failure semantics.","title":"Integrated saved-fit report"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Matched-one-and-multidimensional-MGMFRM","page":"Examples","text":"examples/matched_dimension_refit_plans.jl prepares the one-dimensional restriction and a between-item MGMFRM with common rating identities, splits, item-owned steps, loading count and prior settings. It performs no fitting. Follow the worked comparison workflow to fit training folds, retain diagnostics/failures and compare saved held-out predictions. This example does not equate scalar GMFRM with the one-dimensional MGMFRM restriction or validate dimensionality selection.","title":"Matched one- and multidimensional MGMFRM"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Numerical-precision-of-saved-K-fold-comparisons","page":"Examples","text":"examples/saved_kfold_comparison.jl accepts mcse=:delta and separate independent_fold_rng / independent_model_rng declarations. It reads existing fits and reports first-order numerical error where estimable, preserving short-chain and diagnostic warnings. Follow the numerical-error example for formulas, field meanings and the distinction from data-sampling SE.","title":"Numerical precision of saved K-fold comparisons"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Classification-from-a-saved-fit","page":"Examples","text":"Inspect the coordinate catalogue before selecting the people and ability dimension relevant to the study. Keep the cutoff on this fitted scale:\n\nfit = load_fit_cache(\"my-fit.jls\")\nBayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit).rows\n\n# Supply exact selected names and justified study rules, not example defaults.\ndecisions = BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_classification(fit;\n    parameters=person_names_A, cutoff=study_cutoff,\n    pass_probability=study_probability_rule, interval=0.99)\ndecisions.rows                       # Ability SD/99% ETI, probability, decision, risk\ndecisions.cohort                     # Expected error counts and any-error probability\n\nreport = fit_report(fit; view=:public,\n    reporting=(;classification=(;parameters=person_names_A, cutoff=study_cutoff,\n        pass_probability=study_probability_rule, interval=0.99)),\n    include_posterior_predictive=false, include_artifact=false,\n    include_waic=false, include_loo=false, require_complete=true)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"classification-report\", report; require_complete=true)\nrestored = load_fit_report_bundle(\"classification-report\"; require_complete=true)\nrestored[\"posterior_classification\"][\"rows\"]\n\nThese calls reuse all original retained draws. The qualified API supports the eight common fit types. Read the rule, dependence and uncertainty explanation. Ranking, multidimensional composite rules and repeated-study error calibration are separate tasks; a complete saved report does not imply scientific acceptance.","title":"Classification from a saved fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Ranking-uncertainty-from-a-saved-fit","page":"Examples","text":"Use exact estimated person coordinates from one ability dimension. Select the cohort and rank direction deliberately; a top-k query is optional:\n\nfit = load_fit_cache(\"my-fit.jls\")\nBayesianMGMFRM.recovery_quantities(fit).rows\n\nranks = BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_ranking(fit;\n    parameters=person_names_A, direction=:higher, top_k=3, interval=0.99)\nranks.rows[1].rank                       # Mean, SD, median, discrete 99% interval, MCSE\nranks.rows[1].top_k_probability.lower    # Posterior mean and numerical MCSE\nranks.rows[1].top_k_probability.upper\n\nreport = fit_report(fit; view=:public,\n    reporting=(;ranking=(;parameters=person_names_A, direction=:higher,\n        top_k=3, interval=0.99)), include_posterior_predictive=false,\n    include_artifact=false, include_waic=false, include_loo=false,\n    require_complete=true)\nsave_fit_report_bundle(\"rank-report\", report; require_complete=true)\nrestored = load_fit_report_bundle(\"rank-report\"; require_complete=true)\nrestored[\"posterior_ranking\"][\"rows\"]\n\nThe selected cohort must have at least three people for this top_k=3 example; the API itself permits cohorts of two or more. Omit top_k for rank summaries without an inclusion query. Use direction=:lower when smaller values should come first. A top-k boundary tie produces probability bounds, not an arbitrary winner. Read the cohort, tie and uncertainty explanation. Both ranking and classification may be requested in the same report; their person lists and substantive purposes need not be identical.","title":"Ranking uncertainty from a saved fit"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Matched-item-slope-workflow","page":"Examples","text":"examples/matched_slope_workflow.jl provides matched_slope_workflow(spec; prior, output_dir, ndraws, warmup, chains, seed). It requires an explicit one-dimensional MGMFRM specification and normalized exchangeable prior. The function fits fixed-one and estimated-positive item slopes with unit rater consistency, saves separate caches and reopens both. All numerical controls are supplied by the caller. It prints the retained sampling flags and returns the fits and paths; it does not select a model. Use a new output directory. If one fit fails, any earlier saved fit remains.\n\nRead matched fixed and estimated item slopes for the equation, inactive prior inputs, free-coordinate order and common heldout comparison workflow. This is an unreleased candidate example; obtain it from the same candidate checkout as the package.","title":"Matched item-slope workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#BayesianMGMFRM.jl","page":"Home","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThis snapshot includes the saved-result comparison, sensitivity, classification, ranking, ordinal alpha and complete-crossed ICC candidates. Candidate-only examples require the matching development source; updating this website does not install those interfaces or make them part of a released package. Consult Scope and Releases before choosing an analysis.","title":"BayesianMGMFRM.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install-this-development-preview","page":"Home","text":"Use Julia 1.10.8 or newer and a separate analysis project:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"rating-preview\")\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/Ryuya-dot-com/BayesianMGMFRM.jl\",\n    rev=\"development/20261013-guide\")\nPkg.pin(\"BayesianMGMFRM\")\n\nKeep the project's Project.toml and Manifest.toml. For the exact published snapshot, replace rev with the commit shown on the preview landing page. This development branch contains the candidate APIs described here; main and the registered release may differ. Installation is not scientific acceptance or a stable release.\n\nExamples ship with the package and can be located independently of your working directory:\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(BayesianMGMFRM), \"examples\", \"inspect_ratings.jl\"))\n\nThis first example inspects artificial ratings and constructs a specification; it performs no fitting. Continue with the examples.\n\nSee the Visualization Gallery for Wright Maps, Pathway Maps and MGMFRM response surfaces with worked reading guidance.\n\nBayesianMGMFRM.jl provides conservative Bayesian workflows for many-facet Rasch measurement. It combines long-format rating-data validation, identified model construction, Bayesian fitting, diagnostics, predictive checks, portable reports, and optional figures directly from saved or newly fitted models.","title":"Install this development preview"},{"category":"section","location":"#New-to-Many-Facet-Models?","page":"Home","text":"Start with the beginner's guide. It follows a concrete rating example and separates the research question, data design and model choice. No Julia or Bayesian modeling experience is needed to read it.\n\nGetting Started: persons, items, raters, dimensions and the differences between MFRM, GMFRM and MGMFRM.\nHow Rating Models Work: category probabilities, a worked calculation, priors, identification and ability correlation.\nReading Results and Comparing Models: uncertainty, diagnostics, predictive comparison and simulation for study planning.\n\nFor executable Julia workflows, continue to the short runnable examples.","title":"New to Many-Facet Models?"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-Is-Supported","page":"Home","text":"The stable fitting surface covers MFRM with rating-scale or partial-credit steps. The following additional configurations require explicit experimental opt-in:\n\na fixed-coefficient multidimensional MFRM with independent abilities;\na two-dimensional between-item MFRM with estimated population correlation;\neither admitted multidimensional MFRM route with an explicit exchangeable rater prior and a supplied rater kernel SD;\na one-dimensional scalar GMFRM with item discrimination and rater consistency;\na multidimensional fixed-Q confirmatory MGMFRM with independent abilities;\nthat independent MGMFRM with an explicitly normalized exchangeable or source prior, whose scales and target differ from the raw prior;\nan explicit two-dimensional correlated MGMFRM, currently covering fitting, summaries, diagnostics, MCSE, prior/conditional posterior prediction, manual caches and report/figure bundles. This workflow remains experimental.\n\nBroader discrimination structures, exploratory loadings, higher-dimensional correlation estimation, and fitted DFF effects are not supported. See Scope and Releases for the exact boundary and Experimental Models for the limited API. Before choosing MGMFRM, review what is estimated and what is fixed: its fixed Q pattern still has estimated active loadings, and its rater consistency parameter has a different meaning from reliability. The common-operation table connects each route to fitting, comparison, recovery and reporting, while the scientific-evidence table distinguishes implemented operations from supported inferential claims.","title":"What Is Supported"},{"category":"section","location":"#Recommended-Path","page":"Home","text":"The sequence below uses the stable MFRM entry points. For multidimensional MFRM or GMFRM/MGMFRM, follow the model-specific fitting and prior routes.\n\nBuild long-format ratings with FacetData.\nRun validate_design and inspect coverage, connectedness, category use, optional groups, and anchors.\nCreate an mfrm_spec and review its constraints. getdesign provides optional parameter inspection.\nUse prior_predictive_check before fitting.\nCall fit(spec) for the supported stable model.\nReview sampler_diagnostics, mcmc_diagnostics, parameter_block_diagnostics, and diagnostics.\nInspect estimand-specific posterior_mcse, posterior, predictive, calibration, residual, and sensitivity results. The plotting guide shows figures from a fit.\nExport fit_report(fit; view = :public) or fit_report_public for reader-facing structured data, or use fit_report_markdown for Markdown. Check report completeness with fit_report_health or set require_complete = true.","title":"Recommended Path"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Data Validation explains the input contract and design checks.\nModel Equations records the mathematical and source contracts.\nBayesian Workflow presents the analysis sequence and interpretation checks.\nBayesian Fitting covers backends, experimental restrictions, diagnostics, and reports.\nExperimental Models documents the provisional namespace and its stability boundary.\nExamples points to runnable scripts.\nMigrating from FACETS and ACER ConQuest maps the overlapping RSM/PCM models, sign and identification conventions, estimator differences, and supported individual hard anchors.\nScope and Releases states supported and unsupported surfaces.\nAPI lists the public functions by workflow.\n\nPages = [\n    \"learning-guide.md\",\n    \"rating-model-theory.md\",\n    \"interpreting-results.md\",\n    \"data-validation.md\",\n    \"model-equations.md\",\n    \"bayesian-workflow.md\",\n    \"fitting.md\",\n    \"experimental.md\",\n    \"examples.md\",\n    \"migration-facets-conquest.md\",\n    \"scope.md\",\n    \"api.md\",\n    \"api-data-design.md\",\n    \"api-fitting-artifacts.md\",\n    \"api-workflow-diagnostics.md\",\n    \"api-validation-evidence.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Migrating-from-FACETS-and-ACER-ConQuest","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"note: Unreleased documentation preview — 13 October 2026\nThis manual includes unreleased interfaces and examples. It is not the documentation of an installed release. Model support and scientific acceptance remain as stated on each page.\n\nThis page maps the smallest genuinely overlapping many-facet models from FACETS and ACER ConQuest to BayesianMGMFRM.jl. It is a semantic migration guide, not a parser for FACETS specification files or ConQuest command files. The source likelihood, category structure, sign convention, identification, weights, anchors, and estimator must be checked before comparing any numbers.\n\nThe fit-supported destination on this page is the one-dimensional additive MFRM with either shared rating-scale steps or item-specific partial-credit steps. Arbitrary facets, multiple rating-scale groups, observation weights, fitted interactions, soft anchors, threshold anchors, and group-mean anchors are not silently approximated by the current stable API. Exact individual rater/item hard anchors are supported in the minimal MFRM path.","title":"Migrating from FACETS and ACER ConQuest"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Source-features-worth-preserving","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"The migration design follows several useful conventions documented by the two source programs:\n\nFACETS separates the facet selectors, response-scale definition, and model weight in Models=. A # selector gives each matched element its own partial-credit scale, while named scales can be assigned to selected observations. The official FACETS model help also warns that weights are external value judgments rather than parameters learned from the response data.\nFACETS distinguishes starting values, individually fixed element anchors, and group-mean anchors. Group members may vary relative to one another while their mean remains fixed; see the official element and group-anchor help. Rating-scale thresholds can also be fixed for equating across analyses; see anchoring rating-scale structures.\nFACETS supports model-, element-, and observation-level weights, multiplying them and treating the result as response replication. This changes the estimating target and the apparent amount of information; see weighting the data.\nFACETS bias/interaction output is a secondary analysis: main-analysis estimates are fixed and interaction sizes are then estimated from residuals. This is not the same target as jointly estimating an interaction in the main likelihood; see the official bias-estimation description.\nConQuest turns additive terms and their interactions into a design matrix and a score matrix. It can also import an arbitrary design matrix. The construction rules and multifaceted example are described in ConQuest Manual, Chapter 3, Section 3.1.7.\nConQuest's model grammar makes the threshold regime explicit: item + step is a rating-scale model, while item + item*step is a partial-credit model. Terms such as rater*step, criteria*step, and rater*criteria*step define different scale-sharing structures. See ConQuest Manual, Chapter 4, Section 4.7.43.\nConQuest separates initial values from fixed anchors and can export estimates in a form that can be read back as initial values or anchors. See Chapter 4, Section 4.7.33, and the multifaceted example in ConQuest Manual, Chapter 2.\n\nThese are design principles for transparent compilation and interchange. They do not imply that BayesianMGMFRM.jl should reproduce either product's entire model catalog or estimation engine.","title":"Source features worth preserving"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Identify-the-overlapping-model-first","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Only migrate directly when the source model reduces to one of these targets:\n\nSource intent Representative source specification Destination\nOne common category-step structure FACETS Models = ?, ?, ?, R2; ConQuest model rater + item + step; thresholds = :rating_scale\nItem-specific category-step structures FACETS uses # for the item facet; ConQuest model rater + item + item*step; thresholds = :partial_credit\n\nThe FACETS example assumes three ordered facets in the data record, such as person, rater, and item, followed by a response with categories 0:2. FACETS can reverse the direction of a facet or alter the reported scale. ConQuest uses a plus sign for a difficulty-oriented term and a minus sign for an easiness-oriented term. Record the actual source setting rather than inferring orientation from a column name.\n\nThe current destination model uses\n\neta_prik=(k-1)(theta_p-rho_r-beta_i)\n             -sum_m=1^k-1delta_im\n\nwith delta[m] shared across items for :rating_scale. Positive rater values mean greater severity and positive item values mean greater difficulty, so both reduce the probability of a higher score. Transform source estimates to this convention before comparing them.","title":"Identify the overlapping model first"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Executable-minimal-migration","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"First convert the source data to one row per observed rating. Preserve source labels rather than replacing them with source-software parameter numbers.\n\nusing BayesianMGMFRM\nusing Statistics\n\nratings = (\n    person = repeat([\"E1\", \"E2\", \"E3\"], inner = 4),\n    rater = repeat([\"R1\", \"R2\", \"R1\", \"R2\"], outer = 3),\n    item = repeat([\"I1\", \"I1\", \"I2\", \"I2\"], outer = 3),\n    score = repeat([0, 1, 2], inner = 4),\n)\n\ndata = FacetData(ratings;\n    person = :person,\n    rater = :rater,\n    item = :item,\n    score = :score,\n)\n\nvalidation = validate_design(data)\n@assert validation.passed\n\n# Choose :rating_scale for FACETS R / ConQuest + step, or\n# :partial_credit for item-specific FACETS # / ConQuest item*step.\nthreshold_regime = :rating_scale\nspec = mfrm_spec(data;\n    thresholds = threshold_regime,\n    validation_report = validation,\n)\ndesign = getdesign(spec)\n\n# Inspect the constraints and record the model before sampling.\nconstraint_rows = constraint_table(design)\nmanifest = model_manifest(design; view = :public)\n\nfit_result = fit(design;\n    backend = :advancedhmc,\n    ndraws = 500,\n    warmup = 500,\n    chains = 4,\n    seed = 20260721,\n)\n\nposterior_rows = posterior_summary(fit_result)\nrater_rows = rater_diagnostics(fit_result)\nbayesian_fit_rows = fit_stats(fit_result; by = :rater)\n\n# This separately labelled table is a posterior-mean plug-in approximation,\n# not a claim of equality to FACETS JMLE output.\nfacets_labelled_rows = facets_report(fit_result; by = :rater)\n\nposterior_mean = vec(mean(fit_result.draws; dims = 1))\nthreshold_rows = threshold_map_data(design; params = posterior_mean)\n\nFor a substantive analysis, increase sampling effort as needed, predeclare sampler controls, and inspect sampler_diagnostics, mcmc_diagnostics, parameter_block_diagnostics, posterior predictive checks, calibration, and sensitivity results. The numerical settings above demonstrate the migration path; they are not universal production defaults.\n\nRun the rating-scale and partial-credit specifications as separate models. Do not select the regime after inspecting which produces the preferred result. If a FACETS or ConQuest analysis uses multiple named scale groups or a rater*step/rater*item*step structure, it is outside this direct migration example.","title":"Executable minimal migration"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Offline-bridge-for-a-licensed-execution-host","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"The machine that prepares an analysis does not need either external executable. The package compiles a manual-syntax transfer bundle locally, while execution remains a separate operator action on an authorized host. FACETS bundles include a Windows launcher. ConQuest bundles include Windows and macOS launchers. Neither product executable nor its licence is copied into a bundle.\n\nVersion 1 deliberately compiles only the fit-supported, one-dimensional, additive family = :mfrm overlap with unit weights and either one shared RSM step structure or item-specific PCM steps. It rejects generalized discrimination, fitted bias or interaction terms, nonpassing design validation, and nonempty parameter anchors. The ConQuest compiler also rejects repeated person–item–rater cells, any observed rater–item generalized item that does not contain both declared category endpoints, and an unobserved PCM category between an item's minimum and maximum scores. FACETS requires both declared endpoints in the data as a whole for RSM and PCM; PCM additionally requires both endpoints within every item. These category-universe guards prevent an external program from silently fitting a narrower response denominator. Sparse observed designs remain sparse: the bridge does not create missing rating rows, so a sparse design that cannot meet these guards is rejected rather than padded with synthetic responses.\n\nOn the Mac, compile and save one bundle. Retain the returned bundle_id separately so the returned directory can be checked against the exact original manifest contract, including its input inventory and required-output list:\n\nfacets_bundle = facets_bridge_bundle(spec;\n    title = \"Minimal RSM FACETS bridge\",\n)\nfacets_saved = save_external_bridge_bundle(\n    \"facets_minimal_rsm_bridge\",\n    facets_bundle,\n)\nfacets_bundle_id = facets_saved.bundle_id\nfacets_host_preflight = facets_saved.host_preflight\n\nconquest_bundle = conquest_bridge_bundle(spec;\n    title = \"Minimal RSM ConQuest bridge\",\n)\nconquest_saved = save_external_bridge_bundle(\n    \"conquest_minimal_rsm_bridge\",\n    conquest_bundle,\n)\nconquest_bundle_id = conquest_saved.bundle_id\nconquest_host_preflight = conquest_saved.host_preflight\n\nEach directory contains the control and rating files, ASCII-safe transfer identifiers, category and observation maps, a SHA-256 manifest, a PowerShell input verifier, a Windows runner, and a declaration of the required return files. A ConQuest directory additionally contains verify_bundle_macos.sh and run_conquest_macos.sh. Original person, rater, and item labels are omitted from id_map.tsv by default, although unsalted deterministic SHA-256 hashes of their canonical representations are retained. This is pseudonymization, not anonymization: guessable labels can be dictionary matched, and equal canonical labels can be linked across bundles. Setting include_original_labels = true is an explicit disclosure choice. Row-level ratings are present in every bundle and remain sensitive even when labels are omitted.\n\nOn an authorized Windows host, set FACETS_EXE or CONQUEST_EXE to the full path of the corresponding console executable. Also set BRIDGE_BUNDLE_ID to the bundle_id retained separately on the Mac; do not recover it from a possibly modified transfer manifest. Before running either transferred script, use a trusted host-side hash tool to compare the verifier and runner against the SHA-256 values retained from host_preflight through a separate channel. A launcher contained in the same transfer is not its own trust anchor; without this independent comparison it detects accidental corruption but does not protect against hostile replacement. In a fresh bundle copy whose results/ directory is absent or empty, run run_facets_windows.cmd or run_conquest_windows.cmd. The checked launcher verifies the manifest ledger, rebuilds the complete manifest identity, compares the separately supplied bundle ID, checks every input-file hash and byte length, and refuses stale results before starting the external process. It then writes the console log and exit code under results/. The bundle ID is an out-of-band integrity reference, not a secret or a digital signature.\n\nFor example, a FACETS host can run:\n\nset \"FACETS_EXE=<full path to licensed Facets executable>\"\nset \"BRIDGE_BUNDLE_ID=sha256:<Mac-side retained 64-hex digest>\"\nrun_facets_windows.cmd\n\nFor ConQuest on Windows, use CONQUEST_EXE and run_conquest_windows.cmd in the same sequence.\n\nOn macOS, first compare the two script digests against conquest_host_preflight.macos_verifier.sha256 and conquest_host_preflight.macos_runner.sha256 retained outside the transfer. Then run a fresh bundle copy from a normal Terminal session:\n\ncd conquest_minimal_rsm_bridge\nexport CONQUEST_EXE='/Applications/ConQuest/ConQuest'\nexport BRIDGE_BUNDLE_ID='sha256:<retained 64-hex bundle digest>'\n/bin/sh run_conquest_macos.sh\n\nThe macOS runner neither removes quarantine attributes, re-signs the executable, nor disables Gatekeeper. If macOS blocks the product, resolve access for the exact ACER executable through the normal Privacy & Security workflow. Some ConQuest builds also write product state outside the bundle; a restrictive sandbox can therefore fail before command processing even when the bridge is valid. Use a normal user Terminal rather than weakening system-wide security.\n\nOn either platform, separately record the product-reported software version, SHA-256 of the exact executable, and UTC execution time. Keep these three values outside the returned directory: adding an operator note under results/ violates the declared-output contract, and adding an undeclared root file is also rejected. Return the whole directory without editing its inputs.\n\nThe receipt accepts a lowercase 64-hex executable digest and an execution time in the exact YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ssZ form (without fractional seconds). From Command Prompt, these Windows PowerShell commands produce the accepted forms; copy their outputs to the separate operator record rather than redirecting them into the bundle:\n\npowershell -NoProfile -Command \"(Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $env:FACETS_EXE -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()\"\npowershell -NoProfile -Command \"[DateTime]::UtcNow.ToString('yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ')\"\n\nFor ConQuest, replace FACETS_EXE with CONQUEST_EXE. Record the product-reported version separately. The generated scripts use syntax intended for Windows PowerShell 5.1, but successful execution on that path must still be established on the actual Windows host. On macOS, the equivalent values can be recorded outside the bundle with:\n\n/usr/bin/shasum -a 256 \"$CONQUEST_EXE\"\n/bin/date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ'\n\nScript generation is never treated as execution evidence.\n\nBack on the Mac, first recheck the input bundle and then bind all declared raw return files to a receipt. The example reads the three separately recorded operator values from Mac environment variables so a placeholder version, digest, or timestamp cannot be mistaken for evidence:\n\nvalidate_external_bridge_bundle(\n    \"facets_minimal_rsm_bridge\";\n    expected_bundle_id = facets_bundle_id,\n)\n\nfacets_receipt = external_bridge_result_receipt(\n    \"facets_minimal_rsm_bridge\";\n    expected_bundle_id = facets_bundle_id,\n    software_version = ENV[\"FACETS_REPORTED_VERSION\"],\n    executable_sha256 = ENV[\"FACETS_EXE_SHA256\"],\n    executed_at_utc = ENV[\"FACETS_EXECUTED_AT_UTC\"],\n)\n\nUse the same sequence for ConQuest. Its parameter export can then be read with load_conquest_parameter_export, preferably with the parameter-file hash from the receipt. The parser accepts a decimal parameter number/value pair and the optional single trailing /* ... */ comment emitted by ConQuest 5.47.5. It returns the trimmed comment as source_comment, but deliberately leaves semantic_parameter_identity_resolved = false: a comment is source evidence, not yet a version-independent item/rater/step adapter. The reader streams the source, rejects non-decimal or malformed rows and logical lines longer than 4096 bytes, and caps an export at 1,000,000 parameter pairs.\n\nConQuest 5.47.5 can create a zero-byte conquest_labels.txt when the generated bridge identifiers already appear in the model. The file remains required and hash-bound by the receipt; only its content is allowed to be empty. Semantic reconstruction should use the commented parameter export together with the returned design matrix, not assume that a nonempty labels export exists. The gamma column in the threshold export is a generalized rater–item threshold, not the source step-parameter vector.\n\nFor the exact ConQuest 5.47.5, three-category RSM/PCM overlap, the complete returned bundle can instead be passed through the public semantic layer:\n\nconquest_semantic = load_conquest_semantic_parameters(\n    \"conquest_minimal_rsm_bridge\",\n    spec;\n    expected_bundle_id = conquest_bundle_id,\n    software_version = ENV[\"CONQUEST_REPORTED_VERSION\"],\n    executable_sha256 = ENV[\"CONQUEST_EXE_SHA256\"],\n    executed_at_utc = ENV[\"CONQUEST_EXECUTED_AT_UTC\"],\n)\n\n@assert conquest_semantic.semantic_parameter_identity_resolved\n@assert conquest_semantic.source_gauge_validated\n@assert !conquest_semantic.destination_gauge_aligned\n@assert !conquest_semantic.numerical_comparison_allowed\n\nThis is intentionally not a path-only interpretation of the raw parameter file. The function revalidates the complete bundle, constructs a receipt from the current returned-file snapshot, and requires the hash-bound control, ratings, identifier map, category map, and observation map to match spec, requires parameter numbers and comments to match the design- matrix columns in their exact order, and verifies every generalized-item by category design coefficient. It then reconstructs the final rater and item, and the final common or item-specific step, as the negative sum of that block's free values. The source orientation is retained directly: positive rater values mean greater severity, positive item values mean greater difficulty, and step values are subtractive transitions. No global sign reversal is used.\n\nThe result remains in ConQuest's term-wise sum-to-zero source gauge. It does not create package parameters, anchors, or a numerical agreement claim, and it does not turn receipt completion into convergence evidence. Versions other than 5.47.5 and category counts other than three fail closed until matching execution fixtures and structural tests are added.","title":"Offline bridge for a licensed execution host"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#What-a-returned-bundle-establishes","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"A valid receipt establishes input continuity, a zero recorded process exit, the presence and byte-exact hashes of the declared files, no undeclared root or result entries, and the absence of a small set of recognized fatal markers. It records operator-reported completion but does not independently establish that the executable ran, authenticate it, establish convergence, resolve parameter labels, align gauges, demonstrate numerical agreement, or establish product equivalence. The bridge therefore does not turn a reported external run into validation evidence by itself.\n\nThe current bridge accepts unanchored specifications only. The ConQuest semantic adapter reconstructs the supported source gauge; it does not align it to the package gauge or create anchors. anchor_refit_plan checks destination declarations separately and runs neither an external calibration nor a package refit. A supported numerical declaration can be passed to fit, as shown below.","title":"What a returned bundle establishes"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Parameter-and-output-crosswalk","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Source concept BayesianMGMFRM.jl surface Interpretation boundary\nPerson ability/location posterior_summary, wright_map_data A posterior distribution, not a FACETS JMLE measure or ConQuest MLE/WLE/EAP.\nRater severity/harshness posterior_summary, rater_diagnostics Positive destination values lower expected scores. Confirm source direction first.\nItem/criterion difficulty posterior_summary, wright_map_data Positive destination values lower expected scores. Re-express source constraints before comparing.\nRating-scale or PCM steps threshold_map_data Uses the package's sum-to-zero step convention; compare reconstructed steps, not raw free coordinates.\nFair average fair_average_summary Posterior balanced-reference expected score; document the reference grid.\nInfit/outfit with uncertainty fit_stats Default Bayesian posterior diagnostic.\nFACETS-labelled MnSq/df/ZSTD facets_report Unit-weighted posterior-mean plug-in approximation; posterior uncertainty is not propagated and FACETS numerical equality is not claimed.\nSeparation/reliability separation_reliability_summary (legacy); BayesianMGMFRM.posterior_reliability (explicit cohort) The legacy screen warns and must not supply reportable Strata. The new posterior variance ratio has an explicit finite-cohort target; it is not FACETS separation or population/design reliability.\nCommon-response score agreement BayesianMGMFRM.rater_agreement Observed exact/adjacent rates and joint-posterior model expectations use original categories and explicit response/item matching. No recentering at each rater's lowest PCM category, predictive interval or FACETS numerical identity is claimed. See the agreement guide.\nMachine-readable report fit_report(fit_result; view = :public) Hashable package report; it is not a FACETS Scorefile or ConQuest export parser.\n\nFACETS can write measure tables for transfer to SPSS, Excel, R, and text files; the available fields include measure, standard error, fit, group, weight, and status. See the official score-and-measure output description. Migration code should preserve the original labels and status fields alongside the package rows instead of joining only by row order.","title":"Parameter and output crosswalk"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Important-non-equivalences","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Axis FACETS ACER ConQuest Current destination\nEstimator Primarily JMLE/UCON, with a specialized PMLE path Marginal maximum likelihood using EM and numerical integration by default; JML and other methods are selectable Bayesian joint posterior with explicit priors, sampled by the selected package backend\nPerson treatment Person is another measured facet Person/case ability is latent under MML, or jointly estimated under JML One parameter per observed person with a proper prior in the minimal model\nIdentification Centering, non-centering, individual anchors, group anchors, and user scaling are configurable Constraints follow the generated/imported design; fixed parameter, covariance, regression, score, or case anchors are available First rater/item fixed to zero by default; exact individual rater/item hard anchors replace that block gauge; threshold steps sum to zero\nThreshold sharing Common, element-specific, or named scale structures Controlled by step interactions One common RSM structure or item-specific PCM structures per fit\nWeights Model, element, and observation weights multiply as replications caseweight changes item-response-model estimation Unit-weighted likelihood only; weighted source analyses are not an exact overlap\nInteractions Secondary residual-based bias analysis is available Interactions can be columns of the main design matrix DFF/bias output is screening-only; fitted interaction effects are not supported in the stable minimal fit\nFacet breadth Many arbitrary facets and response formats Generalized items, additive/interacted terms, and imported design matrices The fitted minimal model has person, rater, and item facets; optional fields are metadata unless explicitly documented otherwise\nUncertainty Frequentist standard errors and fit summaries Asymptotic errors, latent estimates, or plausible values depend on method Posterior intervals and posterior predictive uncertainty\n\nFACETS documents the JMLE/PMLE behavior and its finite-sample considerations in Estimation considerations. ConQuest documents its marginal likelihood and EM algorithm in Chapter 3, Section 3.1.3, and its selectable estimation methods in Chapter 4, Section 4.7.22. An absence of JMLE or MMLE in this package is therefore an estimator-scope difference, not a missing component of the Bayesian estimator.","title":"Important non-equivalences"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Unsupported-source-features-must-fail-visibly","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Use the following migration rules:\n\nIf source weights are not all one, stop the direct migration. Record whether each weight is a case, model, element, or observation weight and why it was used. Do not drop it silently.\nIf more than one rating-scale group is present, record the group assignment and threshold convention. Do not substitute one global RSM or independent item PCMs without a new model decision.\nIf additional facets or interactions affect the source likelihood, retain their columns as metadata for review, but do not describe the destination fit as equivalent. Collapsing an interaction into a compound item changes the estimand and requires a separate justification.\nIf source anchors or user scaling are active, transform them to the destination logit direction and identification before any comparison. The stable fit applies only exact individual rater/item hard anchors; soft, threshold, and group-mean anchors remain unsupported.\nCompare source and destination results only after matching observed rows, response categories, signs, constraints, threshold sharing, and parameter labels. Compare point recovery separately from uncertainty calibration.","title":"Unsupported source features must fail visibly"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Anchor-constrained-refitting-policy","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"The stable minimal path accepts exact individual rater/item hard anchors. The anchored level is removed from the sampled coordinates and restored at its declared value in the likelihood, prediction, Wright-map rows, rater diagnostics, and design manifest. This replaces the default first-level-zero gauge for that block; it is not stacked on top of it.\n\nThe numerical declaration is intentionally small: block, an explicit level (or target), a finite logit value, and type = :hard. Source hashes and external artifact metadata are not fitting prerequisites for a distributed package:\n\nanchored_spec = mfrm_spec(data;\n    thresholds = :rating_scale,\n    anchors = [(\n        block = :rater,\n        level = \"R1\",\n        value = 0.0,\n        type = :hard,\n    )],\n)\n\nplan = anchor_refit_plan(anchored_spec; require_provenance = false)\n@assert plan.status == :hard_anchor_candidate_ready\n@assert plan.candidate_supported\n@assert plan.numerical_fit_supported\n@assert plan.capability == :stable_hard_anchor_fit_available\n\nanchored_fit = fit(anchored_spec; backend = :advancedhmc)\n\nanchor_refit_plan remains a non-mutating declaration check; it does not itself run a fit. With require_provenance = true, it can additionally audit the following optional handoff metadata:\n\nField Accepted contract\nvalue A non-Bool Real that remains finite and non-underflowing after conversion to Float64\nsource A Symbol or string matching [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]{0,63}\nsource_version A nonempty, printable string of at most 128 bytes, without leading or trailing whitespace\nsource_model :mfrm_rsm for thresholds = :rating_scale, or :mfrm_pcm for :partial_credit\nsource_estimator One of :jml, :pmle, :mml, or :mcmc\nsource_hash A lowercase 64-hex SHA-256 string, optionally prefixed by sha256:; the plan checks format, not source-byte correspondence\nsource_scale :logit\nsign :severity_positive for a rater anchor or :difficulty_positive for an item anchor\n\nHere source_scale and sign describe the already transformed numeric anchor value. A FACETS user scale, reversed orientation, or nonidentity transformation must be resolved before constructing the declaration. The optional provenance audit never converts the value and never controls whether the stable numerical fit is available.\n\nsource_hash_format_valid = true means only that the declaration has the accepted lowercase SHA-256 shape. The plan does not receive the source bytes and therefore always reports source_bytes_verified = false; callers must verify and preserve the byte-to-digest correspondence outside this plan. Likewise, provenance_complete = true means the required fields are present and satisfy their field contracts, not that the external artifact is authentic or substantively correct.\n\nrequire_provenance = false permits all provenance fields to be absent. It never makes a supplied but invalid audit field acceptable for a strict audit. Every row records normalized_value, value_issue, declared_scale, normalized_scale, scale_issue, missing_provenance_fields, invalid_provenance_fields, and provenance_issues so rejection is machine-readable.\n\nA hard anchor carrying scale, sd, or prior_scale is ambiguous and is rejected; uncertainty metadata must not silently turn an exact constraint into a soft prior. Conversely, a soft anchor requires a positive finite, non-Boolean prior scale that remains positive and finite after Float64 conversion. Soft anchors remain inspection-only. In particular, a soft anchor on the default reference would otherwise create a constant prior; the package rejects that boundary rather than silently pretending it is informative.\n\nThe existing distinction between parameter anchors and common-response linking must remain. A larger number of common responses does not turn them into fixed parameter anchors, and fixed parameter anchors do not create observed links. They do not waive the ordinary connectedness/rank validation gate. This package therefore rejects disconnected person-rater-item graphs even when an individual hard anchor is declared in every component. FACETS-style group anchoring instead sets a facet-group mean and assumes the anchored groups are exchangeable; that is a different, currently unsupported model. Wind and Stager (2019) show that within-component design and misfit affect estimates under group anchoring, while Myford and Wolfe (2000) distinguish minimal connectivity from link quality. No universal anchor percentage should be recommended.","title":"Anchor-constrained refitting policy"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Individual-hard-anchors-for-the-minimal-model","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Exact item and rater anchors use the affine selection map\n\nbeta=b+Cz\n\nwhere b contains fixed values, C selects free coordinates, and z is sampled. Unknown levels, missing targets, Boolean/nonfinite/unrepresentable values, duplicate targets, hard anchors carrying a prior scale, and unsupported blocks fail closed before stable compilation. Multiple exact anchors are allowed; each fixed coordinate is omitted from the prior and sampler.\n\nAt the likelihood level, one exact anchor in a rater or item block only chooses that block's location gauge: shifting the anchor can be offset by the free facet coordinates and person locations without changing response probabilities. Two or more anchors in the same block additionally fix within-block contrasts. A contaminated multi-anchor contrast is therefore a substantive model restriction, not a harmless change of origin. Sensitivity work should cross anchor count, facet location, and plausible value perturbations; adding anchors is not automatically stabilizing. The zero-centered-prior qualification appears below.\n\nKeep this parameter-anchor question separate from common-response linking. Wind and Jones (2018) vary linking- set size, location, and model-data fit, but explicitly do not provide parameter- recovery evidence and did not manipulate rater effects. Uto (2021) fixes common-rater and common-task values from a base test and shows that required commonality changes with missingness, test scale, population differences, and drift. Neither establishes a universal exact-parameter anchor count. Kopf et al. (2015) provide indirect motivation for the contamination stress from a DIF-anchor context, not direct validation of this MFRM implementation. Likewise, Robitzsch (2024) analytically derives bias and linking error for fixed-item-parameter calibration under random uniform DIF in a 2PL model. It supports treating fixed-parameter contamination as a model risk, but it is not direct validation of this package's polytomous MFRM path. Robitzsch (2024) further separates person-sampling standard error from item-selection linking error and compares robust and nonrobust linking under DIF. This supports keeping anchor-set sensitivity separate from ordinary posterior uncertainty; its dichotomous 2PL results do not supply a robust-linking method for the present MFRM.\n\nChanging a single anchor can preserve the response likelihood after shifting free facet coordinates and person locations, but the fixed zero-centered priors are not translated with it. Thus a likelihood-equivalent change of origin need not preserve the posterior. More diffuse priors attenuate this coordinate effect; that fact alone does not justify choosing a diffuse prior.\n\nFor two incompatible anchors in one facet, removing either member leaves one anchor whose value can be absorbed by a location shift. Leave-one-anchor-out sensitivity can expose an incompatible pair but cannot identify the contaminated member from the response likelihood alone. That attribution requires external information or a model for source uncertainty.\n\nPredictions from this fixed-effect model concern the observed facet levels. New-person, new-item, or new-rater prediction is unsupported because the model has no hierarchical facet population to marginalize.\n\nFixed coordinates are recorded as unsampled in the design/fit manifest and as is_fixed = true with status = :hard_anchor in Wright-map rows. They receive exact zero-width intervals there, not artificial R-hat or ESS values. The normalized anchor declaration participates in the semantic model identity. Cache keys use that identity, so reordering the same declarations does not create a different cache request; changing an anchor or its recorded provenance still does.\n\nfit_report(fit; view = :public) keeps those constants in the dedicated fixed_coordinates.rows table and out of posterior-summary rows. Each report row states sampled = false, prior_applied = false, and posterior_estimated = false. When at least one declared hard anchor is present, fixed_coordinates.warning_rows contains two baseline warnings: the listed values have no posterior, prior, or sampling uncertainty, and the zero-centered prior on free identified coordinates is not shifted with the anchor. Any estimation or linking error in externally obtained anchor values is therefore not propagated. Consequently, likelihood-equivalent anchor changes need not be prior- or posterior-invariant. The Markdown renderer places both warnings near the top of the report before the section summary. If two or more anchors occur in the same facet, a third warning marks the resulting within-facet contrast restriction and requests contamination or drift sensitivity. Anchoring one rater and one item still selects one gauge per block and does not trigger it.","title":"Individual hard anchors for the minimal model"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Unsupported-anchor-types-and-imported-records","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Threshold, group-mean, and soft anchors are not supported for fitting. Do not silently recenter imported threshold values or replace a group-mean constraint with individual fixed values: those operations change the declared model. A soft anchor expresses uncertainty through an informative prior; the current exact-anchor path does not propagate that uncertainty.\n\nStarting values and anchors serve different purposes. Resolve source labels, signs, location and scale conventions before declaring a destination anchor, and retain the original values and transformations with the analysis. load_conquest_semantic_parameters returns source-gauge records only. It does not import destination starting values or anchors, and the package does not provide a general anchor-file round trip. The optional provenance fields accepted by anchor_refit_plan are listed above.","title":"Unsupported anchor types and imported records"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-facets-conquest/#Migration-acceptance-checklist","page":"Migrating from FACETS and ConQuest","text":"Before describing a source and destination run as an overlap comparison, record:\n\nthe exact source software version, command/specification file, estimator, and convergence settings;\nthe matched response rows and category coding;\nrating-scale versus partial-credit sharing and every scale group;\nsign, location, user-scale, and threshold transformations;\nanchors, starting values, linking responses, and weights as separate fields;\nthe source and destination parameter-label map;\npoint-estimate agreement, uncertainty behavior, predictive checks, and nonconvergence or unsupported cells as separate results;\nhashes for source data/config/output and destination data/spec/fit/report.\n\nPassing this checklist establishes a reproducible comparison of a named overlap target. It does not establish estimator equivalence, product parity, or superiority of one program over another.","title":"Migration acceptance checklist"}]
}
